#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ccrc — the lifecycle CLI for a Claude Code fleet box: installs, updates and
# diagnoses a box, as a sibling to `ccd`, which drives sessions once one is
# already running. Stage 2b of the 2026-08-15-stage2b-ccrc-cli-and-doctor plan:
# `version` (Task 1), `doctor` (Tasks 4-5), `status` (Task 7) and `adopt`
# (Task 8) are all implemented, so the "not implemented yet" register that
# stood in for the unbuilt verbs has no caller and is gone with the last one
# that used it. Every verb name in the usage line now does its job.
#
# ── WHY `set -uo pipefail`, NOT `-euo` ─────────────────────────────────────
# `ccrc-adopt` (this file's own model for argument handling) uses `-e`
# because it is a straight-line pipeline of "measure, or die" steps: any
# failure really should stop everything right there. `ccrc doctor` (Task 3+)
# is the opposite shape — a LIST of independent checks, each of which SIGNALS
# by returning non-zero, and the whole point of running doctor is to see every
# check's verdict in one pass, not to abort at the first failing one. `-e`
# here would turn "the box is missing three things" into "the box is missing
# one thing, and doctor crashed" the moment the first check's `return 1` hit a
# `set -e` shell. So: no `-e`, ever, in this file. If a future change adds
# `-e` back "because it caught a real bug", it has broken doctor; don't.
# `-u` (unset variables are errors) and `pipefail` (a failing left-hand side
# of a pipe fails the whole pipeline) carry no such conflict and stay on.
#
# ── EXIT CODES (house convention — see ccd/ccrc-adopt:83-106 and
#    deploy/verify-service.sh:50-54, both already committed to this table) ──
#   0 = success.
#   1 = the tool ran and the answer was bad (a check failed, a stamp did not
#       parse, a verb is not implemented yet).
#   2 = USAGE error — an unknown verb or argument. Kept distinct from 1 so a
#       caller can tell "you typed it wrong" apart from "doctor found a real
#       problem": scripting `ccrc doctor; case $? in 1) …` must never trip on
#       a typo instead of a genuine finding.
#
# ── DIAGNOSTIC REGISTERS (adopt's own convention, reused here) ─────────────
# Every line this script prints to stderr is prefixed "$PROG: ", then one of:
#   - a bare refusal — "$PROG: <what went wrong>" (`_ccrc_die`, exits 1).
#   - a usage error — "$PROG: unknown argument: …" (`_ccrc_usage_die`, exits 2).
# The RESULT of a verb (what the operator actually asked for) goes to
# stdout, same split as ccrc-adopt's JSON-on-stdout / diagnostics-on-stderr.
#
# Adopt's THIRD register, "note: …" (informational, does not change the exit
# code), has no caller in `ccrc` and its `_ccrc_say` helper has been REMOVED
# (Task 4 review, Minor 4). It was carried from Task 1 on the prediction that
# doctor's `remedy:` lines would ride it; they do not (see the next paragraph),
# so it stood as untested, uncalled code whose only documentation was a
# statement of what it was for that had become false. Task 1 guessed `ccrc
# status` would be its first caller; Task 7 measured that to be wrong too —
# status prints FACTS, and a fact is the RESULT of the verb, so every line it
# writes goes to stdout. Re-add `_ccrc_say` in one line together with a real
# caller, and not before.
#
# DOCTOR'S "remedy: …" LINES ARE NOT IN THAT STDERR TABLE, and this paragraph
# is the correction of a guess Task 1 made before doctor existed: it predicted
# that a non-pass verdict's remedy would ride `_ccrc_say` on stderr. It does
# not. A verdict line and its remedy are two halves of ONE result — the
# contract `server/test/ccrc-doctor.test.ts` pins is "the line immediately
# after a FAIL is that FAIL's remedy" — so splitting the pair across two
# streams would break it for every caller that redirects one of them, which is
# every caller that pipes doctor into a report. Both halves are the RESULT and
# both go to stdout; see `ccd/ccrc-doctor-checks`'s own header for the full
# shape.
#
# Do NOT add a `# ccrc:generated …` marker line to this file by imitation of
# `ccd`'s line 2: that marker is `shared/mark.mjs`'s format for files a
# generator writes and would refuse to run over hand edits to; `ccd` carries
# one spuriously (it predates the marker meaning something), and `ccrc` is,
# and stays, hand-written.
set -uo pipefail

# ── THE PLATFORM LAYER — one file, two userlands ────────────────────────
# ccd was written against GNU/Linux and says so out loud: `_reg_set`'s header
# (below) names `mv -T`, `stat -c`, `readlink -f` and `df -P` as assumptions
# this file makes rather than probes, and three sites already `die` by name
# when `flock` is absent. macOS is the second userland this file now runs on,
# and it is BSD: no `mv -T`, no `stat -c`, no `sha256sum`, no `/proc`.
#
# EVERY FUNCTION BELOW IS THE GNU COMMAND, VERBATIM, ON LINUX. That is the
# whole contract of this block. Both production fleet boxes are GNU/Linux and
# a macOS port is not allowed to become a rewrite of the platform they run on,
# so the Linux arm of each function is the exact bytes the call site used
# before this block existed; the Darwin arm is reached only when `uname` says
# so. A test pins that property (`macos-platform.test.ts`) by running each
# function's two arms against the same fixture.
#
# `CCD_OS` IS COMPUTED ONCE, AT SOURCE TIME, and never re-probed. `_reg_set`
# is the hottest path in this file — ~11 live sessions re-stamping
# `supervised` every 30s — and it calls `_plat_mv_notdir`. A `uname` fork per
# registry write is precisely the per-call cost `_reg_set`'s own header
# refused to pay for a `command -v`, and the answer cannot change under a
# running process.
# The session registry, spelled here so this block is self-contained and can
# be the SAME BYTES in ccd and ccrc — see the pin test. ccd defines $REG to
# this identical path a few lines down for its own ~200 call sites; that is
# one path with two names by necessity, not a second source of truth, and
# `macos-platform.test.ts` pins the two spellings equal.
_SVC_REG="$HOME/.cc-sessions"
#
# `$OSTYPE` FIRST, AND `uname` ONLY AS A FALLBACK. OSTYPE is a bash variable
# baked in at COMPILE time (`darwin25`, `linux-gnu`), so reading it needs no
# fork and — the part that matters — no PATH. `uname` needs both, and a box
# whose PATH does not carry it would silently answer `linux` on macOS: every
# Darwin arm skipped, every systemd call attempted, and the refusal an
# operator meets naming `systemctl` on a machine that never had one.
#
# NOT HYPOTHETICAL: found by `ccrc install: a macOS box missing what ccd needs`,
# whose PATH is built explicitly and had no `uname` on it. A stripped PATH is
# exactly the shape a service manager hands a job, which is where this would
# have bitten for real.
CCD_OS=linux
case "${OSTYPE:-}" in
  darwin*) CCD_OS=darwin ;;
  linux*)  CCD_OS=linux ;;
  *) case "$(uname -s 2>/dev/null)" in Darwin) CCD_OS=darwin ;; esac ;;
esac

# GNU `mv -fT`: rename, and REFUSE when the destination is a directory.
#
# THE REFUSAL IS LOAD-BEARING, not incidental — see the note in `cmd_ws_hold`
# (`mv -fT`'s `-T` … a bare `mv -f` would move the tmp inside the directory
# and exit 0): `ccd-hold.test.ts` stands in for an unwritable registry by
# making `$REG/<id>.hold` a DIRECTORY, because chmod does not constrain root
# and a directory does. Dropping `-T` turns that guard's refusal into a false
# success, which is the one outcome it exists to prevent.
#
# `-L` BEFORE `-d`, and that order is the whole correctness of the Darwin arm:
# `-T` treats a symlink-to-directory as the NAME and replaces it, where
# `[ -d ]` follows the link and would refuse a rename GNU performs.
#
# NOT ATOMIC THE WAY `-T` IS, and written down rather than hidden: between the
# test and the rename another process could create a directory at the
# destination. Nothing in ccd can reach that window — every destination is
# `$REG/<id>.<field>`, and this function is its only writer — so the race is
# unreachable here rather than tolerated.
_plat_mv_notdir() {   # <src> <dest> -> 0 iff <src> is now at <dest>
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    if [ ! -L "$2" ] && [ -d "$2" ]; then return 1; fi
    mv -f -- "$1" "$2"
  else
    mv -fT -- "$1" "$2"
  fi
}

# `stat -c` -> `stat -f`. Three format letters, three functions, because a
# single `_plat_stat <fmt> <file>` would put the GNU spelling at every call
# site and leave this block translating a string it did not choose.
_plat_mtime() {   # <file> -> mtime, epoch seconds
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then stat -f %m "$@"; else stat -c %Y "$@"; fi
}
_plat_size() {    # <file> -> size in bytes
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then stat -f %z "$@"; else stat -c %s "$@"; fi
}
_plat_devino() {  # <file> -> "<device>:<inode>", the identity of a lock
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then stat -f '%d:%i' "$@"; else stat -c '%d:%i' "$@"; fi
}
# `du -sb` — APPARENT SIZE IN BYTES — is GNU coreutils, and BSD's `du` rejects
# `-b` outright ("invalid option"). It is not a formatting difference that can
# be papered over with `-k`: BSD reports 512-byte or 1K BLOCKS, so a 10-byte
# `.env` sizes as 4096 and every byte figure ccd prints, sums and compares
# would be wrong by orders of magnitude on one platform.
#
# THE DARWIN ARM SUMS FILES' AND SYMLINKS' OWN SIZES, COUNTING EACH INODE
# ONCE — because dedup is part of what `du` MEANS, not an optimisation: the
# ws-gc aggregate caller passes every worktree in ONE invocation precisely
# so inodes shared between them are counted once ("the exact figure that
# removing the lot would free"), and transcripts here deliberately wear one
# inode under several names (`_plat_cp_replace`'s own header). The first cut
# summed `-type f` sizes with no inode tracking: every hardlink name counted
# again, and the report's "rows can sum to more than the total" sentence was
# false on macOS — the total EQUALLED the row sum and overstated the
# reclaim. `-type l` rides along because `du -sb` counts a symlink's own
# st_size too (directories need nothing: GNU du charges a directory 0 in
# byte totals — measured, coreutils 9.4). `stat -f '%d %i %z'` hands awk the
# identity to dedup on (device:inode) and the size in one exec batch.
#
# `awk` PRINTS `s+0`, so an empty walk answers `0` rather than the empty
# string — every caller validates the result with `^[0-9]+$` and would read a
# blank as "could not size this", which is a refusal, not a zero.
# A PARTIAL TOTAL MUST BE A REFUSAL, NOT A NUMBER, and that is the one thing
# this shim gets wrong if it is written the obvious way. GNU `du` returns
# NON-ZERO when it could not read everything — it still prints a total, and
# every caller here checks the status precisely because the printed figure is
# then a partial truth. A `find | awk` that swallows errors answers a smaller
# number with rc 0, which is the shape this file bans by name: "a size gate
# that reads the file anyway when it cannot measure it is not a gate".
#
# So the Darwin arm watches `find`'s OWN stderr and status. Anything on either
# — an unreadable directory, a `stat` that failed — and the whole call refuses,
# leaving the caller to report "could not size this" exactly as it does on
# Linux. (Pinned by `ccd-ws-gc.test.ts`'s "refuses a PARTIAL du total — the
# failure that looks like an answer".)
_plat_bytes() {   # <path>... -> total apparent bytes, one integer; rc 1 if not all of it
  # THE LINUX ARM IS THE TWO ORIGINAL PIPELINES, VERBATIM — including which
  # one runs for how many paths. `du -sb <one>` prints ONE line and `du -scb
  # <many>` adds a `total`, so a single shim spelling cannot serve both: the
  # `-c` form over one path is still fine for real du, but `ccd-archive`'s
  # fixture stubs `du()` as a shell function printing a single `0\tx` line, and
  # an awk looking for `total` reads that as a failed measurement. A genuine
  # zero would then be indistinguishable from an unreadable tree, which is the
  # exact conflation the surrounding code exists to prevent.
  #
  # `pipefail` IS WHAT MAKES THE STATUS HONEST here (ccd sets it at the top of
  # the file): the pipeline's rc is du's whenever du fails, so neither arm
  # needs a guard of its own.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then
    # `head -n1` IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidying: a filename may contain a
    # NEWLINE, and `du -s` then spans two physical lines — `cut -f1` alone
    # emits a field from each, the caller's `^[0-9]+$` test fails, and a
    # perfectly readable tree is reported unmeasurable. One call site always
    # had it and the other did not; folding it in here gives both the
    # newline-proof reading, which is a superset of what either had.
    # (`ccd-ws-gc.test.ts`: "a gitignored filename holding a NEWLINE cannot
    # forge a row or move the tally".)
    if (( $# == 1 )); then du -sb "$1" | head -n1 | cut -f1; return $?; fi
    du -scb "$@" | awk '$2 == "total" {print $1}'
    return $?
  fi
  local p errf err out rc
  for p in "$@"; do [ -e "$p" ] || return 1; done
  errf=$(_plat_mktemp) || return 1
  out=$(find "$@" \( -type f -o -type l \) -exec stat -f '%d %i %z' {} + 2>"$errf" \
        | awk '{ if (!seen[$1":"$2]++) s+=$3 } END{print s+0}'); rc=$?
  err=$(cat "$errf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$errf"
  # THE DIAGNOSTIC IS RELAYED, not swallowed. GNU `du` writes "cannot read
  # directory …" to stderr and the callers CAPTURE it — `_ws_gc_bytes` puts it
  # in its refusal, and a test reads it back. A shim that kept the error to
  # itself would leave every macOS refusal saying only "could not size this",
  # dropping the one sentence that says WHICH path and WHY.
  [ -z "$err" ] || printf '%s\n' "$err" >&2
  # THE PARTIAL TOTAL IS STILL PRINTED, and the status is what says it is
  # partial — `du`'s own shape, kept deliberately. A caller that reads stdout
  # and ignores the status gets the same wrong-but-plausible number it would
  # get on Linux, so the two platforms fail identically rather than one of them
  # failing in a new way. (`ccd-ws-gc.test.ts` pins it: "du prints \"0\" on
  # stdout for a root it cannot enter, not nothing".)
  printf '%s\n' "$out"
  { (( rc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$err" ]]; } || return 1
}

# `date +%s%3N` IS GNU. BSD's date has no `%N` at all: it
# prints the literal letter, so `+%s%3N` answers `17876553263N` — a string
# that is not a number, which `jq --argjson` rejects outright. Measured: the
# session hook's whole state write failed on macOS because of this one format,
# silently (`|| exit 0`), leaving every session with no hookstate file.
#
# `EPOCHREALTIME` IS THE ANSWER ON BOTH PLATFORMS — FROM BASH 5.0, WHICH IS
# NOT THE FLOOR. A bash builtin (`<seconds>.<microseconds>`), so no fork and
# no coreutils; on Linux it agrees with `date +%s%3N` to the millisecond
# (measured side by side). But the declared floor is 4.4 (install.sh's macOS
# preflight and `ccrc`'s own gate, pinned equal by single-definition — 4.4
# because empty-array "${a[@]}" under `set -u` is fatal below it, not 5.0),
# so a 4.4 box passes install and lands on the fallback — and on macOS the
# bare fallback used to reproduce the exact `<epoch>3N` failure this
# function was written to remove. So the fallback VALIDATES: a non-numeric
# answer degrades to whole seconds ×1000 — millisecond precision lost, the
# write kept — instead of handing `jq --argjson` a string it rejects.
# D-944: the review offered raise-the-floor-to-5.0 as the alternative fix;
# fixing the fallback was chosen instead, because with a correct fallback a
# 4.4 box WORKS (degraded to second precision), where the 5.0 floor would
# refuse it outright — and the floor stays one value in three files. (The
# adversarial pass then measured the floor itself: 4.2/4.3 die on ccd's
# empty-array set -u expansions regardless, so the declared floor is 4.4.)
# `ccd/session-hook.sh`'s `_hook_epoch_ms` is a deliberate local copy of
# this body (the hook sources nothing); a test pins the two identical.
#
# THE SEPARATOR IS NOT ASSUMED TO BE A DOT: `EPOCHREALTIME` is formatted with
# the LOCALE's decimal point, and a box running a comma locale would otherwise
# have its timestamps silently truncated to seconds.
_plat_epoch_ms() {   # -> epoch milliseconds, 13 digits
  if [ -n "${EPOCHREALTIME:-}" ]; then
    local s="${EPOCHREALTIME%%[.,]*}" f="${EPOCHREALTIME#*[.,]}"
    f="${f}000"; printf '%s%s' "$s" "${f:0:3}"
    return 0
  fi
  local t; t=$(date +%s%3N 2>/dev/null)
  if [[ "$t" =~ ^[0-9]{13,}$ ]]; then printf '%s' "$t"; else printf '%s000' "$(date +%s)"; fi
}

# `cp --remove-destination` IS GNU. BSD's cp rejects the flag outright, and
# the difference it names is not cosmetic: without it `cp` OPENS an existing
# destination and writes through it, so a destination that is a HARDLINK to
# another name rewrites that other name too. This function carries transcripts
# between account config dirs, where one inode deliberately wears several
# names — writing through would corrupt the sibling it was trying to preserve.
#
# `rm -f` FIRST is exactly what the GNU flag does: unlink, then create. Two
# calls instead of one, so a crash between them leaves no destination rather
# than a half-written one — the same window `cp` has internally, and the same
# recovery (the next carry re-places it).
_plat_cp_replace() {   # <src> <dest> — copy, preserving times, REPLACING the inode
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then cp -p --remove-destination "$1" "$2"; return $?; fi
  rm -f -- "$2" 2>/dev/null
  cp -p -- "$1" "$2"
}

# BSD `mktemp` IGNORES `$TMPDIR` when it is given no template — measured:
# with TMPDIR pointed at a directory of the caller's choosing, macOS still
# answers inside its own per-user Darwin temp dir, while GNU mktemp honours
# the variable. An operator who points TMPDIR at a volume with room, or at a
# directory they control, is silently overruled on one platform.
#
# An explicit template fixes it, and the LINUX ARM STAYS THE BARE CALL so the
# path shape that platform produces is unchanged.
_plat_mktemp() {     # -> a new temp FILE, honouring $TMPDIR on both
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/ccd.XXXXXXXXXX"; else mktemp; fi
}
_plat_mktemp_d() {   # -> a new temp DIRECTORY, honouring $TMPDIR on both
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/ccd.XXXXXXXXXX"; else mktemp -d; fi
}

_plat_mode() {    # <file> -> its permission bits, octal, e.g. 600
  # `%Mp%Lp`, NOT `%Lp` ALONE. `%Lp` is the low NINE bits (user/group/other)
  # and `%Mp` the special nibble (setuid/setgid/sticky); GNU's `%a` prints
  # both, so under the first cut a 4755 file answered `4755` on Linux and
  # `755` here — the arms answered different questions, and the comment that
  # claimed `%Lp` was "the low twelve, measured on both" had been measured
  # on a 0600 fixture, which cannot tell (an adapter may not narrow a
  # distinction it received — the L3 rule). The sed strips LEADING ZEROS the
  # way GNU prints: BSD writes the special nibble as a literal `0` on an
  # ordinary file (`0600` where GNU says `600`), and the one live caller
  # compares `= 600`. All-zero collapses to a single `0`, GNU's own answer.
  # D-945: ALL leading zeros, not the one `%Mp` zero the review's caution
  # named — whether BSD pads `%Lp` on a sub-three-digit mode (007 as `007`)
  # is unmeasured from this box, and the all-zero strip answers GNU's bare
  # digits under either padding behaviour.
  #
  # THE STRIP IS PURE BASH, NOT A PIPE THROUGH sed — the same lesson
  # `_svc_is_active` records for grep: doctor runs these under a
  # deliberately minimal PATH, and the first macos CI run measured a sed
  # pipe here answering "mode unreadable" on a perfectly healthy box,
  # failing the exposure check fleet-wide. Nothing below needs anything
  # but stat and the shell.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    local _out _rc _line
    _out=$(stat -f%Mp%Lp "$@"); _rc=$?
    while IFS= read -r _line; do
      while [ "${#_line}" -gt 1 ] && [ "${_line#0}" != "$_line" ]; do _line="${_line#0}"; done
      printf '%s\n' "$_line"
    done <<<"$_out"
    return $_rc
  else stat -c%a "$@"; fi
}

# `date -d <string>` PARSES an arbitrary date; BSD's `date` has no such flag
# and needs the input format spelled out. The one string ccrc parses this way
# is openssl's `notAfter=` (`Aug 25 10:00:00 2027 GMT`), so that is the format
# named here — and `-u` because the field carries its own zone and BSD would
# otherwise read it in local time, which is a certificate expiring on the
# wrong day.
#
# ANSWERS NOTHING AND rc 1 ON A STRING IT CANNOT PARSE, exactly as GNU's does,
# because the caller branches on that.
_plat_date_to_epoch() {   # <date string> -> epoch seconds
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    date -j -u -f '%b %d %H:%M:%S %Y %Z' "$1" +%s 2>/dev/null
  else
    date -d "$1" +%s 2>/dev/null
  fi
}

# `sha256sum` -> `shasum -a 256`. BSD's `shasum` prints the SAME two-field
# "<digest>  <name>" line GNU does, so every caller that cuts the first field
# and every `-c` check file written by one and read by the other match without
# translation — measured, not assumed.
_plat_sha256() {        # [file...] -> "<digest>  <name>" lines (stdin if none)
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then shasum -a 256 "$@"; else sha256sum "$@"; fi
}
_plat_sha256_check() {  # <sums-file> -> 0 iff every listed digest matches
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then shasum -a 256 -c "$@"; else sha256sum -c "$@"; fi
}

# `/proc/sys/kernel/random/uuid` -> `uuidgen`. LOWERCASED: the kernel emits
# lowercase hex and this value is a session's identity — it lands in the
# registry, in `--session-id`, and in the transcript filename the swap finds
# a conversation BY. `uuidgen` emits uppercase, and two spellings of one uuid
# is the swap looking for a file that is on disk under the other case.
_plat_uuid() {
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then uuidgen | tr 'A-F' 'a-f'; else cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/uuid; fi
}

# `/proc/<pid>/status`'s PPid line -> `ps -o ppid=`. ALWAYS rc 0 and silent on
# a pid that is gone, matching `_lc_ppid_of`'s existing contract exactly.
_plat_ppid() {   # <pid> -> its parent pid, or nothing
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    { ps -o ppid= -p "${1-}" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' '; } 2>/dev/null
  else
    { sed -n 's/^PPid:[[:space:]]*//p' "/proc/${1-}/status" | head -1; } 2>/dev/null
  fi
  return 0
}

# `/proc/$$/cgroup` -> NOTHING, on purpose. macOS has no cgroups, so the
# question this answers ("which unit/slice am I running under?") has no macOS
# answer at all — and the caller already treats an empty read as "not under
# one", which is the truthful answer on a box with no systemd. Degrading to
# empty is therefore not a stub: it is the correct value.
_plat_cgroup() {
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then return 0; fi
  { sed -n 's/^0:://p' "/proc/$$/cgroup" | head -1; } 2>/dev/null
}

# `timeout(1)` is GNU coreutils and is NOT in the BSD userland. Preferred by
# name, then `gtimeout` (Homebrew's coreutils installs it g-prefixed), and
# only then the bash fallback — which exists so a box that never installed
# coreutils still gets a BOUNDED call rather than a hang.
#
# THE FALLBACK REPORTS GNU's OWN 124 on expiry, because that is the code the
# call sites branch on. It learns which of the two racers won from a stamp
# file the sleeper writes BEFORE it signals, rather than from `kill -0` on the
# sleeper — that probe answers "already exited" both for the sleeper that
# fired and the sleeper that lost and was reaped, which is the bug that makes
# a hand-rolled timeout report success on expiry.
_plat_timeout() {   # <seconds> <cmd> [args...]
  local bin
  for bin in timeout gtimeout; do
    if command -v "$bin" >/dev/null 2>&1; then "$bin" "$@"; return $?; fi
  done
  local secs="$1"; shift
  local stamp; stamp="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/.ccd-timeout.$BASHPID.$$.stamp"
  rm -f -- "$stamp" 2>/dev/null
  "$@" &
  local pid=$! rc=0 watcher
  # `|| exit 0` ON THE SLEEP, and it is the difference between a deadline and
  # a guillotine. If `sleep` is absent or refuses — a contained PATH, a box
  # with no coreutils — it returns INSTANTLY, and without this guard the
  # watcher would stamp and signal in the same breath: every call would report
  # 124 having given the command no time at all. A sleep that cannot run means
  # THIS CALL GETS NO DEADLINE, which is the same honest degradation the
  # callers had before a shim existed ("a box without coreutils' `timeout`
  # still gets an answer, just without a deadline"). Caught by
  # `ccrc-doctor.test.ts`'s "answers on a box with no coreutils timeout".
  ( sleep "$secs" 2>/dev/null || exit 0; printf 1 > "$stamp" 2>/dev/null; kill -TERM "$pid" 2>/dev/null ) &
  watcher=$!
  wait "$pid" 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  kill -TERM "$watcher" 2>/dev/null; wait "$watcher" 2>/dev/null
  if [ -s "$stamp" ]; then rc=124; fi
  rm -f -- "$stamp" 2>/dev/null
  return "$rc"
}


# ── THE SERVICE LAYER — systemd on Linux, launchd on macOS ───────────────
# Every ccd session is a supervised unit, and `claude-session@.service` states
# what supervision MEANS here: ExecStart=`ccd supervise %i`, Restart=always,
# RestartSec=3, KillMode=process, and a start limit of 5 starts per 120s whose
# expiry leaves a FAILED unit. Four of those five map onto launchd keys. The
# fifth does not, and that gap is handled in `cmd_supervise`, not hidden here.
#
# THE UNIT NAME IS THE INTERFACE. Every function below takes the systemd
# spelling (`claude-session@<id>.service`, `ccrc.service`) because that is what
# ~90 call sites across ccd and ccrc already say, and translating at this
# boundary keeps the port out of all of them. `_svc_label` is the ONE place a
# unit name becomes a launchd label.
_svc_label() {   # <unit> -> the launchd label for it
  local u="${1%.service}"
  case "$u" in
    claude-session@*) printf 'app.ccrc.session.%s' "${u#claude-session@}" ;;
    *)                printf 'app.ccrc.%s' "$u" ;;
  esac
}
_svc_plist() { printf '%s/Library/LaunchAgents/%s.plist' "$HOME" "$(_svc_label "$1")"; }
# `$UID`, NOT `id -u`. It is a bash variable, so this costs no fork and — the
# part that bit — needs nothing on PATH. The forking version wrote
# `id: command not found` to stderr on every contained-PATH run, which is both
# noise on a healthy box and a dependency this file has no business having in
# a function called several times per status read.
_svc_domain() { printf 'gui/%s' "$UID"; }

# THE BOX'S SERVICE MANAGER, whatever it is called here — systemd on Linux,
# launchd on macOS.
#
# ITS OWN FUNCTION so a test harness can stub it — `command -v` finds a shell
# function before it finds a binary, so nothing else could — and the NAME is
# deliberately the one it had before macOS existed: four call sites in ccd and
# two more in ccrc/ccrc-doctor-checks read it, and a rename would silently
# un-stub every harness that patches it by name. What changed is the question
# it answers, from "is systemctl on PATH" to "can this box supervise a session
# at all", which is what every caller was ever asking.
_have_systemctl() {
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then command -v launchctl >/dev/null 2>&1; return $?; fi
  command -v systemctl >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# ── THE ONE PLACE launchctl IS INVOKED, AND THE GUARD ON IT ─────────────
# `launchctl` IGNORES $HOME. Every other path in this file is rooted at
# $HOME — the registry, the plists, the roster — so a caller that sets HOME
# to a sandbox gets a fully isolated ccd. launchd does not work that way: its
# per-user domain is keyed on the UID, so `bootstrap` from inside a sandbox
# installs a job in the REAL session, where it outlives the sandbox and shows
# up in the operator's own `launchctl list`.
#
# MEASURED, NOT THEORISED: the first full run of this port's test suite left
# five live jobs — app.ccrc.ccrc and four app.ccrc.session.* — registered in
# the developer's gui/501 domain, with their plists already deleted along with
# the temp homes they were written into. systemd never had this failure mode
# because `systemctl --user` is stubbed by PATH in exactly the tests that need
# it, and an unstubbed call in a sandbox simply found no manager.
#
# SO THE RULE: when $HOME is not this user's real home, this box is being
# driven from a sandbox, and a REAL launchctl must not be touched. A STUBBED
# one still runs — resolution goes through PATH exactly as it does for
# `systemctl` on the other platform, so a harness that puts its own launchctl
# ahead of /bin keeps full control and full observability.
#
# `_SVC_REAL_HOME` is resolved ONCE via tilde expansion, which reads the
# password database rather than $HOME — that is the whole point, and it is why
# this cannot be `[ "$HOME" = "$HOME" ]` in disguise.
# RESOLVED LAZILY, AND ONLY ON DARWIN. Two reasons, both learned the hard
# way: at source time this ran on every ccd invocation on both platforms
# including Linux, where nothing reads it; and it forked `id`, which on a
# contained PATH is a `command not found` on stderr — see `_svc_domain` above.
#
# `$USER`/`$LOGNAME` first because they are already in the environment;
# `id -un` only if neither is, and silenced. The tilde expansion is what
# actually consults the password database, which is the whole point: it
# answers the real home even when `$HOME` has been pointed somewhere else.
_SVC_REAL_HOME=""
_svc_real_home() {
  [ -n "$_SVC_REAL_HOME" ] && { printf '%s' "$_SVC_REAL_HOME"; return 0; }
  local u="${USER:-${LOGNAME:-}}"
  [ -n "$u" ] || u="$(id -un 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$u" ] && _SVC_REAL_HOME="$(eval printf '%s' "~$u" 2>/dev/null)"
  printf '%s' "$_SVC_REAL_HOME"
}
_svc_launchctl() {   # <launchctl args...>
  local bin; bin="$(command -v launchctl 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  [ -n "$bin" ] || return 1
  # A sandbox HOME plus the SYSTEM launchctl is the combination that must not
  # proceed. Either alone is fine: the real home is an operator's box, and a
  # stub anywhere is a harness that asked for this call.
  if [ "$HOME" != "$(_svc_real_home)" ] \
     && [ "${CCRC_ALLOW_SANDBOX_LAUNCHCTL:-}" != 1 ] \
     && case "$bin" in /bin/launchctl|/usr/bin/launchctl) true ;; *) false ;; esac; then
    return 1
  fi
  "$bin" "$@"
}

# THE PER-SESSION PLIST, written on demand rather than installed once: systemd
# has ONE template unit and instantiates it per id with `%i`, and launchd has
# no templates at all — every job is its own file. So the file `_svc_enable`
# would have found is the file it writes.
#
# `EnvironmentVariables` CARRIES PATH, and that line is the difference between
# a working session and a mystery. A systemd --user unit inherits the user
# manager's environment; a LaunchAgent gets launchd's own minimal PATH, which
# holds neither Homebrew's `bash` (this file needs >=4.4 and macOS ships 3.2)
# nor `tmux` (the substrate every pane lives in). A session started from a
# terminal would work and the same session started by the supervisor would
# not — the worst shape a bug can take.
#
# `AbandonProcessGroup` IS `KillMode=process`, and it is load-bearing for the
# same reason the unit spells that out: the tmux server is the durable
# substrate and MUST survive a supervisor restart. Without this key launchd
# kills the whole process group on stop, taking every pane with it — a restart
# of the supervisor would become a restart of the fleet.
# THE PATH A JOB FILE BAKES IN, and why it is not simply `$PATH`.
#
# `EnvironmentVariables` is a fixed dictionary: whatever PATH is at the moment
# a plist is written is the PATH every future run of that job gets. That is
# fine when an operator enables a session from their own shell and wrong in
# exactly the case that already bit this port once — a caller whose PATH has
# been stripped. A supervisor started from a job whose PATH lost Homebrew
# would spawn `bash` 3.2 and find no `tmux`, and the failure would surface as
# a syntax error inside ccd rather than as a missing directory.
#
# So the two Homebrew prefixes are APPENDED when absent — appended, not
# prepended, so an operator who deliberately puts something ahead of Homebrew
# keeps their order. `/opt/homebrew/bin` is Apple-silicon, `/usr/local/bin`
# Intel; a box has one of them and the other is an inert entry.
_svc_job_path() {
  local p="${PATH:-}" d
  for d in /opt/homebrew/bin /usr/local/bin; do
    case ":$p:" in *":$d:"*) ;; *) [ -d "$d" ] && p="${p:+$p:}$d" ;; esac
  done
  printf '%s' "$p"
}

_svc_write_session_plist() {   # <unit> -> 0 iff its plist is on disk
  local unit="$1" id label plist tmp
  id="${unit%.service}"; id="${id#claude-session@}"
  label="$(_svc_label "$unit")"; plist="$(_svc_plist "$unit")"
  mkdir -p "$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents" || return 1
  tmp="$plist.tmp.$$"
  cat > "$tmp" <<PLIST || { rm -f -- "$tmp"; return 1; }
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>${label}</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>${HOME}/.local/bin/ccd</string>
    <string>supervise</string>
    <string>${id}</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key><true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key><integer>3</integer>
  <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key><true/>
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key><string>$(_svc_job_path)</string>
    <key>HOME</key><string>${HOME}</string>
  </dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>${_SVC_REG}/${id}.supervise.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>${_SVC_REG}/${id}.supervise.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
PLIST
  _plat_mv_notdir "$tmp" "$plist" || { rm -f -- "$tmp"; return 1; }
}

# `systemctl --user enable --now` — install the unit AND start it now.
# On launchd those are one verb (`bootstrap` both loads and, with RunAtLoad,
# starts), which is why `_svc_enable` below is the same call minus the start:
# a plist in ~/Library/LaunchAgents IS boot persistence, exactly as an enable
# symlink is under systemd.
_svc_enable_now() {   # <unit>
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then systemctl --user enable --now "$1" 2>/dev/null; return $?; fi
  case "$1" in claude-session@*) _svc_write_session_plist "$1" || return 1 ;; esac
  local d; d="$(_svc_domain)"
  _svc_launchctl enable "$d/$(_svc_label "$1")" 2>/dev/null
  # ALREADY-BOOTSTRAPPED IS SUCCESS, not an error to relay: `enable --now` on a
  # running unit is a no-op under systemd, and every caller here is written
  # against that. `bootstrap` returns 5 ("Input/output error") for a label that
  # is already loaded, so the kickstart below is what makes the two agree.
  _svc_launchctl bootstrap "$d" "$(_svc_plist "$1")" 2>/dev/null
  _svc_launchctl kickstart "$d/$(_svc_label "$1")" >/dev/null 2>&1
}
_svc_enable() {   # <unit> — boot persistence only, no start
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then systemctl --user enable "$1" 2>/dev/null; return $?; fi
  case "$1" in claude-session@*) _svc_write_session_plist "$1" || return 1 ;; esac
  _svc_launchctl enable "$(_svc_domain)/$(_svc_label "$1")" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}
_svc_disable_now() {   # <unit>
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then systemctl --user disable --now "$1" 2>/dev/null; return $?; fi
  # THE MANAGER'S REFUSAL HAS TO REACH THE CALLER. `systemctl --user disable
  # --now` reports one, and `_ws_unsupervise` prints "could not disable unit …"
  # on it — a warning an operator acts on, because a session that stayed
  # enabled comes back at the next login. Returning 0 unconditionally here made
  # that warning unreachable on macOS: the one platform where it would be
  # printed is the one where it never could be.
  #
  # `3` IS launchctl's "No such process" — the job is not loaded, which is what
  # `disable --now` on an already-disabled unit means, and systemd calls that
  # success too. Any OTHER non-zero is a manager that refused.
  local d rc=0; d="$(_svc_domain)"
  _svc_launchctl bootout "$d/$(_svc_label "$1")" 2>/dev/null || rc=$?
  { [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] || [ "$rc" -eq 3 ]; } || return 1
  _svc_launchctl disable "$d/$(_svc_label "$1")" 2>/dev/null
  rm -f -- "$(_svc_plist "$1")" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}
_svc_start() {   # <unit>
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then systemctl --user start "$1" 2>/dev/null; return $?; fi
  case "$1" in claude-session@*) _svc_write_session_plist "$1" || return 1 ;; esac
  local d; d="$(_svc_domain)"
  _svc_launchctl bootstrap "$d" "$(_svc_plist "$1")" 2>/dev/null
  _svc_launchctl kickstart "$d/$(_svc_label "$1")" >/dev/null 2>&1
}
_svc_stop() {   # <unit>
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then systemctl --user stop "$1" 2>/dev/null; return $?; fi
  _svc_launchctl bootout "$(_svc_domain)/$(_svc_label "$1")" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}
_svc_restart() {   # <unit>
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then systemctl --user restart "$1" 2>/dev/null; return $?; fi
  _svc_launchctl kickstart -k "$(_svc_domain)/$(_svc_label "$1")" >/dev/null 2>&1
}
# `try-restart` restarts ONLY what is already running, and that conditional is
# the whole verb: `kickstart` on an unloaded label would START it, turning a
# sweep meant to refresh live supervisors into one that resurrects every
# session the operator had stopped.
_svc_try_restart() {   # <unit>
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then systemctl --user try-restart "$1" 2>/dev/null; return $?; fi
  _svc_is_loaded "$1" || return 0
  _svc_launchctl kickstart -k "$(_svc_domain)/$(_svc_label "$1")" >/dev/null 2>&1
}
_svc_is_loaded() {   # <unit> -> 0 iff launchd holds this label
  _svc_launchctl print "$(_svc_domain)/$(_svc_label "$1")" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# THE REMEDY LINE, and it must name a command the operator's box actually has.
# Every "read what it says" message in this file used to end in `systemctl
# --user status <unit>`; on macOS that is a command not found, which turns a
# diagnostic into a second dead end at the exact moment someone is debugging.
# THE RESTART REMEDY, in the spelling this box has. Same reason as
# `_svc_status_hint` below: a message that ends in `systemctl --user restart`
# on macOS hands the operator a command that does not exist, at the moment
# they are already trying to fix something.
_svc_restart_hint() {   # <unit> -> the command that restarts it
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    printf 'launchctl kickstart -k %s/%s' "$(_svc_domain)" "$(_svc_label "$1")"
  else
    printf 'systemctl --user restart %s' "$1"
  fi
}

_svc_status_hint() {   # <unit> -> the command that shows this unit's state
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    printf 'launchctl print %s/%s' "$(_svc_domain)" "$(_svc_label "$1")"
  else
    printf 'systemctl --user status %s' "$1"
  fi
}

# `systemctl --user is-active` -> active|inactive|failed|unknown, and the four
# words matter: callers branch on them by name.
#
# `failed` HAS NO LAUNCHD SOURCE — see the start-limit note at the top of this
# block — so it is read from the stamp `cmd_supervise` writes when it gives up.
# That keeps the one state launchd cannot express owned by the code that CAN
# observe it, rather than guessed from a `print` field that means something
# else.
_svc_is_active() {   # <unit> -> one word on stdout
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then
    systemctl --user is-active "$1" 2>/dev/null || true
    return 0
  fi
  local id="${1%.service}"; id="${id#claude-session@}"
  if [ -f "$_SVC_REG/$id.svcfailed" ]; then printf 'failed'; return 0; fi
  # NO `_svc_is_loaded` RUNG HERE, deliberately: it is a boolean, and folding
  # it in front of the three-way read below would turn "the manager did not
  # answer" back into `inactive` — the exact conflation the block underneath
  # exists to prevent. One `print`, read for all three answers.
  # MATCHED BY THE SHELL, not by `grep`. This is read on every `ccrc status`
  # and every doctor run, so it is a fork saved — but the reason it is written
  # this way is correctness, not speed: the fixture PATHs this suite runs
  # under are deliberately contained, and a check that shells out to `grep`
  # answers `inactive` on a perfectly healthy box whenever grep is not on it.
  # (Measured: `ccrc doctor: services` failed exactly that way.)
  # THREE ANSWERS, NOT TWO, and the third one is the whole reason this is not
  # a boolean: "the manager did not answer" is NOT "the service is stopped".
  # Collapsing them reports every workspace on a box with an unreachable
  # manager as `inactive` — "nothing is watching this session" — which is the
  # precise false positive `_ws_unit_state` and `_box_units` exist to make
  # visible. systemd's `is-active` keeps the distinction by printing nothing;
  # launchd keeps it in the exit code.
  #
  # `113` IS launchctl's "Could not find service": a POSITIVE answer that no
  # such job is loaded, and the only non-zero status that means `inactive`.
  # Anything else — the binary missing, the platform guard refusing, a domain
  # that cannot be reached — is a question that was never asked, and answers
  # EMPTY like systemd's does.
  local _out _rc
  _out="$(_svc_launchctl print "$(_svc_domain)/$(_svc_label "$1")" 2>/dev/null)"; _rc=$?
  case "$_out" in
    *"state = running"*) printf 'active'; return 0 ;;
  esac
  if [ "$_rc" -eq 113 ]; then printf 'inactive'; fi
  return 0
}
# `reset-failed` clears the refusal a start limit left behind. Under launchd
# the refusal is ours, so clearing it is removing our own stamp.
_svc_reset_failed() {   # <unit>
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then systemctl --user reset-failed "$1" 2>/dev/null; return $?; fi
  local id="${1%.service}"; id="${id#claude-session@}"
  rm -f -- "$_SVC_REG/$id.svcfailed" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}
# `daemon-reload` has no launchd counterpart AND NEEDS NONE: launchd reads a
# job's plist at `bootstrap`, so the read this verb forces under systemd has
# already happened by construction on the other platform. A no-op that returns
# success is the honest translation, not a stub.
_svc_daemon_reload() {
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then systemctl --user daemon-reload; return $?; fi
  return 0
}
# `list-units 'claude-session@*'` -> the unit names, one per line. Read from
# the LaunchAgents directory rather than from `launchctl print` output: the
# directory is the enumerable, stable list (it is what boot persistence means
# here), where `launchctl print gui/<uid>` prints a large nested block whose
# format Apple has changed between releases.
_svc_list_sessions() {   # -> claude-session@<id>.service, one per line
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then
    systemctl --user list-units 'claude-session@*' --all --plain --no-legend 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $1}'
    return 0
  fi
  local f b
  for f in "$HOME"/Library/LaunchAgents/app.ccrc.session.*.plist; do
    [ -e "$f" ] || continue
    b="${f##*/}"; b="${b%.plist}"
    printf 'claude-session@%s.service\n' "${b#app.ccrc.session.}"
  done
  return 0
}

# `systemd-run --user --collect` — fire a detached job and return immediately.
# The swap's own tail runs through this so the swap survives the death of the
# session that asked for it; on launchd the equivalent is not a job at all but
# a disowned child, because a transient LaunchAgent would need a plist written,
# bootstrapped and reaped for a command that runs once for two seconds.
#
# `setsid` WHERE THERE IS ONE, and a plain background job where there is not:
# what this needs is a process that outlives its parent's process group, and
# macOS has no setsid(1). `nohup` plus the disown that leaving the function
# performs is what remains, and it is enough here — the caller is exiting, not
# being SIGKILLed with its group.
_svc_run_detached() {   # <cmd> [args...]
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then
    systemd-run --user --collect --quiet "$@"
    return $?
  fi
  # PROBED BEFORE BACKGROUNDING, because the caller checks this status and a
  # bare `nohup … &` would answer 0 for a command that does not exist — the
  # detached swap's `|| die "systemd-run failed. Nothing was moved"` would
  # then never fire, and a swap that never ran would report as dispatched.
  # `systemd-run` reports whether the JOB WAS CREATED, not what the command
  # went on to do, and this probe is the same promise, kept the same way.
  command -v "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  nohup "$@" >/dev/null 2>&1 &
  return 0
}
# ── END PLATFORM LAYER — the byte-identical region ends HERE ────────────
# Add a new `_plat_*`/`_svc_*` helper ABOVE this line, in BOTH files, or the
# pin test (`macos-platform.test.ts`) cannot see it: the compared region is
# sliced by the two sentinels, so a helper appended below them is two files
# free to drift — the exact failure the duplication invites and the pin
# exists to refuse. The test also asserts every `_plat_`/`_svc_` definition
# sits inside the sentinels, so an appended-below helper is a red suite, not
# a quiet gap.

PROG=ccrc

# Where the rest of the shipped tree is, relative to THIS FILE rather than to
# $PWD — `ccrc-adopt:139-140`'s idiom, so `ccrc` works run as `bash ccd/ccrc`
# from anywhere, and on a bare installed box (~/ccrc/ccd/ccrc) with no checkout
# in sight. Two deliberate differences from adopt's line, both measured:
#   - `${BASH_SOURCE[0]}`, not `$0`: THIS script is meant to be source-able
#     (the guard at the bottom exists for that), and under `source` `$0` is the
#     calling shell's argv[0], so it would answer for the caller's directory.
#   - `${CCRC_HERE%/*}`, not `$(dirname …)`: `dirname` is coreutils, i.e. a PATH
#     lookup, and the one box `ccrc doctor` most needs to work on is the box
#     whose environment is broken. Measured in ccrc-doctor.test.ts, whose
#     fixture PATH contains no system directory at all: with `dirname` here,
#     ccrc died with "doctor's check table is missing", blaming the install for
#     what was actually a PATH problem. Parameter expansion cannot fail that
#     way. The `*/*` guard covers `bash ccrc` from inside ccd/, where
#     BASH_SOURCE carries no directory at all and `%/*` would strip nothing.
CCRC_HERE="${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
[[ "$CCRC_HERE" == */* ]] || CCRC_HERE="./$CCRC_HERE"
CCRC_HERE="$(cd "${CCRC_HERE%/*}" && pwd)"

# ── THE TWO FILES THIS BOX KEEPS ITS OWN FACTS IN ─────────────────────────
# Spelled once each, because `version`, `status` and `doctor` all read them and
# three copies of a path is how two of them end up reading a file the third
# does not write. That last clause stopped being hypothetical in stage 2d: as
# of Task 6 `install` WRITES ccrc.env (once, when the box has none), so this
# file now holds both the reader and the writer of it, and `_inst_env` writes
# through this variable rather than re-spelling the path — the sentence above
# is the reason, and D-88 in the stage-2d plan records the choice. build.json
# stays read-only here until Task 7 gives `install` a stamp of its own; when it
# does, it writes `"$BOX_STAMP_FILE"` for the same reason.
#   build.json — what a deploy stamped (deploy.sh's `stamp_build`).
#   ccrc.env   — the box's machine config (deploy/ccrc.env.example). It ALSO
#                carries tokens (CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN, CCRC_VAPID_PRIVATE, a
#                Hetzner token), which is why it is never sourced and never
#                read whole — see `_box_env_value`.
BOX_STAMP_FILE="$HOME/.ccrc/build.json"
BOX_ENV_FILE="$HOME/.ccrc/ccrc.env"

# ── THE EXPOSURE FILE, WHICH `expose` WRITES AND THE SERVER UNIT READS ────
# `~/.ccrc/exposure.env` — stage 3b, spec D3. Exposure keys (CCRC_ORIGIN,
# CCRC_RP_ID, the DuckDNS trio) get their OWN EnvironmentFile because ccrc.env
# is seed-once: `_inst_env` writes it when the box has none and NEVER again
# (D-88), so a verb that had to rewrite it would break that contract, and one
# that appended would fight the operator's own hand edits. The server unit
# reads this file SECOND (`deploy/ccrc.service`) — systemd's later-file-wins —
# so `cmd_expose`'s keys override a hand-set placeholder without either file
# touching the other. Spelled once, pinned like CCRC_RC_FILE
# (single-definition.test.ts, "one ~/.ccrc/exposure.env").
CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE="$HOME/.ccrc/exposure.env"

# ── THE CADDYFILE, WHICH `expose` REGENERATES ON EVERY RUN ────────────────
# `~/.ccrc/Caddyfile` — ccrc-OWNED, the REGENERATE class: `cmd_expose`
# rewrites it whole from the origin and CCRC_PORT on every run, and nothing in
# this tree ever reads it back. CADDY reads it, through the symlink or copy the
# OPERATOR makes at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile in the printed root ceremony (spec D2:
# ccrc has never run sudo and does not start here — the `_inst_linger`
# doctrine, verb-sized). Spelled once, pinned like CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE
# (single-definition.test.ts, "one ~/.ccrc/Caddyfile"); the ceremony lines
# print the path at the operator THROUGH this variable, so the command an
# operator pastes and the file this verb writes cannot drift apart.
CCRC_CADDYFILE="$HOME/.ccrc/Caddyfile"
# `/etc/caddy/Caddyfile` — CADDY'S OWN, on the root side of the boundary. ccrc
# never writes it; the operator copies our file onto it in the printed
# ceremony, and doctor's `caddyfile` check measures whether that copy happened
# and is still current (D-166). It is a CONSTANT and not a literal for the
# reason CCRC_CADDYFILE is: the line that PRINTS the destination and the check
# that MEASURES it must name the same path by construction, or the remedy
# sends an operator to a file the check is not looking at. Overridable so the
# suite can point it at a fixture — nothing else may write an absolute /etc
# path, and a test that needed root would simply not be written.
CCRC_CADDY_SYSTEM_FILE="${CCRC_CADDY_SYSTEM_FILE:-/etc/caddy/Caddyfile}"
# The INTERNAL CA's root certificate, where Debian's caddy package keeps it
# (the caddy user's data dir) — what `expose ip`'s trust ceremony hands to
# each device, once. A CONSTANT for `CCRC_CADDY_SYSTEM_FILE`'s reason: the
# verb PRINTS this path and doctor's `cert` check names it as the WARN remedy
# on mode ip, and a printer and a remedy that spelled it separately is the
# drift D-166's comment names. ccrc never reads the file — it sits on the
# root side of the boundary; the operator copies it to their phones.
CCRC_CADDY_ROOT_CA="${CCRC_CADDY_ROOT_CA:-/var/lib/caddy/.local/share/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt}"

# ── THE DDNS UNIT PAIR, WHICH THE duckdns ARM INSTALLS AND ENABLES ────────
# `ccrc-ddns.service` + `ccrc-ddns.timer` — stage 3b, spec D4: a user timer
# (the ccd-cap-scopes.timer precedent) curling the DuckDNS update endpoint
# every 5 minutes, reading its token from $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE at RUN time —
# the 0644 unit text never carries the 0600 secret. The name is spelled once
# because `_exp_ddns_units` uses it three ways — install destination, enable
# argument, printed remedy — and a drift between any two is a box whose timer
# was installed under one name and enabled under another. Pinned like
# CCRC_CADDYFILE (single-definition.test.ts, "one ccrc-ddns unit name").
CCRC_DDNS_UNIT="ccrc-ddns"

# ── THE ACCOUNT SECRETS DIRECTORY, WHICH `account` WILL WRITE AND NEVER READ ──
# `~/.cc-secrets/<id>-oauth.env` for an OAuth lane, `<id>-<provider>.env` for an
# api-key one (the rule is `_acct_write_secret`'s, Task 22) — one 0600 file per token lane, holding
# exactly one `export` line, sourced by that lane's generated wrapper at exec
# time (`shared/wrapper.mjs:141-143`). Spelled once for BOX_AUTH_FILE's reason:
# Task 22 is what makes `cmd_account` WRITE these files, and Task 33 is what
# adds a doctor check that MEASURES them by existence — NEITHER EXISTS YET.
# This build has no `accounts` entry in `CCRC_DOCTOR_CHECKS`
# (ccrc-doctor-checks:166-195), and nothing anywhere in this repository reads
# the variable defined below — it is spelled here, ahead of its first use, so
# the writer Task 22 adds and the reader Task 33 adds spell the same directory
# from day one rather than drifting the way D-166's comment describes.
#
# THE MODE WILL BE THE FILE'S OWN, once Task 22 lands. `mkdir -p -m 0700` sets
# a mode on a directory it CREATES and on nothing else (POSIX), so a
# `~/.cc-secrets` that already exists will keep whatever mode the operator gave
# it — `cmd_wrappers`' rule for `~/.local/bin`, stated at :2347-2351 ("an
# EXISTING directory is left exactly as the operator has it") and implemented
# at :2361, is the shape Task 22 is expected to follow. No `mkdir` for this
# directory exists anywhere in `ccd/` or `deploy/` today (measured by grep);
# when Task 22 adds one it should not chmod an existing directory in either
# direction: widening one silently would be worse than the state it found, and
# narrowing one is a decision about a directory `deploy/deploy.sh`'s
# `ship_secret` also writes into. The 0600 on the FILE is what will protect the
# secret, exactly as CLAUDE.md argues for `~/.ccrc` being 0775 on the reference
# fleet.
ACCT_SECRETS_DIR="$HOME/.cc-secrets"

# ── THE SUBCOMMANDS THIS BUILD IMPLEMENTS, SPELLED ONCE ───────────────────
# DATA, in `CCRC_DOCTOR_CHECKS`'s shape (ccrc-doctor-checks:166) and for its
# reason. `cmd_account` matches the operator's word against THIS list, and BOTH
# refusals print it — so a subcommand that dispatches without being named, or a
# name printed that nothing dispatches, cannot happen. Adding one is one word
# here plus one arm in the case below, and `ccrc-account.test.ts`'s
# "both directions" case reads this line out of the source and fails on either
# half alone.
ACCT_SUBS="add candidates check credential declare roster"

# ── THE PASSPHRASE SECRET, WHICH THIS FILE NEVER READS ────────────────────
# `~/.ccrc/auth.scrypt` — the scrypt line the server's session gate verifies a
# login against (`server/src/auth/secret.ts` owns the format). Spelled here for
# the reason the two above it are: `cmd_passwd` WRITES it and doctor's `auth`
# check MEASURES it, and a writer and a reader that spelled the path separately
# is the drift that comment names. `server/src/config.ts` derives the same
# default (`CCRC_AUTH_SECRET_PATH || ~/.ccrc/auth.scrypt`) in the other
# language; that pair is the same cross-language second spelling D-92 already
# records for `CCRC_UNIT_DIR`, and it is not fixable from either side.
#
# NEITHER OF THIS FILE'S USES EVER OPENS IT. `cmd_passwd` hands the path to
# `deploy/gen-auth-hash.mjs`, which writes it; `_check_auth` hands the same path
# to the same helper, which reports a CLASS and never a byte. Bash holds the
# path and nothing else — the rule `_check_wrappers` already keeps for the
# accounts' secrets files ("PATHS ONLY. THE SECRETS FILE IS NEVER OPENED").
BOX_AUTH_FILE="$HOME/.ccrc/auth.scrypt"

# ── THE SESSION FILE, WHICH THIS FILE NEVER READS EITHER ──────────────────
# `~/.ccrc/sessions.json` — the flat file the gate's live sessions live in
# (`server/src/auth/sessions.ts`). Spelled here for one reason only: doctor's
# `auth` remedy for a broken secret has to NAME it, because a fresh secret
# restarts at generation 1 and a session minted under this box's first
# passphrase would otherwise revalidate against it.
#
# LOSING IT IS FREE, by design — it holds `sha256(token)` and nothing else, so
# deleting it logs everyone out and costs nothing more. That is why a remedy
# may safely print `rm -f` for it at all.
BOX_SESSIONS_FILE="$HOME/.ccrc/sessions.json"

# ── WHERE A BOX KEEPS THE SHIPPED TREE ────────────────────────────────────
# `~/ccrc` is not this file's choice and is not a default: it is the layout
# every sibling contract in this repository already assumes. Both deploy lanes
# rsync into it (`deploy.sh:343,686`), the launcher on PATH execs
# `$HOME/ccrc/ccd/ccrc`, `_dr_pkg_candidates` reads the shipped node floor at
# `$CCRC_HERE/../{server,agent}/package.json`, and `_check_node`'s remedy prints
# that path to operators. `install` converges TO that layout rather than
# inventing one.
#
# Spelled once, for the reason the two files above are: `_inst_tree` PLACES the
# tree here and `_inst_bins`/`_inst_files` install OUT of it, so a tree placed
# at one path and read from another is an install that ships bytes nobody put
# there. The single place the path is written out again is the launcher heredoc
# in `_inst_shim`, and that is DATA — bytes destined for a file on the box,
# pinned equal to `deploy.sh`'s copy of the same launcher — not a path this
# file resolves.
BOX_TREE_DIR="$HOME/ccrc"

# ── WHERE A BOX KEEPS ITS systemd --user UNITS ────────────────────────────
# Not this file's choice either: it is `systemd --user`'s own search path, and
# both deploy lanes already `cp` into it (`deploy.sh:409-416,697`). Spelled here
# for the reason `BOX_ENV_FILE` is — as of Task 8 this file WRITES the directory
# (`_inst_units`) and `_inst_enable` then asks systemd to read it, so a writer
# and a reader that spelled it separately is the failure that comment names.
#
# ── THE ONE DELIBERATE SECOND SPELLING, AND HOW IT IS HELD ────────────────
# `ccrc-doctor-checks` declares its own `CCRC_UNIT_DIR` with the same value.
# That is not laziness and it is not fixed by pointing it here: the check table
# is sourced under `set -u` by things that are not this file (its own test
# suite reads `CCRC_DOCTOR_CHECKS` out of it that way), so a top-level
# `CCRC_UNIT_DIR="$BOX_UNIT_DIR"` would make sourcing it fail outright, and a
# `${BOX_UNIT_DIR:-…}` fallback IS the second spelling with an extra branch in
# front of it. `_check_path` records the same trade for `WRAPPER_BIN_DIR` and
# the same remedy: keep the mechanism instead of the promise —
# `ccrc-install.test.ts` compares the two literals and goes red on a drift.
# (D-92.)
# WHERE THIS BOX'S SERVICE MANAGER READS ITS JOB FILES. The comment above
# explains why this is one literal and not two; the platform arm below does
# not change that — it is still ONE spelling per box, chosen by the same
# `CCD_OS` every other arm in this file reads. macOS keeps user agents in
# ~/Library/LaunchAgents, and there is no per-unit drop-in directory there:
# launchd has no drop-in mechanism at all, which is why `_inst_units`' Darwin
# arm writes whole plists instead of a base file plus overrides.
if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
  BOX_UNIT_DIR="$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents"
else
  BOX_UNIT_DIR="$HOME/.config/systemd/user"
fi

# ── WHERE A BOX KEEPS ITS BACKUPS (stage 4, Task 6) ───────────────────────
# `~/ccrc-backups/<ts>/` is deploy's own layout (deploy.sh:33-34: "every
# backup this run takes lands under the same ~/ccrc-backups/<ts>/ … so a
# rollback is one directory, not a hunt"), and the timestamp shape below it
# is `prune_backups`' glob (deploy.sh:68-72) — a backup named outside that
# shape is a backup the pruner can never reclaim. Spelled once, for the
# reason every BOX_* path above is: `_upd_backup` WRITES under it and
# `_upd_report` prints restore commands OUT of it, and two spellings is how
# the printed command comes to name a directory nothing wrote.
BOX_BACKUP_ROOT="$HOME/ccrc-backups"

# ── THE RELEASE SOURCE (stage 4, Task 6) ──────────────────────────────────
# One owner/repo pair, and `install.sh:23-24` spells the same one — a
# DELIBERATE cross-file second spelling of D-92's class: `install.sh
# --release` runs before any ccrc exists on the box, so it cannot read this
# pair, and this file runs on boxes install.sh has long left. The two are
# held equal by `server/test/ccrc-update.test.ts` (the pin IS the mechanism;
# a comment would be the promise). `CCRC_RELEASE_BASE_URL` overrides the
# derived `…/releases` prefix — the same test seam and mirror escape hatch
# install.sh documents. Stage 5 de-brands the pair in both files at once.
CCRC_RELEASE_OWNER="Synapsium-Labs"
CCRC_RELEASE_REPO="ccrc-pwa"

# How long the ONE network call in this file (`GET /api/fleet/health`) may take.
# Overridable for the reason `deploy/verify-service.sh:56-62` makes its
# settle/window knobs overridable, and `ccrc-doctor-checks` makes
# `CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT` overridable: a route that hangs must not hang the
# tool, and a test must not wait out a production timeout. The DEFAULT is what
# an operator gets.
: "${CCRC_HEALTH_TIMEOUT:=5}"

# ── ccrc account check: how long the CHEAP health question may take ───────
# `claude auth status` is a LOCAL read — no inference, no network to the model
# endpoint — so this is a deadline against a hung binary, not a budget.
# Overridable for `CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT`'s reason (ccrc-doctor-checks:152-158):
# a route that hangs must not hang the tool, and a test must not wait out a
# production timeout. The DEFAULT is what an operator gets. It joins BOTH test
# runners' deletion arrays in the same commit (ccrc-cli.test.ts's `ccrcEnv` and
# ccrc-account.test.ts's own copy), or the suite's answer starts depending on
# the developer's exported shell — the rule `_acct_live`'s "A LITERAL 5, AND NO
# KNOB" paragraph states from the other side.
: "${CCRC_ACCOUNT_AUTH_TIMEOUT:=15}"

# Out-parameters for the two readers below. `set -u` makes an unset array fatal
# on read, so they are declared here rather than at first assignment. `BOX_*`
# and `_box_*` are a THIRD namespace, deliberately kept clear of the two already
# in play in any shell that sources this tree: `_ccrc_*` (this file's own
# refusal helpers AND the five functions `shared/generate.mjs` emits into
# ~/.ccrc/accounts.sh) and `_wrap_*`/`WRAPPER_*` (ccrc-wrapper-shape).
BOX_BUILD=()
BOX_HEALTH=()
BOX_HEALTH_WHY=""

# `cmd_update`'s out-parameters (stage 4, Task 6), declared at file scope for
# the `set -u` reason the arrays above are: `_upd_fetch` fills the first
# three, `_upd_backup` the fourth, and `cmd_update`'s tail (and its EXIT
# trap) read them whether or not the writer ran.
UPD_STAGE=""
UPD_TREE=""
UPD_VERSION=""
UPD_BACKUP_DIR=""
# `set -u` out-parameter discipline, same as the UPD_* four above: the _inst_*
# steps read it, cmd_install overwrites it — a direct _inst_* call must meet a
# stated default, never an unbound-variable death.
INST_ROLE=both

# The ONE spelling of the graphify engine version ccrc installs (spec §A, O1).
# Bumping it is a reviewed commit; the corpus guard and graph-sweep row 13 are
# the acceptance tests. Everything outside this file reads the STAMP
# (~/.ccrc/graphify.pin) that _inst_graphify_engine writes — never a second copy.
GRAPHIFY_PIN=0.9.9  # installed as graphifyy==$GRAPHIFY_PIN — the pip spec's one home until _inst_graphify_engine lands

# ── WHICH STEPS DEGRADED, so the closing line can say so ──────────────────
# `cmd_install`'s steps either converge or die — with ONE exception by design,
# `_inst_linger`, which cannot converge without a privilege the operator may
# not have and must not abort a run over it. Until fix round 1 the tail said
# "every step above converged" unconditionally, which is literally false in
# exactly that case (and a test pinned the false line). A step that degrades
# appends its NAME here and the tail reads the array, rather than the tail
# re-deriving the condition from something it can only guess at.
#
# Declared at file scope for the reason the three arrays above it are: `set -u`
# makes an unset array fatal on read, and the reader (`cmd_install`'s tail) runs
# whether or not the one writer did. `cmd_install` clears it before the first
# step, so a sourced-and-called-twice shell cannot carry one run's degradation
# into the next.
INST_DEGRADED=()

usage() {
  cat <<EOF
usage: $PROG {doctor|status|adopt|wrappers|account|install|update|uninstall|backup|logs|passwd|expose|version}

The lifecycle CLI for a Claude Code fleet box — installs, updates and
diagnoses a box, as a sibling to ccd (which drives sessions once one is
already running).

  version   print the build this box is running, from ~/.ccrc/build.json
  doctor    check this box's prerequisites — one PASS/WARN/FAIL line per
            check, each non-pass followed by its remedy; exit 1 if any failed
  status    print what this box IS: its build stamp, its services, how many
            ccd sessions it holds, and — when it knows where its server is —
            whether the two boxes agree. Read-only; always exit 0
  adopt     rediscover this box's accounts from \$HOME/.local/bin and write
            ~/.ccrc/accounts.json (runs ccrc-adopt; pass --out/--force to it)
  wrappers  write \$HOME/.local/bin/<id> for every account the roster marks
            "generated", and REFUSE every file ccrc did not write itself.
            Backs up before any overwrite; never touches an upstream or
            external account. [--dry-run] [--adopt] [--force]
  account   connect, check and remove the accounts this box runs sessions
            on — one JSON object on stdout per subcommand, remedies on
            stderr, and the credential only ever on stdin (--credential -).
            Writes ~/.ccrc/accounts.json, a 0600 file under ~/.cc-secrets
            and each account's own settings.json; every lane it connects is
            left DISABLED until you enable it. Run it with no subcommand to
            see which subcommands this build has
  install   converge this box from the shipped tree: seed ~/.ccrc config
            (never overwriting yours), install executables, units and hooks,
            generate wrappers, then run doctor. Single-box, localhost.
            Re-running converges; it never damages an existing install.
            [--role server|fleet|both] (default both, today's single box).
            fleet: prompts (tty-only) for the server URL and agent token,
            writes ~/.ccrc/agent.env 0600 seed-once, and runs
            ccrc-agent.service instead of ccrc.service. server: today's
            spine minus nothing — the difference from 'both' is reserved
            for later stages
  update    fetch a published release and move this box onto it: verify the
            download (checksum, then the per-file MANIFEST) before anything
            changes, back up coord.db, dists, ccd and the units to
            ~/ccrc-backups/<ts>/, then re-run the install spine from the
            verified staged tree (role-aware, from ccrc.env's CCRC_ROLE) and
            print the from -> to report. [--to vX.Y.Z] pins a version — the
            rollback path too: a downgrade prints the coord.db restore
            commands and never auto-restores. Never automatic; per box;
            fleet box first, then the server box
  uninstall move this box off ccrc, leaving reinstall safe: stop, disable
            and remove the units (incl. both drop-ins and the slice
            escape), remove ccrc's managed settings.json hook entries
            (unmanaged entries survive byte-identically, rewritten files
            are backed up first), remove marker-verified wrappers ONLY, and
            remove ccrc's own files in ~/.cc-sessions, ~/ccrc and the
            executables. PRESERVES ~/.ccrc, the session registry rows,
            worktrees and ~/ccrc-backups. Refuses while live sessions
            exist. [--force] proceeds anyway; [--purge] also removes
            ~/.ccrc and ~/ccrc-backups — never worktrees, never tmux state
  backup    take update's backup step standalone: coord.db snapshot, dists,
            ccd and the units to ~/ccrc-backups/<ts>/, then prune to the
            newest \$CCRC_BACKUP_KEEP (default 10) timestamped backups —
            hand-made siblings in that directory are never touched
  logs      this box's service journal: journalctl --user -u ccrc.service
            (or ccrc-agent.service when ccrc.env records CCRC_ROLE=fleet).
            [-f] follows, [-n LINES] limits — both pass straight through
  passwd    set (or rotate) this box's PWA passphrase in ~/.ccrc/auth.scrypt.
            Prompts twice on a tty; the gate also needs CCRC_AUTH=on in this
            box's ccrc.env. Rotating invalidates every logged-in SESSION —
            enrolled passkeys keep working, by design, so a lost device is
            "revoke its passkey in the PWA, THEN run this"
  expose    give this box a public name and a real certificate: writes the
            exposure config (its own env file — ccrc.env is never touched)
            and a Caddyfile for a loopback reverse proxy, then prints the
            three root steps ccrc will not run itself. Subcommands: duckdns
            (prompts for a subdomain and a token, tty only), byo (your own
            domain — DNS is yours), ip (no domain at all: HTTPS on this
            box's bare address through caddy's internal CA — each device
            trusts the printed root once, and login is passphrase-only:
            passkeys need a domain), status (what is configured; the token
            is reported SET/NOT SET, never printed)

  -h, --help   print this message and exit 0

Exit codes: 0 success, 1 the tool ran and the answer was bad, 2 a usage
error (unknown verb or argument).
EOF
}

# ── diagnostic helpers (stderr; the RESULT of a verb goes to stdout) ───────
# `_ccrc_die` — a bare refusal: this run cannot continue. Always exits 1 —
# never 2, which is reserved for a USAGE error (see `_ccrc_usage_die` below).
_ccrc_die() { echo "$PROG: $*" >&2; exit 1; }
# The one place "unknown argument" is spelled, so the unknown-verb and
# unknown-top-level-flag paths below cannot drift onto two different
# messages for what is, to an operator, the same mistake.
_ccrc_usage_die() { echo "$PROG: unknown argument: $1" >&2; usage >&2; exit 2; }
# `_ccrc_not_implemented` lived here and is GONE (Task 8), with the last verb
# that reached it. It was the deliberate middle refusal between "you typed it
# wrong" (exit 2) and a real answer, for verb names that were reserved before
# they were built — and every one of the four is built now. Re-add it in one
# line together with the next reserved name, and not before; an uncalled
# refusal is the same untested, uncallable code `_ccrc_say` was removed for.

# cmd_version / _box_build_fields — what THIS box is running, per its own build
# stamp, never a guess. Same three-way discipline as `ccd version`
# (ccd/ccd:1946-1969):
#   - no ~/.ccrc/build.json  -> a dev checkout, or a box `ccrc install` never
#     touched. "unstamped" at exit 0 is the honest ANSWER here, not an error.
#   - a stamp that parses    -> print the facts.
#   - a stamp that exists but does not parse (or is missing a field) ->
#     something wrote garbage where a deploy writes facts; refuse loudly
#     rather than print a version nobody measured.
# Parsed with jq, not `python3` (ccd's own choice) — this project already
# depends on jq for exactly this kind of small-JSON reads (ccrc-adopt uses it
# throughout) and there is no reason to add a second interpreter dependency
# for one file read. The whole filter is wrapped in `try…catch empty` and
# checks each field's TYPE rather than just its presence: `.dirty` is a real
# boolean that is legitimately `false` on most boxes, so a naive `jq -e` (which
# treats a `false`/`null` last output as failure) would misreport every clean
# build as "unreadable" — this checks `.dirty|type=="boolean"` instead, which
# accepts `false` as the valid, common value it is.
#
# `version` takes NO arguments — not even `-h`/`--help`. An extra argument
# here (including `--help`) is a USAGE error, exit 2, same as an unrecognised
# top-level verb/flag: `_ccrc_usage_die` prints "unknown argument: <it>" plus
# the full usage text and quits. This deliberately does not special-case
# `--help` into its own per-verb help screen — asking for help is a top-level
# concern (`ccrc -h` / `ccrc --help`, before the verb is even read); once a
# verb has been selected, `version` has exactly one job and no flags of its
# own to explain. (Round-1 review, Important 2: this used to `_ccrc_die` at
# exit 1, which collided with this very file's own exit-code table — 1 means
# "the tool ran and the answer was bad", not "you typed it wrong".)
#
# A missing `jq` is probed for and refused BY NAME before the parse is
# attempted (round-1 review, Important 3) — without this, "jq not on PATH",
# "jq parse error" and "permission denied reading the stamp" all fell through
# to the same "build stamp unreadable" message, which sends an operator to
# rebuild/redeploy when the actual fix is `apt install jq`. That collapse is
# exactly the overloaded-seam mistake this codebase's conventions ban: two
# conditions ("this box lacks a dependency" vs. "this box's stamp is
# corrupt") that an operator would act on completely differently must not
# share one signal.
#
# ── ONE READER, TWO RENDERINGS ────────────────────────────────────────────
# `_box_build_fields` is the ONLY reader of ~/.ccrc/build.json in this file, and
# `version` and `status` both call it and each writes its own sentence from the
# SAME four fields — a second parser here is how the two verbs would come to
# disagree about what "dirty" means. (`ccd version` still has a reader of its
# own, in python3, in the other tool; Task 8 of this plan is where
# `single-definition` is pointed at that pair.) It answers with a return code
# per CONDITION, because the callers do different things about each:
#
#   0  the stamp parsed  -> BOX_BUILD=(sha ref builtAt true|false version)
#                           (version = "" when the stamp carries none — the
#                           additive fifth field, stage 4 Task 1)
#   3  no stamp at all   -> a dev checkout, or a box no deploy has touched
#   4  the path is there but is not a regular file (a directory, say)
#   5  jq is not on PATH — a MISSING DEPENDENCY, never a corrupt stamp
#   6  it exists and does not parse as a build stamp
#
# 5 is separate from 6 for the reason the whole file is built on: two conditions
# an operator acts on completely differently (`apt install jq` vs. redeploy)
# must not share one signal.
#
# The output is SIX lines — the five fields (version as `""` when absent) and
# an `END` sentinel — and both
# are checked. `$( )` strips trailing newlines, so a sentinel-less form would lose
# an empty last field silently; the sentinel makes any truncation loud. A field
# carrying a control byte (a newline in `ref` would break the line format; a
# backspace would let the printed sha lie on a terminal) is rejected as
# unreadable rather than printed — the same rule `_check_wrappers` applies to
# roster ids, for the same reason.
_box_build_fields() {
  # The optional argument (stage 4, Task 6) points this SAME parser at a
  # stamp that is not the box's own — `_inst_stamp`'s shipped-stamp arm
  # validates a release artifact's `build.json` before installing it. One
  # parser, two files, rather than a second jq filter that would come to
  # disagree with this one about what a well-formed stamp is
  # (single-definition.test.ts counts the `jq -r` parses in this file and
  # fails on a second). No caller but that arm passes an argument.
  local stamp="${1:-$BOX_STAMP_FILE}"
  BOX_BUILD=()
  [[ -e "$stamp" ]] || return 3
  # Exists but is not a plain file (e.g. a directory) — a shape problem, not
  # an absence; must not be read as "unstamped" (round-1 review, Minor 8).
  [[ -f "$stamp" ]] || return 4
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 5
  local out
  # `version` (stage 4, Task 1) is ADDITIVE: absent on every pre-release stamp
  # — the `has` arm below accepts that as the ordinary case — and, when
  # present, held to the same non-empty-string rule as the other three, the
  # exact split `shared/buildinfo.ts` makes (two validators that disagree
  # about a well-formed stamp would have this verb printing one the server
  # refuses to serve). It travels as a fifth line, `""` for absent, so the END
  # sentinel keeps its truncation duty at index 5.
  out="$(jq -r '
    try (
      if (type == "object")
         and (.sha | type == "string") and (.sha | length > 0)
         and (.ref | type == "string") and (.ref | length > 0)
         and (.builtAt | type == "string") and (.builtAt | length > 0)
         and (.dirty | type == "boolean")
         and ((has("version") | not)
              or ((.version | type == "string") and (.version | length > 0)))
      then "\(.sha)\n\(.ref)\n\(.builtAt)\n\(.dirty)\n\(.version // "")\nEND"
      else empty
      end
    ) catch empty
  ' -- "$stamp" 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ -n "$out" ]] || return 6
  mapfile -t BOX_BUILD <<< "$out"
  [[ "${#BOX_BUILD[@]}" -eq 6 && "${BOX_BUILD[5]}" == END ]] || { BOX_BUILD=(); return 6; }
  unset 'BOX_BUILD[5]'
  local f
  for f in "${BOX_BUILD[@]}"; do
    [[ "$f" =~ [[:cntrl:]] ]] && { BOX_BUILD=(); return 6; }
  done
  return 0
}

cmd_version() {
  [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || _ccrc_usage_die "$1"
  local rc=0
  _box_build_fields || rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) ;;
    3) echo "$PROG unstamped (no $BOX_STAMP_FILE — this box has no build stamp yet)"
       return 0 ;;
    4) _ccrc_die "build stamp unreadable: $BOX_STAMP_FILE (not a regular file)" ;;
    5) _ccrc_die "jq is required by 'ccrc version' but is not on PATH" ;;
    *) _ccrc_die "build stamp unreadable: $BOX_STAMP_FILE" ;;
  esac
  local dirty=""
  [[ "${BOX_BUILD[3]}" == true ]] && dirty=", dirty"
  echo "$PROG ${BOX_BUILD[0]} (${BOX_BUILD[1]}, built ${BOX_BUILD[2]}$dirty)"
  # The release tag, iff the stamp carries one (stage 4, Task 1) — its own
  # line, so an unversioned box's output is byte-identical to before.
  if [[ -n "${BOX_BUILD[4]}" ]]; then
    echo "version ${BOX_BUILD[4]}"
  fi
}

# ── WHERE IS THIS BOX'S SERVER? ───────────────────────────────────────────
# `status` and doctor's `fleet` check both need one thing before they can ask
# anything: an address. There are exactly two places one can come from, and
# NEITHER of them is a literal in this file.
#
#   1. `$CCRC_ADDR` — `deploy/notify.sh`'s own seam, which every fleet-host
#      hook already honours. notify.sh once carried one operator's tailnet
#      address as a baked third-tier default; Stage 5 deleted it (a notify
#      that cannot resolve an address now sends nothing), and the same rule
#      was always in force here: a doctor that silently probed a baked
#      address on somebody else's box would be reporting on a machine they
#      have never heard of.
#   2. `~/.ccrc/ccrc.env`'s `CCRC_HOST` + `CCRC_PORT` — the box's own machine
#      config, and the same two values `ccrc.service` reads through
#      `EnvironmentFile=` (deploy/ccrc.env.example).
#
# BOTH keys are required, and a lone `CCRC_HOST` is answered "I cannot tell"
# rather than completed from `server/src/config.ts`'s `?? 7788`. That default is
# real, but it lives in TypeScript, on the other side of a language boundary
# this bash cannot import across — copying it here would be one value declared
# twice, which is the drift `single-definition.test.ts` exists to stop, and
# guessing it wrong produces a confident answer about a box nobody asked.
#
# MEASURED, and worth knowing before reading the SKIP: the FLEET host has no
# `~/.ccrc/ccrc.env` at all (it has `agent.env`, which configures the agent and
# holds a token). So on that box the answer today is "no address" and the fleet
# check SKIPS — correctly: nothing there knows where the server is. Export
# `CCRC_ADDR` for a run, or give the box a `ccrc.env`, and it answers.
#
#   0 -> two lines: the address, then WHERE it came from (for the detail line)
#   1 -> nothing configured at all
#   2 -> one line: a sentence naming the source and what is wrong with it.
#        NEVER the offending value: it comes off a config file and lands on a
#        terminal that acts on control bytes.
_box_server_addr() {
  local addr="" src=""
  if [ -n "${CCRC_ADDR:-}" ]; then
    addr="$CCRC_ADDR"; src="CCRC_ADDR"
  elif [ -e "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    # "there is no config file" (return 1) and "there is one and I could not
    # read it" (return 2) are two different things to tell an operator, and
    # without these two guards the second arrives as the first — the read below
    # would fail, bash would put its own diagnosis on stderr, and every key
    # would come back empty, which is indistinguishable from a file that
    # declares nothing.
    [ -f "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ] \
      || { printf '%s\n' "$BOX_ENV_FILE is not a regular file"; return 2; }
    [ -r "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ] \
      || { printf '%s\n' "$BOX_ENV_FILE cannot be read (check its mode: ls -l $BOX_ENV_FILE)"; return 2; }
    local host port
    host="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_HOST)"
    port="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_PORT)"
    if [ -n "$host" ] && [ -n "$port" ]; then
      addr="$host:$port"; src="$BOX_ENV_FILE"
    elif [ -n "$host" ]; then
      printf '%s\n' "$BOX_ENV_FILE declares CCRC_HOST but no CCRC_PORT"
      return 2
    elif [ -n "$port" ]; then
      printf '%s\n' "$BOX_ENV_FILE declares CCRC_PORT but no CCRC_HOST"
      return 2
    else
      return 1
    fi
  else
    return 1
  fi
  # A host:port and nothing else — an IPv6 literal in its URL brackets counts.
  # This is a sanity gate on a value that goes into a URL and into printed
  # output, not a resolver: a name that does not resolve is curl's answer to
  # give, not this function's.
  local addr_re='^([A-Za-z0-9]([A-Za-z0-9._-]*[A-Za-z0-9])?|\[[0-9A-Fa-f:]+\]):[0-9]{1,5}$'
  if [[ ! "$addr" =~ $addr_re ]]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$src does not spell a host:port address"
    return 2
  fi
  printf '%s\n%s\n' "$addr" "$src"
}

# ONE KEY OUT OF AN ENV FILE, BY NAME, AND NOTHING ELSE.
#
# `~/.ccrc/ccrc.env` holds this box's secrets as well as its addresses —
# `CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN`, `CCRC_VAPID_PRIVATE`, `CCRC_HETZNER_TOKEN`. Three rules
# follow, and all three are load-bearing:
#   - it is NEVER sourced. Sourcing executes it and exports every one of those
#     into this process and into every child it spawns (curl, node, jq).
#   - only the requested key's value is ever returned; every other line is read
#     and dropped. Nothing here prints, logs or exports another line of it.
#   - the shape read is a SUBSET of what systemd's own `EnvironmentFile=` parser
#     accepts — this file's actual consumer: optional leading whitespace, then
#     `KEY=value`, with one layer of surrounding quotes stripped. Deliberately
#     NOT supported, and named rather than implied (round-2 review, Minor 2):
#     backslash line-continuations, and escape sequences inside a quoted value.
#     A subset is safe HERE because the failure is one-directional — a form this
#     misses yields no address, which SKIPS the check with a detail, never a
#     wrong address confidently probed — but it is a subset, and a comment that
#     claimed the whole parser was a claim that could quietly stop being true.
#     THE SUBSET HAS TO STAY A SUBSET, which is why the whitespace set below is
#     spelled out rather than borrowed from POSIX (Task 8): `[[:space:]]` also
#     contains vertical tab and form feed, and systemd's whitespace is space,
#     tab, CR and the newline that already ended the line. Skipping a \v that
#     systemd does not skip reads a key systemd never set, i.e. it answers with
#     an address this box is NOT configured with — the one direction the
#     paragraph above says this reader must never fail in.
# The LAST assignment wins, as it does in systemd and in a shell.
_box_env_value() {
  local file="$1" key="$2" line v=""
  # Space, tab, CR — systemd's set, minus the newline `read` has consumed. A
  # variable rather than a literal bracket expression because a tab and a CR
  # typed inline are invisible in a diff and one stray edit from being deleted.
  local ws=$' \t\r'
  while IFS= read -r line || [ -n "$line" ]; do
    # Leading whitespace, exactly as systemd skips it — `_dr_trim`'s left half,
    # in the one namespace this file can reach, over systemd's character set
    # rather than POSIX's. `$ws` expands INSIDE the bracket expression, which
    # is why the pattern is unquoted here.
    line="${line#"${line%%[!$ws]*}"}"
    case "$line" in
      "$key="*) v="${line#"$key="}" ;;
      *) continue ;;
    esac
  done < "$file"
  # Trailing CR (a file edited on Windows) and surrounding quotes.
  v="${v%$'\r'}"
  case "$v" in
    \"*\") v="${v#\"}"; v="${v%\"}" ;;
    \'*\') v="${v#\'}"; v="${v%\'}" ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$v"
}

# ── _box_auth_path — WHICH FILE IS THIS BOX'S PASSPHRASE SECRET (D-143) ───
# `server/src/config.ts:339` is `env.CCRC_AUTH_SECRET_PATH || <default>`, so a
# box CAN redirect the gate's secret, and the two things in this tree that
# touch that file — `cmd_passwd`, which writes it, and doctor's `auth` check,
# which measures it — must both follow the redirect or both refuse. Neither may
# quietly use the default.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT COSMETIC. With the override set and a valid file at the
# override path, a `passwd` that wrote the default would leave: a server that
# keeps working (it reads the override), a `passwd` that reports success, and a
# doctor that then reports `PASS auth: … gen=1` about the file it just wrote.
# An operator rotating after a compromise gets a green transcript and a LIVE
# SECRET THAT NEVER CHANGED — a silent security no-op, which is the failure
# class this codebase's "no overloaded seam" rule exists to make impossible.
#
# THE ANSWER IS THE PATH; THE RETURN CODE IS WHERE IT CAME FROM, because the
# two callers say different things about each:
#   0  the default ($BOX_AUTH_FILE) — no override, or an empty one, which is
#      absent to `||` exactly as it is to config.ts's own reader.
#   2  an ABSOLUTE override. Honoured, and both callers SAY SO — a file this
#      tool writes somewhere other than the documented path must never be a
#      silent choice.
#   3  a RELATIVE override. REFUSED by both callers, and this is the one case
#      where following the config would be worse than refusing: config.ts does
#      not resolve it either, so the server resolves it against whatever
#      working directory systemd gives `ccrc.service`, and this tool would
#      resolve it against the operator's `$PWD`. Two different files, and
#      nothing on the box can say which one is the secret. The path is still
#      printed, for the message.
#
# ── AND THE SAME QUESTION FOR THE SESSION FILE (D-148) ───────────────────
# `CCRC_SESSIONS_PATH` is the same kind of key with the same `||` reader in
# `config.ts`, and doctor's `auth` remedy NAMES the session file — so it had
# the same defect one key over: it resolved the SECRET through this function
# and then printed a hard-coded `~/.ccrc/sessions.json`, which on a box that
# overrides the key tells an operator to delete a file nothing reads, in the
# same breath as a correctly-resolved secret path.
#
# ONE FUNCTION, PARAMETERISED, rather than a second near-identical copy: two
# resolvers with two spellings of the same three-way rule is precisely the
# drift this file's header bans, and the whole point of D-143 was that the
# tools which touch a file must not each hold their own opinion about which
# file it is. `_box_auth_path` and `_box_sessions_path` are now the same code
# with a different key and a different default, so they cannot disagree.
_box_path_for() {   # $1 = env key, $2 = the default when it is unset or empty
  local v=""
  if [ -f "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ] && [ -r "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    v="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" "$1")"
  fi
  if [ -z "$v" ]; then
    printf '%s' "$2"
    return 0
  fi
  printf '%s' "$v"
  case "$v" in
    /*) return 2 ;;
    *)  return 3 ;;
  esac
}

_box_auth_path()     { _box_path_for CCRC_AUTH_SECRET_PATH "$BOX_AUTH_FILE"; }
_box_sessions_path() { _box_path_for CCRC_SESSIONS_PATH "$BOX_SESSIONS_FILE"; }

# ── THE ONE NETWORK CALL: GET /api/fleet/health ───────────────────────────
# The server is the only party that can compare the two boxes — it holds this
# box's stamp and the fleet host's, and both rosters' digests — so this asks it
# and reports what it says. It DECIDES nothing about the comparison:
# `rosterAgreement`/`buildAgreement` (server/src/fleetstate.ts) own that.
#
#   0 -> BOX_HEALTH=(mode connected build roster)
#   4 -> curl is not on PATH (nothing was asked)
#   5 -> the server did not answer;      BOX_HEALTH_WHY = curl's exit code
#   6 -> it answered an HTTP error;      BOX_HEALTH_WHY = "HTTP <code>"
#   7 -> what came back is not the health JSON
#   8 -> node is not on PATH, so nothing could parse the answer
#   9 -> THE SESSION GATE REFUSED IT (D-150). Its own code, split out of 6.
#
# ── WHY 9 EXISTS (D-150) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `/api/fleet/health` is GATED, deliberately and after review: it publishes
# roster digests, build stamps and divergence, and exempting a route that says
# that much to widen a diagnostic's reach is a worse trade than losing the
# diagnostic. So on a box with `CCRC_AUTH=on` this call answers 401 — which is
# CORRECT BEHAVIOUR BY BOTH PARTIES and used to land in code 6, whose callers
# say "it answered HTTP 401 … read the server's own log". That sends an
# operator to a journal where nothing is wrong, on exactly the box the runbook
# tells them to run doctor on right after arming the gate.
#
# The distinction is real and the callers act on it differently, which is the
# whole test for a separate code here: 6 means something is wrong at the other
# end, 9 means nothing is wrong and this measurement is simply not available
# from outside the gate.
#
# CLASSIFIED FROM THE BODY, AND STILL NOTHING FROM THE BODY IS PRINTED (see
# below). A bare 401 is not enough: a reverse proxy or a captive portal in
# front of this address answers 401 too, and telling that operator "your
# session gate refused it" would be the same class of wrong answer this code
# exists to stop. The test is for THIS server's refusal envelope — the
# `error: 'unauthenticated'` + `verdict` pair `server/src/auth/gate.ts` sends
# and nothing else does. Matched with bash's own `=~`, not node: this arm has
# to work on a degraded box, and the 200 path already reports code 8 when node
# is missing precisely because it could not avoid it. Whitespace-tolerant so
# it is a claim about the JSON rather than about one serializer's spacing.
#
# NOTHING FROM THE BODY IS EVER PRINTED. The four fields are CLASSIFIED against
# the vocabulary `shared/api.ts` declares and only ever surface as one of those
# tokens, or as `?` for "present and not one of them". A body off a network,
# echoed into a verdict line an operator pastes into a ticket, is the same
# hazard `_check_wrappers` escapes roster ids for — except that here nothing has
# to be escaped, because nothing is quoted back.
#
# Parsed with node rather than jq for D-70's reason, restated: `jq` is stubbed
# as `exit 0` throughout the doctor suite (it is a PRESENCE check there), so a
# jq-based reader would have seen an empty answer in every fixture and passed
# everything. `_check_wrappers` reads the roster with node for the same reason.
_box_health() {
  local addr="$1"
  BOX_HEALTH=(); BOX_HEALTH_WHY=""
  command -v curl >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 4
  # `-w '\n%{http_code}'` rather than `-f`: `-f` collapses "no answer" and "an
  # HTTP error" into one nonzero exit, and those are two different problems
  # (the box is down / the box is broken). The body and the code come back in
  # one stream, split on the LAST newline.
  local out rc=0
  # `-w` carries curl's OWN `\n` escape (two characters in argv, one newline in
  # the output) rather than `$'\n'`: a real newline inside an argument shows up
  # in `ps`, in a log line and in this suite's own stub log as a second record,
  # and an argv that cannot be printed on one line is an argv nobody can read.
  #
  # `-s` and NOT `-sS` (round-2 review, Minor 1): `-S` asks curl to print its
  # error message despite `-s`, and this redirects stderr away, so the two
  # cancelled out. Discarding it is the deliberate half of that pair — curl's
  # exit code IS the diagnosis this reports, and a check's subprocess writing
  # to stderr mid-run interleaves with the verdict lines an operator is reading
  # on stdout. (Deliberately unlike `_check_gh_auth`, which captures gh's stderr
  # and quotes its first line: gh's exit codes are not distinguishing, curl's
  # are, and gh answers once where this can answer on every doctor run.)
  out="$(curl -s --max-time "$CCRC_HEALTH_TIMEOUT" -H 'accept: application/json' \
    -w '\n%{http_code}' "http://$addr/api/fleet/health" 2>/dev/null)" || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then BOX_HEALTH_WHY="$rc"; return 5; fi
  case "$out" in
    *$'\n'*) ;;
    *) BOX_HEALTH_WHY=""; return 7 ;;
  esac
  local code="${out##*$'\n'}" body="${out%$'\n'*}"
  if [ "$code" != 200 ]; then
    # WHY carries the whole PHRASE, not a bare code, so both arms read as
    # English in the one sentence the callers wrap it in ("… answered <why>
    # when asked for /api/fleet/health"). It used to carry either a number or
    # the words "an unreadable status", which produced "answered HTTP an
    # unreadable status" — dead text nobody had read aloud (round-2 review).
    if [ "$code" = 401 ] \
       && [[ "$body" =~ \"error\"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*\"unauthenticated\" ]] \
       && [[ "$body" =~ \"verdict\"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*\" ]]; then
      # WHY stays EMPTY: the callers' arm-9 sentences are self-contained, and a
      # leftover "HTTP 401" sitting in this variable is one copy-paste away from
      # reappearing in the very message this code exists to replace.
      BOX_HEALTH_WHY=""
      return 9
    fi
    case "$code" in
      [0-9][0-9][0-9]) BOX_HEALTH_WHY="HTTP $code" ;;
      *) BOX_HEALTH_WHY="a status this check could not read" ;;
    esac
    return 6
  fi
  command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 8
  local fields
  fields="$(CCRC_HEALTH_BODY="$body" node -e '
    let j;
    try { j = JSON.parse(process.env.CCRC_HEALTH_BODY); } catch (e) { process.exit(3); }
    if (!j || typeof j !== "object" || Array.isArray(j)) process.exit(4);
    // ABSENT READS AS "unknown" for build and roster, and that is the wire
    // contract itself (shared/api.ts: both fields are optional, "absent reads
    // as unknown", the absence-permits rule). ONE reader per field, so an older
    // server omitting one cannot arrive here as a fourth state. `mode` is NOT
    // optional on the wire, so its absence is a malformed answer and lands in
    // the same "?" as an unrecognised one — the caller does the same thing
    // about both, which is the only licence to collapse two conditions.
    const one = (v, allowed, whenAbsent) =>
      (v === undefined || v === null) ? whenAbsent : (allowed.indexOf(v) >= 0 ? String(v) : "?");
    process.stdout.write([
      one(j.mode, ["local", "remote"], "?"),
      one(j.connected, [true, false], "?"),
      one(j.build, ["agreed", "skewed", "unknown"], "unknown"),
      one(j.roster, ["agreed", "divergent", "unknown"], "unknown"),
      "END",
    ].join("\n"));
  ' 2>/dev/null)" || return 7
  mapfile -t BOX_HEALTH <<< "$fields"
  [[ "${#BOX_HEALTH[@]}" -eq 5 && "${BOX_HEALTH[4]}" == END ]] || { BOX_HEALTH=(); return 7; }
  unset 'BOX_HEALTH[4]'
  return 0
}

# The two units ccrc itself installs (deploy/ccrc.service, ccrc-agent.service),
# measured with `is-active` and nothing else — status never touches a unit, and
# every `ccd supervise` unit beside them is long-lived by design (CLAUDE.md).
# Sets `BOX_UNITS=(<ccrc.service state> <ccrc-agent.service state>)`, in the
# order of `BOX_UNIT_NAMES`, because TWO lines are written from this one
# measurement — `services:` (what was measured) and `role:` (what it implies) —
# and measuring twice is how the two would come to disagree on a box whose units
# changed state between them.
#
# XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is defaulted exactly as `ccd:93-95` defaults it, and for the
# same reason: without it `systemctl --user` cannot find the bus from a
# non-interactive SSH context, and status would report "unknown" on a perfectly
# healthy box. `$UID` is a bash builtin — no `id` subprocess to fail on the
# broken box this tool exists for.
BOX_UNIT_NAMES=(ccrc.service ccrc-agent.service)
BOX_UNITS=()
_box_units() {
  BOX_UNITS=()
  local u state
  # THE PROBE IS THE PLATFORM'S, and `_have_systemctl` is the one place that
  # decides which — same function, same stub point, that ccd reads. The
  # `unknown` fallback and the rc 1 are unchanged: a box whose manager cannot
  # be asked reports `unknown` per unit, never `inactive`, because "nothing
  # answered" and "the service is down" are two different sentences and this
  # file's callers print them differently.
  if ! _have_systemctl; then
    for u in "${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[@]}"; do BOX_UNITS+=("unknown"); done
    return 1
  fi
  # The two systemd session-bus defaults are LINUX-ONLY by construction:
  # launchctl needs neither, and exporting XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/<uid> on
  # macOS would invent a path that does not exist for the benefit of nothing.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then
    : "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:=/run/user/$UID}"; export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
    : "${DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS:=unix:path=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}/bus}"; export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
  fi
  for u in "${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[@]}"; do
    state="$(_svc_is_active "$u")"
    [ -n "$state" ] || state="unknown"
    BOX_UNITS+=("$state")
  done
  return 0
}

# WHAT THIS BOX IS FOR, INFERRED — and labelled as inferred, every time.
# Nothing on a box records its own role today (D-73): the topology lives in
# CLAUDE.md and in an operator's head, and the only evidence on disk is which
# unit is running. So this reports the inference AND names it as one, rather
# than asserting a role the box never declared. The moment a box does declare
# one, this becomes a read of that declaration and the word "inferred" goes.
#
# ── `unknown` IS NOT `inactive`, AND NEITHER IS ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE ───────
# `_box_units` answers `unknown` for a unit it could not ask — no systemctl, no
# bus, an empty reply — and that is a THIRD state, not a synonym for "not
# running". Every branch below is therefore written against what was MEASURED:
#
#   - "neither" is a negative claim about BOTH units, so it requires both to
#     have answered. It used to require only that they were not both unknown
#     (`&&` where this now has `||`), so a box where one unit could not be
#     asked and the other was merely inactive was told "no ccrc unit is active
#     here" — a statement about a unit nobody asked. Task 8 fixed it; the
#     nine-state table in server/test/ccrc-doctor.test.ts pins every cell.
#   - one unit ACTIVE and the other UNKNOWN is a real role plus an open
#     question ("is it also the other half?"), and both halves are said out
#     loud. Answering a bare "server" there would settle, silently, the
#     single-box question that separates it from the first branch.
#
# Same defect class either way, and the same one the `connected` arm of
# `cmd_status` had: asserting a state that was never measured.
_box_role() {
  local ccrc_state="${BOX_UNITS[0]}" agent_state="${BOX_UNITS[1]}"
  local role
  if [ "$ccrc_state" = active ] && [ "$agent_state" = active ]; then
    role="server AND fleet host (both units active — a single-box install)"
  elif [ "$ccrc_state" = active ] && [ "$agent_state" = unknown ]; then
    role="server (and ${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[1]} could not be asked, so whether this box is ALSO a fleet host was not measured)"
  elif [ "$agent_state" = active ] && [ "$ccrc_state" = unknown ]; then
    role="fleet host (and ${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[0]} could not be asked, so whether this box is ALSO a server was not measured)"
  elif [ "$ccrc_state" = active ]; then
    role="server"
  elif [ "$agent_state" = active ]; then
    role="fleet host"
  elif [ "$ccrc_state" = unknown ] || [ "$agent_state" = unknown ]; then
    # At least one unit could not be asked. "I could not tell" is not "no unit
    # is running", and inferring the second from missing evidence is the one
    # thing this line exists not to do — so the unasked unit is NAMED, because
    # "unknown" without it leaves an operator nothing to go and check.
    local unasked=""
    [ "$ccrc_state" = unknown ] && unasked="${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[0]}"
    [ "$agent_state" = unknown ] && unasked="${unasked:+$unasked and }${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[1]}"
    printf '%s' "unknown ($unasked could not be asked, so nothing was inferred — \"I could not tell\" is not \"no unit is active\")"
    return 0
  else
    role="neither (no ccrc unit is active here — a dev checkout, or a box mid-install)"
  fi
  printf '%s' "$role (inferred from the units below, not declared — no box records its own role yet)"
}

# How many ccd sessions this box holds, counted the way ccd itself counts them:
# one `<id>.uuid` file per session in the flat-file registry (ccd:8499). No
# `ls`, no `find` — a glob and an array length, so this answers on a box with no
# coreutils on PATH.
_box_sessions() {
  local reg="$HOME/.cc-sessions"
  if [ ! -d "$reg" ]; then
    printf '%s' "0 (no $reg on this box — it runs no ccd sessions)"
    return 0
  fi
  local -a f=()
  local had_nullglob=0
  shopt -q nullglob && had_nullglob=1
  shopt -s nullglob
  f=("$reg"/*.uuid)
  [ "$had_nullglob" -eq 1 ] || shopt -u nullglob
  printf '%s' "${#f[@]} in $reg (one registered ccd session each)"
}

# cmd_status — WHAT THIS BOX IS, as facts, one per line. The question doctor
# cannot answer: doctor judges a box's fitness, status describes it, and the
# two are different jobs on purpose. A skewed fleet is a FAIL from doctor and a
# plain line of fact here.
#
# READ-ONLY, and exit 0 whatever it finds — including a fleet that disagrees.
# An operator scripting `ccrc status` gets a report; the thing that returns a
# judgement is `ccrc doctor`, whose exit code means exactly one thing. A status
# that exited 1 on a skewed fleet would give the fleet check a second, quieter
# home, and two answers to one question is how they come to disagree.
#
# EVERY LINE GOES TO STDOUT: it is the RESULT of the verb, in this file's own
# stdout/stderr split. And every line names what was measured — the same "never
# the bare word ok" rule the doctor checks keep — because a status that prints
# `fleet: ok` is indistinguishable from a status that never asked.
cmd_status() {
  [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || _ccrc_usage_die "$1"

  local rc=0
  _box_build_fields || rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) local dirty="clean"
       [[ "${BOX_BUILD[3]}" == true ]] && dirty="DIRTY — what runs here is not the commit it names"
       printf 'build:     %s (%s) built %s, %s\n' \
         "${BOX_BUILD[0]}" "${BOX_BUILD[1]}" "${BOX_BUILD[2]}" "$dirty"
       # The release tag, iff the stamp carries one (stage 4, Task 1) — its
       # own fact line; absent field, absent line.
       if [[ -n "${BOX_BUILD[4]}" ]]; then
         printf 'version:   %s\n' "${BOX_BUILD[4]}"
       fi ;;
    3) printf 'build:     unstamped (no %s — no deploy has ever stamped this box)\n' "$BOX_STAMP_FILE" ;;
    4) printf 'build:     unreadable (%s is not a regular file)\n' "$BOX_STAMP_FILE" ;;
    5) printf 'build:     unreadable (jq is not on PATH, so %s cannot be parsed)\n' "$BOX_STAMP_FILE" ;;
    *) printf 'build:     unreadable (%s does not parse as a build stamp)\n' "$BOX_STAMP_FILE" ;;
  esac

  # ONE measurement, TWO lines: what is running, and what that implies this box
  # is for. `_box_units` is called here, in cmd_status's own shell rather than
  # in a `$( )`, so both lines are written from the same reading.
  _box_units || true
  printf 'role:      %s\n' "$(_box_role)"
  printf 'services:  %s\n' "${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[0]} ${BOX_UNITS[0]}, ${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[1]} ${BOX_UNITS[1]}"
  printf 'sessions:  %s\n' "$(_box_sessions)"

  local addr_out addr_rc=0
  addr_out="$(_box_server_addr)" || addr_rc=$?
  local -a af=()
  [ -z "$addr_out" ] || mapfile -t af <<< "$addr_out"
  if [ "$addr_rc" -eq 1 ]; then
    printf 'server:    none configured (no CCRC_ADDR, and no CCRC_HOST/CCRC_PORT in %s)\n' "$BOX_ENV_FILE"
    printf 'fleet:     not measured (this box has no server address to ask)\n'
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "$addr_rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    printf 'server:    misconfigured (%s)\n' "${af[0]:-$BOX_ENV_FILE}"
    printf 'fleet:     not measured (this box has no usable server address)\n'
    return 0
  fi
  local addr="${af[0]}" src="${af[1]}"
  printf 'server:    %s (from %s)\n' "$addr" "$src"

  local h_rc=0
  _box_health "$addr" || h_rc=$?
  case "$h_rc" in
    4) printf 'fleet:     not measured (curl is not on PATH, so the server could not be asked)\n'; return 0 ;;
    5) printf 'fleet:     not measured (the server did not answer — curl exited %s)\n' "$BOX_HEALTH_WHY"; return 0 ;;
    6) printf 'fleet:     not measured (the server answered %s when asked for /api/fleet/health)\n' "$BOX_HEALTH_WHY"; return 0 ;;
    7) printf 'fleet:     not measured (the server answered something that is not the health JSON)\n'; return 0 ;;
    8) printf 'fleet:     not measured (node is not on PATH, so the answer could not be parsed)\n'; return 0 ;;
    # D-150. Not a fault, and it must not read as one: the gate is armed and
    # /api/fleet/health is behind it, so this measurement is unavailable from
    # outside the gate BY DESIGN. `services:` and the doctor's `auth` check
    # above still say everything else about this box.
    9) printf 'fleet:     not measured (this box'"'"'s session gate is armed, and /api/fleet/health is behind it — expected on an armed box, not a fault)\n'; return 0 ;;
  esac

  local mode="${BOX_HEALTH[0]}" connected="${BOX_HEALTH[1]}"
  local build="${BOX_HEALTH[2]}" roster="${BOX_HEALTH[3]}"
  if [ "$mode" != remote ]; then
    if [ "$mode" = local ]; then
      printf 'mode:      local — that server drives its own box directly\n'
      printf 'fleet:     not measured (the server is in local mode; there is no second box to disagree with)\n'
    else
      printf 'mode:      unreadable — the server did not say which mode it is in\n'
      printf 'fleet:     not measured (the server did not say which mode it is in)\n'
    fi
    return 0
  fi
  printf 'mode:      remote (fleet host connected: %s)\n' "$connected"
  # ONE WORD FOR THE PAIR, then both answers behind it. `agreed` is the strongest
  # claim this verb can make and it requires everything: both answers agreed AND
  # the server currently in contact with the fleet host. Anything else gets a
  # weaker word — never `agreed` for something nobody measured, and never
  # `agreed` for something measured at a contact that has since dropped.
  #
  # THE `connected` ARM IS THREE-WAY, and the middle case is the one that used to
  # be wrong (round-2 review, Important): `[ "$connected" = false ]` alone made
  # `?` — the classifier's token for absent-or-unrecognised — take the `true`
  # branch, so a body with no `connected`, or with `"yes"`, or with the STRING
  # `"false"`, printed `agreed` beside `connected: ?`. `shared/api.ts:1484`
  # declares `connected` REQUIRED, so its absence is a malformed answer and not
  # the absence-permits rule that `roster?`/`build?` carry; and either way an
  # answer this tool did not understand must not become a claim that the
  # opposite is true. The classifier carried the distinction — this line was
  # narrowing it, which is the one rule the architecture doc calls
  # highest-yield.
  local overall link=""
  case "$connected" in
    true) ;;
    false) link="; the server is NOT currently in contact with the fleet host, so both answers describe the last contact" ;;
    *)     link="; the server did not say whether it is in contact with the fleet host, so neither answer can be trusted to describe now" ;;
  esac
  if [ "$build" = skewed ] || [ "$roster" = divergent ]; then overall=disagreed
  elif [ "$build" != agreed ] || [ "$roster" != agreed ]; then overall=unknown
  elif [ -n "$link" ]; then overall=stale
  else overall=agreed
  fi
  printf 'fleet:     %s — build %s, roster %s%s\n' "$overall" "$build" "$roster" "$link"
}

# cmd_doctor — run every check in `ccd/ccrc-doctor-checks`'s table and report
# each one's verdict, THEN a summary count. Exit 0 when every check passed or
# warned, 1 when any failed (2 stays reserved for a usage error, which is why
# doctor needed 1 free — see the exit-code table above).
#
# The loop never stops early, and that is the same reason this file does not
# use `set -e`: an operator running doctor wants the whole list, not the first
# problem. A check SIGNALS with its return code (0 pass / 1 fail / 2 warn /
# 3 SKIPPED) and prints its own line(s); this function only counts and tallies.
# It deliberately does NOT decide anything about a check's subject — that is the
# check's job, and keeping the decision inside the check is what makes "one
# table entry plus one test" the true cost of adding one.
#
# The tally is over VERDICT LINES, and a check may print two of them when it
# has findings of two classes (a FAIL and a WARN, each with its own remedy —
# see `ccrc-doctor-checks`'s header). Its return code is then the worse class,
# and this function CROSS-CHECKS the two: a code that is not the worst class
# printed is reported as a bug in ccrc rather than silently believed. The
# summary's "N checks" stays a count of table entries, so a run in which one
# check answered twice really does show more verdicts than checks.
#
# ── THE FOURTH OUTCOME: A CHECK THAT DID NOT RUN (Task 7) ────────────────
# Some checks have a subject that may not exist on this box at all — `fleet`
# compares TWO boxes, and a box with no server to ask has nothing to compare.
# That check must not PASS (it would claim an agreement nobody measured, the
# overloaded-null defect this codebase bans by name) and must not WARN (nothing
# is wrong). It prints exactly one
#
#     SKIP <name>: <why there was nothing to measure>
#
# line, NO verdict line and no remedy, and exits 3. This function counts it
# against CHECKS and never against verdicts, and the summary prints the number
# of skips — so "N checks, M verdicts" stays a true statement about how much of
# the box was actually measured, and an operator can see the difference between
# a check that answered and one that could not.
#
# The two halves are CROSS-CHECKED against each other exactly as the return code
# and the verdict lines already are: exit 3 with a verdict line, a SKIP line
# with any other exit code, or exit 3 with nothing printed at all are each
# reported as a bug in ccrc. "I did not run" and "here is my answer" cannot both
# be true, and a silent skip is a check that vanished from the run.
#
# A COMPLETE INSTALL OF THE CLI IS FOUR FILES IN ONE DIRECTORY, not two: this
# script, `ccrc-doctor-checks`, `ccrc-wrapper-shape` (which the checks file
# sources for the account-wrapper contract it shares with `ccrc-adopt`) and
# `ccrc-adopt` itself, which `cmd_adopt` execs. DOCTOR alone needs only the
# first three, which is what `ccrc-doctor-checks` and `ccrc-wrapper-shape` say
# in their own headers — the same sentence about different sets, not a
# contradiction: an install that ships three has a working `doctor` and a
# broken `adopt`. All four resolve each other through
# `${BASH_SOURCE[0]}`, which bash does NOT follow through a symlink, so linking
# `ccrc` onto PATH does not drag its siblings along — whatever installs one has
# to install all four. A missing library is not fatal here: `_check_wrappers`
# reports its own absence as a FAIL with a remedy, so the other ten checks
# still answer.
#
# THAT IS WHY THE DEPLOY SHIPS A SHIM (Task 8, `install_ccrc_shim` in
# deploy/deploy.sh) rather than a copy: `~/.local/bin/ccrc` execs
# `~/ccrc/ccd/ccrc`, the rsynced tree where all four files sit together and
# where `_dr_pkg_candidates` finds the shipped `package.json` one level up.
# Copying this file alone onto PATH breaks doctor's sourcing; copying all four
# into `~/.local/bin` breaks the node check instead, whose floor would then be
# looked for at `~/.local/{server,agent}/package.json`.
#
# Ways this refuses rather than reporting a healthy box it never measured — a
# doctor that runs zero checks and exits 0 is the worst failure mode this verb
# has:
#   - the checks file is missing or unreadable   -> refuse (exit 1)
#   - it defined no table at all                 -> refuse (exit 1)
#   - a table entry has no `_check_<name>`       -> that entry FAILS loudly
#   - a check dies without printing a verdict    -> that entry FAILS loudly
# The last two are bugs in ccrc rather than facts about the box, and say so.
#
# EVERY CHECK RUNS IN A SUBSHELL — the `out="$( … )"` below — and that is the
# whole point of the capture, not a stylistic choice about output (Task 4
# review, Minor 5). A check that trips `set -u` (an unbound variable) is FATAL
# to the shell it runs in, not a `return 1`: called directly, one such check
# would take the entire run down mid-table, printing no summary and leaving
# every later check unmeasured — precisely the failure this file's own header
# rails against when it explains why `-e` is off. A subshell bounds the blast
# radius to that one check; the parent tallies it and carries on. (A stray
# `return 3` was already survivable; only the fatal class was not.)
#
# The captured output is then CHECKED for a verdict line rather than echoed
# blindly. A check that dies mid-run prints nothing, and without this the run
# would emit 9 verdict lines under a summary claiming 10 checks — internally
# consistent and silently missing the one that matters. stderr is deliberately
# NOT captured: bash's own "unbound variable" message is the best diagnosis of
# that state and belongs in front of the operator.
cmd_doctor() {
  [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || _ccrc_usage_die "$1"
  local checks="$CCRC_HERE/ccrc-doctor-checks"
  [[ -f "$checks" ]] \
    || _ccrc_die "doctor's check table is missing: $checks — is this a complete ccrc install?"
  # shellcheck source=ccrc-doctor-checks
  . "$checks" \
    || _ccrc_die "doctor's check table failed to load: $checks"
  declare -p CCRC_DOCTOR_CHECKS >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _ccrc_die "doctor's check table declares no CCRC_DOCTOR_CHECKS: $checks"
  [[ "${#CCRC_DOCTOR_CHECKS[@]}" -gt 0 ]] \
    || _ccrc_die "doctor's check table is empty: $checks — refusing to report a box nobody measured"

  # `ran` counts CHECKS and `skipped` counts the ones with nothing to measure;
  # pass/warn/fail count VERDICT LINES. They were one number until a check
  # needed to answer in two classes at once; keeping the summary's "N checks"
  # honest means counting the table entries themselves.
  local name rc out ran=0 skipped=0 pass=0 warn=0 fail=0
  for name in "${CCRC_DOCTOR_CHECKS[@]}"; do
    ran=$((ran + 1))
    if ! declare -F "_check_$name" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      printf 'FAIL %s: the check table names it but no _check_%s function exists\n' "$name" "$name"
      printf '  remedy: this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — the table and the checks in %s have drifted\n' "$checks"
      fail=$((fail + 1))
      continue
    fi
    out="$( "_check_$name" )"; rc=$?
    [[ -z "$out" ]] || printf '%s\n' "$out"

    # THE VERDICT LINES ARE COUNTED, NOT THE RETURN CODE, because a check may
    # answer in two classes at once — a FAIL for the findings that are defects
    # and a WARN for the ones that may well be deliberate, each with its own
    # remedy (ccrc-doctor-checks, "ONE CHECK MAY ANSWER IN TWO CLASSES").
    # Counting the return code alone printed `0 warned` under a run with warn
    # lines on the screen, which is a false count of the thing an operator
    # skims (Task 5 review, Important 1). The summary's leading number stays
    # the number of CHECKS, so a run where one check answered twice shows more
    # verdicts than checks — deliberately: that is what happened.
    local vp=0 vw=0 vf=0 vs=0 line
    while IFS= read -r line; do
      case "$line" in
        "PASS $name: "*) vp=$((vp + 1)) ;;
        "WARN $name: "*) vw=$((vw + 1)) ;;
        "FAIL $name: "*) vf=$((vf + 1)) ;;
        "SKIP $name: "*) vs=$((vs + 1)) ;;
      esac
    done <<< "$out"

    # A SKIPPED CHECK, and the three ways of getting it wrong. Both halves have
    # to agree — exactly one SKIP line, no verdict line, exit 3 — because each
    # half alone is a statement the other can contradict, and a run that
    # believed either one on its own would either count a measurement nobody
    # made or drop a check off the tally without saying so.
    if [[ "$rc" -eq 3 || "$vs" -gt 0 ]]; then
      if [[ "$rc" -eq 3 && "$vs" -eq 1 && $((vp + vw + vf)) -eq 0 ]]; then
        skipped=$((skipped + 1))
        continue
      fi
      printf 'FAIL %s: the check exited %s having printed %s SKIP and %s verdict line(s) — a skipped check prints exactly one SKIP line, no verdict line, and exits 3\n' \
        "$name" "$rc" "$vs" "$((vp + vw + vf))"
      printf '  remedy: this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — see _check_%s in %s\n' "$name" "$checks"
      fail=$((fail + 1))
      continue
    fi

    if [[ $((vp + vw + vf)) -eq 0 ]]; then
      # No verdict line, whatever the exit code said. The usual cause is a
      # check that died before it could print one (see the subshell note
      # above); the other is a check that printed a verdict for the wrong name.
      printf 'FAIL %s: the check printed no verdict line of its own (it exited %s)\n' "$name" "$rc"
      printf '  remedy: this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — see _check_%s in %s, and any error above this line\n' "$name" "$checks"
      fail=$((fail + 1))
      continue
    fi

    case "$rc" in
      0|1|2) ;;
      *)
        # A check may only answer 0/1/2/3 (3 is handled above). A stray
        # `return 4` lands here and is counted as a failure, named as the bug it
        # is, rather than being read as one of the legal outcomes.
        printf 'FAIL %s: the check exited %s, which is not one of 0 (pass), 1 (fail), 2 (warn) or 3 (skipped)\n' "$name" "$rc"
        printf '  remedy: this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — see _check_%s in %s\n' "$name" "$checks"
        fail=$((fail + 1))
        continue ;;
    esac

    # The return code and the verdict lines are two statements of ONE answer,
    # so they are cross-checked rather than one being quietly preferred: the
    # code must be the WORST class the check printed. Without this, moving the
    # tally onto the lines would have moved the exit code with it — a check
    # that returned 1 while printing only a WARN would have made doctor exit 0.
    local worst=PASS want=0
    if [[ "$vf" -gt 0 ]]; then worst=FAIL; want=1
    elif [[ "$vw" -gt 0 ]]; then worst=WARN; want=2
    fi
    if [[ "$rc" -ne "$want" ]]; then
      printf 'FAIL %s: the check exited %s, but the worst verdict line it printed is a %s, which is %s\n' \
        "$name" "$rc" "$worst" "$want"
      printf '  remedy: this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — see _check_%s in %s\n' "$name" "$checks"
      fail=$((fail + 1))
      continue
    fi

    pass=$((pass + vp)); warn=$((warn + vw)); fail=$((fail + vf))
  done

  # BOTH NOUNS ARE PRINTED, because the three tail numbers count verdicts and
  # the leading one counts checks, and a line where 9+1+2 does not equal 11
  # reads as an arithmetic bug to anyone who has not read this function. The
  # repair a later reader would reach for — count each check once, by its worst
  # class — is exactly the `0 warned`-with-warnings-on-screen lie this round
  # removed, so the line says what each number is instead of inviting the fix.
  # The skip count rides the CHECKS half for the same reason: it is a number of
  # checks that did not answer, not a fourth kind of answer, and printing it
  # always (including as `(0 skipped)`) keeps the line one shape for whatever
  # reads it next.
  printf 'summary: %s checks (%s skipped), %s verdicts — %s passed, %s warned, %s failed\n' \
    "$ran" "$skipped" "$((pass + warn + fail))" "$pass" "$warn" "$fail"
  [[ "$fail" -eq 0 ]] || return 1
}
# ── the remedy for a `foreign` file, said once ────────────────────────────
# TWO arms of `cmd_wrappers`'s decision table print this same sentence since
# D-156 — the file that IS a wrapper but says something else, and the file this
# reader cannot parse at all — and a second copy of it is how the two would come
# to disagree about what an operator should do.
#
# `_ccrc_*` and not `_wrap_*`: `ccrc-wrapper-shape:37-46` reserves the latter for
# the shape library, where `_wrap_set_diff`'s dynamic-scope contract makes a
# shadowed name a correctness bug rather than a style one.
_ccrc_foreign_remedy() {
  local id="$1" stamp="$2"
  printf 'move it aside (mv "%s/%s" "%s/%s.saved-%s") and re-run, or set exec.kind to "external" for "%s" in %s/.ccrc/accounts.json so ccrc stops claiming that name\n' \
    "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR" "$id" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR" "$id" "$stamp" "$id" "$HOME"
}

# ── cmd_wrappers — THE ROSTER WRITES THE WRAPPERS ─────────────────────────
# Stage 2c. `~/.ccrc/accounts.json` stops being a DESCRIPTION of what is in
# `$HOME/.local/bin` and becomes the thing that PRODUCES it. This verb converges
# the two, one account at a time, and refuses — loudly, with a remedy — for
# every file it did not write itself.
#
# ── THE ONE ABSOLUTE RULE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `upstream` and `external` accounts are NEVER written, backed up, moved or
# removed, by this verb, under any flag. `upstream` is the ~304 MB Claude Code
# ELF; `external` is somebody else's hand-written launcher (`gpt` on the
# reference box is 142 lines of it). Both would be destroyed by one careless
# write and neither can be regenerated from the roster. That rule is
# STRUCTURAL here, not a test's opinion, and it is held by four things at once:
#
#   1. `deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs` NAMES every non-generated account out loud, in
#      a `protected <id>` manifest record (D-80), and this function refuses the
#      WHOLE RUN — not merely that id — if an id appears in both that list and
#      the `wrapper` list. An overlap is never a fact about a box; it is proof
#      the generator is broken, and its other verdicts are then untrustworthy
#      too, so there is nothing left worth converging.
#   2. This function iterates the MANIFEST's `wrapper` records and nothing else,
#      and `deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs` emits a `wrapper` record only for an
#      account whose `exec.kind` is "generated" (plan D5: node stages, bash
#      mutates). An upstream or external id therefore never reaches the loop.
#   3. Every path this function writes to is built as `$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id`
#      from an id that was first matched against `WRAPPER_ID_RE`. There is no
#      other write, and no removal at all beyond this function's own temp files.
#   4. Before installing, the STAGED text is read back through
#      `_wrap_parse_shape` and refused if its exec target is the id being
#      installed. A wrapper at `~/.local/bin/claude` that ends
#      `exec "$HOME/.local/bin/claude" "$@"` is a fork bomb, and it is exactly
#      what a generator talked into staging the upstream account would produce.
#   5. THE WITNESS INDEX (D-156). Before any write, this function reads every
#      id-shaped file already in `$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR` and records which ids they
#      `exec` as their upstream binary. It then refuses — under every flag, per
#      id — to write an id anything else already execs. Built once, before the
#      action pass, so the answer cannot depend on the order records happen to
#      be processed in.
#
# WHY FIVE AND NOT FOUR — lock 5 is the only one whose evidence is not something
# the generator said:
#   - Locks 2 and 4 both ultimately key on `execKind === 'generated'` (see
#     below), and lock 3 constrains only the SHAPE of a path, never its
#     meaning. All three therefore believe a mis-edited roster, because a
#     mis-edited roster is internally consistent — `parseRoster` requires
#     exactly one upstream and it has exactly one.
#   - Measured: with `claude` flipped to "generated" and another account made
#     upstream, `ccrc wrappers --force` overwrote `~/.local/bin/claude` — a
#     2741-byte launcher — with a generated wrapper, and exited 0. Because
#     claude2, claude-corp and claude-dev0 all end
#     `exec "$HOME/.local/bin/claude" "$@"`, that closed an exec loop across
#     EVERY LANE AT ONCE: `claude2 --version` went from rc=127 to rc=124, i.e.
#     it stopped terminating at all. Lock 4 does not catch it — the staged
#     wrapper execs the OTHER account, so it is not self-referential, and lock
#     4 tests only self-reference.
#   - `--force` was never the only route, which is why lock 5 sits above the
#     decision table rather than inside the `foreign` arm. Obeying this
#     function's OWN printed remedy — move the file aside and re-run — makes
#     the path `absent`, and the absent arm writes with no flag at all;
#     thereafter the file is `ccrc-unmodified` and is rewritten on every roster
#     change. A lock keyed on the subject file's bytes cannot survive the
#     subject file being moved away. One keyed on the OTHER files can, and that
#     is the whole design.
#   - Until 2026-08-20 none of this was reachable, and not by design: the file
#     at that path was ~334 MB, so it classified `oversize`, which no flag
#     overrides. The box's launcher shrank to 2.7 KB and a lock nobody had
#     written down stopped existing. That is the incident this lock is the
#     answer to, recorded here so it is not later removed as redundant.
#
# WHY FOUR AND NOT THREE — the measurements that bought lock 1, because a lock
# added without its incident becomes a lock somebody removes as redundant:
#   - Locks 2 and 4 are NOT independent of each other in the way they look.
#     Both ultimately key on one predicate, `execKind === 'generated'`: lock 2
#     directly, lock 4 because only a wrapper generated FOR the upstream account
#     is self-referential. So a bug in that predicate defeats both together.
#   - Lock 4 covers `upstream` ONLY. `external` has no self-referential
#     property to derive a violation from — `gpt`'s staged text would exec
#     `claude`, which is perfectly well-formed — so bash cannot tell, from the
#     bytes in front of it, that `gpt` is somebody else's launcher. Node has to
#     say so.
#   - Measured, and worse than "needs a flag": with a manifest carrying
#     `wrapper<TAB>gpt<TAB>ccrc-unmodified<TAB>no`, a PLAIN `ccrc wrappers` —
#     no `--force`, no `--adopt` — rewrote the external account's launcher and
#     exited 0, printing "(upstream and external are never written)" in its own
#     summary line. Only the default `foreign` arm refused such a file, and that
#     is generic foreign protection, not protection of an external ACCOUNT.
#   - An earlier round reported `gpt` as saved by lock 4. It was not: it was
#     saved by ROSTER ORDER. The five-account fixture roster it was measured
#     against (since replaced by the in-memory DEFAULT_TEST_ROSTER) listed `claude`
#     first, so the run aborted at record #1 before `gpt` was reached. A roster
#     listing `gpt` first loses `gpt` and keeps `claude`. An accident of
#     ordering is not a lock.
#
# TWO INDEPENDENT LOCKS ON ONE DOOR, and THIS side is the strict one. The
# generator holds the same fact twice — the `execKind === 'generated'` filter
# that produces the `wrapper` records, and the independent walk of
# `roster.accounts` that produces the `protected` records. This reader trusts
# NEITHER over the other: it refuses on overlap rather than preferring a list,
# and it asserts the protected count against the summary's own
# `<upstream>` + `<external>` so a manifest truncated exactly at those records
# cannot drop the lock in silence.
#
# ── ITERATE THE MANIFEST, NEVER THE STAGING DIRECTORY ─────────────────────
# `deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs` writes only the ids it staged and never reads the
# directory back, so a stray file left in the staging dir by anything else
# survives unreported (measured in the Task 5 review; its header states the
# same rule from the other side). A converger driven by `readdir` of the
# staging dir would install that stale entry under an account id the CURRENT
# roster no longer declares. The manifest is the complete and authoritative
# list of what this run staged and for which ids; a directory listing is not.
#
# ── THE DECISION TABLE (plan D3), one branch per state, no collapses ──────
#   absent                          -> write
#   ccrc-unmodified, text == staged -> converged, nothing touched
#   ccrc-unmodified, text != staged -> rewrite (backup first)
#   ccrc-edited                     -> REFUSE; --force rewrites (backup first)
#   foreign, EQUIVALENT (D4)        -> REFUSE, reported as adoptable;
#                                      --adopt or --force rewrites (backup)
#   foreign, parses as a wrapper    -> REFUSE; only --force rewrites (backup)
#   foreign, does NOT parse (D-156) -> REFUSE under every flag
#   unreadable                      -> REFUSE under every flag
#   oversize                        -> REFUSE under every flag (D-81)
#
# …and ABOVE all of it, lock 5 (D-156): any id another file on disk already
# execs is REFUSED under every flag, whatever the row above would have said —
# including `absent`, which is the row this table's own foreign remedy creates.
#
# D-156 SPLIT THE `foreign` ROW because one classify was covering two different
# sentences. "A wrapper that says something else" is a file ccrc has read and
# can name every field of, so `--force` answers a real question. "A script this
# reader cannot parse at all" is not that: overwriting it is the same act as
# overwriting `unreadable` or `oversize` — destroying something on the strength
# of a judgement this process never made — so it refuses in those arms' terms.
#
# `unreadable` refuses even under `--force` because "I could not read it" is not
# "I know what it is" — the one refusal no flag can talk out of. `oversize`
# refuses under the same absolute terms for a sibling reason: a file over
# `deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs`'s `OVERSIZE_BYTES` is never even read (D-81's whole
# point — past V8's string cap `readFileSync` used to THROW and land such a
# file in `unreadable`, whose remedy could never work on something perfectly
# readable), so bash never has bytes to judge, and there is nothing for
# `--force` to override either.
#
# THE REMEDY FOR A NON-EQUIVALENT `foreign` FILE MUST NOT MENTION `--force`,
# and that is a guard with a test of its own, not a matter of taste. Its remedy
# is "move it aside, or set exec.kind to external in the roster". An operator
# working down a list of refusals types the next thing the tool printed;
# printing `--force` beside a file ccrc has just said it does not understand is
# how a 142-line bespoke launcher gets destroyed by someone doing as they were
# told. `--force` is real, it is documented in `--help`, and it is deliberately
# absent from THAT remedy.
#
# Exit 0 = converged and nothing was refused. 1 = something was refused, or a
# write failed. 2 = a usage error (this file's standing table).
cmd_wrappers() {
  local dry=0 adopt=0 force=0
  # `ccrc-adopt:110-118`'s loop, minus the value-taking flags it needs and this
  # verb does not. Unlike `version`, this verb HAS flags of its own — three, and
  # each one changes what it is willing to overwrite — so `-h`/`--help` prints
  # the usage that explains them rather than being the usage error it is for a
  # verb with nothing to explain (see `cmd_version`'s header for that decision).
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      --dry-run) dry=1; shift ;;
      --adopt)   adopt=1; shift ;;
      --force)   force=1; shift ;;
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      *) _ccrc_usage_die "$1" ;;
    esac
  done

  # The shape contract, sourced rather than restated — `ccrc-adopt:76-94`'s
  # reasoning applies unchanged, and this verb is the one that most needs it:
  # it is the WRITER, and the writer judging "is this file mine" by a second,
  # looser copy of the reader's rules is how a generator comes to disown its own
  # output. Same "is this a complete ccrc install?" wording `cmd_adopt` uses.
  local shape="$CCRC_HERE/ccrc-wrapper-shape"
  [[ -f "$shape" ]] \
    || _ccrc_die "the wrapper shape contract is missing: $shape — is this a complete ccrc install?"
  # shellcheck source=ccrc-wrapper-shape
  . "$shape" \
    || _ccrc_die "the wrapper shape contract failed to load: $shape"

  # One directory up from THIS FILE, exactly as `ccrc-adopt` resolves
  # `gen-accounts.mjs` (:109-125): true in a checkout and at `~/ccrc/deploy` on
  # a deployed box, because both deploy lanes rsync `deploy/` into `~/ccrc/`.
  local gen="$CCRC_HERE/../deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs"
  [[ -f "$gen" ]] \
    || _ccrc_die "the wrapper generator is missing: $gen — it ships one directory up from this script (at ~/ccrc/deploy on a deployed box); is this a complete ccrc install?"

  # BY NAME, and before anything is created or written. Without this, a box with
  # no node fails somewhere inside a command substitution and reports a manifest
  # it could not read — "a missing dependency" and "a corrupt manifest" are two
  # conditions an operator acts on completely differently, which is the
  # overloaded-seam mistake this file's own header bans (see `cmd_version`'s jq
  # branch for the same refusal in the same shape).
  command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _ccrc_die "node is required by 'ccrc wrappers' — it runs $gen to read the roster and stage each wrapper — but is not on PATH. Install Node (this repo's floor is in server/package.json's \"engines\") or put it on PATH, then re-run."
  # D-156: `stat` joins node in the up-front dependency list, and that placement
  # is the whole point. The witness index below size-gates every file it reads
  # out of $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR before reading it, and a size gate that falls back
  # to "read it anyway" when `stat` is missing is not a gate. Naming the
  # dependency HERE makes the in-loop fallback branch unreachable, which is how
  # this avoids copying `ccrc-doctor-checks`'s fall-open — doctor may go on
  # REPORTING without `stat`, because reporting destroys nothing; this verb
  # WRITES, and `cmd_wrappers` already hard-requires node, mktemp, date, cp,
  # chmod and mv, so coreutils is not a new class of dependency for it.
  command -v stat >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _ccrc_die "stat is required by 'ccrc wrappers' — it size-gates every file this verb reads out of $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR before reading it — but is not on PATH. Nothing was written."
  # The same mixed-install question `_check_wrappers` asks (D-155): the shape
  # library is a SEPARATE install artifact, landed by its own `install_atomic`,
  # so "it loaded" and "it is the build this file expects" are two facts. Under
  # `set -u` the second one, unasked, takes the whole verb down on an unbound
  # variable mid-run — after staging, which is exactly when "nothing was
  # written" stops being true. `:-` is not the answer: it would put a second
  # spelling of each threshold back into this file.
  [ -n "${WRAPPER_WITNESS_MAX_BYTES:-}" ] && [ -n "${WRAPPER_SHEBANG:-}" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "$shape loaded but is older than this ccrc — it declares no WRAPPER_WITNESS_MAX_BYTES/WRAPPER_SHEBANG, which this verb needs to tell what execs what before it writes anything. The files ship together and must be one build: re-run the install (or redeploy). Nothing was written."

  # A FRESH BOX HAS NO ~/.local/bin, AND THAT IS NOT AN ERROR: this verb is the
  # installer, so a missing bin dir is a box at the start of its life rather
  # than a box that is broken. `-m 0755` applies to the FINAL directory only
  # (POSIX `mkdir -p`), which is the one whose mode matters because it goes on
  # PATH; an EXISTING directory is left exactly as the operator has it.
  #
  # BEHIND `--dry-run`, because a dry run that creates directories is a dry run
  # nobody can trust. Measured: it used to create BOTH `$HOME/.local` and
  # `$HOME/.local/bin` on a fresh box and exit 0, while the README promised it
  # reports without touching anything. The report was the thing that was wrong,
  # not the promise.
  if [ "$dry" -eq 0 ]; then
    # shellcheck disable=SC2174  # -m applying to the deepest directory ONLY is
    # the intent, not an oversight: it is the one that goes on PATH.
    mkdir -p -m 0755 "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR" \
      || _ccrc_die "cannot create $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR — nothing was written"
  fi

  local staging
  staging="$(_plat_mktemp_d)" \
    || _ccrc_die "cannot create a staging directory (mktemp -d failed) — nothing was written"
  # ARMED BEFORE ANYTHING IS WRITTEN INTO IT, and with the path expanded HERE
  # rather than at exit: `staging` is a function-local, so a `trap 'rm -rf
  # "$staging"' EXIT` would fire under `set -u` against an unbound variable on
  # every path that returns normally, leaving the directory behind. This suite's
  # own fixture helper (server/test/tmpHelpers.ts) exists because 7,830 leaked
  # temp directories were once measured on this box; a leak in the shipped tool
  # is the same defect with no afterAll underneath it.
  # shellcheck disable=SC2064
  trap "rm -rf -- $(printf '%q' "$staging")" EXIT

  # WHAT THE GENERATOR IS POINTED AT TO *READ*. Normally the bin dir itself.
  # On a dry run against a box that has no `~/.local/bin` at all, there is
  # nothing to point it at and creating one would be the very thing a dry run
  # must not do — so it reads an empty directory INSIDE the staging tree (which
  # the EXIT trap already owns) and answers, correctly, that every wrapper is
  # `absent` and there are no orphans. That is the true answer for a box with no
  # bin directory; nothing is invented and nothing outside the temp tree is
  # created. Every path this verb PRINTS still comes from `$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR`,
  # never from here, so the report names the box's own paths.
  local scan_dir="$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR"
  if [ ! -d "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR" ]; then
    scan_dir="$staging/.bin-scan"
    mkdir -p "$scan_dir" \
      || _ccrc_die "cannot create a scratch directory inside $staging — nothing was written"
  fi

  # STDOUT is the manifest; STDERR is deliberately NOT captured, so node's own
  # diagnosis reaches the operator verbatim. For an invalid roster that
  # diagnosis includes a `gen-wrappers: remedy: …` line naming the fix, and that
  # line is the useful message — swallowing it to re-word the failure here would
  # replace a fix with a shrug.
  local manifest rc=0
  manifest="$(node "$gen" "$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json" "$scan_dir" "$staging")" || rc=$?
  [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] \
    || _ccrc_die "$gen exited $rc without a usable manifest, so nothing was written — its own message above says why"

  # ── READING THE MANIFEST ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # D-71 bans `IFS=$'\t' read` for any tab-delimited record that can carry an
  # EMPTY field: a tab is IFS whitespace, so bash collapses a run of them and
  # every field after the empty one shifts left — silently, with no parse error
  # to notice. This call is SAFE, and the reason lives in the producer rather
  # than here (plan D6, restated in `deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs`'s header): every
  # field of every record is NON-EMPTY BY CONSTRUCTION — `id` matched `ID_RE`,
  # and the record type, `classify` and `equal` are literals drawn from small
  # closed sets. The trailing vars are the only ones that come back empty, on
  # the shorter record types, and a trailing empty field is exactly the case
  # `read` gets right.
  #
  # IF A FUTURE FIELD CAN EVER BE EMPTY — an optional annotation, say — DO NOT
  # keep this idiom. Split the line by hand on literal tabs, the way
  # `_check_wrappers`'s roster reader does (`ccd/ccrc-doctor-checks`). Keeping
  # `IFS=$'\t' read` with a possibly-empty field is how a reader starts reading
  # the wrong column without ever producing an error — here that would mean
  # taking a wrapper's `classify` out of the `equal` column and overwriting a
  # file on the strength of it.
  #
  # FIVE variables, not four: the `summary` record carries four fields after its
  # type. Reading it into three would hand the last variable "1<TAB>1" — the
  # remainder, tabs and all — and the record-count assertion below would then be
  # comparing against a string that is not a number.
  local kind a b c d
  local sum_total="" sum_gen="" sum_up="" sum_ext="" saw_summary=0
  local -a w_id=() w_class=() w_equal=() protected=() orphans=()
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r kind a b c d; do
    case "$kind" in
      '') continue ;;
      summary)   saw_summary=1; sum_total="$a"; sum_gen="$b"; sum_up="$c"; sum_ext="$d" ;;
      wrapper)   w_id+=("$a"); w_class+=("$b"); w_equal+=("$c") ;;
      protected) protected+=("$a") ;;
      orphan)    orphans+=("$a") ;;
      *) _ccrc_die "the manifest from $gen carries a record type this verb does not know — this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box, and nothing was written" ;;
    esac
  done <<< "$manifest"

  [ "$saw_summary" -eq 1 ] \
    || _ccrc_die "the manifest from $gen has no summary line, so there is nothing to check the wrapper records against — nothing was written"
  local f
  for f in "$sum_gen" "$sum_up" "$sum_ext"; do
    [[ "$f" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] \
      || _ccrc_die "the manifest from $gen has a summary whose counts are not all numbers — nothing was written"
  done
  # THE TRUNCATION GATE. bash cannot tell a complete manifest from a half-
  # delivered one by looking at what arrived (`deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs`'s header
  # says so from the writer's end, which is why it assembles the whole thing
  # before writing a byte). This is the reader's half of that contract: a run
  # that installed the records it happened to receive would converge half a box
  # and report success.
  [ "${#w_id[@]}" -eq "$sum_gen" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "the manifest from $gen is truncated: its summary claims $sum_gen generated account(s) and it carries ${#w_id[@]} wrapper record(s). Nothing was written."
  # THE SAME GATE FOR THE `protected` LIST (D-80), and it is not symmetry for
  # its own sake: the protected records are the LAST thing before the orphans,
  # so a manifest truncated a few bytes earlier would arrive with every wrapper
  # record intact and the entire untouchable-account lock silently absent. A
  # lock that a truncation can delete is not a lock. Counted against the
  # summary's own upstream + external, because those two numbers come off a
  # different walk of `roster.accounts` than the protected records do.
  [ "${#protected[@]}" -eq "$((sum_up + sum_ext))" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "the manifest from $gen is truncated: its summary claims $sum_up upstream + $sum_ext external account(s) and it carries ${#protected[@]} protected record(s), so the list of accounts ccrc must never write is incomplete. Nothing was written."

  # ONE timestamp for the whole run, so every backup a single run writes reads
  # as one event. (Two convergences inside the same second would reuse the name;
  # that needs a file to be edited between two runs less than a second apart,
  # and the alternative — refusing to converge because the clock has not
  # ticked — is worse.)
  local stamp
  stamp="$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)" \
    || _ccrc_die "cannot read the clock (date -u failed), and a backup needs a name — nothing was written"

  # ── PRE-PASS: EVERY RECORD IS VALIDATED BEFORE THE FIRST BYTE IS WRITTEN ──
  # Each refusal below ends "nothing was written", and this loop is what makes
  # that sentence TRUE rather than merely reassuring. These checks used to live
  # inside the action loop, where a manifest whose SECOND record was bad printed
  # `WRITE claude2 …` and then, two lines later, `… and nothing was written` —
  # two statements about one run, contradicting each other, with the first one
  # right. (It was invisible to the tests because every stub manifest that
  # reached these branches carried a single record.) These are all statements
  # about the MANIFEST, not about the box, so none of them needs to see disk
  # state that a write could change: hoisting them costs nothing and restores
  # the all-or-nothing the messages promise.
  #
  # The staged parse is cached here for the action loop, which is the other half
  # of the same argument — re-parsing per record in two places is how the
  # pre-pass and the writer would come to disagree about the same file.
  local i id
  local sok starget ssuffix ssecrets p
  local -a s_target=() s_suffix=() s_secrets=()
  for ((i = 0; i < ${#w_id[@]}; i++)); do
    id="${w_id[$i]}"

    # The id off the manifest is about to become a FILENAME under
    # $HOME/.local/bin. The gate is on the ID, before any path is built from it,
    # which is what makes it hold for a traversing id (`../../.bashrc`) as well
    # as for a merely illegal one (`claude2.bak`) — a check on the resulting
    # path would have to enumerate the ways a path can escape.
    [[ "$id" =~ $WRAPPER_ID_RE ]] \
      || _ccrc_die "the manifest from $gen names \"$id\", which is not a legal account id, and this verb will not build a path under $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR out of it — this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box, and nothing was written"

    # LOCK 1 (see the header): an id cannot be both a wrapper ccrc writes and an
    # account ccrc must never touch. THE WHOLE RUN STOPS, not just this id: the
    # two lists come off two independent walks of the same roster, so an overlap
    # means the generator disagrees with itself, and once that is true its
    # verdicts about every OTHER account are worth nothing either. Refusing one
    # id and converging the rest would be trusting a source this very line has
    # just caught contradicting itself.
    for p in ${protected[@]+"${protected[@]}"}; do
      [ "$p" != "$id" ] \
        || _ccrc_die "the manifest from $gen lists \"$id\" as an account ccrc must never write (exec.kind upstream or external) AND asks for a wrapper to be installed at \$HOME/.local/bin/$id. Those cannot both be true; this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box, and the whole run is refused — nothing was written."
    done

    [ -f "$staging/$id" ] \
      || _ccrc_die "the manifest from $gen names \"$id\" but staged no text for it — this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box, and nothing was written"

    # THE STAGED TEXT IS READ BACK THROUGH THE READER, on every box, on every
    # run. `server/test/wrapper-roundtrip.test.ts` pins that the emitter and
    # `_wrap_parse_shape` agree; this is the same check made on the actual bytes
    # about to be installed, which is the only place it can catch a file that
    # was staged, or truncated, by something other than this pipeline.
    IFS=$'\t' read -r sok starget ssuffix ssecrets < <(_wrap_parse_shape "$staging/$id")
    [ "$sok" = ok ] \
      || _ccrc_die "the text staged for \"$id\" is not the generated wrapper shape, so this verb will not install it — this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box, and nothing was written"
    # LOCK 4 (see the header). A wrapper whose exec target is its own id would
    # exec itself forever; it is also precisely the file a generator talked into
    # writing the UPSTREAM account would stage. It covers `upstream` only —
    # `external` has no such self-referential property, which is why lock 1
    # exists.
    [ "$starget" != "$id" ] \
      || _ccrc_die "refusing to install \"$id\": the wrapper the roster produced would exec \$HOME/.local/bin/$id — itself. That is an upstream account's own name, and ccrc never writes an upstream or external account. Nothing was written."

    s_target+=("$starget"); s_suffix+=("$ssuffix"); s_secrets+=("$ssecrets")
  done

  # ── LOCK 5 (D-156): THE WITNESS INDEX — who already execs what ───────────
  # Built ONCE, off disk, before a single write. Every other lock in this
  # function reasons about what the GENERATOR said; this one reasons about what
  # is already on the box, and that is the whole of its value: it is the only
  # lock that still refuses after the subject file has been moved aside, because
  # its evidence is the OTHER files and they are still there.
  #
  # Hoisted rather than computed per record for two reasons, and the second is
  # the load-bearing one. It saves re-scanning the directory once per generated
  # account — but more importantly it makes the answer ORDER-INDEPENDENT. This
  # file's own header records that `gpt` was once believed saved by lock 4 and
  # was actually saved by roster ORDER, and calls an accident of ordering "not a
  # lock". A per-record scan would have the same shape: by the time a late
  # record is reached, this run may already have rewritten the very wrappers
  # that witness it, and the lock would evaporate exactly when the roster is
  # most wrong.
  #
  # `$scan_dir`, never `$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR` directly — on a fresh box that is the
  # empty scratch dir inside the staging tree, so `--dry-run` still creates
  # nothing.
  #
  # NO `-x` FILTER, deliberately diverging from `ccrc-adopt`'s candidate rule
  # and doctor's: a witness is a fact about TEXT, and mode 0644 today is 0755
  # after one chmod.
  local -A wit_by=()
  local wit_blind=""
  local -a wit_cand=() wit_size=()
  local wf wname wsz wln1 wok wtarget wsuf wsec whad=0 wi
  shopt -q nullglob && whad=1
  shopt -s nullglob
  for wf in "$scan_dir"/*; do
    [ -f "$wf" ] || continue
    [[ "${wf##*/}" =~ $WRAPPER_ID_RE ]] || continue
    wit_cand+=("$wf")
  done
  [ "$whad" -eq 1 ] || shopt -u nullglob
  # ONE batched `stat`, not one per candidate: measured on the real 86-entry bin
  # dir, the per-file spelling cost 1.15 s against 0.10-0.15 s batched, and the
  # cost was the ~75 forks rather than the reads. `-L`, because a symlink's own
  # size is the length of its target string — `gpt -> ccgpt` stats as 33 bytes
  # while the file behind it is 7.8 KB, and a scan that measured the link would
  # then read the target whole. (`ccrc-doctor-checks:2542` passes `-L` for the
  # same reason, and so does `_acct_candidates` at :4026 — every sizing caller
  # in this tree spells it the same way. This parenthetical used to say doctor's
  # gate OMITTED `-L` and call that "a separate defect, recorded"; it names no
  # deviation because there is none, and it was false when written. Corrected at
  # Task 21's review round 1, which is when a third `-L` caller landed 1300
  # lines below and cited doctor's gate AS a `-L` caller, leaving the tree
  # asserting both things about one line.)
  #
  # `stat` prints one line per operand IN OPERAND ORDER and simply omits one it
  # could not answer for, so a count mismatch is the ONLY way a size could be
  # attached to the wrong name — and it is fatal rather than per-id, because a
  # misaligned index cannot be trusted about any id.
  if [ "${#wit_cand[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    mapfile -t wit_size < <(_plat_size -L -- "${wit_cand[@]}" 2>/dev/null)
    [ "${#wit_size[@]}" -eq "${#wit_cand[@]}" ] \
      || _ccrc_die "stat answered for ${#wit_size[@]} of the ${#wit_cand[@]} id-shaped files in $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR, so this run cannot line a size up with a name and cannot tell what execs what. Nothing was written."
  fi
  for ((wi = 0; wi < ${#wit_cand[@]}; wi++)); do
    wf="${wit_cand[$wi]}"; wname="${wf##*/}"; wsz="${wit_size[$wi]}"
    [[ "$wsz" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || { wit_blind="$wit_blind $wname"; continue; }
    [ "$wsz" -gt "$WRAPPER_WITNESS_MAX_BYTES" ] && continue
    # ITS OWN `-r` TEST, and this is not belt-and-braces. `_wrap_parse_shape`
    # folds "I could not read it" into "not a wrapper" (its `[ -r ]` guard),
    # which is right for its other callers and WRONG here: an unreadable file
    # could be a witness, so trusting that fold would make this lock fail OPEN
    # on exactly the file it cannot see. Same overloaded-null rule, applied to a
    # shared function's return value rather than to a variable.
    [ -r "$wf" ] || { wit_blind="$wit_blind $wname"; continue; }
    wln1=""; IFS= read -r wln1 < "$wf" 2>/dev/null || true
    [ "$wln1" = "$WRAPPER_SHEBANG" ] || continue
    IFS=$'\t' read -r wok wtarget wsuf wsec < <(_wrap_parse_shape "$wf")
    # STRICT: a witness must parse as the full generated shape. That is what
    # makes the size cap an entailment rather than a hole (a file too big to be
    # 2-3 significant lines is provably not one), and it is the same
    # one-reader-for-both-sides discipline the equivalence test states. The cost
    # is stated rather than hidden: a BESPOKE launcher casts no vote. Measured
    # live, the loose witness set for `claude` is 7 files and the strict set is
    # 3 — non-empty, so the launcher is protected here, but on a box where the
    # only thing exec'ing the upstream is bespoke this lock is silent and the
    # `foreign` arm is the remaining guard.
    [ "$wok" = ok ] || continue
    wit_by["$wtarget"]="${wit_by[$wtarget]:-} $wname"
  done

  # ── ACTION PASS: from here on, a refusal is about the BOX, not the manifest,
  #    and is reported per account rather than aborting the run. ─────────────
  local klass eq action why remedy backup tmp equivalent
  local dok dtarget dsuffix dsecrets
  local wit w
  local n_written=0 n_converged=0 n_refused=0
  for ((i = 0; i < ${#w_id[@]}; i++)); do
    id="${w_id[$i]}"; klass="${w_class[$i]}"; eq="${w_equal[$i]}"
    starget="${s_target[$i]}"; ssuffix="${s_suffix[$i]}"; ssecrets="${s_secrets[$i]}"

    action=""; why=""; remedy=""; backup=""

    # LOCK 5's gate, ABOVE the table on purpose. It has to fire on `absent`,
    # because `absent` is precisely the state ccrc's own foreign remedy
    # manufactures ("move it aside and re-run") and the absent arm writes with
    # no flag at all. Placed here it also pre-empts `--force` on a foreign file,
    # which is what lets this refusal say "No flag overrides this one" in the
    # same absolute terms as `unreadable` and `oversize`.
    #
    # `unreadable` and `oversize` are EXCLUDED, and that exclusion is measured
    # rather than tidy: both already refuse under every flag, so pre-empting
    # them protects nothing, and a mode-000 file at a write-target path is BOTH
    # an unreadable candidate and a record with its own table verdict — whose
    # voice is the right one, and whose remedy is pinned elsewhere. The property
    # this gate needs is "pre-empt every class that can WRITE", which is exactly
    # {absent, ccrc-unmodified, ccrc-edited, foreign}.
    #
    # Per-id, not whole-run: lock 1 aborts everything because the generator
    # contradicted ITSELF and its other verdicts are then worthless. A witnessed
    # id is not that — the roster is internally consistent and merely wrong
    # about one name, and refusing that one write is sufficient. `return 1` at
    # the end still makes it a failed `ccrc install`.
    case "$klass" in
      unreadable|oversize) ;;
      *)
        wit=""
        for w in ${wit_by[$id]:-}; do [ "$w" = "$id" ] || wit="$wit $w"; done
        if [ -n "$wit" ]; then
          n_refused=$((n_refused + 1))
          printf 'REFUSE %s: %s — the roster calls "%s" a generated account, but%s in %s already exec it as their upstream binary\n' \
            "$id" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id" "$id" "$wit" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR"
          printf '  remedy: %s\n' "set \"$id\"'s exec.kind to \"upstream\" (or \"external\") in $HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json so ccrc stops claiming that name. Moving the file aside does NOT help: what refuses this write is the OTHER files. No flag overrides this one."
          continue
        fi
        if [ -n "$wit_blind" ]; then
          n_refused=$((n_refused + 1))
          printf 'REFUSE %s: %s — this run could not read%s in %s, so it cannot tell whether anything there execs "%s"\n' \
            "$id" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id" "$wit_blind" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR" "$id"
          printf '  remedy: %s\n' "make those file(s) readable and stattable, or move them aside, then re-run. No flag overrides this one."
          continue
        fi ;;
    esac

    case "$klass" in
      absent)
        action="write"; why="nothing was at that path" ;;
      ccrc-unmodified)
        if [ "$eq" = yes ]; then
          action="none"
        else
          action="rewrite"; why="ccrc wrote it, nothing has edited it since, and the roster has changed"
        fi ;;
      ccrc-edited)
        if [ "$force" -eq 1 ]; then
          action="rewrite"; why="ccrc wrote it and it has been edited since (--force)"
        else
          action="refuse"
          why="ccrc wrote it and something has edited it since"
          remedy="keep the edit by setting exec.kind to \"external\" for \"$id\" in $HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json, or discard it and take the roster's version with: ccrc wrappers --force"
        fi ;;
      foreign)
        # D4: EQUIVALENCE IS JUDGED BY THE READER, ON BOTH SIDES. The on-disk
        # file and the staged file both go through `_wrap_parse_shape`, and the
        # two (target, suffix, secrets) triples are compared. It deliberately
        # does NOT compare the on-disk parse against roster fields: one reader
        # answering for both sides means there is no second opinion to drift,
        # and the staged file is what would actually be installed, which is the
        # thing the operator is being asked about. A `no` from either side —
        # a file the reader cannot make sense of at all — is never equivalent.
        equivalent=0
        IFS=$'\t' read -r dok dtarget dsuffix dsecrets < <(_wrap_parse_shape "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id")
        if [ "$dok" = ok ] && [ "$dtarget" = "$starget" ] \
           && [ "$dsuffix" = "$ssuffix" ] && [ "$dsecrets" = "$ssecrets" ]; then
          equivalent=1
        fi
        if [ "$equivalent" -eq 1 ]; then
          if [ "$adopt" -eq 1 ] || [ "$force" -eq 1 ]; then
            action="rewrite"; why="ccrc did not write it, but it says exactly what ccrc would say — adopted"
          else
            action="refuse"
            why="ccrc did not write it, but it says exactly what ccrc would say (same target, same config dir, same secrets file)"
            remedy="take it over with: ccrc wrappers --adopt — the file you have now is backed up first"
          fi
        elif [ "$dok" = ok ]; then
          # CUT 2 (D-156). `--force` MAY ONLY OVERWRITE A FILE THIS READER CAN
          # PARSE AS A WRAPPER. `$dok` is that parse and it is already in hand —
          # the equivalence test above read it — so this costs no second read.
          #
          # `foreign` is one classify covering two genuinely different
          # sentences, and until D-156 they shared an arm that honoured
          # `--force`:
          #   dok = ok — "a wrapper, saying something else". ccrc has read the
          #     file and can name every field it would replace, so `--force` is
          #     a real answer to a real question: I know what that is,
          #     overwrite it. This is the capability cut 2 KEEPS, and the one
          #     the README advertises.
          #   dok = no — "a script this reader cannot make sense of at all".
          #     Measured on the reference box: `~/.local/bin/claude` is 2741
          #     bytes of version-picking, token-injecting launcher. Under
          #     `OVERSIZE_BYTES` so `$gen` reads it; no ccrc marker so it
          #     classifies `foreign`; and one `--force` under a mis-edited
          #     roster overwrote it with a wrapper exec'ing
          #     `$HOME/.local/bin/claude` — itself — while claude-corp, claude2
          #     and claude-dev0 all end `exec "$HOME/.local/bin/claude" "$@"`.
          #     A FLEET-WIDE EXEC LOOP, from one flag. Until 2026-08-20 the
          #     only thing stopping it was that the file was ~334 MB and
          #     classified `oversize`; shrinking it to 2.7 KB removed a lock
          #     nobody had written down.
          #
          # Overwriting a file ccrc has just said it cannot parse is the same
          # category of act as overwriting `unreadable` or `oversize`:
          # destroying something on the strength of a judgement this process
          # never made. It refuses in those two arms' own absolute terms.
          if [ "$force" -eq 1 ]; then
            action="rewrite"; why="ccrc did not write it, it is a wrapper, and it says something else (--force)"
          else
            action="refuse"
            why="ccrc did not write it, and it is not the wrapper this roster describes"
            # NO `--force` IN THIS REMEDY, EVER — see the header. This is the
            # branch that holds somebody's bespoke launcher.
            remedy="$(_ccrc_foreign_remedy "$id" "$stamp")"
          fi
        else
          # No flag reaches this branch, exactly as in `unreadable` and
          # `oversize`. Move-aside is still offered, because for an id that
          # nothing else execs it genuinely works — the same shape `oversize`'s
          # own remedy has: an escape hatch AND "No flag overrides this one."
          action="refuse"
          why="ccrc did not write it and this reader cannot parse it as a wrapper at all"
          remedy="$(_ccrc_foreign_remedy "$id" "$stamp"). No flag overrides this one."
        fi ;;
      unreadable)
        # No flag reaches this branch. "I could not read it" is not "I know what
        # it is", and every other refusal here is a judgement about a file this
        # process has actually read.
        action="refuse"
        why="something is at that path and this process could not read it"
        remedy="make it readable (ls -l \"$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id\") and re-run, or move it aside. No flag overrides this one." ;;
      oversize)
        # D-81. No flag reaches this branch either, for the sibling reason to
        # `unreadable`'s: `$gen` never even read this file (a cheap `statSync`
        # gate ahead of `readFileSync`, so past-V8's-string-cap never THROWS
        # and never misclassifies a huge-but-readable file as `unreadable`
        # instead), so there are no bytes here for `--force` to judge either.
        action="refuse"
        why="it is over 1 MiB — categorically not a ccrc wrapper"
        remedy="if \"$id\" should be generated, move the big file aside (mv \"$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id\" \"$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id.saved-$stamp\") and re-run; if that file is the upstream binary, set \"$id\"'s roster entry to exec.kind \"upstream\" in $HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json instead. No flag overrides this one." ;;
      *)
        action="refuse"
        why="$gen classified it as \"$klass\", which this verb does not know"
        remedy="this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — $gen and cmd_wrappers in $CCRC_HERE/ccrc have drifted" ;;
    esac

    case "$action" in
      none)
        n_converged=$((n_converged + 1))
        printf 'CONVERGED %s: %s is already exactly what the roster says\n' "$id" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id"
        continue ;;
      refuse)
        n_refused=$((n_refused + 1))
        # The verdict line and its remedy are two halves of ONE result and both
        # go to STDOUT, adjacent — doctor's contract (this file's header, and
        # `server/test/ccrc-doctor.test.ts` pins the same adjacency there).
        printf 'REFUSE %s: %s — %s\n' "$id" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id" "$why"
        printf '  remedy: %s\n' "$remedy"
        continue ;;
    esac

    [ "$action" = rewrite ] && backup="$id.pre-ccrc-$stamp"
    if [ "$dry" -eq 1 ]; then
      n_written=$((n_written + 1))
      printf 'WOULD-%s %s: %s — %s%s\n' "${action^^}" "$id" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id" "$why" \
        "${backup:+ (the file there now would be copied to $backup first)}"
      continue
    fi

    if [ -n "$backup" ]; then
      # BEFORE the overwrite, always, and named so it can NEVER be mistaken for
      # an account. `WRAPPER_ID_RE` is `^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31}$` — a "." can
      # never appear in a legal id — so `<id>.pre-ccrc-<UTC>` is invisible to
      # `ccrc adopt`'s candidate scan and to `ccrc doctor`'s `_check_wrappers`
      # alike. That is LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness: a backup that looked like an
      # id would be adopted as a second account fighting the first over one
      # CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, and doctor would report a wrapper the roster never
      # declared. (It is the same "." that drops the `ccd.bak-<date>` and
      # `claude2.bak-<date>` files a real box accumulates — see
      # `ccd/ccrc-wrapper-shape:63-67`.)
      # `cp -p` so the backup keeps the mode and timestamps of what it replaced.
      cp -p -- "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$backup" \
        || _ccrc_die "could not back up $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id to $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$backup, so nothing was overwritten"
    fi

    # ATOMIC, in `deploy/deploy.sh`'s `install_atomic` shape (:50-58): the
    # staged text lands beside the target under a temp name, gets its mode, and
    # is RENAMED into place. Two things follow, and both matter on a live box:
    # a reader never sees a half-written wrapper (a half-written wrapper is an
    # account that cannot start a session), and the rename needs write
    # permission on the DIRECTORY rather than on the file — so a wrapper an
    # operator has chmod-ed read-only is still converged instead of failing
    # halfway. The temp name begins with a "." for the same reason the backup
    # name carries one: it can never match `WRAPPER_ID_RE`, so a leftover from
    # an interrupted run is never read as an account.
    tmp="$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/.$id.tmp.$$"
    if ! { cp -- "$staging/$id" "$tmp" && chmod 0755 "$tmp" \
             && mv -f -- "$tmp" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id"; }; then
      rm -f -- "$tmp"
      _ccrc_die "could not install $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id — nothing at that path was replaced${backup:+ (the copy at $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$backup is the file you already had)}"
    fi
    n_written=$((n_written + 1))
    printf '%s %s: %s — %s%s\n' "${action^^}" "$id" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id" "$why" \
      "${backup:+ (the file that was there is now $backup)}"
  done

  # ── SWEEP THIS TOOL'S OWN STRAYS ────────────────────────────────────────
  # `install_atomic` ENDS with `rm -f $dest.incoming-*`, and its comment records
  # that as a reproduced review finding rather than tidiness: a run killed
  # between the copy and the rename leaves a mode-0755 file in a directory that
  # is ON PATH, and nothing else will ever remove it. The "." in the name keeps
  # it out of the ACCOUNT namespace (adopt and doctor never see it), which is
  # the other half of that finding and the half the comment above already
  # answers — this is the half about a stray executable.
  #
  # It runs AFTER the loop, so this run's own temp files have already been
  # renamed away and the glob can only match leftovers — `install_atomic`'s own
  # reasoning, and the reason a concurrent run's in-flight temp is not something
  # this can hit either (two convergences racing over one file is already lost,
  # with or without this line). The glob is per manifest id and carries the
  # full `.<id>.tmp.` shape, so it can only match names THIS function makes:
  # nothing else in `~/.local/bin` is at risk from it. It runs for EVERY id,
  # not just the ones written, because the common case — a stray for an account
  # that is now converged — is never reached by a per-write sweep.
  if [ "$dry" -eq 0 ]; then
    local had_nullglob=0 stray
    shopt -q nullglob && had_nullglob=1
    shopt -s nullglob
    for id in ${w_id[@]+"${w_id[@]}"}; do
      for stray in "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/.$id.tmp."*; do
        # Reported, not silent: this is ccrc removing a file from a directory
        # the operator owns, and a removal nobody is told about is one nobody
        # can audit.
        rm -f -- "$stray" \
          && printf 'SWEPT %s: removed %s, a stray temp file an interrupted ccrc run left behind\n' \
               "$id" "$stray"
      done
    done
    [ "$had_nullglob" -eq 1 ] || shopt -u nullglob
  fi

  # ORPHANS ARE REPORTED AND NEVER REMOVED. A file carrying ccrc's marker whose
  # id no account claims is a fact about the box, not a refusal — nothing about
  # it stopped this run converging — so it does not touch the exit code.
  # Removal belongs to `ccrc uninstall` (Stage 4), which does not exist; a
  # converger that deleted files as a side effect of writing them would be a
  # verb an operator could not run without reading its source first.
  local o
  for o in ${orphans[@]+"${orphans[@]}"}; do
    printf 'ORPHAN %s: %s carries ccrc%s marker and no account in the roster claims it — reported, never removed\n' \
      "$o" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$o" "'s"
    printf '  remedy: add an account "%s" to %s if it should exist, or remove %s by hand once you are sure nothing runs it\n' \
      "$o" "$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$o"
  done

  printf 'summary: %s account(s) in %s — %s generated, %s upstream, %s external (upstream and external are never written); %s %s, %s converged, %s refused, %s orphaned\n' \
    "$sum_total" "$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json" "$sum_gen" "$sum_up" "$sum_ext" \
    "$n_written" "$([ "$dry" -eq 1 ] && printf 'would be written' || printf 'written')" \
    "$n_converged" "$n_refused" "${#orphans[@]}"

  [ "$n_refused" -eq 0 ] || return 1
  return 0
}

# cmd_adopt — rediscover this box's accounts from disk. The work is entirely
# `ccd/ccrc-adopt`'s, and this verb is how it becomes reachable: adopt is a
# tool that until now was installed by nothing, reached the fleet host only
# incidentally inside the `ccd/` rsync, and sat off PATH at
# `~/ccrc/ccd/ccrc-adopt` where nobody would find it. It is also, precisely, a
# LIFECYCLE operation — "give this hand-built box a roster" — which is this
# CLI's whole subject.
#
# ── EXEC, NOT SOURCE, AND NOT A SUBSHELL ─────────────────────────────────
# `ccrc-adopt` runs `set -euo pipefail` because it is a straight-line "measure,
# or die" pipeline; this file runs `set -uo pipefail` because doctor is a LIST
# of independent checks and `-e` would abort the run at the first failing one
# (see this file's header). Sourcing adopt would drag `-e` into the shell that
# argument is about, and its `exit`s would be ccrc's. `exec` hands the process
# over whole: adopt's stdout/stderr split, its argv handling (`--out`,
# `--force`, `-h`) and its exit codes arrive unaltered — and the two tools
# already share ONE exit-code table (0 success, 1 the tool ran and the answer
# was bad, 2 a usage error), so nothing has to be translated at the seam.
#
# `$BASH` — the absolute path of the interpreter already running this file —
# rather than `"$adopt"` directly or a bare `bash`: no PATH lookup on the box
# whose PATH may be the broken thing, and no dependency on the exec bit
# surviving whatever copied the tree.
#
# Arguments are NOT parsed here. `ccrc adopt --nope` must be adopt's own
# "unknown argument" at exit 2, not this file's — one tool, one usage message.
cmd_adopt() {
  local adopt="$CCRC_HERE/ccrc-adopt"
  [[ -f "$adopt" ]] \
    || _ccrc_die "adopt's script is missing: $adopt — is this a complete ccrc install?"
  exec "$BASH" "$adopt" "$@"
}

# ── cmd_passwd — THE ONE WRITER OF ~/.ccrc/auth.scrypt ────────────────────
# Sets, or rotates, the passphrase the server's session gate checks a login
# against. `install` deliberately does not do this and never will (see
# `_inst_env`'s seed-once doctrine and the landing block's own line): a
# passphrase is the operator's, typed by the operator, once.
#
# ── STDIN MUST BE A TTY, AND A PIPE IS REFUSED OUTRIGHT ───────────────────
# Not politeness — `install.sh` is documented as `curl … | bash`, where stdin
# IS THE SCRIPT. A `read` on that stdin does not fail and does not block: it
# silently consumes the installer's own next line and hashes it as the box's
# passphrase, leaving a box that is credentialed with a fragment of shell
# nobody can retype. The refusal is what makes that unreachable, and
# `ccrc-passwd.test.ts` pins it by piping one.
#
# ── THE PASSPHRASE NEVER TOUCHES argv, A FILE, OR THE SCREEN ──────────────
#   - `read -rs` — no echo, no history, backslashes literal (`-r`).
#   - it reaches the hasher through a pipe from `printf`, which is a bash
#     BUILTIN: no second process is forked, so nothing ever appears in
#     /proc/<pid>/cmdline, which is world-readable. A here-string (`<<<`) is
#     NOT used for the same class of reason — bash implements one with a temp
#     file on disk.
#   - it is `unset` the moment the hasher has had it, and nothing in this
#     function prints it, logs it, or puts it in a diagnostic.
#
# ── THE TRAP IS ABOUT THE OPERATOR'S TERMINAL, AND IT MUST ABORT (D-141) ──
# `read -s` turns echo off and restores it itself on a normal return — but not
# when a Ctrl-C arrives mid-prompt, which leaves the shell alive with a
# terminal that no longer shows what is typed into it. `stty echo` on
# EXIT/INT/TERM is half the fix; its failure is discarded because a terminal
# that cannot be stty'd is not a reason to change this command's exit code.
#
# THE OTHER HALF IS `exit 130`, AND LEAVING IT OUT IS A DISCLOSURE BUG, not a
# tidiness one. Bash RESTARTS an interrupted `read` after running a trapped
# handler, so a handler that only re-enables echo hands control straight back
# to the still-running prompt — with echo now ON. MEASURED against the shipped
# verb, exactly as the review reported it:
#
#   New passphrase (at least 12 characters): VISIBLE-PASSPHRASE
#   Repeat it:                                    <- typed, echoed, ACCEPTED
#
# The passphrase and its confirmation were echoed into the terminal and the
# scrollback, and the run then exited 0 having written the file — under a
# banner that says "Ctrl-C aborts". WITHOUT any trap the same Ctrl-C kills the
# script cleanly, so the trap that was added to protect the terminal is the
# thing that broke the abort. Two traps, therefore: EXIT restores echo, and
# INT/TERM restore echo and LEAVE. 130 is 128+SIGINT, which is what the shell
# reports for an interrupted foreground command anyway — deliberately outside
# this file's 0/1/2 table, because "the operator interrupted it" is not a
# refusal and not a usage error.
#
# ── WHICH FILE IT WRITES (`_box_auth_path`) ──────────────────────────────
# The DEFAULT is `$BOX_AUTH_FILE`, but `~/.ccrc/ccrc.env` may redirect the
# server with `CCRC_AUTH_SECRET_PATH`, and this verb has to follow it. Writing
# the default on a box that redirected the server is not a cosmetic mismatch:
# the server keeps reading the override, `passwd` reports success, and doctor
# then PASSes on the file nobody reads — so an operator rotating after a
# compromise gets a green transcript and a live secret that never changed.
# `_box_auth_path` is the one resolver both this verb and doctor's `auth` check
# go through.
#
# ── WHAT IT INVALIDATES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT (D-138) ────────────────────
# A rotation bumps the file's `generation`, and `SessionStore.verify` answers
# `'expired'` for every session stamped with an older one — so every logged-in
# browser is logged out, with no restart. ENROLLED PASSKEYS ARE UNAFFECTED, by
# design (the Task 8 operator ruling: `StoredCredential` carries no generation
# stamp). An operator who believes `passwd` is a panic button for a lost phone
# has the failure mode exactly backwards, so the success block says so: revoke
# the passkey in the PWA FIRST, then rotate.
cmd_passwd() {
  # `cmd_wrappers`/`cmd_install`'s loop. `-h`/`--help` prints usage at exit 0
  # rather than being `cmd_version`'s usage error: this is a verb that CHANGES
  # THE BOX and locks people out of a console, so "what will this do" must be
  # answerable without running it.
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      *) _ccrc_usage_die "$1" ;;
    esac
  done

  [ -t 0 ] \
    || _ccrc_die "stdin is not a terminal, and this verb will not read a passphrase from a pipe or a file. A piped passphrase cannot be confirmed, cannot be typed without echo, and — the case this refusal exists for — under \`curl … | bash\` stdin is the INSTALLER SCRIPT, so a read here would hash a line of shell as your passphrase. Run 'ccrc passwd' from a terminal. Nothing was written."

  # BY NAME, BEFORE ANY PROMPT — `cmd_install:1626-1641`'s idiom and its exact
  # reason: without it an absent interpreter arrives as the hasher "failing",
  # after the operator has typed a passphrase twice, and the message is about
  # the wrong thing entirely.
  command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _ccrc_die "node is required by 'ccrc passwd' — it runs ${CCRC_HERE%/*}/deploy/gen-auth-hash.mjs to hash the passphrase — but is not on PATH. Install Node (this repo's floor is in server/package.json's \"engines\") or put it on PATH, then re-run. Nothing on this box was written or changed."

  local helper="$CCRC_HERE/../deploy/gen-auth-hash.mjs"
  [ -f "$helper" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "the passphrase hasher is missing: $helper — is this a complete ccrc install? Nothing was written."

  # THE THIRD BY-NAME PROBE, AND THE SAME RULE AS THE TWO ABOVE IT. The hasher
  # imports the SERVER's own compiled reader, so a box with no server build
  # cannot hash — and until this ran here, that refusal arrived AFTER the
  # operator had typed a passphrase twice, which is precisely the sequencing
  # the `node` probe eight lines up exists to prevent (and says so in its own
  # comment). It is the fleet host's case: `~/ccrc` there has no `server/`.
  #
  # The helper probes ITSELF (`--probe` loads the module and exits), rather
  # than this file testing for `../server/dist/server/src/auth/secret.js`:
  # that path is the hasher's own business and a second spelling of it here is
  # how the two would come to disagree about which build they need. The
  # helper's own sentence is on stderr; this adds one line rather than
  # re-wording it.
  node "$helper" --probe \
    || _ccrc_die "the hasher cannot load the server's own secret reader — read the line above. The passphrase file is only meaningful on the box that runs the server. Nothing was written."

  local target apath_rc
  target="$(_box_auth_path)"; apath_rc=$?
  case "$apath_rc" in
    0) ;;
    2) printf '%s\n' "$PROG: note: $BOX_ENV_FILE redirects this box's secret with CCRC_AUTH_SECRET_PATH, so the passphrase goes to $target" >&2 ;;
    *) _ccrc_die "$BOX_ENV_FILE sets CCRC_AUTH_SECRET_PATH to a RELATIVE path ($target), and nothing can say which file that is: the server resolves it against the working directory systemd gives ccrc.service, and this command would resolve it against yours. Make it absolute (or remove it, and the box uses $BOX_AUTH_FILE), then re-run. Nothing was written." ;;
  esac

  # 12, and the number is a floor rather than a policy. Online guessing is
  # already bounded by the login brake (`server/src/auth/ratelimit.ts`'s
  # MAX_FAILURES = 8 failures per window, each holding a scrypt slot), so what
  # a length floor buys is resistance to an OFFLINE attack on the hash if the
  # file ever leaves the box, and a stop on the one-character passphrase
  # somebody types to "test it quickly" and then keeps.
  local min=12

  # TWO TRAPS, NOT ONE — see the header. A single EXIT/INT/TERM handler that
  # only runs `stty echo` turns Ctrl-C into "echo back on, prompt still
  # running", i.e. the passphrase typed in cleartext. The INT/TERM handler
  # must LEAVE.
  trap 'stty echo 2>/dev/null' EXIT
  trap 'stty echo 2>/dev/null; printf "\n" >&2; exit 130' INT TERM

  local p1="" p2=""
  printf '%s\n' "$PROG: setting the PWA passphrase in $target (nothing is echoed; Ctrl-C aborts)" >&2
  # `IFS=` ON BOTH READS (D-142), exactly as every other `read` in this file has it
  # (:473, :932, :2008) — and here it is the difference between a working box
  # and a silent lockout. Without it bash splits on $IFS and hands back the
  # value with leading and trailing whitespace STRIPPED: measured,
  # `"  spaced pass  "` arrives as `"spaced pass"`. Both entries are trimmed
  # identically, so they still match, the file that gets written is perfectly
  # valid, and doctor PASSes — while the browser sends the raw string the
  # operator actually typed, which no longer verifies. A lockout with no red
  # anywhere is the worst shape a defect in this verb can have.
  if ! IFS= read -rsp "New passphrase (at least $min characters): " p1; then
    printf '\n' >&2
    _ccrc_die "no passphrase was entered — nothing was written"
  fi
  printf '\n' >&2
  if ! IFS= read -rsp "Repeat it: " p2; then
    printf '\n' >&2
    unset p1 p2
    _ccrc_die "no passphrase was entered — nothing was written"
  fi
  printf '\n' >&2

  # LENGTH BEFORE MATCH would tell an operator who mistyped the second entry
  # that their FIRST one was too short, so the comparison comes first — and
  # both refusals name only the property that failed, never any part of what
  # was typed.
  if [ "$p1" != "$p2" ]; then
    unset p1 p2
    _ccrc_die "the two entries do not match — nothing was written"
  fi
  if [ "${#p1}" -lt "$min" ]; then
    unset p1 p2
    _ccrc_die "the passphrase is shorter than $min characters — nothing was written"
  fi

  # The hasher writes the file (atomically, 0600), bumps the generation, and —
  # the part that matters — reads its own output back through the server's own
  # parser AND verifies this passphrase against it before installing it. Its
  # stdout is the RESULT and goes straight to the terminal; a refusal is its
  # own sentence on stderr and this adds one line rather than re-wording it
  # (`_inst_accounts_sh`'s rule).
  local rc=0
  printf '%s' "$p1" | node "$helper" "$target" || rc=$?
  unset p1 p2
  [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] \
    || _ccrc_die "the passphrase was NOT set — read the line above; $target is exactly as it was"

  echo "$PROG: the gate is still OFF until CCRC_AUTH=on is set in $BOX_ENV_FILE and ccrc.service is restarted — a passphrase on its own changes nothing"
  echo "$PROG: set CCRC_RP_ID and CCRC_ORIGIN in the SAME edit that arms CCRC_AUTH — with the flag on and those two wrong, every /ws/* upgrade and every non-exempt write is refused from the real origin, and the server prints no warning about it at boot"
  echo "$PROG: every logged-in session is now expired. ENROLLED PASSKEYS ARE NOT — they keep working across a rotation, by design. For a lost device: revoke its passkey in the PWA first, THEN run this"
}

# ── cmd_expose — a public name and a real certificate, sudo never run ─────
# Stage 3b (spec D1–D3). The verb writes TWO ccrc-owned files and executes
# nothing privileged:
#   - `$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE` (0600) — CCRC_ORIGIN/CCRC_RP_ID (+ the DuckDNS
#     trio on the duckdns arm), the server unit's SECOND EnvironmentFile
#     (Task 1's seam; later file wins, so these keys override a hand-set
#     placeholder without touching the seed-once ccrc.env).
#   - `$CCRC_CADDYFILE` — the complete Caddyfile, regenerated every run: the
#     host, `reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:<CCRC_PORT>`, and nothing else. Stock
#     Caddy's automatic HTTPS does the certificate (spec D1: HTTP-01/
#     TLS-ALPN-01, which is why forwarding ports 80/443 to this box is a
#     PREREQUISITE the landing block states loudly).
# Everything root-side — install caddy, place the Caddyfile at
# /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, enable the service — is PRINTED as an exact three-step
# ceremony and never run: `_inst_linger`'s degraded-step doctrine, verb-sized.
# Doctor (Task 4) carries the not-yet-done ceremony as FAIL-with-remedy.
#
# ── THE ip ARM (stage 5, S10) — HTTPS WITH NOBODY ELSE INVOLVED ───────────
# No DNS service, no ACME, no domain: the box's first GLOBAL IPv4 (measured,
# never typed — `hostname -I` walked through `_dr_ip4_global`, the name
# check's own classifier) becomes the origin, and the Caddyfile says
# `tls internal` so caddy's own CA signs the certificate. The landing block
# gains a TRUST ceremony (each device installs the CA root once) and the
# passkey truth: an rpId must be a DOMAIN (webauthn.ts refuses an address
# literal), so no CCRC_RP_ID is written and the gate runs passphrase-only —
# enrolment simply stays hidden, the `enrolledRpIds` wire already handles it.
#
# ── STDIN MUST BE A TTY on the prompting arms — `cmd_passwd`'s refusal ────
# Same hazard, same sentence shape: under `curl … | bash` stdin IS THE SCRIPT,
# and a `read` here would take a line of shell as this box's DuckDNS token.
# The token itself follows `cmd_passwd`'s rules end to end: `read -rs` (never
# echoed), never argv (the file is written by a bash builtin redirect — no
# /proc/<pid>/cmdline exposure), never printed by any path including `status`,
# which reports SET/NOT SET (`_check_config`'s standing rule).
#
# ── THE SHADOW REFUSAL (spec D3) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# An operator who hand-set CCRC_ORIGIN/CCRC_RP_ID in ccrc.env (the 3a runbook
# path) must not end up with the same key in two files that disagree: the unit
# reads exposure.env SECOND, so this verb's copy would WIN and the operator's
# line would silently stop mattering — the overloaded-seam shape this codebase
# bans. The verb refuses, names both files and the winner, and writes nothing.
#
# ── CCRC_RP_ID IS STATED, NEVER DERIVED, on the byo arm ───────────────────
# `config.ts`'s own warning, quoted at the prompt: how many labels to strip
# off a hostname takes the Public Suffix List (`duckdns.org` and `ts.net` are
# both public suffixes), a dependency this repo does not take. The duckdns arm
# derives it safely because the shape is FIXED: one label under duckdns.org.
cmd_expose() {
  local sub="${1:-}"
  [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift
  case "$sub" in
    -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
    duckdns|byo|ip) ;;
    status)
      [ $# -eq 0 ] || _ccrc_usage_die "$1"
      _exp_status
      return 0
      ;;
    '')
      echo "$PROG: expose: missing subcommand (duckdns|byo|ip|status)" >&2
      usage >&2
      exit 2
      ;;
    *) _ccrc_usage_die "$sub" ;;
  esac
  [ $# -eq 0 ] || _ccrc_usage_die "$1"

  # The shadow refusal comes BEFORE the tty gate: a box in the refused state
  # is refused identically from a terminal and from a pipe, and nobody types
  # two answers into a run that was never going to write them.
  local shadow_keys="" k
  if [ -f "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ] && [ -r "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    for k in CCRC_ORIGIN CCRC_RP_ID CCRC_AUTH; do
      # `-n`: a bare `KEY=` line is an EMPTY value, which `config.ts` reads
      # with `||` — i.e. unset. Refusing over a line that decides nothing
      # would block the verb on exactly the boxes it exists to fix.
      [ -n "$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" "$k")" ] && shadow_keys="$shadow_keys $k"
    done
  fi
  # D-162: CCRC_AUTH joined the list when `_exp_env_write` started writing it.
  # A box put on the public internet must be armed, and arming has exactly one
  # owner for the same reason the origin does — the 3a runbook's hand-edit in
  # ccrc.env would be silently outvoted by this verb's copy, which is the
  # overloaded seam D3 bans, applied to the one key whose silent loss takes a
  # PUBLIC box dark (measured live 2026-08-22: a deploy shipped a ccrc.env
  # without it and the box ran unauthenticated for 3m17s).
  [ -z "$shadow_keys" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "refusing to write $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE — $BOX_ENV_FILE already sets$shadow_keys (the stage-3a hand-edit path). The server reads BOTH files and $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE wins for any key in both — so this verb's copy would silently override yours. Remove those keys from $BOX_ENV_FILE (that file is yours; this verb owns the other), then re-run. Nothing was written."

  [ -t 0 ] \
    || _ccrc_die "stdin is not a terminal, and this verb will not read a DuckDNS token or an origin from a pipe or a file. A piped token cannot be typed without echo, and — the case this refusal exists for — under \`curl … | bash\` stdin is the INSTALLER SCRIPT, so a read here would take a line of shell as this box's answers. Run 'ccrc expose $sub' from a terminal. Nothing was written."

  # `cmd_passwd`'s two traps, for `cmd_passwd`'s measured reason (D-141): the
  # token prompt is `read -s`, and a Ctrl-C mid-prompt without the INT handler
  # leaving would hand control back to a still-running prompt with echo ON.
  trap 'stty echo 2>/dev/null' EXIT
  trap 'stty echo 2>/dev/null; printf "\n" >&2; exit 130' INT TERM

  local origin="" rpid="" host="" subdomain="" token=""
  if [ "$sub" = duckdns ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$PROG: exposing this box through DuckDNS (the token is never echoed; Ctrl-C aborts)" >&2
    # `IFS=` on every read — `cmd_passwd`'s D-142 rule: bash strips leading and
    # trailing whitespace without it, and a silently-trimmed answer is a box
    # configured with a value nobody typed.
    if ! IFS= read -rp "DuckDNS subdomain (the <sub> of <sub>.duckdns.org): " subdomain; then
      printf '\n' >&2
      _ccrc_die "no subdomain was entered — nothing was written"
    fi
    case "$subdomain" in
      ''|*[!a-zA-Z0-9-]*)
        _ccrc_die "\"$subdomain\" is not a bare DuckDNS subdomain — one label: letters, digits and hyphens only (mybox, not mybox.duckdns.org). Nothing was written." ;;
    esac
    if ! IFS= read -rsp "DuckDNS token (from duckdns.org; not echoed): " token; then
      printf '\n' >&2
      unset token
      _ccrc_die "no token was entered — nothing was written"
    fi
    printf '\n' >&2
    [ -n "$token" ] || _ccrc_die "the token is empty — nothing was written"
    host="$subdomain.duckdns.org"
    origin="https://$host"
    rpid="$host"
  elif [ "$sub" = ip ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$PROG: exposing this box on its bare address — caddy's internal CA signs the certificate, and nobody else is involved (Ctrl-C aborts)" >&2
    # The address is MEASURED, never asked: the one true answer is already on
    # the box (`hostname -I`), and a typed address would be the only input of
    # this verb no doctor check could re-measure. First GLOBAL IPv4, through
    # the same classifier the name check compares DNS records with
    # (`_dr_ip4_global`): private/CGNAT space is what a NAT'd box carries, and
    # a certificate for an address browsers cannot reach is a config that lies
    # from birth — so that box is refused, and pointed at the arms that DO
    # work behind NAT. An absent or -I-less hostname folds into the same
    # refusal deliberately: the refusal PRINTS what was measured, so "answered
    # nothing" and "answered only private space" stay distinguishable to the
    # operator without a second arm nothing else would share.
    local hout="" hip=""
    hout="$(hostname -I 2>/dev/null)" || hout=""
    for hip in $hout; do
      if _dr_ip4_global "$hip"; then host="$hip"; break; fi
    done
    [ -n "$host" ] \
      || _ccrc_die "no global IPv4 to expose — hostname -I answered: ${hout:-nothing (is hostname installed?)}. Behind NAT or CGNAT the address browsers would reach is not on this box, so there is nothing true to certify; use a name instead: ccrc expose duckdns (zero-cost), or ccrc expose byo (your own domain). Nothing was written."
    origin="https://$host"
    rpid=""
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$PROG: exposing this box under your own name — the DNS record, and keeping it pointed here, are yours (Ctrl-C aborts)" >&2
    printf '%s\n' "$PROG: CCRC_RP_ID is the registrable domain a passkey is scoped to — it is configured and NEVER derived: how many labels to strip off a hostname takes the Public Suffix List (duckdns.org and ts.net are both public suffixes), so you state it (server/src/config.ts's own warning)" >&2
    if ! IFS= read -rp "Public origin (e.g. https://box.example.com): " origin; then
      printf '\n' >&2
      _ccrc_die "no origin was entered — nothing was written"
    fi
    case "$origin" in
      https://?*) ;;
      *) _ccrc_die "\"$origin\" is not an https:// origin — state the full scheme and host a browser will be at (e.g. https://box.example.com). Nothing was written." ;;
    esac
    host="${origin#https://}"
    case "$host" in
      */*|*' '*)
        _ccrc_die "\"$origin\" carries a path or a space — an origin is scheme and host only, no trailing slash. Nothing was written." ;;
      # `?`/`#`/`@` pass a browser's URL parser but land verbatim in the
      # Caddyfile site address, where Caddy refuses to load — surfacing the
      # typo at the caddy/cert doctor checks instead of at this prompt
      # (branch review, 2026-08-21).
      *'?'*|*'#'*|*'@'*)
        _ccrc_die "\"$origin\" carries '?', '#' or '@' — an origin is scheme and host only (a query, fragment or userinfo would be written into the Caddyfile as a site address Caddy refuses to load). Nothing was written." ;;
    esac
    if ! IFS= read -rp "Passkey rp id (usually the same: $host): " rpid; then
      printf '\n' >&2
      _ccrc_die "no rp id was entered — nothing was written"
    fi
    [ -n "$rpid" ] || _ccrc_die "the rp id is empty — nothing was written"
  fi

  # ── the caddy bind address (D-163) ────────────────────────────────────
  # Stock caddy binds :80/:443 on EVERY address. `tailscale serve` — a path
  # ccrc's own README documents — already holds :443 on the tailnet address,
  # so on a box running both, caddy dies at start with EADDRINUSE. The cure is
  # one Caddyfile directive, and until now it was a HAND EDIT to a file this
  # verb regenerates: measured live 2026-08-21, added by hand, and standing
  # one `ccrc expose` away from being silently deleted. Asked here so it is
  # DATA (exposure.env), which regeneration reproduces instead of destroying.
  #
  # Asked on both arms, and asked LAST: it is the only question whose answer
  # depends on what else this box runs, so the measurement below is worth
  # more than the question.
  local bind="" holders=""
  holders="$(_exp_443_holders)"
  if [ -n "$holders" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$PROG: something already listens on :443 here — $holders. Caddy binds every address by default and will fail to start against that; give it one address of its own (usually this box's PUBLIC ip)." >&2
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$PROG: nothing else appears to hold :443 on this box, so caddy can take every address (blank is the right answer). Name one only if you run something like 'tailscale serve' that binds :443 later." >&2
  fi
  if ! IFS= read -rp "Address for caddy to bind (blank = all interfaces): " bind; then
    printf '\n' >&2
    _ccrc_die "no answer was given — nothing was written"
  fi
  case "$bind" in
    '') ;;
    # The value lands VERBATIM as a Caddyfile directive argument. The same
    # class of refusal the origin gets, for the same reason: a character
    # caddy's parser treats structurally turns a config into one caddy will
    # not load, and the failure would surface two ceremony steps later.
    *[[:space:]]*|*'{'*|*'}'*|*'#'*)
      _ccrc_die "\"$bind\" carries whitespace, a brace or a '#' — a bind address is one host or ip, written verbatim into the Caddyfile as a directive caddy would refuse to load. Nothing was written." ;;
  esac

  _exp_env_write "$sub" "$origin" "$rpid" "$host" "$token" "$bind"
  unset token
  _exp_caddyfile "$host" "$bind" "$sub"
  # AFTER the env write, deliberately: the ddns service's EnvironmentFile has
  # no leading '-', so a timer enabled before exposure.env existed would fire
  # a unit systemd refuses to start.
  if [ "$sub" = duckdns ]; then
    _exp_ddns_units
  fi
  _exp_landing "$origin" "$sub"
}

# ── _exp_443_holders — who already owns :443, in the operator's words ─────
# Read-only, best-effort, and SILENT when it cannot tell: `ss` is not on every
# box, and a bind prompt that invented a holder would be worse than one that
# admits it looked. Prints a comma-joined list of listening addresses, or
# nothing at all — the caller phrases both cases.
_exp_443_holders() {
  command -v ss >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  # -H (no header) is not in every ss; the awk filter does not need it to be.
  ss -ltn 2>/dev/null | awk '
    NR > 1 && $4 ~ /:443$/ { a[$4] = 1 }
    END { n = 0; for (k in a) { printf "%s%s", (n++ ? ", " : ""), k } }
  '
}

# A GLOBAL IPv4 — the only interface address worth exposing, or comparing a
# public DNS record against. Private (RFC1918), loopback and link-local are
# what a NAT'd box carries, and a "mismatch" against those would fail every
# home box forever. IPv6 is deliberately not classified: the duckdns updater's
# empty `ip=` writes an A record. Declared HERE and not in ccrc-doctor-checks
# (its home until stage 5, Task 7) because it now has two callers on two
# sides of that seam — doctor's `name` check and `cmd_expose`'s ip arm — and
# the checks table already assumes ccrc is loaded first.
_dr_ip4_global() {
  case "$1" in
    ''|*[!0-9.]*) return 1 ;;
    10.*|127.*|169.254.*|192.168.*) return 1 ;;
    172.1[6-9].*|172.2[0-9].*|172.3[01].*) return 1 ;;
    # CGNAT 100.64/10 (RFC 6598) — tailscale's own address range, so on
    # this project's fleet topology a wildcard here is not hypothetical:
    # counting 100.x as global made _check_name WARN "propagation lag" forever
    # on every tailscale box behind NAT (branch review, 2026-08-21).
    100.6[4-9].*|100.[7-9][0-9].*|100.1[01][0-9].*|100.12[0-7].*) return 1 ;;
    *.*.*.*) return 0 ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
}

# ── _exp_ddns_units — the DuckDNS updater, installed and (best-effort) armed ─
# Spec D4. The duckdns arm ONLY — a BYO domain's DNS is the operator's. The
# two templates ship in the tree (`deploy/systemd/`) and land VERBATIM through
# `_inst_atomic`, out of the tree this ccrc runs from (`_inst_enable`'s
# `$CCRC_HERE/..` idiom) — verbatim is load-bearing: the service reads
# CCRC_DDNS_DOMAIN/CCRC_DDNS_TOKEN from $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE at run time
# through systemd's own ${...} expansion, so this function never touches the
# token and the 0644 unit never becomes a second copy of the 0600 secret
# (ccrc-expose.test.ts pins the installed bytes contain the literal
# placeholder and not the value).
#
# THE ENABLE IS DEGRADED, NOT FATAL — `_inst_linger`'s doctrine at verb scale:
# by this line both config files and both units have landed, and a box without
# a reachable session bus (a container, ssh without XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, this
# repo's own test fixture) must not turn a converged run into an abort over a
# thing one command fixes. The exact command is printed; doctor's `name` check
# (Task 4) re-measures the record and points back at the timer.
_exp_ddns_units() {
  # ── THE DARWIN ARM: one plist where systemd has a unit and a timer ───────
  # systemd splits "what to run" (.service) from "when" (.timer). launchd has
  # one job with a `StartInterval`, so the pair collapses into a single plist
  # — and `OnCalendar=*:0/5` becomes 300 seconds, the same five minutes.
  #
  # `Persistent=true` HAS NO COUNTERPART, and this is the one place the two
  # differ in kind: systemd runs a missed timer as soon as the box is back,
  # where launchd's StartInterval simply fires next interval. For a record
  # that refreshes every five minutes the miss costs one cycle, and saying so
  # is better than implying parity.
  #
  # THE TOKEN STAYS IN THE 0600 FILE. systemd expands ${CCRC_DDNS_TOKEN} from
  # its EnvironmentFile at run time, which is why the shipped 0644 unit never
  # carries the secret. launchd cannot read an env file at all — so the job is
  # a shell that sources it, exactly as the server's plist does, and the
  # secret still never lands in this 0644 file.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    local dir="$BOX_UNIT_DIR" lbl="app.ccrc.$CCRC_DDNS_UNIT" gen
    mkdir -p "$dir" || _ccrc_die "cannot create $dir"
    gen="$dir/$lbl.plist.tmp.$$.new"
    cat > "$gen" <<PL || { rm -f -- "$gen"; _ccrc_die "could not generate the $lbl job"; }
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>$lbl</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>set -a; . '$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE'; set +a; exec /usr/bin/curl -fsS --max-time 30 "https://www.duckdns.org/update?domains=\${CCRC_DDNS_DOMAIN}&amp;token=\${CCRC_DDNS_TOKEN}&amp;ip="</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>StartInterval</key><integer>300</integer>
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
PL
    if command -v plutil >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      plutil -lint "$gen" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
        || { rm -f -- "$gen"; _ccrc_die "the generated $lbl job is not a valid plist — refusing to install it. Nothing was placed."; }
    fi
    _inst_atomic "$gen" "$dir/$lbl.plist" 644
    rm -f -- "$gen"
    echo "expose: installed $lbl in \$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents (the token stays in $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE — the job reads it, never carries it; a missed refresh waits for the next 5-minute tick, where systemd's Persistent=true would catch up immediately)"
    # ── ARMED HERE, IN THE SAME ARM THAT INSTALLED IT ────────────────────
    # The first cut of this function wrote the plist and returned; a second
    # Darwin block forty lines down held the bootstrap — BELOW this arm's
    # `return 0`, so it was unreachable: on macOS the job file landed and was
    # never bootstrapped, the record never refreshed, and neither transcript
    # line below could ever print (found by PR #11's review; no test asserted
    # a bootstrap). ONE LABEL where Linux enables the .timer and lets it pull
    # its .service — the pair collapses into this single plist, per the
    # header above. bootout first for `_inst_enable_darwin`'s reason: it is
    # how launchd spells "load the file I just rewrote".
    _svc_launchctl bootout "$(_svc_domain)/$lbl" 2>/dev/null
    if _svc_launchctl enable "$(_svc_domain)/$lbl" 2>/dev/null \
       && _svc_launchctl bootstrap "$(_svc_domain)" "$dir/$lbl.plist" 2>/dev/null; then
      echo "expose: $lbl enabled — the DuckDNS record refreshes every 5 minutes"
    else
      echo "expose: DEGRADED — could not bootstrap $lbl. The job file is installed; when you can, run: launchctl bootstrap $(_svc_domain) $dir/$lbl.plist"
    fi
    return 0
  fi
  local tree="$CCRC_HERE/.." dir="$BOX_UNIT_DIR"
  mkdir -p "$dir" || _ccrc_die "cannot create $dir"
  _inst_atomic "$tree/deploy/systemd/$CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.service" "$dir/$CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.service" 644
  _inst_atomic "$tree/deploy/systemd/$CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.timer" "$dir/$CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.timer" 644
  echo "expose: installed $CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.service + $CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.timer in \$HOME/.config/systemd/user (the token stays in $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE — the units read it, never carry it)"
  # The bus-location defaults `_inst_enable` sets, for its reason: an install
  # is most often run over non-interactive ssh, where systemctl --user cannot
  # find the bus without them. daemon-reload BEFORE enable, also its reason.
  : "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:=/run/user/$UID}"; export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
  : "${DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS:=unix:path=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}/bus}"; export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
  if systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user enable --now "$CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.timer"; then
    echo "expose: $CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.timer enabled — the DuckDNS record refreshes every 5 minutes"
  else
    echo "expose: DEGRADED — could not enable $CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.timer (no session bus?). The units are installed; when you can, run: systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user enable --now $CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.timer"
  fi
}

# ── _exp_env_write — the ONE writer of $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE ────────────────
# tmp + `mv -f` in the same directory (`_inst_atomic`'s discipline): the
# server unit may source this file at any restart, and a half-written
# EnvironmentFile is a box with half an origin. `umask 077` in the writing
# subshell so the temp file is 0600 FROM ITS FIRST BYTE — the token must never
# sit world-readable even for the window between create and chmod.
_exp_env_write() {   # <arm> <origin> <rpid> <host> <token> <bind>
  local arm="$1" origin="$2" rpid="$3" host="$4" token="$5" bind="${6:-}" dest="$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" tmp
  mkdir -p "$HOME/.ccrc" || _ccrc_die "cannot create \$HOME/.ccrc"
  tmp="$dest.tmp.$$"
  if ! (
    umask 077
    {
      printf '%s\n' "# ccrc exposure config — regenerated by 'ccrc expose'; delete it to go dark."
      printf '%s\n' "CCRC_ORIGIN=$origin"
      # OMITTED, never written empty, on the ip arm: an rpId must be a DOMAIN
      # (webauthn.ts refuses an address literal), and config.ts reads the key
      # with `||`, so a bare `CCRC_RP_ID=` line would be a key that decides
      # nothing — the empty distinction the bind key's own comment bans.
      [ -n "$rpid" ] && printf '%s\n' "CCRC_RP_ID=$rpid"
      # D-162. Written by this verb because it is a FACT ABOUT BEING EXPOSED,
      # not a preference: the box now answers from the public internet, and
      # the alternative to arming is publishing the fleet. It lives here
      # rather than in ccrc.env because this file is the one deploy does not
      # ship — the measured failure (2026-08-22) was a deploy overwriting
      # ccrc.env from a workstation whose copy predated arming, which dropped
      # the key and took a public box dark with nothing to notice.
      printf '%s\n' "CCRC_AUTH=on"
      # The ip arm's two facts (stage 5, S10), recorded so doctor and a re-run
      # judge the same box the verb did: the MODE routes the exposure/cert/
      # name checks down their ip arms, and the ADDR is what a changed box IP
      # is diffed against (the remedy is a re-run of this verb).
      if [ "$arm" = ip ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "CCRC_EXPOSE_MODE=ip"
        printf '%s\n' "CCRC_EXPOSE_ADDR=$host"
      fi
      # Absent = every address, which is stock caddy's own default: writing a
      # bare `CCRC_CADDY_BIND=` would be a key that decides nothing, and
      # `_box_env_value`'s readers already treat empty as unset.
      [ -n "$bind" ] && printf '%s\n' "CCRC_CADDY_BIND=$bind"
      if [ "$arm" = duckdns ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "CCRC_DDNS_PROVIDER=duckdns"
        printf '%s\n' "CCRC_DDNS_DOMAIN=$host"
        printf '%s\n' "CCRC_DDNS_TOKEN=$token"
      fi
    } > "$tmp"
  ) || ! chmod 600 "$tmp" || ! mv -f "$tmp" "$dest"; then
    rm -f "$tmp"
    _ccrc_die "writing $dest failed — nothing was installed"
  fi
  echo "expose: wrote $dest (0600) — CCRC_ORIGIN=$origin, CCRC_AUTH=on${bind:+, CCRC_CADDY_BIND=$bind}"
  # The one way arming can bite: `CCRC_AUTH=on` with no passphrase file is a
  # box that refuses EVERYTHING (auth/secret.ts fails shut, by design). Said
  # as `_inst_linger`'s degraded step — loud, exact, and not a reason to
  # abort a run that has already written the file correctly.
  if [ ! -e "$BOX_AUTH_FILE" ]; then
    echo "expose: DEGRADED — this box now arms its gate but has no passphrase, so it will refuse every request including yours. Set one before you restart the server: ccrc passwd"
  fi
}

# The complete Caddyfile (spec D1): the host, a loopback reverse_proxy, and
# NOTHING else — no TLS directives, no plugin syntax. Stock Caddy's automatic
# HTTPS reads the bare host line and does the certificate. The port comes from
# ccrc.env through `_box_env_value` (D-85's reader), defaulting to 7788 only
# when the file names no CCRC_PORT — the same pair of defaults
# `cmd_install`'s landing block uses, for the same reason: hardcoding the
# port would be right on a fresh box and a lie on every edited one.
_exp_caddyfile() {   # <host> [bind] [arm]
  local host="$1" bind="${2:-}" arm="${3:-}" port="" dest="$CCRC_CADDYFILE" tmp site
  if [ -f "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ] && [ -r "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    port="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_PORT)"
  fi
  [ -n "$port" ] || port=7788
  # The ip arm (stage 5, S10) states the scheme and adds `tls internal`, so
  # the generated file READS as the contract it is: caddy's own CA signs the
  # certificate, no ACME, nothing leaves the box. (Caddy would route a bare
  # IP site address to its internal CA anyway — saying it is the point.)
  site="$host"
  [ "$arm" = ip ] && site="https://$host"
  tmp="$dest.tmp.$$"
  if ! {
    {
      printf '%s\n' "$site {"
      [ -n "$bind" ] && printf '%s\n' "    bind $bind"
      [ "$arm" = ip ] && printf '%s\n' "    tls internal"
      printf '%s\n' "    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:$port"
      printf '%s\n' "}"
    } > "$tmp" && chmod 644 "$tmp"
  }; then
    rm -f "$tmp"
    _ccrc_die "writing $dest failed"
  fi
  # ── D-164: REGENERATE, but never in silence ─────────────────────────────
  # This file is ccrc-owned and rewritten every run, which is the contract —
  # but "regenerated" had been indistinguishable from "your edits are gone".
  # Measured live 2026-08-21: an operator hand-added `bind` and a `log` block
  # to make caddy start at all, and the next run of this verb would have
  # deleted both with a line that said only "wrote". The bind is now data
  # (asked for, stored, reproduced); everything else an operator adds is
  # still theirs to re-apply, so it is PRESERVED and NAMED instead of lost.
  # The backup lands under deploy's own ~/ccrc-backups/<ts>/ layout so
  # `prune_backups`' glob can reclaim it — a backup outside that shape is one
  # nothing ever collects (BOX_BACKUP_ROOT's own note).
  if [ -f "$dest" ] && ! cmp -s "$tmp" "$dest"; then
    local kts kdir
    kts="$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)"
    kdir="$BOX_BACKUP_ROOT/$kts"
    if mkdir -p "$kdir" && cp -p "$dest" "$kdir/Caddyfile"; then
      echo "expose: the Caddyfile already here differed from the one this run generates — the previous file is kept at $kdir/Caddyfile (re-apply anything of yours it carried; only the bind address survives a regeneration on its own)"
    else
      rm -f "$tmp"
      _ccrc_die "$dest differs from what this run would write and the backup at $kdir failed — refusing to overwrite an edited Caddyfile with nothing kept. Nothing was written."
    fi
  fi
  mv -f "$tmp" "$dest" || { rm -f "$tmp"; _ccrc_die "writing $dest failed"; }
  echo "expose: wrote $dest — caddy will serve https://$host${bind:+ on $bind} and proxy to 127.0.0.1:$port"
}

# ── _exp_landing — what this run did NOT do, said in exact commands ───────
# The `_inst_linger` shape at verb scale: every privileged step is printed,
# never run, and `sudo` appears in this transcript ONLY inside the printed
# remedy lines — the numbered steps, plus the ip arm's `caddy trust` line
# (ccrc-expose.test.ts greps for exactly that, per arm). The port-forward
# prerequisite is stated here because it is the one thing neither this verb
# nor doctor can detect from inside the box (spec D1's consequence, "stated
# loudly instead of papered over").
_exp_landing() {   # <origin> [arm]
  local origin="$1" arm="${2:-}"
  echo "expose: this run started NO system service — caddy binds ports 80/443 as root, and ccrc never escalates (the commands below are printed, not run). Three steps are yours:"
  echo "expose:   1. install caddy from your distro (Debian/Ubuntu: apt install caddy) — the stock binary; no plugins needed"
  # ── D-165: a COPY, and never the symlink this line used to lead with ────
  # The symlink cannot work, and measured why (2026-08-22): caddy runs as its
  # own user (uid 996 on Debian), and reaching $CCRC_CADDYFILE means
  # traversing $HOME and $HOME/.ccrc. This box's own numbers were 750 and
  # 700 — and the 700 is CCRC'S OWN DOING, so the symlink is not merely
  # unlucky here, it is unreachable on every stock ccrc box. Caddy then fails
  # to load with a permission error two steps away from the instruction that
  # caused it. `install -m 0644` in one command: mode stated, not inherited
  # from whatever umask the operator's root shell happens to carry.
  echo "expose:   2. sudo install -m 0644 $CCRC_CADDYFILE $CCRC_CADDY_SYSTEM_FILE   (a COPY — do NOT symlink it: caddy runs as another user and cannot read inside your home. Re-run this line after every 'ccrc expose'; 'ccrc doctor' measures whether you did)"
  echo "expose:   3. sudo systemctl enable --now caddy"
  if [ "$arm" = ip ]; then
    # ── the ip arm's landing (stage 5, S10) ─────────────────────────────
    # The ACME port-forward sentence is the OTHER arms' prerequisite, and
    # ACME is exactly what this arm does not do — its prerequisite is bare
    # reachability. What replaces it is the TRUST ceremony: the certificate
    # chain ends at caddy's own root, so each device must install that root
    # once, and the ceremony names the file, this box's own command, and
    # the two phone platforms at two lines each. The passkey sentence is
    # said verbatim (the README and the runbook quote it): passkeys need a
    # domain RP ID, so there is no re-enrol step to print here — there is
    # nothing to enrol.
    echo "expose: prerequisite: nothing outside this box — no DNS record and no ACME issuance (the certificate is caddy's own internal CA); just make sure :443 is reachable from your devices (open it in this box's firewall if one runs)"
    echo "expose: the certificate is signed by caddy's INTERNAL CA — self-signed via a local root, nothing leaves the box — so each device must trust that root ONCE:"
    echo "expose:   on this box: sudo caddy trust   (registers the root with this box's own trust store)"
    echo "expose:   for phones, the root certificate file is $CCRC_CADDY_ROOT_CA — get that FILE onto the device"
    echo "expose:   iOS: send root.crt to the phone (AirDrop, or mail it to yourself), install the downloaded profile: Settings > General > VPN & Device Management,"
    echo "expose:        then trust it: Settings > General > About > Certificate Trust Settings — enable full trust for the root"
    echo "expose:   Android: copy root.crt to the phone, then Settings > Security > Encryption & credentials > Install a certificate > CA certificate,"
    echo "expose:        and pick the copied file (vendors move this menu — searching Settings for 'CA certificate' finds it)"
    echo "expose: passkeys need a domain — on a bare IP the gate runs on passphrase login only (enrolment simply never appears; a changed box IP is a re-run of this verb)"
    echo "expose: then restart the server so it reads the new origin: $(_svc_restart_hint ccrc.service)"
  else
    echo "expose: prerequisite: your router must forward ports 80 and 443 to this box — stock caddy's certificate issuance (ACME HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN-01) fails loudly without it, and nothing inside the box can check it for you"
    echo "expose: then restart the server so it reads the new origin: $(_svc_restart_hint ccrc.service) — and RE-ENROL every passkey (passkeys are origin-bound; the old ones will be refused by name)"
  fi
  echo "expose: once all of the above are done, $origin is this box — ccrc doctor measures each piece"
}

# ── _exp_status — read-only, and the token is a CLASS, never a value ──────
# `_check_config`'s standing rule (SET/NOT SET), applied to the one secret
# this file's own verb writes. Reads through `_box_env_value` — the same
# reader the shadow refusal and the landing block use — so `status` and the
# server cannot come to parse the same line differently.
_exp_status() {
  if [ ! -f "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" ]; then
    echo "expose: not configured — this box has no exposure file (run: ccrc expose duckdns, or ccrc expose byo)"
    return 0
  fi
  local origin rpid token bind expmode
  origin="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_ORIGIN)"
  rpid="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_RP_ID)"
  token="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_DDNS_TOKEN)"
  bind="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_CADDY_BIND)"
  expmode="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_EXPOSE_MODE)"
  # On mode ip there IS no rp id, by design — `(rp id: (missing))` would be a
  # fault costume on a box configured exactly as built (stage 5, S10).
  if [ "$expmode" = ip ]; then
    echo "expose: configured — CCRC_ORIGIN=${origin:-(missing)} (mode ip: no rp id — passkeys need a domain, so login is passphrase-only)"
  else
    echo "expose: configured — CCRC_ORIGIN=${origin:-(missing)} (rp id: ${rpid:-(missing)})"
  fi
  # Absent is a real answer here, not a gap: no bind means caddy takes every
  # address, which is its own default and the right answer on most boxes.
  echo "expose: caddy binds ${bind:-every address (no CCRC_CADDY_BIND)}"
  if [ -n "$token" ]; then
    echo "expose: duckdns token: SET (never printed; it lives in $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE, 0600)"
  else
    echo "expose: duckdns token: NOT SET (a byo or ip box, or an incomplete duckdns run)"
  fi
  return 0
}

# ── cmd_account — the account-connection verb (spec §5) ───────────────────
# The one verb in this CLI whose CALLER is a program. `expose` and `passwd`
# prompt a human on a terminal; `account` is driven from a phone, through the
# server, through the agent, and every answer it gives is parsed. That single
# difference is what shapes everything below:
#
#  - EXACTLY ONE JSON OBJECT ON STDOUT, on every reachable path including every
#    refusal. `_ccrc_die` prints NOTHING on stdout (:1198), so a refusal under it
#    hands the caller an empty body and a bare exit code — "the id you gave is
#    taken" and "node is not installed" arriving identically, which is the
#    overloaded seam this repository forbids for every refusal this file CAN
#    phrase as JSON. TWO exceptions, not one: `-h`, which prints usage exactly
#    as every other verb does (the agent builds flags from typed fields and
#    never builds that one), and node-missing (below) — the one refusal that
#    genuinely cannot be JSON, because with no node on the box there is nothing
#    left to run the file that would write it. jq-missing is NOT a third
#    exception: jq is probed second, with node already proven present, so that
#    refusal goes through `_acct_refuse` like any other box-said-no.
#
#    THE RULE BINDS WHAT THIS VERB CALLS, NOT ONLY WHAT IT PRINTS.
#    `_inst_accounts_sh` (:5569, :5575) and `cmd_wrappers` (:2904) both write
#    their transcript to STDOUT, which is right for `install` and wrong here, so
#    `_acct_converge` (Task 24) redirects them and contains their `_ccrc_die`.
#
#  - THE ENVELOPE IS `ccd/ccrc-api:122-126`'s, WRITTEN BY NODE. That file's
#    `refuse()` is a bash `printf` of two `%s`, and it is safe THERE because both
#    values are literals it controls. Here `detail` carries the operator's own
#    bytes — an id, a suffix, a URL — and a `"` or a `\` in one of them turns a
#    hand-rolled envelope into a body nobody can parse. So bash owns argv,
#    refusal classes, tty rules and side effects; `deploy/account-op.mjs` owns
#    every byte of stdout. That is the split `deploy/gen-accounts.mjs:4-11` and
#    `_inst_accounts_sh` (:5548-5576) already draw, applied to an answer instead
#    of a generated file.
#
#  - ONE EXIT-CODE TABLE, TWO DECIDERS. Whoever decides owns the code, on this
#    file's own table (:24-33): 2 when the request was not legal on its face —
#    decidable from argv alone, which is `cmd_install`'s "the operator typed the
#    right flag and the wrong value" class (:5297-5301, refused at :5316) —
#    and 1 when the request was legal and the BOX said no (an id already in the
#    roster, a suffix another account holds). Nothing translates at the seam,
#    which is exactly `cmd_adopt`'s argument for `exec "$BASH"` (:2929-2930).
#
#  - THE SECRET NEVER LEAVES BASH. `--credential -` is the only spelling that
#    reads one (Task 22), it arrives on stdin through a builtin, and it is
#    written by this file in `_exp_env_write`'s umask-077 subshell (:3492-3544).
#    `deploy/account-op.mjs` is handed the roster PATH and never the value, which
#    is why that CLI can be run by hand and logged without a containment
#    argument.

_acct_node() {   # <op> [--<key> <value>]… — the ONE writer of this verb's stdout
  # `$CCRC_HERE/../deploy/…`, `cmd_wrappers`' idiom (:2318-2323): true in a
  # checkout and at ~/ccrc/deploy on a deployed box, because both deploy lanes
  # rsync `deploy/` into `~/ccrc/`. NOT `bash` — this is node.
  #
  # NOTHING IS RE-WORDED HERE: stdout is the caller's answer and stderr is the
  # caller's remedy, and re-writing either would replace a fix with a shrug.
  # `_acct_answer` below does CAPTURE stdout — that is a different claim, and
  # its own header argues it: it re-emits the body byte for byte and never
  # touches stderr, so the remedy still reaches the operator verbatim; what it
  # buys is the one thing this function cannot tell its caller, namely whether
  # an answer was written at all.
  node "$CCRC_HERE/../deploy/account-op.mjs" "$@"
}

_acct_refuse() {   # <class> <code> <detail> — prints the envelope, exits <class>
  # THE CLASS IS THIS FILE'S, THE ENVELOPE IS NODE'S. The `refuse` op exits 0
  # ("the envelope printed") precisely so that one refusal never has two owners.
  #
  # A DOCUMENTED SEAM HAZARD, LEFT AS A TRAP RATHER THAN CLOSED (review round 1,
  # M7). `_acct_node refuse --code "$2" --detail "$3"` is treated below as
  # binary: exit 0 means the envelope printed, ANY non-zero means it did not.
  # That second half is not quite true. `deploy/account-op.mjs`'s `refuse` op
  # can ALSO exit non-zero (2, bad-argv) while having already written a JSON
  # envelope to stdout — for the WRONG error, not for "$2"/"$3" — if the call
  # to it is itself malformed (an unknown --key, a missing value, a value
  # starting with "--", M6). If that ever happens here, this line's `||`
  # fires `_ccrc_die` on top of an envelope already sitting in the pipe: the
  # caller sees a stray `{"ok":false,"error":"bad-argv",…}` on stdout AND a
  # "could not print a refusal envelope" sentence on stderr, at exit 1 — two
  # disagreeing signals instead of one clean refusal.
  #
  # UNREACHABLE TODAY: every call site below passes fixed argv shape
  # (`--code "$2" --detail "$3"`, two literal keys, two positionally-consumed
  # values), and a bash "$2"/"$3" expansion is always exactly one word — even
  # empty — so it can never be interpreted as a THIRD `--key` and can never
  # make this call's own argv malformed. Deliberately left as a documented
  # trap rather than rewritten here: closing it would mean either validating
  # $2/$3 twice (once here, once inside account-op.mjs) or teaching this
  # function to distinguish "account-op.mjs could not run at all" (no
  # envelope) from "account-op.mjs ran and refused ITS OWN call" (an
  # envelope, just the wrong one) — a real seam change, not a comment fix,
  # and one this review round did not TDD. Re-examine this comment the day a
  # future task (Task 21+) makes $2 or $3 something other than a fixed
  # literal.
  #
  # RE-EXAMINED AT TASK 21. THE CONCLUSION HOLDS; THE FIRST WORDING OF THIS
  # PARAGRAPH DID NOT (corrected at review round 1). Task 21 added NINE call
  # sites, not four: `_acct_answer` x1, `_acct_shape` x3, `_acct_candidates`
  # x3, and `cmd_account` x2 — the two `unknown-argument` refusals at the
  # `roster` and `candidates` arms (:5213, :5218 — this pair read :4040, :4045
  # until review round 1, which is the citation Task 22's own insertion broke;
  # :4040 had by then landed inside the comment block that insertion added).
  # Those last two are the ones
  # the first wording both under-counted and mis-described: it said "nothing yet
  # passes an operator's own bytes here", and they pass `"$1"`, which is exactly
  # the operator's own argument after `shift`. So this IS the day the paragraph
  # above asked to be re-examined on, and it must not be read as saying that day
  # has not come.
  #
  # WHY IT STILL HOLDS — AND THE REASON IS POSITIONAL, NOT STRUCTURAL, WHICH IS
  # THE HALF A FUTURE TASK MUST CARRY AWAY. Two facts, both about `$3`:
  #   - it is a DOUBLE-QUOTED expansion, so whatever the operator typed —
  #     spaces, a `"`, a `\`, an embedded newline — arrives as exactly ONE argv
  #     word and can never be read as a third `--key`;
  #   - every sentence puts those bytes MID-SENTENCE, after prose, so `$3` can
  #     never BEGIN with `--` and can never be swallowed as a key by a
  #     value-taking flag (M6's shape).
  # Measured at review round 1 with `--code`, `--detail`, `x" --code y` and an
  # embedded newline: one argv word each, never leading with `--`, and the
  # newline emerges JSON-escaped so the one-line stdout contract survives.
  #
  # The second fact is a property of how each sentence is WRITTEN, not of the
  # mechanism. A future site spelled `_acct_refuse 2 bad-value "$sub"` — the
  # operator's bytes FIRST — would lose it, and is the shape to refuse.
  # THE SEAM ABOVE IS NOW CLOSED, AND THE PARAGRAPHS ABOVE ARE KEPT AS THE
  # HISTORY OF WHY (D-1983, review round 1). Task 22 made the trap reachable —
  # all three of its refusal sentences opened with the literal `--credential`,
  # M6 fired on each, and each produced exit 1 with a stray `bad-argv` envelope
  # on stdout AND a `_ccrc_die` sentence on stderr. Rewording them so they open
  # with prose fixed those three sentences; it did not fix the function, because
  # the property it relies on is POSITIONAL (how a sentence happens to be
  # written) and positional properties do not survive twelve more subcommands.
  # A comment is a request; this is the mechanism.
  #
  # CAPTURE, THEN DECIDE ON THE EXIT CODE — AND DISCARD THE BODY ON NON-ZERO.
  # `refuse` is the ONE op whose contract is "exit 0 means the envelope
  # printed" (`deploy/account-op.mjs:15-21`), so for it alone a non-zero exit
  # means the body on stdout is not the caller's answer: `main()`'s `refuse` arm
  # has exactly one exit-0 path, and every reachable non-zero return is either a
  # `usage:` with no body at all or a `bad-argv` envelope about the WRONG error.
  # There is no exit code at which node's body here is the answer to "$2"/"$3",
  # so emitting it could only ever hand the caller a well-formed object about an
  # error that did not happen.
  #
  # THE DISCRIMINATOR HERE IS THE EXIT CODE. `_acct_answer`'s IS THE BODY
  # (`[ -n "$body" ] ||`, :3937). THAT DIFFERENCE IS DELIBERATE AND MUST NOT BE
  # "HARMONISED" — harmonising them BREAKS `_acct_answer`. Every op except
  # `refuse` decides its own class and exits with it, so for those a non-zero
  # exit CARRYING a body is the normal refusal and `_acct_answer` must re-emit
  # it and return that code. `refuse` is the sole exception, and this is the
  # sole function that calls it.
  #
  # WHY THE CLOSE IS HERE AND NOT IN NODE. `_acct_answer`'s own header says it:
  # only the caller can cover for a callee that is older than itself. `ccd/ccrc`
  # lands by `install_atomic` and `deploy/` by rsync, so a fixed `ccrc` can meet
  # an OLD `account-op.mjs` on a half-updated box. This capture works against
  # EVERY version of the callee; teaching `account-op.mjs` a `--detail=VALUE`
  # single-token form — otherwise the cleaner parser change — works only against
  # the new one.
  #
  # NOTHING IS LOST BY DYING ON STDERR ALONE: `refuse()`
  # (`deploy/account-op.mjs:262-265`) writes its diagnosis to STDERR as well as
  # stdout, and stderr is never captured here, so node's own remedy still
  # reaches the operator verbatim. The die carries node's exit code in the
  # sentence and exits 1 — not "$1", and not 2: once the intended envelope never
  # printed, the class is unknowable, and 1 is this file's "the tool ran and the
  # answer was bad" (:24-32). One refusal, one signal, one owner.
  local envelope rc
  envelope="$(_acct_node refuse --code "$2" --detail "$3")"; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] \
    || _ccrc_die "could not print a refusal envelope for \"$2\" (deploy/account-op.mjs exited $rc): $3 — and the caller of this verb parses stdout, so it must hear about that rather than read a body about some other error. This is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box: a malformed call to the refusal printer, whose own message is on stderr above."
  printf '%s\n' "$envelope"
  exit "$1"
}

# ── THE EMPTY-BODY SEAM, ANSWERED IN ONE PLACE (D-2051) ───────────────────
# THREE callers reach the same condition — a captured stdout that is EMPTY — and
# until review round 2 only one of them said anything about it. `_acct_answer`
# below triaged it; `_acct_add`'s two re-emit sites printed the blank line they
# had captured and exited with the callee's own code, which is `JSON.parse('')`
# for the server. Measured on a real half-updated box (`deploy/account-op.mjs`
# from 5fb8b24d, which has `check-add` and not `add-entry`): exit 2, stdout one
# byte, a `usage:` line on stderr, and the 0600 secret already on disk.
#
# WHY THE BODY IS EMPTY AT ALL: `deploy/account-op.mjs`'s `main` answers an op it
# does not have with exit 2, a `usage:` line on STDERR and NOTHING on stdout —
# right for a human who mistyped an op at a shell, where the usage line is what
# they are looking at, and wrong for this verb, whose whole contract is exactly
# one JSON object on stdout. Propagated blindly it hands the caller an exit code
# and an empty body: "the box said no, here is why" and "this build cannot answer
# you at all" arriving as the same two bytes, which is the overloaded seam
# CLAUDE.md forbids.
#
# THE CONDITION IS REAL. `ccd/ccrc` lands by `install_atomic` and `deploy/` by
# rsync, so a run can find a ccrc that knows `roster`, `check-add` or `add-entry`
# beside an `account-op.mjs` that does not. AND BASH CLOSES IT, NOT NODE:
# teaching that file to print an envelope for an unknown op would fix every skew
# EXCEPT the one that exists, because the copy that has to do the printing is the
# OLD one, already on the box. Only the caller can cover for a callee that is
# older than itself.
#
# WHAT IT MEASURES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. "stdout was empty" — nothing else. It
# does not guess WHY, so the code is `no-answer` and the exit node gave is
# carried in the detail rather than translated away; the CAUSE is conditioned on
# that exit alone, because skew has a SIGNATURE (an older `main` answers an op it
# lacks with exactly 2) while an exit above 128 is a KILL no re-install changes.
#
# THE CLASS IS 1, NEVER THE CALLEE'S 2. This file's table (:24-33) makes 2 "not
# legal on its face, decidable from argv alone", and nothing reaching here is
# that: `_acct_add_parse` accepted the request, and on the `add-entry` path
# `check-add` accepted it too and a byte was written for it. AND `$3` IS THE
# CALLER'S SENTENCE, because what was WRITTEN differs by path: nothing on
# `_acct_answer`'s or at `check-add`, a 0600 credential file at `add-entry`. One
# helper ending all three "Nothing was written." would flatten two true
# statements into one false one, the narrowing this repository forbids.
_acct_no_answer() {   # <rc> <op> <what-was-written> — refuses at 1, never returns
  local rc="$1" cause
  if [ "$rc" -eq 2 ]; then
    cause="The known cause is a half-updated box: ccrc and deploy/account-op.mjs ship together and must be one build, and an older copy of that file answers an op it does not have with exactly this — exit 2, a usage line on stderr and no body at all. Re-run the install (or redeploy), then re-run this."
  elif [ "$rc" -gt 128 ]; then
    cause="An exit above 128 is a process that was KILLED rather than one that answered: $rc is signal $((rc - 128)). Look for an OOM kill or a timeout on this box, not for a stale build — re-installing will not change it."
  else
    cause="Nothing in this tree is known to exit $rc with an empty body, so this run cannot name a cause. deploy/account-op.mjs's own message on stderr above, if it wrote one, is the only evidence there is."
  fi
  _acct_refuse 1 no-answer "deploy/account-op.mjs exited $rc without writing an answer to '$2', so this run has nothing to tell you. $cause $3"
}

_acct_answer() {   # <op> [--<key> <value>]… — the answer, or a refusal that HAS a body
  # THE SEAM ABOVE, ON A PATH THAT ONLY PASSES AN ANSWER THROUGH: nothing this
  # function has called writes anything, so its clause is the simple one, and
  # the triage and the wording are `_acct_no_answer`'s, one function up.
  #
  # WHAT STILL CANNOT CLOSE HERE: `account-op.mjs` MISSING or broken — the other
  # half of the seam `_acct_refuse` documents, whose M7 half IS closed (D-1983,
  # :3813-3861). Measured at Task 21's review round 1 on a box with no
  # `ccrc/deploy/account-op.mjs` — exit 1, stdout EMPTY, `_ccrc_die` on stderr —
  # because the writer of every refusal on either side is the file that is gone,
  # so `_acct_refuse` falls through its own `||`. Closing it needs a refusal path
  # that does not go through node at all, and every path here does.
  #
  # CAPTURING IS NOT RE-WORDING. `_acct_node`'s "stdout is the caller's answer,
  # stderr is the caller's remedy" rule survives intact: the body is re-emitted
  # byte for byte and stderr is never captured at all, so node's own remedy
  # still reaches the operator verbatim. `$( )` strips trailing newlines and the
  # `printf` puts exactly one back — the shape every op writes, and what
  # `oneObject` pins.
  local body rc
  body="$(_acct_node "$@")"; rc=$?
  [ -n "$body" ] || _acct_no_answer "$rc" "$1" "Nothing was written."
  printf '%s\n' "$body"
  return "$rc"
}

_acct_roster_path() { printf '%s' "$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json"; }

# The shape contract, sourced rather than restated — `ccrc-adopt:105-112`'s
# reasoning, and NOT `cmd_wrappers:2307-2310`'s, which this comment cited until
# review round 1. That one is writer-specific by its own words ("this verb is
# the one that most needs it: it is the WRITER, and the writer judging 'is this
# file mine' by a second, looser copy of the reader's rules is how a generator
# comes to disown its own output") and `_acct_candidates` writes nothing at all.
# The reason that DOES bind a pure reader is adopt's: a second copy of the
# wrapper shape "is exactly how a checker and a generator drift into agreeing
# with each other about a shape neither tool writes" — and this verb is the
# checker in that sentence. It needs three things from the contract:
# `WRAPPER_ID_RE`, which every `--id` is measured against before a path is built
# from it, the two predicates that define a candidate, and the size bound those
# predicates oblige their caller to apply first.
_acct_shape() {
  local shape="$CCRC_HERE/ccrc-wrapper-shape"
  [ -f "$shape" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 install-incomplete "the wrapper shape contract is missing: $shape — is this a complete ccrc install?"
  # shellcheck source=ccrc-wrapper-shape
  . "$shape" \
    || _acct_refuse 1 install-incomplete "the wrapper shape contract failed to load: $shape"
  # `cmd_wrappers:2351-2352`'s guard, for this verb's names: a shape library
  # older than this ccrc loads cleanly and then decides nothing.
  [ -n "${WRAPPER_ID_RE:-}" ] && [ -n "${WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES:-}" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 install-incomplete "$shape loaded but is older than this ccrc — it declares no WRAPPER_ID_RE/WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES, which this verb needs before it names a file after an id or reads one to its last line. The files ship together and must be one build: re-run the install (or redeploy). Nothing was written."
}

_acct_candidates() {
  _acct_shape
  # `projection-*`, NOT `roster-*` (D-1923). Two files, two remedies: this one
  # is `ccrc install`'s GENERATED projection of `~/.ccrc/accounts.json`, and its
  # fix is to regenerate it, while `readRoster`'s three `roster-*` codes are
  # about the JSON an operator wrote by hand and their fix is to edit it. The
  # pair (subcommand, code) does not rescue the overload, which is what makes it
  # a defect rather than a wrinkle: `declare` (Task 27) and `add` (Task 24) read
  # BOTH files from ONE subcommand, so a caller hearing `roster-absent` could
  # not tell which file to go and fix.
  local sh="$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh"
  [ -f "$sh" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 projection-absent "$sh does not exist, so this box cannot say which launchers are already DECLARED — run 'ccrc install' to generate it from ~/.ccrc/accounts.json. Nothing was written."
  # SOURCED IN A SUBSHELL — `_inst_dirs`' rule (:6615-6626): the projection
  # declares `_ccrc_cfg_dir`, `_ccrc_label`, `_ccrc_hue` in the SAME `_ccrc_*`
  # namespace as `_ccrc_die`, and sourcing it here would let a generator change
  # redefine a refusal helper mid-verb. Only the id list crosses back.
  local declared
  declared="$(
    # shellcheck source=/dev/null
    . "$sh" || exit 1
    printf '%s' "${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[*]+${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[*]}}"
  )" || _acct_refuse 1 projection-invalid "$sh could not be sourced, so this box cannot say which launchers are already declared. Nothing was written."

  # `_check_wrappers`' `wr_cands` block (ccrc-doctor-checks:2388-2401) run
  # through the same predicates, with the size hoisted ahead of the two that
  # read the file (`ccrc-wrapper-shape:146-154`, obligation 2). Anything looser
  # reports every tool in ~/.local/bin as an account; anything narrower misses
  # the launcher somebody added and never wrote down.
  local -a args=()
  local f name other alias_of sz had_nullglob=0
  shopt -q nullglob && had_nullglob=1
  shopt -s nullglob
  for f in "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR"/*; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    [ -x "$f" ] || continue
    name="${f##*/}"
    [[ "$name" =~ $WRAPPER_ID_RE ]] || continue
    case " $declared " in *" $name "*) continue ;; esac
    # SIZED BEFORE ANYTHING READS IT. An id-shaped, undeclared file this verb
    # cannot size is a REFUSAL, not a skip: it might be a candidate and this run
    # cannot tell, which is `ccrc-adopt`'s direction for the same unmeasurable
    # (ccrc-wrapper-shape:153-154 names both directions and why they differ).
    #
    # `-L`, AND THAT IS THE ONE PLACE THIS LOOP DIVERGES FROM ITS PLAN. The
    # shape library states the fact that decides it (`ccrc-wrapper-shape`
    # :128-132): its predicates OPEN the file, so a symlink is followed
    # transparently, while a bare `stat` of a link answers "the length of that
    # path string" — the measured `claude` -> versions/<ver> case. A size taken
    # without `-L` would therefore gate on a number that is not about the bytes
    # about to be read, and an oversize launcher reached through a short link
    # would sail past the cap and be read to its last line: the exact obligation
    # this hoist exists to honour, defeated by the hoist. It would also print
    # the link's length as the launcher's size in a pick list. Both sizing
    # callers already in this tree pass `-L` for the same reason (:2601,
    # ccrc-doctor-checks:2542).
    sz="$(_plat_size -L -- "$f" 2>/dev/null)" || sz=""
    [[ "$sz" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] \
      || _acct_refuse 1 unmeasurable "stat could not size $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$name, and a pick list that reported a launcher without its size would be inviting a choice on no evidence. Nothing was written."
    [ "$sz" -le "$WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES" ] || continue
    _wrap_is_script "$f" || continue
    _wrap_declares_config_dir "$f" || continue
    # THE ALIAS COLLAPSE (ccrc-doctor-checks:2403-2419). `-ef` is a bash builtin
    # comparing device+inode THROUGH the symlink, so this needs no `readlink` —
    # and it catches a hard link, which `readlink` would not (:2404-2408). The
    # measured case is one file under two names; offering it as a NEW account
    # would propose two accounts fighting over one CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR.
    #
    # THE SELF-SKIP IS NOT COSMETIC, and it is the one line where this loop must
    # differ from doctor's. Doctor `continue`s a DECLARED name at :2412 — before
    # its `-ef` loop at :2414-2417 ever runs — so no name of its can reach the
    # comparison and be compared to ITSELF. Here the declared-skip is a separate
    # guard several lines above, and without this line a declared account's own
    # launcher would satisfy `$f -ef $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$name` and be dropped as an
    # "alias" of itself. That is the right ANSWER by the wrong MECHANISM: the two
    # guards would then cover for each other, and deleting the declared-skip
    # would change nothing that any test could see — a guard with no mutation
    # that reds it, which is the discipline CLAUDE.md states. Mutation 5 in
    # Step 6 is that mutation, and this line is what makes it red.
    alias_of=""
    for other in $declared; do
      [ "$other" != "$name" ] || continue
      [ -e "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$other" ] || continue
      if [ "$f" -ef "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$other" ]; then alias_of="$other"; break; fi
    done
    [ -z "$alias_of" ] || continue
    args+=(--name "$name" --bytes "$sz")
  done
  [ "$had_nullglob" -eq 1 ] || shopt -u nullglob

  _acct_answer candidates ${args[@]+"${args[@]}"} || exit $?
}

# ── THE CREDENTIAL: ONE DOOR, AND IT IS SHUT WHEN NOTHING IS BEHIND IT ─────
# `--credential -` is the ONLY spelling that reads a secret (spec §5). Two
# guards, for two different failures:
#
#  1. A LITERAL IS REFUSED, not merely discouraged. `cmd_passwd`'s header
#     (:2960-2968) records the measurement: anything on argv is world-readable
#     in /proc/<pid>/cmdline for the life of the process, and a here-string is
#     not an escape — bash implements `<<<` with a temp file on disk. A flag
#     whose safe use is a convention is not a guard.
#
#  2. THE TTY GATE INVERTS HERE, and nowhere else in this file. All three of
#     this file's other `[ -t 0 ]` gates — `cmd_passwd` (:3026), `cmd_expose`
#     (:3221) and `_inst_agent_env` (:5640) — refuse a PIPE, because
#     under `curl … | bash` stdin is the installer script and a `read` would
#     take a line of shell as the operator's answer. This flag exists for the
#     machine caller and requires a pipe, so it refuses a TERMINAL — a human
#     typing a token at an unprompted read is `cmd_passwd`'s shape, with its
#     two traps and its echo discipline, and this is not that verb.
#
# EMPTY IS A REFUSAL, NOT AN EMPTY VALUE. A caller that opened the pipe and
# wrote nothing has told us nothing, and a 0600 file holding
# `export ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=` is a key that decides nothing — the empty
# distinction `_exp_env_write` (:3501-3505) already refuses one level down.
#
# `IFS=` on the read is `cmd_passwd`'s D-142 rule (:3081): without it bash
# strips leading and trailing whitespace, and a token is exactly its bytes.
#
# EVERY REFUSAL SENTENCE BELOW BEGINS WITH PROSE, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING, not
# style. `_acct_refuse`'s header (:3757-3809) states the rule — the `--` bytes go
# MID-SENTENCE, because `$3` reaches `account-op.mjs` as the value of `--detail`
# and a value that BEGINS with `--` is refused there as a mis-spelled key. The
# first draft of these three opened with a flag name, and each then produced a
# stray `bad-argv` envelope on stdout AND a `_ccrc_die` sentence on stderr, at
# exit 1 instead of 2 — the two-disagreeing-signals shape that header calls a
# documented trap. Task 23 reintroduced it in FIVE sites 200 lines below here
# (D-2006), so the rule is no longer this paragraph: `ccrc-account.test.ts`'s
# "no refusal sentence may begin with a flag" scans this whole file for both.
#
# THE argv CLAIM ABOVE IS MEASURED, NOT REASONED (Task 22, `strace -f -s 4000
# -e trace=execve,openat,write` over a full read-then-write of a canary token):
# the run forks exactly THREE processes — mkdir, chmod, mv — and none of their
# argv carries the token, because `printf` is a builtin and so there is no
# fourth execve for it to ride. Exactly ONE write() in the whole run carries the
# token, to the temp fd inside ~/.cc-secrets that `mv -f` then renames. Nothing
# is written to /dev/tty — bash opens it for job control and never writes there
# — which is the one path that would have escaped the captured stdout and stderr
# the tests assert on.
ACCT_CREDENTIAL=""
_acct_read_credential() {   # <spelling> — sets ACCT_CREDENTIAL, or refuses
  # THE TWO BOUNDS THE argv/file/stream CLAIM ABOVE DOES NOT COVER, CLOSED HERE
  # (review round 1). Both are CALLER-imposed shell options, not defects in this
  # function, and `cmd_passwd` has the identical exposure — so this is a
  # narrowing, not a regression fix. Measured with a marked non-secret, counting
  # occurrences rather than printing any: under `bash -x` the value appeared on
  # stderr 3 times and now 0; under `set -a` it appeared in a child's environ
  # once and now 0. `local -` makes the two `set` changes local to this function
  # and bash restores them on return (measured: tracing resumes at the caller's
  # very next line), so an operator debugging the REST of a run keeps xtrace.
  # `export -n` is the third token because `set +a` alone cannot close the case
  # where `ccrc` was SOURCED into a shell that already had allexport on: the
  # file-scope `ACCT_CREDENTIAL=""` below carries the export attribute by then,
  # and only removing it un-exports the variable (measured: 1 occurrence, then
  # 0). What this does NOT claim: a caller who exports the value itself, or
  # `strace` on this process, or a core dump — none of those go through here.
  local -; set +x +a; export -n ACCT_CREDENTIAL
  local spelling="${1:-}"
  [ "$spelling" = '-' ] \
    || _acct_refuse 2 credential-not-stdin "the --credential flag takes exactly one spelling, \"-\", which reads the secret from stdin. A value given here would sit in this process's argv, which is world-readable on this box for as long as the process lives. Nothing was written."
  [ -t 0 ] \
    && _acct_refuse 2 credential-needs-a-pipe "the --credential - flag reads the secret from a PIPE, and stdin here is a terminal. This verb does not prompt: it is driven by the server, and a prompt would have no echo discipline and no interrupt handling. Pipe the secret in, or use 'ccrc passwd' for the one secret this box does prompt for. Nothing was written."
  local v=""
  IFS= read -r v
  # `read` returns 1 at EOF with no delimiter, which is the ordinary shape of a
  # one-line pipe with no trailing newline — so the RETURN CODE is not the test.
  # The VALUE is.
  [ -n "${v//[[:space:]]/}" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 2 credential-empty "the --credential - flag was given and stdin carried nothing. This verb fails closed rather than writing a credential file with an empty value in it, which would be a lane holding a key that decides nothing. Nothing was written."
  ACCT_CREDENTIAL="$v"
}

# ── THE SECRETS-FILE NAME, DERIVED ONCE IN BASH (Task 28) ─────────────────
# `_acct_write_secret` builds its destination from this, and `_acct_credential`
# re-measures the roster's `exec.secretsFile` against it. Two callers, one rule:
# the writer's own header below says "THE TWO DERIVATIONS MUST STAY ONE RULE"
# about this exact derivation, and a second hand-typed copy in the reader would
# have been the drift that sentence forbids — measured, this task's plan snippet
# spelled the reader's copy `.cc-secrets/$id-$ACCT_PROVIDER.env`, which refuses
# every anthropic lane ccrc itself created, because that lane's file is
# `<id>-oauth.env`.
#
# NODE KEEPS THE THIRD SPELLING and cannot be made to share this one:
# `deploy/account-op.mjs:1075`'s `secretTag` is the same rule in the other
# language, and a bare `node` cannot import bash. Two spellings, one per
# language, is this tree's stated floor for exactly this shape
# (`ccrc-wrapper-shape`'s note on `WRAPPER_SUFFIX_SAFE_RE`); `add` writing the
# path it derives into the roster and `credential` refusing when the roster
# disagrees is what makes the pair measurable rather than remembered.
ACCT_SECRET_TAG=""
_acct_secret_tag() {   # <provider> <envvar> -> ACCT_SECRET_TAG
  # THE THIRD ARGUMENT DECIDES, NOT THE SECOND: a lane exporting
  # `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` holds an OAuth token whatever its provider is
  # called, and `check-add` reads it out of the same column for the same reason.
  ACCT_SECRET_TAG="$1"
  [ "$2" = CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN ] && ACCT_SECRET_TAG=oauth
  # `return 0` because the `&&` above is this function's last command and would
  # otherwise hand every api-key lane a non-zero status for having a provider
  # tag rather than an oauth one.
  return 0
}

_acct_write_secret() {   # <id> <provider> <envvar> — the 0600 file, then forget it
  # `_exp_env_write`'s discipline (:3492-3544) at a different address: `umask
  # 077` INSIDE the writing subshell so the temp is 0600 from its FIRST byte —
  # never world-readable in the window between create and chmod, the reason that
  # function's header states at :3489-3491 — then chmod and `mv -f` in the same
  # directory, and `rm -f "$tmp"` on any failure (:3532-3535's shape).
  #
  # WHAT EACH HALF OF THAT ACTUALLY DECIDES, measured at Task 22 because the
  # mutation table would otherwise credit the wrong one — and NEITHER of them
  # was pinned until review round 1, which is the half this paragraph used to
  # leave out. The END STATE is over-determined: deleting `chmod 600` alone is
  # GREEN and deleting `umask 077` alone is GREEN, because either line on its
  # own delivers 0600 to the destination; only deleting BOTH reds (436 where 384
  # was expected). So the mode IS pinned and the MECHANISM was not, and a reader
  # of this paragraph would have credited the umask for a property no assertion
  # measured. What the umask uniquely buys is the WINDOW — the temp is 0600 from
  # its first byte, never world-readable between create and chmod, the reason
  # `_exp_env_write`'s header states at :3489-3491 — and a window is not visible
  # in an end state. `ccrc-account.test.ts` now plants a `chmod` recorder on PATH
  # that stats the temp BEFORE delegating to the real one: pristine records 600,
  # and with `umask 077` deleted it records 644 while every other assertion in
  # the file stays green. `chmod 644` in the chmod's place still reds the mode
  # assertion (420 where 384 was expected), which is the other line's own pin.
  # And the SUBSHELL
  # is not decoration: measured, moving `umask 077` outside it leaves the
  # process's umask at 0077 AFTER this function returns, so every file the rest
  # of the run creates is silently narrowed (a later `touch` came back 600 where
  # the unmutated run gives 664). `add` writes the roster and the wrappers after
  # calling this, so that is a consequence rather than a style point — and no
  # assertion in this file can see it, which is why it is written down here.
  #
  # `mkdir -p -m 0700` AND NO CHMOD. The mode applies to a directory this call
  # CREATES and to nothing else (POSIX); an EXISTING ~/.cc-secrets is left
  # exactly as the operator has it, which is `cmd_wrappers`' rule for the
  # analogous directory (:2354-2358, implemented :2368). Widening a directory an
  # operator narrowed would be worse than the state this found; the 0600 on the
  # FILE is what protects the secret.
  #
  # NO STRAY SWEEP, DELIBERATELY UNDECIDED (review round 1). `cmd_wrappers`
  # carries the idiom for one (:2874-2884) and this function does not: the
  # failure PATH removes its own temp, but a process KILLED between the create
  # and the `mv` leaves `.<id>-<tag>.env.tmp.<pid>` behind, 0600 and holding a
  # live credential, and the `$$` in the name means no retry ever reuses it. The
  # `secret-write` sentence below says so rather than promising otherwise, which
  # is all this round changed; adding the sweep is a design decision (it would
  # have to age or PID-check the strays, because a concurrent `add` for another
  # account is a legitimate owner of one) and is recorded here as open.
  #
  # The temp name carries a leading dot, `cmd_wrappers`' spelling for a
  # roster-derived destination (the rule is stated in the negative at
  # :5867-5872). Nothing below distinguishes it from `<dest>.tmp.$$`, because
  # `.env` already puts both outside `WRAPPER_ID_RE`; it is the consistent
  # spelling, not a measured guard.
  # The same shell-option bound `_acct_read_credential` states above, for the
  # same reason: this function handles the value too. `export -n` is not
  # repeated because the read always runs first and it is that call which
  # removes the attribute.
  local -; set +x +a
  local id="$1" provider="$2" var="$3" tag dest tmp
  # shellcheck disable=SC2174  # -m applying to the deepest directory ONLY is
  # the intent, and an EXISTING directory keeping the operator's mode is the
  # rule this function is written to obey — see the paragraph above.
  mkdir -p -m 0700 "$ACCT_SECRETS_DIR" \
    || _acct_refuse 1 secrets-dir "cannot create $ACCT_SECRETS_DIR (0700) — nothing was written"
  # THE NAME SAYS WHAT THE FILE CARRIES, and it is derived from the THIRD
  # argument rather than from the second, for the reason `check-add` derives it
  # the same way (Task 23's plan literal, `secretTag`): a lane exporting
  # `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` holds an OAuth token and its file is
  # `<id>-oauth.env` — §6:507, §7:472, §11 and §12.5's spelling, and the one
  # every anthropic lane on this fleet already has
  # (`server/test/helpers.ts:69`) — while an api-key lane's file is
  # `<id>-<provider>.env`, §4.3:318's spelling. §5:413's `X-P.env` is the
  # general shape both obey. THE TWO DERIVATIONS MUST STAY ONE RULE: `add`
  # writes this path into `exec.secretsFile`, the generated wrapper sources
  # whatever that field says (`shared/wrapper.mjs:141-143`),
  # `ccd-account-auth`'s `setup-token` capture writes the same name on re-auth
  # (Task 54), and Task 28 REFUSES rather than guessing when the roster and this
  # derivation disagree (`secrets-path-unmanaged`).
  #
  # THE RULE ITSELF IS ONE LINE UP, IN `_acct_secret_tag`, SINCE TASK 28 — the
  # sentence above says the derivations must stay one rule, and `credential`
  # became the second bash caller of it. It was lifted rather than copied for
  # the reason `_acct_id_or_refuse` was: a second spelling in bash is drift
  # nothing in this tree can see, because `single-definition.test.ts` filters
  # `/\.tsx?$/` and never reads `ccd/`.
  _acct_secret_tag "$provider" "$var"
  tag="$ACCT_SECRET_TAG"
  dest="$ACCT_SECRETS_DIR/$id-$tag.env"
  tmp="$ACCT_SECRETS_DIR/.$id-$tag.env.tmp.$$"
  # `%q`, NOT `%s`, AND THE FILE IS THE REASON (D-1984, review round 1). This
  # file is SOURCED, not read: `shared/wrapper.mjs:141-143` generates
  # `[ -r "$HOME/<secretsFile>" ] && . "$HOME/<secretsFile>"` into every
  # wrapper, so whatever lands here is CODE running as the fleet user at every
  # launch. Written with `%s` it had two defects in one line, both measured with
  # a fixture whose bytes are shell syntax (the value was never printed; the
  # effect was): the sourced variable came back 14 bytes of a 48-byte token, and
  # an embedded command substitution EXECUTED. So a token carrying a space, a
  # `#`, a `$`, a `'` or a trailing CR was silently truncated while this verb
  # exited 0 and reported success — and any byte of it could run.
  #
  # `%q` is bash's own quoter and it is IDENTITY for an ordinary token, which is
  # why the canary never noticed: today's fixtures are `[A-Za-z0-9-]`. The
  # rejected alternative was a fourth refusal code (`credential-charset`): it
  # would match this block's "three conditions, three codes" shape and would
  # REFUSE legitimate tokens, abandoning the principle the read above exists to
  # honour — a token is exactly its bytes. Quoting keeps that principle; a
  # charset refusal trades it away.
  #
  # THE ONLY IN-TREE CONSUMER OF `.cc-secrets/*.env` IS A BASH SOURCE, which is
  # what makes `%q` safe rather than merely stricter: `deploy/notify.sh:29`
  # reads `ccrc-mail.token` raw and never sources it, and that is a different
  # file. Any future non-bash reader of THIS file would have to unquote, and
  # would be the caller that has to say so.
  if ! (
    umask 077
    printf 'export %s=%q\n' "$var" "$ACCT_CREDENTIAL" > "$tmp"
  ) || ! chmod 600 "$tmp" || ! mv -f "$tmp" "$dest"; then
    rm -f "$tmp"
    ACCT_CREDENTIAL=""
    _acct_refuse 1 secret-write "writing $dest failed — nothing was installed. Check that $ACCT_SECRETS_DIR exists and is writable by the user this box runs as; a retry overwrites the DESTINATION file, and this path has already removed its own temp. What nothing cleans up is a temp left by a run that was KILLED between the create and the mv: it is .$id-$tag.env.tmp.<pid> in that same directory, 0600 and holding a live credential, and no retry ever reuses the name because it carries the pid. Remove one by hand if you find it."
  fi
  # UNSET THE MOMENT THE FILE HAS IT — `cmd_passwd`'s `unset p1 p2` (:3123) — AND
  # RECORD THE PATH, never the value (D-2051: `_acct_add`'s `add-entry` refusal
  # has to say this file exists, and only its writer knows that it does).
  # Everything after this point can be traced, logged and echoed without a
  # containment argument, which makes the canary test a fact about the verb.
  ACCT_CREDENTIAL=""; ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN="$dest"
}

# ── THE `add` FLAG LOOP ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `cmd_install`'s loop (:5303-5313) widened: BOTH spellings for every
# value-taking flag, a missing value with its own message at exit 2, and a WRONG
# VALUE for a right flag getting its own sentence rather than "unknown argument"
# (:5314-5317) — the operator typed the right flag and the wrong value, which
# are two different mistakes to fix.
#
# THE FLAG NAME IS CAPTURED BEFORE THE SHIFT, and that is the whole reason
# `cmd_install` folds its assignment INTO the first `case` rather than running a
# second one. After `v="$2"; shift`, `$1` is the VALUE — so a second
# `case "${1%%=*}"` would be switching on the operator's data, no arm would
# match, and every `--flag VALUE` spelling would be read as a flag with no
# effect. Measured on the shape this loop replaced: `--id lab-dev0 --provider
# compatible` yielded `id=[] provider=[]` while `--id=lab-dev0
# --provider=compatible` yielded both. `flag` is taken at the TOP of the body,
# from the un-shifted `$1`, and both spellings then land in one place.
#
# THE FOUR REFUSALS THIS FUNCTION OWNS are the ones decidable from argv and the
# shape library alone. Everything that needs the roster or the provider table is
# `check-add`'s, in node, on the same exit-code table.
ACCT_ID=""; ACCT_PROVIDER=""; ACCT_LABEL=""; ACCT_HUE=""; ACCT_SUFFIX=""
ACCT_BASE_URL=""; ACCT_MODELS=""; ACCT_METHOD=""; ACCT_CRED_SPELLING=""
_acct_add_parse() {
  _acct_shape
  local flag="" v=""
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    flag="${1%%=*}"
    case "$1" in
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      --id|--provider|--label|--hue|--suffix|--base-url|--models|--method|--credential)
        [ $# -ge 2 ] \
          || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "the flag $1 takes a value and nothing followed it on the command line. Nothing was written."
        v="$2"; shift ;;
      --id=*|--provider=*|--label=*|--hue=*|--suffix=*|--base-url=*|--models=*|--method=*|--credential=*)
        v="${1#*=}" ;;
      *) _acct_refuse 2 unknown-argument "ccrc account add has no argument \"$1\"" ;;
    esac
    # A FLAG GIVEN AN EMPTY VALUE IS NOT A FLAG NOBODY GAVE (D-2148). D-2144
    # read this defect as `declare`'s and said `add` "refuses the same input
    # with missing-value at exit 2" — true of `--provider` and of nothing else.
    # Measured on the shipped tree, FIVE of this loop's nine flags reached exit
    # 0: `--suffix ''` silently took the `.claude-$ACCT_ID` default below,
    # `--base-url ''`, `--models ''` and `--method ''` were dropped by the
    # `${x:+…}` expansions at the `check-add` call, and `--credential ''` on a
    # login lane satisfied the `-z` test that means "no credential offered".
    # Two of those five carry ACCIDENTAL COVER on one provider and none at all
    # on another — `--base-url ''` answers `base-url-required` under
    # `compatible` and creates the lane under `anthropic`; `--credential ''`
    # answers `credential-required` on a token lane and creates the lane on a
    # login one — which is the shape that hid this for two rounds.
    #
    # THE GATE IS HERE, ON `$v`, and not nine times below, for `declare`'s own
    # reason: both spellings of every flag land in `$v` exactly once. The four
    # downstream "a value is required" gates stay and now answer one condition
    # each — flag-ABSENT — instead of two.
    [ -n "$v" ] \
      || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "the flag $flag was given an empty value. A flag present with nothing in it is not the same as a flag nobody passed, so it is refused rather than dropped: give it a value, or leave it off. Nothing was written."
    case "$flag" in
      --id)         ACCT_ID="$v" ;;
      --provider)   ACCT_PROVIDER="$v" ;;
      --label)      ACCT_LABEL="$v" ;;
      --hue)        ACCT_HUE="$v" ;;
      --suffix)     ACCT_SUFFIX="$v" ;;
      --base-url)   ACCT_BASE_URL="$v" ;;
      --models)     ACCT_MODELS="$v" ;;
      --method)     ACCT_METHOD="$v" ;;
      --credential) ACCT_CRED_SPELLING="$v" ;;
    esac
    shift
  done

  # THE ONE ID GATE, SHARED (Task 27). These three refusals — `missing-value`,
  # `bad-id`, `reserved-id`, in that order — were spelled inline here until
  # `declare` needed the same three, and six more subcommands after it do too.
  # They live in `_acct_id_or_refuse` below and the sentences are unchanged: a
  # lift that reworded an answer would be a behaviour change wearing a
  # refactor's clothes, which is why Task 23's own `bad-id` and `reserved-id`
  # rows are the mechanism that proves this one did not.
  #
  # THE GATE IS STILL BEFORE ANY PATH IS BUILT FROM THE ID, which is why the call
  # sits here: the id is about to become a filename under $HOME/.local/bin, a
  # path segment under $HOME/.cc-secrets and a bash `case` pattern
  # (`cmd_wrappers`' rule, :2504-2510) — and that holds for a traversing id.
  _acct_id_or_refuse "$ACCT_ID"

  [ -n "$ACCT_LABEL" ] || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "a human label is required: pass --label. Nothing was written."
  [ -n "$ACCT_HUE" ]   || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "a hue is required: pass --hue. Nothing was written."
  [ -n "$ACCT_PROVIDER" ] || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "a provider is required: pass --provider. Nothing was written."

  # §4.4's default, and it is inside the read root BY CONSTRUCTION.
  #
  # THE SECOND SPELLING OF ONE RULE, and the reason it is here as well as in
  # `check-add`: a gate cannot measure a value that does not exist yet, and the
  # two gates below are this function's. `check-add` defaults it too, because it
  # is callable by hand and must resolve a complete plan (see that arm's own
  # note); the agreement between the two is measured, not asserted, by
  # `ccrc-account.test.ts`'s "`_acct_add_parse` and `check-add` default the
  # config dir to the same string".
  [ -n "$ACCT_SUFFIX" ] || ACCT_SUFFIX=".claude-$ACCT_ID"
  # THE READ ROOT, NOT A STYLE RULE. `underClaudeGlob`
  # (agent/src/whitelist.ts:48-53) admits only $HOME/.claude*, and every server
  # read of a lane's transcripts and statusline goes through `configDirFor`. A
  # suffix outside the glob is a lane the server can never read — so the flow
  # REFUSES rather than widening the glob.
  case "$ACCT_SUFFIX" in
    .claude*) ;;
    *) _acct_refuse 2 suffix-outside-read-root "config directory \"$ACCT_SUFFIX\" is outside \$HOME/.claude*, which is the only place the ccrc agent may read (agent/src/whitelist.ts). A lane there is one this box could roster and never show you. Use a suffix starting with \".claude\". Nothing was written." ;;
  esac
  # The second, independent gate, SHARED WITH `declare` since review round 2 —
  # `_acct_suffix_or_refuse` below, and the sentence is unchanged for the reason
  # the id lift gives: a lift that reworded an answer would be a behaviour
  # change wearing a refactor's clothes. The read-root gate above stays HERE and
  # is not shared, and D-2142 says why: `add` creates and provisions that
  # directory, `declare` records somebody else's.
  _acct_suffix_or_refuse "$ACCT_SUFFIX"
}

# ── THE TWO CONVERGERS, WITH THEIR TRANSCRIPT ON THE OTHER STREAM ──────────
# `_inst_accounts_sh` and `cmd_wrappers` are `ccrc install`'s own steps and this
# verb calls them rather than re-implementing either. Two things have to change
# at the seam, and neither is cosmetic.
#
#  1. THEY PRINT ON STDOUT. `_inst_accounts_sh` echoes "install: accounts.sh:
#     converged" (:5569) or "… generated from $HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json"
#     (:5575); `cmd_wrappers` prints a line per written account and a
#     `summary: …` line (:2904). Read that off the SOURCE, not off a suite:
#     `ccrc-install.test.ts:2818-2819` pins only `cmd_wrappers`' summary onto
#     `install`'s stdout, and nothing in server/test/ pins the accounts.sh
#     lines at all. For `install` that transcript IS the answer. Here it is
#     the REMEDY, and this verb's stdout is one JSON object — so it goes to
#     stderr, where `_acct_node`'s own rule already puts remedies.
#
#  2. THEY REFUSE THROUGH `_ccrc_die` (:1198), which prints NOTHING on stdout
#     and exits. Uncontained that is the empty body this verb exists to refuse.
#     The subshell contains the exit; the `||` arm gives the refusal a class.
#
# NO FLAGS ON `cmd_wrappers`, deliberately, `_inst_wrappers`' choice
# (:7130-7133): the two flags that decide what may be overwritten are the two an
# install must never pass, "an install that passed either would be an install
# that can destroy a hand-written launcher without anybody typing the flag that
# authorises it". D-1862: there is no per-id mode and this verb does not add one
# — the converged path writes nothing for an unchanged account, which IS the
# narrow blast radius §5 asked for.
_acct_converge() {
  ( _inst_accounts_sh ) >&2 \
    || _acct_refuse 1 accounts-sh "regenerating \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh from the roster failed — read the lines above; the generator's own remedy is among them. The roster entry WAS written and nothing rolls back: run 'ccrc install' once the cause is fixed."
  ( cmd_wrappers ) >&2 \
    || _acct_refuse 1 wrapper-converge "the wrapper converge refused — read the lines above; each refusal carries its own remedy. The roster entry WAS written and nothing rolls back: run 'ccrc wrappers' once the cause is fixed."
}

# ── WHICH settings.json ENV KEYS ARE CCRC'S ───────────────────────────────
# `install-session-hooks.sh:83-86`'s `JQ_UNMANAGED` argument, applied to the
# env block: ONE definition, shared by the writer (`account add`) and the
# remover (`account remove`), because a second spelling in the remover is
# exactly how the two come to disagree about whose key a file holds.
#
# DERIVED, NOT LISTED, which is one step stronger than that installer manages.
# `modelmap` names the four alias -> env-var pairs ONCE; `managed` is computed
# from it plus the two endpoint keys; `unmanaged` filters on `managed`. A fifth
# alias is one pair in one object, not three edits across two modes — CLAUDE.md's
# `PR_REASONS = Object.keys(PR_REASON_MAP)` rule, in jq.
#
# `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. The key lives in the 0600 file the
# generated wrapper sources at exec time; this file sits beside the transcripts, where
# the operator can read it and doctor can check it without ever opening a secret (spec
# §4.3). `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is written EMPTY so the sourced token wins — measured, §4.3.
#
# BESIDE THEIR ONE CONSUMER, NOT IN THE FILE-SCOPE CONSTANTS BLOCK, and that is a
# DEVIATION from this task's plan snippet, argued rather than transcribed. That block
# spells a value once because TWO sections read it (`ACCT_SECRETS_DIR` states the
# reason: a writer and a reader that spelled the path separately is the drift D-166
# names). These three programs have exactly one reader — `_acct_settings_env` below,
# itself the single writer in both directions — so the block's own argument does not
# reach them, while the installer this merge is copied from puts its jq programs
# immediately above the loop that runs them (`install-session-hooks.sh:86`, used at
# `:118-121`). The measured cost of the other placement is the reason it matters:
# inserting at the constants block moves every line below it — ~98 line citations in
# this tree against ~30 here, 20 in files this task may not touch. D-1985's class,
# avoided rather than swept.
JQ_ACCT_MANAGED='
def modelmap: {opus:"ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL", sonnet:"ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL",
               haiku:"ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL", subagent:"CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL"};
def managed: ["ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL","ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"] + (modelmap|to_entries|map(.value));
def unmanaged: with_entries(select(.key as $k | (managed|index($k)) == null));
'
JQ_ACCT_SET="$JQ_ACCT_MANAGED"'
( (if $burl == "" then {} else {ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL: $burl, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: ""} end)
  + ( if ($models|type) == "object"
      then ($models | with_entries(select(modelmap[.key] != null))
                    | with_entries(.key |= modelmap[.]))
      else {} end ) ) as $add
| .env = (((.env // {}) | unmanaged) + $add)
| if .env == {} then del(.env) else . end
'
JQ_ACCT_CLEAR="$JQ_ACCT_MANAGED"'
.env = ((.env // {}) | unmanaged)
| if .env == {} then del(.env) else . end
'

# DID THE MERGE WRITE, OR RUN AND CORRECTLY WRITE NOTHING? The path it wrote, or
# empty. `ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN`'s idiom (:4695) and its reason: from Task 26 on
# the caller publishes a step list as an answer key, and that key must not say "the
# env block was written" about a home where no settings.json exists. The honest way
# to know is to MEASURE the write — this is set by the `mv` that lands the file and
# by nothing else — rather than to infer it from the branch that decided.
#
# EMPTY MEANS "RAN AND WROTE NOTHING", and it may mean only that because the one
# caller reads it on the line after it called this; the same control-flow argument
# `ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN` rests on. Reset at this function's ENTRY rather than at file
# scope, which runs once: a second call in one sourced shell must not inherit the
# first's answer (D-2127's second face, closed at `ACCT_PROVISIONED` too).
ACCT_SETTINGS_WRITTEN=""
_acct_settings_env() {   # <cfgdir> <set|clear> <what-still-stands> [<base-url>] [<models-json>]
  # `install-session-hooks.sh:104-132`, clause for clause: jq validates BEFORE (:109)
  # and AFTER (:128), the converge is byte-level under `jq -S` (:126) so a converged
  # file is never touched, every rewritten file is backed up first (:129), and the write
  # is tmp + `mv -f` (:130-131). Values enter through --arg/--argjson (:120), never
  # shell interpolation. THE BEFORE PROBE IS STRUCTURAL, not `jq empty` (review round
  # 1): an EMPTY settings.json passed `jq empty` and compared CONVERGED, so the lane got
  # no env block at exit 0; and a non-object `env` parses, then dies wearing ccrc's class.
  #
  # $3 IS THE CALLER'S "WHAT STILL STANDS" CLAUSE, ended onto every refusal below —
  # D-2126, D-2051's ruling at `_acct_no_answer`'s own $3. Each here is true about THIS
  # FILE and silent about the run: read as "nothing was written" it sends the operator
  # back into `ccrc account add` and `duplicate-id`. Shared it would be false for
  # `clear`, Task 32's caller, whose entry is on its way out. Required, never `${3:-}`.
  local dir="$1" mode="$2" stands="$3" burl="${4:-}" models="${5:-{\}}" f cur next prog tmp fmode=""
  ACCT_SETTINGS_WRITTEN=""
  f="$dir/settings.json"
  if [ -f "$f" ]; then
    jq -e 'type == "object" and ((.env? // {}) | type == "object")' "$f" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || _acct_refuse 1 settings-invalid "$f is not a JSON object whose \"env\" is an object (or absent), which is the shape this merge needs. This verb never rewrites a settings.json it could not validate, so that file is exactly as it was: fix it, or move it aside. $stands"
    cur="$(cat "$f")" \
      || _acct_refuse 1 settings-invalid "$f could not be read, so nothing was merged and that file is exactly as it was. $stands"
  else
    # NOTHING TO CLEAR FROM. A home with no settings.json must not gain one
    # from a removal — install-session-hooks.sh:112-114's rule.
    [ "$mode" = clear ] && return 0
    cur='{}'
  fi

  prog="$JQ_ACCT_SET"
  [ "$mode" = clear ] && prog="$JQ_ACCT_CLEAR"
  next="$(jq --arg burl "$burl" --argjson models "$models" "$prog" <<<"$cur")" \
    || _acct_refuse 1 settings-merge "merging the account env block into $f failed — that file is exactly as it was. $stands"

  # AND NOTHING TO SET, EITHER. A `login` lane has no endpoint and no model map, so
  # `next` is `{}` — measured against real jq. A settings.json holding `{}` in a home
  # that never had one decides nothing — `_exp_env_write` at :3501-3505, `clear` above.
  if [ ! -f "$f" ] && [ "$(jq -S -c . <<<"$next")" = '{}' ]; then
    return 0
  fi

  # CONVERGED? Do not touch it: the re-derived JSON compares equal under key-sorted
  # normalisation, the bytes stay exactly as they were, idempotence measurable on mtime.
  if [ -f "$f" ] && [ "$(jq -S . <<<"$next")" = "$(jq -S . "$f")" ]; then
    return 0
  fi
  jq empty <<<"$next" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _acct_refuse 1 settings-merge "the merged settings for $f are not valid JSON — this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box, and that file is exactly as it was. $stands"
  if [ -f "$f" ]; then
    # THE FILE'S OWN MODE RIDES THE SWAP, which that installer does NOT do: `mv`
    # replaces the inode, so an operator's 0600 came back at the umask — 664,
    # measured. D-1244's remedy, fallback and macOS reason verbatim, :7004-7010.
    local stamp bdir; fmode="$(_plat_mode "$f" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    # `date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S`, NOT THE PLAN SNIPPET'S `date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ`:
    # `BOX_BACKUP_ROOT`'s own note (:1034-1042) makes the shape contractual — "a backup
    # named outside that shape is a backup the pruner can never reclaim" — and the glob
    # (deploy.sh:224-228, :7653's loop) is `[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{6}`, which `T…Z` matches
    # nowhere: every backup here accumulates. EIGHT writers share this root and spell it
    # so — :3591, :6955, :7097, :7352, `TS=` in all four, install-session-hooks.sh:38.
    stamp="$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)" \
      || _acct_refuse 1 settings-merge "cannot read the clock (date failed), and a backup needs a name — $f is exactly as it was. $stands"
    bdir="$BOX_BACKUP_ROOT/$stamp"
    # `${dir##*/}` and not `$(basename "$dir")`: the installer's naming, reached by pure
    # parameter expansion — `CCRC_HERE`'s argument (:834-841), for a box with no PATH.
    mkdir -p "$bdir" && cp -a "$f" "$bdir/${dir##*/}.settings.json" \
      || _acct_refuse 1 settings-merge "could not back up $f to $bdir — refusing to rewrite it, so that file is exactly as it was. $stands"
  fi
  tmp="$f.tmp.$$"; [ -n "$fmode" ] || fmode=644
  jq . <<<"$next" > "$tmp" && chmod "$fmode" "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$f" \
    || { rm -f "$tmp"; _acct_refuse 1 settings-merge "writing $f failed — the write is tmp + mv, so that file is exactly as it was. $stands"; }
  # THE ONE WRITER OF THIS VARIABLE, and it sits after the `mv` that landed the file
  # so that it is a measurement rather than an intention. Every `return 0` above it
  # leaves it empty, which is the whole of the distinction the caller reads.
  ACCT_SETTINGS_WRITTEN="$f"
}

# THE STEPS THIS RUN ACTUALLY RAN, appended as they happen. An ARRAY and not a
# printed list, deliberately: `_acct_provision` refuses through `_acct_refuse`, which
# prints an envelope and `exit`s, so a caller that had wrapped it in `$( … )` would have
# captured that envelope into a variable while the `exit` killed only the subshell — the
# run going on to answer `ok:true` with a refusal embedded in it. Nothing here runs in a
# subshell, so a refusal ends the verb.
ACCT_PROVISIONED=()
_acct_provision() {   # <cfgdir> <base-url> <models-json> -> appends to ACCT_PROVISIONED
  # PER-HOME, BY THE VERB, NOT BY THE INSTALL SPINE (spec §5). `_inst_dirs` (:6613),
  # `_inst_hooks` (:6812), `_inst_skills` (:6850) and `_inst_graphify_skill` (:6865) take
  # no arguments by contract and converge EVERY rostered home; this verb has one home to
  # provision, and the four standalone scripts all take `--homes <dir>…`
  # (install-session-hooks.sh:63, and the identical line at
  # install-coordinator-skill.sh:36, install-worker-skill.sh:36,
  # install-graphify-skill.sh:77). So the scripts are called directly,
  # `ccrc-install.test.ts:1966-2047`'s 23-entry `_inst_*` spine is untouched, and no
  # agent restart is needed — every one of them is idempotent per home.
  local dir="$1" burl="$2" models="$3" n step stands=""
  # THIS RUN'S ARRAY, NOT THE SHELL'S (D-2127). It becomes `provisioned:[…]` on stdout
  # from this commit on, so a second call in one sourced shell that appended to the
  # first's answer would publish ten steps for a five-step run. Latent while `add`
  # runs once per process, and API the moment a caller loops — which is why it is
  # closed in the commit that publishes the array rather than the one that fills it.
  ACCT_PROVISIONED=()

  # WHAT STILL STANDS, BUILT ONCE AND USED FOUR TIMES — D-2128, which is D-2126's
  # ruling read the way it should have been the first time: the clause belongs to the
  # CALLER, and this function is the caller for all four refusal sites below — its own
  # three, and the one it hands `_acct_settings_env` as $3. Until this commit those
  # three said only "the roster entry was written" while the fourth, one line later in
  # the same run, also named the 0600 credential file: same state on disk, two accounts
  # of it, and the reasonable inference from the shorter one — that no credential was
  # written — is false. Every refusal below ends in this clause and adds nothing after
  # it, which is the property `ccrc-account.test.ts`'s `endsWith` rows measure.
  #
  # The roster entry is proved by control flow (`add-entry` and `_acct_converge` both
  # returned 0 above); the credential file by MEASUREMENT, `ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN` being
  # set only by the `mv` that landed it — so a login lane, which writes none, claims
  # none.
  stands="The roster entry was written and nothing rolls back${ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN:+, and so was the 0600 credential file $ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN that it names}, so 'ccrc account add' refuses this id as a duplicate now: fix the cause and run 'ccrc install', which converges this home."

  mkdir -p "$dir" \
    || _acct_refuse 1 provision-failed "cannot create the config directory $dir — the session hooks would have nowhere to be installed, and this lane's transcripts nowhere to live. $stands"
  _acct_settings_env "$dir" set "$stands" "$burl" "$models"
  # TWO TOKENS, BECAUSE THEY ARE TWO OUTCOMES (D-2127). `settings-env-none` says THIS VERB
  # WROTE NO BYTES, and it covers TWO on-disk states, both correct: a `login` lane, which
  # has no endpoint and no model map so the merge produces no file, and a home whose
  # settings.json already carries this block — reachable from `add`, measured, because a
  # config directory may pre-date the roster entry naming it. Reporting `settings-env` for
  # either would publish a write that did not happen; appending nothing would hide a step
  # that RAN. So the key's vocabulary carries both words and the array's length never changes.
  #
  # The four installer tokens below mean something weaker on purpose — "ran and
  # returned 0" — because each is a separate process converging a home and this verb
  # cannot see what it wrote. The merge is this verb's OWN write, so it can say which.
  if [ -n "$ACCT_SETTINGS_WRITTEN" ]; then
    ACCT_PROVISIONED+=(settings-env)
  else
    ACCT_PROVISIONED+=(settings-env-none)
  fi
  # THE INSTALLED COPIES under $HOME/.cc-sessions, never the tree's — `_inst_hooks`'
  # rule (:6799-6804): "the INSTALLED copy, never the one in the tree … It is also the
  # copy every future run, and every operator, will reach." COORDINATOR BEFORE WORKER:
  # the worker skill points at ../ccrc-coordinator/references/… (`_inst_skills`, :6841-6844).
  for n in install-session-hooks.sh install-coordinator-skill.sh \
           install-worker-skill.sh install-graphify-skill.sh; do
    [ -x "$HOME/.cc-sessions/$n" ] \
      || _acct_refuse 1 provision-failed "the installer $HOME/.cc-sessions/$n is missing or not executable, so this lane's home cannot be converged — and 'ccrc install' is what places it there. $stands"
    bash "$HOME/.cc-sessions/$n" --homes "$dir" \
      || _acct_refuse 1 provision-failed "the installer $n refused for $dir — read its lines above; it converges one home at a time and never rewrites a settings.json it could not validate, so nothing was left half-written. $stands"
    # PURE PARAMETER EXPANSION, no `sed`: this function makes no PATH lookup of its own,
    # `CCRC_HERE`'s argument (:834-841) — a broken-environment box is the box this runs on.
    step="${n#install-}"
    ACCT_PROVISIONED+=("${step%.sh}")
  done
}

# ── WHAT THIS VERB DOES NOT DO ────────────────────────────────────────────
# `cmd_expose`'s three printed root steps, and `_inst_linger`'s degraded-step
# doctrine, in the shape a verb whose caller is a PROGRAM needs: an array on
# stdout rather than lines an operator reads, so the PWA can render them beside
# the done step (spec §12.6). Named once because `declare` prints the same list
# (Task 27) — a lane connected two ways must not be told two different things.
#
# Both are OUT-OF-TREE plumbing: this repository does not own either, does not
# know their shape on any given box, and would be guessing if it edited them.
# `%s` is the id, filled by `_acct_operator_steps`.
#
# BESIDE THEIR ONE CONSUMER, NOT IN THE FILE-SCOPE CONSTANTS BLOCK, which is
# where this task's plan snippet put them — the same DEVIATION `JQ_ACCT_MANAGED`
# above argues, for the same measured reason. That block spells a value once
# because TWO sections read it; these two are read by `_acct_operator_steps`
# below and, from Task 27, by `declare` in this same section. The cost of the
# other placement is what settles it: inserting beside `ACCT_SECRETS_DIR` shifts
# 120 line citations in this tree against 44 here, and D-2053 named exactly that
# — an unrelated citation sweep carried inside an `add` diff — as what makes a
# diff unreviewable for the thing it is for.
ACCT_OPERATOR_STEP_1='claude-usage.timer: this box'"'"'s usage sweep enumerates the accounts it knows about, and it is not ccrc'"'"'s timer — add %s to it, or its usage will not be counted.'
ACCT_OPERATOR_STEP_2='claude-prune-versions: add %s'"'"'s config directory to its keep list, or the next prune will delete the Claude Code versions this lane runs on.'

# AN ARRAY, for `ACCT_PROVISIONED`'s reason and one more: each step is a
# SENTENCE with spaces in it, so a newline-delimited string read back with
# `read` would be one more place for a delimiter to be the wrong one.
ACCT_OPERATOR_STEPS=()
_acct_operator_steps() {   # <id> — appends the two steps
  # RESET AT ENTRY, `ACCT_PROVISIONED`'s reason and D-2127's second face: two
  # calls in one sourced shell would otherwise answer four steps for one lane.
  ACCT_OPERATOR_STEPS=()
  # shellcheck disable=SC2059  # the format string IS the constant, deliberately
  ACCT_OPERATOR_STEPS+=("$(printf "$ACCT_OPERATOR_STEP_1" "$1")")
  # shellcheck disable=SC2059
  ACCT_OPERATOR_STEPS+=("$(printf "$ACCT_OPERATOR_STEP_2" "$1")")
}

_acct_disable_new() {   # <id> <what-still-stands> — the marker `ccd`'s `_lane_enabled` reads
  # Spec §5: both connect verbs leave the new lane SWITCHED OFF, so a lane whose
  # credential nobody has measured cannot be picked by `_ws_least_loaded` the
  # moment it is rostered. This only uses ccd's existing mechanism:
  # `_lane_enabled() { [[ ! -f "$REG/$1-disabled" ]]; }` (ccd/ccd:1024) and
  # `_account_ok() { [[ -x "$WRAPPER_DIR/$1" ]] && _lane_enabled "$1"; }` (:1028).
  # `$_SVC_REG` is ccrc's `$REG` (:96, ccd/ccd:765), pinned by `macos-platform.test.ts`.
  #
  # NOT `homeAble: false`. That field declares what KIND of account this is; this
  # file is an operator switch. Collapsing them would make a measured, working lane
  # permanently unplaceable, and would turn the UI's Enable control into a roster write.
  #
  # $2 IS THE CALLER'S "WHAT STILL STANDS" CLAUSE (D-2135), `_acct_settings_env`'s $3
  # at a second address, and BOUND BELOW so `set -u` reds a caller that forgot it even
  # on the path where the write succeeds. Task 27's `declare` is that second caller.
  #
  # THE REMEDY IS `touch`, NOT the unshipped `ccrc account disable` (measured:
  # `unknown-subcommand`, exit 2). Task 24's `auth-start` names an unshipped door
  # because signing in has no hand form; this switch IS ccd:1024's own file.
  local id="$1" stands="$2"
  mkdir -p "$_SVC_REG" \
    || _acct_refuse 1 disable-marker "cannot create $_SVC_REG, so this lane cannot be left switched off — and a lane nobody has measured must not be pickable. Create that directory, then: touch $_SVC_REG/$id-disabled. $stands"
  : > "$_SVC_REG/$id-disabled" \
    || _acct_refuse 1 disable-marker "cannot write $_SVC_REG/$id-disabled, so this lane cannot be left switched off. Fix that path, then: touch $_SVC_REG/$id-disabled. $stands"
}


# ── THE ORDERED WRITE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Spec §5's order, and the two properties it buys.
#
# THE ROSTER ENTRY IS LAST OF THE THREE — secret, marker, entry (D-2134). A roster
# entry naming a `secretsFile` that does not exist is a lane every reader believes
# in and nothing can run; a 0600 file no roster names is inert — invisible to
# `_lane_enabled`, `_account_ok` and `cmd_wrappers` alike — and a retry overwrites
# it byte for byte. THE MARKER IS INERT BY THAT SAME TEST and weaker still: all it
# can do is make a lane more off. It goes in front of `add-entry` because
# `_account_ok` takes ANY executable at `$WRAPPER_DIR/<id>`, not only one ccrc wrote,
# while `check-add` measures `duplicate-id` and `suffix-collision` against the ROSTER
# and never the filesystem — so those two still fire before all three writes (no
# leftover is ever another lane's), and the only thing then left between the entry
# and a pickable lane is the `accounts.sh` regeneration any concurrent converge
# supplies. The costs are asymmetric: one inert file against unmeasured work.
#
# NOTHING AFTER THE ROSTER ENTRY ROLLS BACK. `_inst_accounts_sh`, `cmd_wrappers`, the
# config dir and the four installers are roster-driven CONVERGERS — `ccrc install`'s
# own spine — so a failure among them leaves a lane rostered and not yet converged,
# and the cure is to converge again. Un-writing an operator's roster entry to tidy up
# a failed chmod would be the destructive act, on the one file this CLI calls theirs.
ACCT_BASE_URL_RESOLVED=""; ACCT_MODELS_RESOLVED="{}"; ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN=""
_acct_add() {
  _acct_add_parse "$@"

  # The check is side-effect-free and its `plan` is what every later step
  # writes from, so no step re-derives a decision this one already made. It runs
  # BEFORE `_acct_read_credential`, and that order is the point rather than an
  # accident: the credential is the one value in this run that must never be
  # read for a request the box was going to refuse anyway.
  #
  # `${VAR:+--flag "$VAR"}` — measured: bash honours the quotes INSIDE the
  # alternate value, so `--base-url` and its value arrive as exactly two words
  # even when the value contains a space, and nothing at all when the variable
  # is empty.
  local plan rc=0
  plan="$(_acct_node check-add \
    --file "$(_acct_roster_path)" --id "$ACCT_ID" --provider "$ACCT_PROVIDER" \
    --label "$ACCT_LABEL" --hue "$ACCT_HUE" --suffix "$ACCT_SUFFIX" \
    ${ACCT_BASE_URL:+--base-url "$ACCT_BASE_URL"} \
    ${ACCT_MODELS:+--models "$ACCT_MODELS"} \
    ${ACCT_METHOD:+--method "$ACCT_METHOD"})" || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    # check-add ALREADY PRINTED the envelope on ITS stdout — which this command
    # substitution captured, unless it printed NOTHING: the half-updated box, not
    # a refusal (D-2051). A body is re-emitted verbatim; one refusal, one owner.
    [ -n "$plan" ] || _acct_no_answer "$rc" check-add "Nothing was written."
    printf '%s\n' "$plan"
    exit "$rc"
  fi

  # ── THE FOUR FIELDS BASH NEEDS OFF THE PLAN, IN ONE FORK ─────────────────
  # Read with node, not with a shell regex over JSON — `ccrc-doctor-checks`
  # already reads JSON this way at :330, :2253 and :3209, and says so at
  # :1177 ("_check_wrappers already reads a JSON file with `node -e` rather
  # than …"). The plan travels in an ENV VAR, which is that idiom's own
  # spelling (`CCRC_DOCTOR_ROSTER="$roster" node -e '…'`, :2253) and carries no
  # secret: it is a path, an env-var NAME, a URL and a model map.
  #
  # NUL-SEPARATED, NOT LINE-SEPARATED. The hazard it was written for is now
  # closed one layer up and the honest statement of that is worth more than the
  # scarier version: `new URL()` strips a raw newline while parsing, so
  # `https://orchard-api/v1<LF>x` PASSES `BASE_URL_OK`, and because Task 23
  # stores the verdict's normalised `url` rather than the operator's bytes, what
  # reaches this reader is `https://orchard-api/v1x` — measured 2026-09-07,
  # node 22. A line reader would have taken `x` as the next field back when the
  # raw string was stored. The NUL separator stays because it costs one
  # character and does not depend on that argument holding: it is the reader
  # that cannot be fooled by ANY field, including a `models` map or a future
  # fifth field nobody has thought about yet.
  # `read -r -d ''` cannot be fooled that way, and `< <(…)` rather than a pipe
  # so the loop runs in THIS shell and the assignments survive it — the same
  # reason `cmd_wrappers` uses `mapfile -t … < <(…)` at :2601.
  #
  # `secretsfile` IS READ AND NOT YET USED, said out loud rather than left for a
  # reader to notice: what the `add-entry` refusal below names is
  # `ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN`, a MEASUREMENT, and Task 26's answer needs this one.
  local secretsfile="" envvar="" nfield=0 fld
  ACCT_BASE_URL_RESOLVED=""; ACCT_MODELS_RESOLVED="{}"; ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN=""
  while IFS= read -r -d '' fld; do
    case "$nfield" in
      0) secretsfile="$fld" ;;
      1) envvar="$fld" ;;
      2) ACCT_BASE_URL_RESOLVED="$fld" ;;
      3) ACCT_MODELS_RESOLVED="$fld" ;;
    esac
    nfield=$((nfield + 1))
  done < <(ACCT_PLAN="$plan" node -e '
    const p = JSON.parse(process.env.ACCT_PLAN).plan;
    process.stdout.write([p.secretsFile ?? "", p.envVar ?? "", p.baseUrl ?? "",
      JSON.stringify(p.models ?? {})].map((x) => x + "\0").join(""));
  ')
  [ "$nfield" -eq 4 ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 internal-plan "could not read the checked plan back — this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box, and nothing was written"

  # ── 1. THE SECRET, for a token lane only ────────────────────────────────
  # THE REMEDY BELOW NAMES A SUBCOMMAND WAVE 1 DOES NOT SHIP, deliberately and
  # stated so nobody "fixes" it into something else: `auth-start` is §5's own
  # name for the sign-in door and lands with wave 2's routes, which is when a
  # login lane can be driven from the PWA at all. On a wave-1 box the same act
  # is `ccd-account-auth <id> login` (Tasks 51-53) or, for a pane method,
  # `ccd account-pane --id <id> --method setup-token` (Task 50) — both of which
  # ARE on the box. The sentence is written for the finished system because a
  # remedy the operator reads a month from now should name the verb they have.
  if [ -n "$envvar" ]; then
    [ -n "$ACCT_CRED_SPELLING" ] \
      || _acct_refuse 2 credential-required "provider \"$ACCT_PROVIDER\" with method \"${ACCT_METHOD:-paste}\" is a token lane and needs the key: pass --credential - and pipe it in. Nothing was written."
    _acct_read_credential "$ACCT_CRED_SPELLING"
    _acct_write_secret "$ACCT_ID" "$ACCT_PROVIDER" "$envvar"
  else
    [ -z "$ACCT_CRED_SPELLING" ] \
      || _acct_refuse 2 credential-not-managed "provider \"$ACCT_PROVIDER\" with method \"${ACCT_METHOD:-login}\" keeps its credential in the config directory Claude Code writes, so there is no file for --credential to fill. Run 'ccrc account auth-start --id $ACCT_ID --method login' after this. Nothing was written."
  fi

  # ── 2. THE SWITCH, OFF ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Before the roster entry, not after it (D-2134) — THE ORDERED WRITE above carries
  # the argument. The CLAUSE is this caller's: at this line only the 0600 file exists.
  local added rc2=0 stands="Nothing was written."
  [ -n "$ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN" ] && stands="The 0600 credential file $ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN WAS written before this step and nothing rolls it back: no roster entry names it yet, so nothing on this box can run it, and re-running this same command overwrites it."
  _acct_disable_new "$ACCT_ID" "$stands"

  # ── 3. THE ROSTER ENTRY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # CAPTURED, NOT DISCARDED. `>/dev/null` would throw away the refusal envelope
  # as well as the success body, leaving `roster-write` a bare exit code — the
  # empty seam this verb exists to close. On success the body is dropped (Task
  # 26's `added` op composes the answer from the file); on failure it is re-emitted
  # verbatim. AN EMPTY capture is NEITHER (D-2051), and here alone the clause may
  # not say "Nothing was written" — since D-2134 the marker is on disk on EVERY lane
  # by now, so it is named here beside the credential rather than left to be found.
  local wrote="$stands The kill-switch marker $_SVC_REG/$ACCT_ID-disabled was written before this step, for the reason THE ORDERED WRITE gives, and is inert for the reason the credential file is: no roster entry names that id, so it switches nothing off, and re-running this same command rewrites it."
  added="$(_acct_node add-entry --file "$(_acct_roster_path)" --plan "$plan")" || rc2=$?
  if [ "$rc2" -ne 0 ]; then
    [ -n "$added" ] || _acct_no_answer "$rc2" add-entry "$wrote"
    printf '%s\n' "$added"
    exit "$rc2"
  fi

  # ── 4. THE PROJECTION, and 5. THE WRAPPERS ──────────────────────────────
  _acct_converge

  # ── 6. THE NEW HOME ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # NOT IN A COMMAND SUBSTITUTION — `ACCT_PROVISIONED`'s own note above says
  # why. Both values are the CHECKED plan's, read once by the NUL reader above:
  # `ACCT_BASE_URL_RESOLVED` is the plan's `baseUrl`, which is null for a login
  # lane and therefore the empty string here — exactly the value `JQ_ACCT_SET`
  # treats as "no endpoint key" — and `ACCT_MODELS_RESOLVED` is the plan's
  # validated `models` object, or `{}`, which is what `--argjson` needs.
  _acct_provision "$HOME/$ACCT_SUFFIX" "$ACCT_BASE_URL_RESOLVED" "$ACCT_MODELS_RESOLVED"
  _acct_operator_steps "$ACCT_ID"

  # ── 7. THE ANSWER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # `${arr[@]+"${arr[@]}"}` — this tree's empty-array idiom under `set -u`
  # (`cmd_wrappers` at :2519 for `protected`, and `_acct_candidates` at :4060,
  # which hands an array to this same seam). It keeps each element ONE word: the
  # quotes inside the alternate value are honoured, so a step sentence with
  # spaces does not arrive as five `--operator-step`s.
  local -a ans=(--file "$(_acct_roster_path)" --id "$ACCT_ID" --disabled true)
  local s=""
  for s in ${ACCT_PROVISIONED[@]+"${ACCT_PROVISIONED[@]}"}; do ans+=(--provisioned "$s"); done
  for s in ${ACCT_OPERATOR_STEPS[@]+"${ACCT_OPERATOR_STEPS[@]}"}; do ans+=(--operator-step "$s"); done

  # THE THIRD RE-EMIT PATH, AND `_acct_answer` CANNOT BE IT — a DEVIATION from
  # this task's plan snippet (`_acct_node added … || exit $?`) and from the line
  # it replaces, argued rather than transcribed. `_acct_answer` is right for a
  # call that has written nothing, and it hard-codes exactly that clause:
  # `_acct_no_answer "$rc" "$1" "Nothing was written."`. By the time this op
  # runs, the credential file, the roster entry, `accounts.sh`, the wrapper,
  # this OFF marker and the provisioned home all stand — so the shipped
  # `_acct_answer roster` on this line was already telling a completed run that
  # nothing was written, which is D-2051's own defect on the one path that task
  # did not reach. Captured here with the clause this site owns, exactly as the
  # `add-entry` call above does and for the identical reason: the triage is
  # shared, the sentence about what was written is the caller's.
  #
  # Bare `_acct_node` will not do either — a half-updated box (one whose
  # `account-op.mjs` has no `added` op) answers exit 2 with an EMPTY body, and
  # that is the seam this whole cluster exists to close.
  local body="" rc3=0
  body="$(_acct_node added "${ans[@]}")" || rc3=$?
  [ -n "$body" ] \
    || _acct_no_answer "$rc3" added "This lane is fully written and nothing rolls back: the roster entry, \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh, the wrapper, the config directory ${HOME}/${ACCT_SUFFIX}${ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN:+, the 0600 credential file $ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN} and the $_SVC_REG/$ACCT_ID-disabled marker that keeps it switched off all stand. Only this report failed: run 'ccrc account roster' to read the entry back."
  printf '%s\n' "$body"
  [ "$rc3" -eq 0 ] || exit "$rc3"
}

# ── THE HELPERS EVERY SUBCOMMAND AFTER `add` SHARES ───────────────────────
# NONE OF THESE MAY BE CALLED INSIDE $( ). `_acct_refuse` ends in `exit`, and an
# `exit` inside a command substitution kills only that subshell: the caller would
# carry on with an empty string while a JSON refusal envelope sat unprinted
# inside a variable nobody prints. Everything here answers in a NAMED GLOBAL for
# that reason — `ACCT_OUT`, `ACCT_ROSTER_IDS`, `ACCT_ALIAS_OF` — and the one
# function that DOES run a substitution runs it on `node`, which cannot exit
# this shell.
#
# WHY THREE FUNCTIONS AND NOT THE TWO THIS TASK'S PLAN NAMED. A node op that
# WRITES is silent on success; one that READS is not. So "the captured body was
# empty" is a REFUSAL for the second and the NORMAL ANSWER for the first — two
# conditions a caller handles differently, which must not share one guard.
# `_acct_run_op` carries the half they agree on and each wrapper adds its own
# half. One function with a "silent" flag would put the distinction on a boolean
# argument; two functions each capturing would give one refusal two authors.
ACCT_OUT=""
_acct_run_op() {   # <what-still-stands> <op> [--<key> <value>]… — the shared half
  # ONE REFUSAL, ONE OWNER, ONE SET OF BYTES. On a non-zero exit the op has
  # ALREADY written its envelope to the stdout this captured, so it is re-emitted
  # verbatim and the verb leaves with node's own class — `_acct_add`'s handling of
  # `check-add` and `add-entry`, lifted because five more callers need it.
  # Re-wording it here would give one refusal two authors.
  #
  # AN EMPTY BODY IS `_acct_no_answer`'S, NOT A SECOND CODE OF ITS OWN, and that
  # is a DEVIATION from this task's plan snippet — which spelled a new
  # `helper-silent` refusal at the CALLEE's exit class. That would have
  # reintroduced all three of D-2051's defects inside the very function written
  # to prevent them: the triage (half-updated box / killed process / unknown)
  # is lost, the class becomes node's 2 where this file's table (:24-33) says 1,
  # and a third vocabulary appears for a condition that already has an owner.
  #
  # `$1` IS THE CALLER'S CLAUSE, for the reason `_acct_no_answer` takes one
  # (D-2126, D-2135): what still stands differs by call site — nothing at all at
  # `declare`'s first write, a kill-switch marker at its second — and one helper
  # ending every path with one sentence would flatten two true statements into
  # one false one. Bound below, so `set -u` reds a caller that forgot it.
  local stands="$1" op="$2" rc=0
  shift
  ACCT_OUT="$(_acct_node "$@")" || rc=$?
  [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && return 0
  [ -n "$ACCT_OUT" ] || _acct_no_answer "$rc" "$op" "$stands"
  printf '%s\n' "$ACCT_OUT"
  exit "$rc"
}

_acct_read_op() {   # <what-still-stands> <op> … — the ANSWER lands in ACCT_OUT
  # A READ OP THAT EXITED 0 AND PRINTED NOTHING HAS ANSWERED NOTHING, and there
  # is no exit code to read that by — which is exactly the third branch of
  # `_acct_no_answer`'s own triage ("nothing in this tree is known to exit $rc
  # with an empty body"), so `0` is passed to it rather than translated away.
  _acct_run_op "$@"
  [ -n "$ACCT_OUT" ] || _acct_no_answer 0 "$2" "$1"
}

_acct_write_op() {   # <what-still-stands> <op> … — a node op silent on success
  # THE MIRROR CONDITION, AND IT IS NOT `no-answer`. `no-answer` is an empty body
  # where one was owed; this is a body where none was. Same measurement, opposite
  # directions, two codes — collapsing them would be the overloaded seam this
  # cluster keeps closing, because the operator's next move differs: `no-answer`
  # is a box to re-install, and this is a build to fix. The class is 1, this
  # file's "the tool ran and the answer was bad" (:24-33), because a write op
  # that printed would have handed the caller two JSON objects where its whole
  # contract is one.
  _acct_run_op "$@"
  [ -z "$ACCT_OUT" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 helper-noisy "the account helper printed on a path that must be silent, so this verb would have handed back two JSON objects where its contract is one. This is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box: ccrc and deploy/account-op.mjs ship together and must be one build. $1"
}

# THE ONE ID GATE. Task 23 spelled THREE refusals inline inside `_acct_add_parse`
# — `missing-value`, `bad-id`, `reserved-id`, measured against that function and
# not the two this task's own prose recalls. `declare`, `credential`, `check`,
# `enable`, `disable` and `remove` all need the same three, and a seventh copy of
# a refusal sentence in bash is drift nothing in this tree can see:
# `server/test/single-definition.test.ts` scans four TypeScript roots (its own
# `:34-37`) and filters `/\.tsx?$/` (its `:54`), so `ccd/` is invisible to it.
#
# THE GATE GOES ON THE ID, BEFORE ANY PATH IS BUILT FROM IT — `cmd_wrappers`'
# rule (:2504-2510). The id is about to become a filename under
# $HOME/.local/bin, a path segment under $HOME/.cc-secrets and a bash `case`
# pattern, and a check on the resulting PATH would have to enumerate the ways a
# path can escape.
_acct_id_or_refuse() {   # <id> — refuses, or returns 0
  # `${1:-}`, not `$1`: this runs under `set -u` and a caller may legitimately
  # have parsed no --id at all, which is the first refusal below and not a crash.
  local id="${1:-}"
  _acct_shape
  [ -n "$id" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "an account id is required: pass --id. Nothing was written."
  [[ "$id" =~ $WRAPPER_ID_RE ]] \
    || _acct_refuse 2 bad-id "\"$id\" is not a legal account id: lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter, at most 32 characters. It becomes a filename under \$HOME/.local/bin. Nothing was written."
  # §4.4. `cc-auth-<id>` is the auth pane's tmux session name, and for the id
  # "auth" that is `cc-auth-auth` — indistinguishable from the pane of a session
  # running on a wrapper called `auth`.
  [ "$id" != auth ] \
    || _acct_refuse 2 reserved-id "\"auth\" is reserved: the auth pane's tmux session is named cc-auth-<id>, which for this id would collide with the pane of a session running on it. Pick another id. Nothing was written."
}

# THE ONE SUFFIX-SAFETY GATE, SHARED (review round 2, D-2143). It was spelled
# inline in `_acct_add_parse` until `declare` needed the same rule, and it is
# lifted here rather than copied for `_acct_id_or_refuse`'s reason: a second
# spelling of a refusal SENTENCE in bash is drift nothing in this tree can see,
# because `server/test/single-definition.test.ts` filters `/\.tsx?$/` and never
# reads `ccd/`.
#
# THE READ-ROOT GATE IS NOT PART OF IT. `add`'s `suffix-outside-read-root` stays
# at its own call site: the two verbs deliberately disagree there (D-2142), and
# folding a rule only one of them holds into a shared helper would need a
# boolean argument — which is how a shared gate becomes two gates wearing one
# name.
#
# `_acct_shape` HERE, NOT ONLY IN THE CALLER, and `${1:-}` beside it: this reads
# `$WRAPPER_SUFFIX_SAFE_RE` off the contract, and under `set -uo pipefail` a
# caller that had not sourced it would get `unbound variable` on stderr and NO
# JSON on stdout — the one thing this verb's contract forbids. Its sibling above
# was already self-sufficient; the two are now the same shape.
_acct_suffix_or_refuse() {   # <suffix> — refuses, or returns 0
  local suffix="${1:-}"
  _acct_shape
  # The same gate `shared/roster.ts` and `shared/wrapper.mjs` keep their own
  # copies of, sourced rather than re-spelled (`ccrc-wrapper-shape:73`,
  # `^\.[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$`). "." and ".." pass a character-class test and resolve
  # to $HOME and its parent.
  { [ "$suffix" != "." ] && [ "$suffix" != ".." ] \
    && [[ "$suffix" =~ $WRAPPER_SUFFIX_SAFE_RE ]]; } \
    || _acct_refuse 2 bad-suffix "config directory \"$suffix\" is not a safe single directory name under \$HOME: letters, digits, \".\", \"-\" and \"_\" only, dot-prefixed, and never exactly \".\" or \"..\". Nothing was written."
}

# THE ROSTERED IDS, from a SUBSHELL source of the projection — `_inst_dirs`' rule
# and `_acct_candidates`' shape, one function up: `~/.ccrc/accounts.sh` declares
# `_ccrc_cfg_dir`, `_ccrc_label` and `_ccrc_hue` in the SAME `_ccrc_*` namespace
# as `_ccrc_die`, so sourcing it in THIS shell would let a generator change
# redefine a refusal helper mid-verb. Only the ids cross back.
#
# `projection-*`, NOT `roster-*` (D-1923), and this function is one of the two
# sites that ruling names. Two files, two remedies: this one is `ccrc install`'s
# GENERATED projection of `~/.ccrc/accounts.json`, and its fix is to regenerate
# it, while `readRoster`'s three `roster-*` codes are about the JSON an operator
# wrote by hand and their fix is to edit it. The pair (subcommand, code) does not
# rescue the overload here, which is what makes it a defect rather than a
# wrinkle: `declare` reads BOTH files in ONE subcommand.
ACCT_ROSTER_IDS=()
_acct_roster_ids() {   # -> fills ACCT_ROSTER_IDS
  local sh="$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh" listed a
  ACCT_ROSTER_IDS=()
  [ -f "$sh" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 projection-absent "$sh does not exist, so this box cannot say which accounts are already declared — run 'ccrc install' to generate it from \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json. Nothing was written."
  listed="$(
    # shellcheck source=/dev/null
    . "$sh" || exit 1
    for a in ${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[@]+"${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[@]}"}; do printf '%s\n' "$a"; done
  )" || _acct_refuse 1 projection-invalid "$sh could not be sourced, so this box cannot say which accounts are already declared. Nothing was written."
  while IFS= read -r a; do [ -n "$a" ] && ACCT_ROSTER_IDS+=("$a"); done <<<"$listed"
}

# `_inst_accounts_sh` PRINTS ("install: accounts.sh: …") and refuses through
# `_ccrc_die`, which writes nothing to stdout. Both are wrong for a verb whose
# entire stdout is one JSON object, and neither can be fixed in that function —
# it is `cmd_install`'s own step and `ccrc-install.test.ts` pins the spine that
# calls it. So: stdout to /dev/null, and the call in a SUBSHELL so `_ccrc_die`'s
# `exit 1` ends the subshell rather than this verb, leaving the `||` to phrase
# the refusal as JSON. stderr is NOT redirected — the generator's own
# `gen-accounts: remedy: …` line is the whole error path for a roster an operator
# broke by hand, and re-wording a fix into a shrug helps nobody.
#
# `$1` IS THE CALLER'S "WHAT STILL STANDS" CLAUSE, and it is a parameter from its
# first line rather than a sentence baked in for the first caller. That is
# D-2135's standing rule — a helper whose sentence mentions what else happened is
# taking a parameter, whether or not it has one yet — and this helper is written
# for six callers of which one exists: after `declare` a roster entry and a
# kill-switch marker stand, after `remove` an entry has just gone, and one shared
# sentence would be false for one of them.
_acct_projection() {   # <what-still-stands>
  ( _inst_accounts_sh >/dev/null ) \
    || _acct_refuse 1 projection-failed "\$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh could not be regenerated from the roster, so ccd does not know about this change yet — read the gen-accounts: line above, then run: ccrc install. $1"
}

# ── declare — adopt a launcher ccrc did not write, and never writes ───────
# TWO OF THE DOCTOR'S FIVE CANDIDATE PREDICATES, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
# `ccrc-doctor-checks:2388-2401` builds `wr_cands` from `[ -f ]`, `[ -x ]`, the
# id shape, `_wrap_is_script` and `_wrap_declares_config_dir`. The last two must
# NOT be applied here, and that file says why in its own words at :2427-2432: an
# EXTERNAL account is excluded from the shape comparison "because its shape is
# nobody's business: it may be compiled, and it may spell its config dir in a way
# no `export` line matches" — a rule that once FAILED a box whose external
# launcher was perfectly good. A `declare` that copied all five would refuse
# exactly the class of launcher it exists for.
#
# What it keeps is the id shape (a launcher's FILENAME is its account id — ccd
# execs `$HOME/.local/bin/<id>` directly) and the `-ef` alias collapse, which is
# load-bearing rather than tidy: `gpt -> ccgpt` is ONE file under two names on
# this fleet (`ccrc-doctor-checks:2403-2408`), and declaring both would put two
# accounts on one CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. `-ef` is a bash builtin comparing
# device+inode THROUGH the symlink, so it needs no `readlink` and it catches a
# hard link too.
#
# SIX CODES, AND THE LAST TWO ARE THE SAME RULE THE FIRST FOUR ARE (D-2144).
# `launcher-absent` and `launcher-not-a-file` are two conditions an operator acts
# on differently (put a launcher there / look at what is there), and so are
# `launcher-not-executable` (chmod +x) and `launcher-alias` (pick a different id,
# or declare the one that already exists). Collapsing any pair is the overloaded
# seam CLAUDE.md forbids — and review round 2 measured two pairs that WERE
# collapsed, by the four codes written to prevent exactly that:
#
#  - `launcher-dangling`. `[ -e ]` FOLLOWS a symlink, so a link whose target is
#    gone read as `launcher-absent`, whose remedy is "put the launcher there
#    first" — and `ln -s` then fails EEXIST, because something IS at that path.
#    What that operator needs is `ls -ld`, which only `launcher-not-a-file`
#    offered. Two conditions, two remedies, one code: the seam itself.
#  - `launcher-empty`. A zero-byte 0755 file was DECLARED — exit 0, marker
#    written, and `ccd` would exec it. Decision 22(c)'s "its shape is nobody's
#    business" defends a COMPILED launcher that spells its config dir in a way no
#    `export` matches; it does not describe zero bytes, which cannot be any
#    launcher at all. `[ -s ]` is a fifth code and not a shape judgement: it
#    reintroduces neither `_wrap_is_script` nor `_wrap_declares_config_dir`, and
#    it asks nothing about what the bytes SAY — only whether there are any.
#
# `-s` COMES BEFORE `-x`, DELIBERATELY. A zero-byte file that is also not
# executable is both, and `chmod +x` is the wrong next act for it: the file has
# no content to run either way, so telling the operator to fix its mode sends
# them to fix the wrong thing — which is this entry's own argument about the
# dangling link, applied to the pair beside it.
#
# NO SELF-SKIP, unlike `_acct_candidates`' loop, and the difference is that this
# function is asked about an id that is NOT supposed to be rostered yet: the
# duplicate-id gate in `_acct_declare` has already refused every rostered id
# before this runs, so a name reaching here can only match a DIFFERENT account's
# launcher. It is also a second guard over the same door — with the duplicate-id
# gate mutated out, a rostered id still refuses here, because a launcher is
# always `-ef` itself.
ACCT_ALIAS_OF=""
_acct_declarable() {   # <id> — refuses, or returns 0 with ACCT_ALIAS_OF empty
  # `_acct_shape` HERE AND NOT ONLY IN THE CALLER (review round 2). This
  # function reads `$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR`, which the shape contract declares, and
  # under `set -uo pipefail` a caller that had not sourced it got `unbound
  # variable` on stderr and NO JSON on stdout — the one thing this verb's
  # contract forbids, reached by nothing but an oversight: `_acct_id_or_refuse`,
  # its sibling one screen up, has always sourced its own contract. `${1:-}` and
  # the empty `f`/`other` are the same rule for the same reason — a hand call
  # with no argument is a refusal, not a crash.
  local id="${1:-}" f="" other=""
  ACCT_ALIAS_OF=""
  _acct_shape
  f="$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id"
  # THE ONLY TEST IN THIS FUNCTION THAT DOES NOT FOLLOW THE LINK, so it has to
  # come first: every predicate below resolves the target, and a dangling link
  # answers all of them as though nothing were there at all.
  { [ -L "$f" ] && [ ! -e "$f" ]; } \
    && _acct_refuse 1 launcher-dangling "the path $f is a symlink and its target does not exist, so there is no launcher to declare and nothing can be put at that path while the link is there — 'ln -s' would fail with EEXIST. Read it with: ls -ld $f — then repoint it at the real launcher, or remove it. Nothing was written."
  [ -e "$f" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 launcher-absent "there is no $f on this box, so there is no launcher to declare — put the launcher there first, or use 'ccrc account add' to have ccrc write one. Nothing was written."
  [ -f "$f" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 launcher-not-a-file "the path $f exists and is not a regular file, and ccd execs it directly — look at what is there: ls -ld $f. Nothing was written."
  [ -s "$f" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 launcher-empty "the launcher $f is zero bytes long, and ccd execs it directly — an empty file is no launcher at all, compiled or scripted, so declaring it would roster a lane whose every session exits at once. Put the real launcher there, then re-run. Nothing was written."
  [ -x "$f" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 launcher-not-executable "the launcher $f is not executable, and ccd runs it directly — chmod +x $f, then re-run. Nothing was written."
  for other in ${ACCT_ROSTER_IDS[@]+"${ACCT_ROSTER_IDS[@]}"}; do
    [ -e "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$other" ] || continue
    if [ "$f" -ef "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$other" ]; then ACCT_ALIAS_OF="$other"; break; fi
  done
  [ -z "$ACCT_ALIAS_OF" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 launcher-alias "the launcher $f is the same file as $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$ACCT_ALIAS_OF, which is already rostered as account $ACCT_ALIAS_OF — two accounts sharing one launcher would fight over one CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. Nothing was written."
}

# THE FLAG NAME IS TAKEN BEFORE THE SHIFT, which is why `flag="${1%%=*}"` is the
# first line INSIDE the loop and not inside either `case`. Both spellings have to
# land in one place: `--id=X` is one token whose name is `${1%%=*}`, and `--id X`
# is two tokens whose first arm runs `shift` to consume the value — so a second
# `case` that re-read `$1` would be matching against `X` for the two-token form,
# every arm would miss, and the value would be silently dropped while the `=`
# spelling kept working. This is `_acct_add_parse`'s shape, repeated rather than
# factored out because the two parsers accept different flag sets and a shared
# one would have to be told which.
#
# ── THE ORDERED WRITE, AND WHY THE MARKER IS FIRST ────────────────────────
# Identity, then the pre-pass, then disk, then the switch, then the roster, then
# the projection. The pre-pass (`check-declare`, D-2143) is the step this order
# gained in review round 2, and it goes where it does because everything before
# it is decidable without forking node and everything after it writes.
#
# THE MARKER GOES IN FRONT OF THE ROSTER ENTRY (D-2134's ruling, applied here).
# The WEAKEST TRUE version of the hazard, because an entry that overstates its
# mechanism invites its own reversal: a roster entry alone does NOT hand this
# launcher to `_ws_least_loaded`. That loop iterates `CCRC_HOME_ABLE`
# (ccd/ccd:3641), a declared entry is `homeAble: false` and never enters it, and
# `~/.ccrc/accounts.sh` has not been regenerated at that point either. The path
# that CAN reach a declared lane is `_swap_target`'s pool, which admits a
# non-home-able account when a session's registry `home` field already names that
# id (`_default_pool`, ccd/ccd:11042-11046) — so the window needs the projection
# regenerated by a concurrent converge AND a session already homed on this exact
# id. Narrower than `add`'s, and the ordering is not decided on its width: the
# COSTS ARE ASYMMETRIC. Marker-too-early leaves one file that disables nothing —
# no roster entry names the id, and the retry rewrites it — while marker-too-late
# hands real work to a lane nobody has probed. Order for the expensive one.
#
# `_acct_declarable` IS STILL BEFORE THE MARKER, and that is the same rule read
# forwards: a refusal an operator can act on must not leave a file behind, and
# all six launcher refusals are that kind. So is every refusal `check-declare`
# can reach, which is what that op was added for — before this task the field
# faults were the one class this verb answered with a file already on disk.
_acct_declare() {
  local id="" provider="" baseurl="" label="" hue="" suffix="" v="" flag="" other=""
  _acct_shape
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    flag="${1%%=*}"
    case "$1" in
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      --id|--provider|--base-url|--label|--hue|--suffix)
        [ $# -ge 2 ] \
          || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "the flag $1 takes a value and nothing followed it on the command line. Nothing was written."
        v="$2"; shift ;;
      --id=*|--provider=*|--base-url=*|--label=*|--hue=*|--suffix=*)
        v="${1#*=}" ;;
      *) _acct_refuse 2 unknown-argument "ccrc account declare has no argument \"$1\"" ;;
    esac
    # A FLAG GIVEN AN EMPTY VALUE IS NOT A FLAG NOBODY GAVE (D-2144). The three
    # optional flags reach node through `${x:+…}`, which drops an empty value
    # SILENTLY — so `ccrc account declare --provider ''` used to exit 0 having
    # rostered a lane with no provider and said nothing about it, while `add`
    # answers `missing-value` at exit 2 for the same bytes. Flag-absent and
    # flag-present-but-empty are two conditions a caller handles differently and
    # must not collapse to one value.
    #
    # THE GATE IS HERE, ON `$v`, and not six times below: both spellings of
    # every flag land in `$v` exactly once, which is the same property the
    # `flag="${1%%=*}"` line above exists for. It also puts `--id ''`,
    # `--label ''` and `--hue ''` on this sentence rather than on the three
    # "a value is required" ones downstream — same code, same class, and one
    # answer for one mistake instead of four.
    [ -n "$v" ] \
      || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "the flag $flag was given an empty value. A flag present with nothing in it is not the same as a flag nobody passed, so it is refused rather than dropped: give it a value, or leave it off. Nothing was written."
    case "$flag" in
      --id)       id="$v" ;;
      --provider) provider="$v" ;;
      --base-url) baseurl="$v" ;;
      --label)    label="$v" ;;
      --hue)      hue="$v" ;;
      --suffix)   suffix="$v" ;;
    esac
    shift
  done

  # IDENTITY FIRST, DISK SECOND. A duplicate id answered `launcher-absent` in the
  # first draft, which sends the operator to install a launcher for an account
  # that already exists. And since D-2134 this guard has a SECOND dependent
  # (D-2137): under the new order a `duplicate-id` that did not fire would let
  # the kill-switch marker be written for a LIVE lane. It is not the only thing
  # standing there — `_acct_declarable` refuses a rostered id again, because a
  # launcher is always `-ef` itself — but it is the one that names the condition.
  _acct_id_or_refuse "$id"
  [ -n "$label" ] || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "declare needs a human label: pass --label. The roster carries one for every account. Nothing was written."
  [ -n "$hue" ]   || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "declare needs a hue: pass --hue. The roster carries one for every account. Nothing was written."
  # ON A SUFFIX THE OPERATOR GAVE, AND ONLY THEN. The DEFAULT is `declaredEntry`'s
  # `.<id>` and is spelled once, in node: it is derived from an id
  # `_acct_id_or_refuse` has already measured against `WRAPPER_ID_RE`, so it is a
  # safe one-segment name by construction and there is nothing here for a gate to
  # find. That is what spares `declare` the second spelling `add` cannot avoid —
  # `_acct_add_parse` must materialise `.claude-$ACCT_ID` before its own two
  # gates can measure it, and `check-add` must default it again for a hand caller.
  [ -z "$suffix" ] || _acct_suffix_or_refuse "$suffix"
  _acct_roster_ids
  for other in ${ACCT_ROSTER_IDS[@]+"${ACCT_ROSTER_IDS[@]}"}; do
    [ "$other" = "$id" ] \
      && _acct_refuse 1 duplicate-id "the id $id is already an account in \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json — 'ccrc account roster' lists what is there. Nothing was written."
  done

  # ── 0. THE PRE-PASS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # SIDE-EFFECT-FREE, AND BEFORE EVERY WRITE THIS VERB MAKES (D-2143). It judges
  # the proposed entry — hue, label, provider, the config directory and whatever
  # else `rosterFromJson` decides — while nothing at all is on disk, which is
  # what `add` has had since Task 23 and `declare` did not: under D-2134's order
  # the kill-switch marker lands BEFORE the roster entry, so the writer that was
  # once "its own only judge" now judges with a file already written.
  #
  # BEFORE `_acct_declarable`, NOT AFTER, and that is this file's standing rule
  # rather than a preference: identity first, disk second (the `duplicate-id`
  # gate above says it in those words, and `_acct_id_or_refuse` is placed by
  # it). An unknown hue is decidable from argv against a constant set — class 2
  # — and answering a class-1 fact about the filesystem first would tell an
  # operator to go and install a launcher for a request this box was never
  # going to accept.
  #
  # `_acct_read_op`, NOT `_acct_write_op`: this op ANSWERS (the entry it
  # judged), so an empty body is `no-answer` and not success. The clause is the
  # honest one at this line and the shortest in the verb.
  _acct_read_op "Nothing was written." check-declare --file "$(_acct_roster_path)" \
    --id "$id" --label "$label" --hue "$hue" \
    ${suffix:+--suffix "$suffix"} \
    ${provider:+--provider "$provider"} \
    ${baseurl:+--base-url "$baseurl"}

  _acct_declarable "$id"

  # ── 1. THE SWITCH, OFF ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The clause is this caller's and it is the honest one at THIS line: nothing
  # has been written yet, and — unlike `add` — nothing ever writes the launcher.
  _acct_disable_new "$id" "Nothing else was written: no roster entry names \"$id\" yet, so this box does not have that account, and the launcher $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id is somebody else's file that ccrc never writes. Re-run this same command once the cause is fixed."

  # ── 2. THE ROSTER ENTRY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # ONE VALIDATOR. The endpoint gate is `BASE_URL_OK`, called by the roster
  # parser and — through `shared/base-url.mjs` — by its .mjs mirror, never a
  # second copy here: the sibling refuses the WHOLE proposed roster and its
  # message reaches the operator verbatim, exactly as `_inst_accounts_sh` passes
  # the generator's through. One validator, one message, and no second URL gate
  # spelled in bash.
  #
  # `_acct_write_op`, NOT `_acct_node`: this op is silent on success, so a bare
  # call could not tell a refusal from an answer, and a half-updated box answers
  # an op it does not have with exit 2 and an EMPTY body.
  local stands="the $_SVC_REG/$id-disabled kill-switch marker WAS written before this step, and it is inert: no roster entry names \"$id\", so it switches nothing off, and re-running this same command rewrites it."
  _acct_write_op "$stands" declare-entry --file "$(_acct_roster_path)" \
    --id "$id" --label "$label" --hue "$hue" \
    ${suffix:+--suffix "$suffix"} \
    ${provider:+--provider "$provider"} \
    ${baseurl:+--base-url "$baseurl"}

  # ── 3. THE PROJECTION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # `_inst_accounts_sh` ALONE, never `_acct_converge`: that function also runs
  # `cmd_wrappers`, and a wrapper converge over a declared account is precisely
  # the data loss decision 22(c) rules out — `ccrc wrappers` would rewrite
  # somebody else's launcher into the four-line generated shape.
  stands="the roster entry for \"$id\" WAS written and nothing rolls back, and so was the $_SVC_REG/$id-disabled marker that keeps the lane switched off. The launcher itself was never touched."
  _acct_projection "$stands"

  # ── 4. THE ANSWER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Captured with this site's own clause rather than passed straight through
  # (D-2130): by this line the entry, the projection and the marker all stand, so
  # a shared "Nothing was written." would state the opposite of what happened.
  _acct_read_op "This lane is fully declared and nothing rolls back: the roster entry for \"$id\", \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh and the $_SVC_REG/$id-disabled marker that keeps it switched off all stand, and the launcher was never touched. Only this report failed: run 'ccrc account roster' to read the entry back." \
    declared --file "$(_acct_roster_path)" --id "$id" --disabled true
  printf '%s\n' "$ACCT_OUT"
}

# ── the roster's exec block, read once, through the node sibling ──────────
# `~/.ccrc/accounts.sh` carries NO exec fields — `generateAccountsSh`
# (shared/generate.mjs:170-238) emits three id arrays (CCRC_ACCOUNTS,
# CCRC_HOME_ABLE, CCRC_MEASURED), one scalar (CCRC_UPSTREAM) and five lookup
# functions, and nothing else — so a subshell source cannot
# answer any of this. One reader, seven values, and the EMPTY STRING for every
# field the roster does not carry: an absent `secretsFile` and a declared one
# are two different lanes, and the caller below branches on exactly that.
#
# EIGHT LINES AND AN `END` SENTINEL, NOT TSV. TAB is an IFS whitespace
# character, so `IFS=$'\t' read -r a b c d e` over `ok\tclaude\t.claude-plain\t\topenrouter`
# COLLAPSES the empty field and yields `d=[openrouter] e=[]` (CLAUDE.md's
# measured hazard). One field per line has no such collapse — but `$( )` strips
# trailing newlines, so a trailing EMPTY field would vanish instead.
# `_box_build_fields` already solved this exact problem in this exact file
# (:1300-1301, read back at :1317-1318), and this is that with eight lines
# instead of six.
#
# `ACCT_PROVIDER` AND `ACCT_SUFFIX` ARE ALREADY WRITTEN BY `_acct_add_parse`,
# where they mean what the OPERATOR ASKED FOR rather than what the roster says.
# Nothing runs both today — `add` never calls this, and every caller of this
# never parses `add`'s flags — and the names are the ones this plan's Task 32
# is written against, so they are kept and the overload is named here rather
# than discovered. A verb that ever needs both must give one pair its own names.
ACCT_KIND=""; ACCT_SECRETS=""; ACCT_SECRET_ENV=""; ACCT_BASEURL=""; ACCT_HOMEABLE=0
_acct_lane() {   # <id>
  local -a f=()
  local x
  # `_acct_read_op`, NOT `_acct_write_op`: this op ANSWERS, so an empty body is
  # `no-answer` and not success — and this op's answer is not JSON, which is the
  # one thing that wrapper does not care about. It captures stdout, re-emits a
  # refusal envelope verbatim on a non-zero exit and leaves with node's class,
  # so nothing below ever judges anything but the rc-0 case. The clause is the
  # honest one at every call site this function has: it runs before any write.
  _acct_read_op "Nothing was written." lane --file "$(_acct_roster_path)" --id "$1"
  mapfile -t f <<<"$ACCT_OUT"
  # `_box_build_fields`' guard (:1318) in shape, but TWO PREDICATES AND TWO
  # SENTENCES (D-2184). The count and the sentinel ask different questions, and
  # one sentence could only describe one of them: driven with a helper whose
  # eighth line is a data field rather than `END`, the shared sentence read
  # "described account \"alt-max\" in 8 lines instead of 8" — a number equal to
  # itself. The count is FIRST because `${f[7]}` below is unbound under `set -u`
  # until it has passed.
  [ "${#f[@]}" -eq 8 ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 lane-unreadable "the account helper described account \"$1\" in ${#f[@]} lines instead of 8 - this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box, and nothing was written"
  # THE CONTROL-CHARACTER LOOP IS NOT `_box_build_fields`' AND IT IS REACHED —
  # D-2183, REVERSING D-2166, which confirmed deleting it as unreachable on the
  # argument that `new URL()` strips control bytes from `baseUrl`. It does not
  # reach this wire: `rosterFromJson` parses `exec.baseUrl` with `new URL()` to
  # VALIDATE it and the roster keeps the ORIGINAL string, which the `lane` op
  # emits verbatim. So a hand-written roster carrying
  # `https://x.example/<CR>tail` validates and arrives here as eight lines with
  # `END` in slot 7, and this loop is the only thing that refuses it. The other
  # seven fields really are refused upstream (`configDirSuffix`, `secretsFile`,
  # `kind`, `provider` against fixed patterns); `baseUrl` is the hole, and a raw
  # LF is caught by the count above instead because it splits the field. Six
  # bytes are driven as a table in `ccrc-account.test.ts`; measured with this
  # loop deleted, five of them (CR, TAB, VT, SOH, DEL) rotate the credential at
  # EXIT 0 with the byte intact and only LF still refuses.
  for x in "${f[@]}"; do
    [[ "$x" =~ [[:cntrl:]] ]] \
      && _acct_refuse 1 lane-unreadable "account \"$1\" carries a control character in its roster entry, which this verb will not interpolate - fix \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json"
  done
  # THE SENTINEL, AND IT RUNS AFTER THE LOOP — the one place this reader's
  # order diverges from `_box_build_fields`, which checks both predicates first
  # (:1318) and then scans. It can: it returns a bare 6 and interpolates
  # nothing. This one interpolates a FIELD rather than a count, and that is the
  # difference: the shape it exists for is a helper that grew a field where
  # `END` was, and such a field would be roster-derived — the very value the
  # loop above has just proven carries no control byte, before this sentence
  # puts it on an operator's terminal. No roster reaches this line today (node
  # writes the literal `END`), which is why the fixture that measures it stubs
  # the helper.
  [ "${f[7]}" = END ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 lane-unreadable "the account helper ended account \"$1\" with \"${f[7]}\" where the END sentinel belongs, so the seven fields above it cannot be trusted to be in their own slots - this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box, and nothing was written"
  ACCT_KIND="${f[0]}"; ACCT_PROVIDER="${f[1]}"; ACCT_SECRETS="${f[2]}"
  ACCT_SECRET_ENV="${f[3]}"; ACCT_SUFFIX="${f[4]}"; ACCT_BASEURL="${f[5]}"
  ACCT_HOMEABLE="${f[6]}"
}

# ── which panes have not seen a rotated token yet ─────────────────────────
# TWO MEASUREMENTS, AND EITHER ONE CAN FAIL (D-2185 — until this round only the
# second could). First the registry: a row is a `<id>.uuid` file (`_box_sessions`
# counts them the same way, :1775-1788) and its `.wrapper` field says which lane
# it runs on. If NO row names this lane, nothing could be live and `[]` is a
# measurement of the registry — so tmux is never asked, and a box with no tmux
# server can still rotate a credential.
#
# BUT AN UNREADABLE REGISTRY IS NOT AN EMPTY ONE, and this function used to
# answer both with `measured=true, live=[]`. Measured: `~/.cc-sessions` at mode
# 000 with a row for the lane inside answered `{"ok":true,"live":[]}` without
# asking tmux at all, and a row whose `.wrapper` was mode 000 was silently
# `continue`d to the same answer. An operator who has just rotated a credential
# then reads "no session is running on this lane" and restarts nothing —
# precisely when they need to know which panes are stale. Three conditions,
# decided at the read rather than at the seam:
#
#   * the registry is ABSENT — a measurement. ccd creates it when it registers
#     its first session, so no directory means no row has ever existed and
#     nothing could be live. `[]`, tmux never asked.
#   * the registry EXISTS and cannot be listed or entered (or is not a
#     directory at all) — `measured=false`. The rows that would have named this
#     lane are behind that mode.
#   * a ROW exists and its `.wrapper` cannot be read — `measured=false` for the
#     WHOLE answer, not a skipped row. The file that could not be read may say
#     `<id>`, so a list built from the rows that did answer would be a partial
#     list presented as a complete one, which is the under-report this entry is
#     about. The wire has no third state to say it in: `--measured false`
#     carries no ids by contract (account-op.mjs's `rotated`).
#
# AN ABSENT `.wrapper` IS STILL A SKIP, and that is the distinction the old
# `[ -r … ] || continue` folded away: absence is a positive fact — the registry
# records no lane for that row — while unreadable is the absence of a fact. It
# is the same rule `statMeasured` and the agent wire's `absent` marker draw on
# the server side (D-1396), spelled here in bash.
#
# Only when a row does name it is tmux asked, ONCE, bounded, for the whole
# answer rather than N has-session calls. A tmux that is absent, or whose server
# is gone, or whose socket holds without answering, has told us NOTHING about
# liveness — and "no session is running on this lane" is a different fact for
# the operator than "nobody could tell". Collapsing them into `[]` would put a
# Restart-each-session list of length zero in front of somebody whose panes are
# all still holding the old token. Any non-zero rc is read as "nobody could
# tell", which is the reading `_check_tmux_skew` takes of the same call
# (ccrc-doctor-checks:443-457 — rc 124 and every other non-zero alike SKIP). The
# polarity is deliberately the opposite of `remove`'s (Task 32), which REFUSES
# on an unmeasurable tmux: reporting a gap is right for a write that has already
# succeeded, and refusing is right for a delete that has not started.
#
# A LITERAL 5, AND NO KNOB. `ccrc doctor` accepts no arguments (:2000), so every
# `CCRC_*` a verb declares must also join `ccrc-cli.test.ts`'s deletion array or
# the suite's answer starts depending on the developer's exported shell. A knob
# earns that line when a test would otherwise have to wait out a production
# timeout (`ccrc-doctor-checks:152-158`). Nothing here waits: the fixture's tmux
# either answers at once or exits 97.
#
# `$_SVC_REG` AND NOT A SECOND `$HOME/.cc-sessions` — the constant is declared
# at :96 for exactly this, and `_box_sessions` spelling it again is the drift
# that comment names, not a precedent.
ACCT_LIVE_MEASURED=false
ACCT_LIVE_IDS=()
_acct_live() {   # <id>
  local id="$1" reg="$_SVC_REG" names="" rc=0 u="" sid="" w="" had_nullglob=0
  local blind=0
  local -a rows=()
  ACCT_LIVE_MEASURED=true
  ACCT_LIVE_IDS=()
  # `-e` FIRST, and the three arms are the three conditions the header names.
  # An absent registry falls through both branches with `rows` empty, which is
  # the measured `[]`; anything that exists but cannot be listed and entered
  # leaves here with nothing measured. `-r` AND `-x`: listing `*.uuid` needs the
  # read bit and reaching the `.wrapper` beside it needs the search bit, and a
  # directory holding one without the other tells us nothing either.
  if [ -e "$reg" ] && { [ ! -d "$reg" ] || [ ! -r "$reg" ] || [ ! -x "$reg" ]; }; then
    ACCT_LIVE_MEASURED=false
    return 0
  fi
  if [ -d "$reg" ]; then
    shopt -q nullglob && had_nullglob=1
    shopt -s nullglob
    # `u`, not `f`: `_acct_lane` one function up uses `f` for an ARRAY, and
    # the linter reads the two as one variable (SC2178, and a comment opening
    # with that tool's own name is parsed as a directive rather than prose,
    # which is why this sentence does not). A name that makes a linter tell the
    # truth costs nothing.
    for u in "$reg"/*.uuid; do
      sid="${u##*/}"; sid="${sid%.uuid}"
      # TWO SKIPS WOULD BE ONE COLLAPSE — the `[ -r … ] || continue` this
      # replaces answered the same way for a row that records no lane and a row
      # whose lane could not be read. `-r` before `-e` because the readable case
      # is the one every row on a working box takes.
      if [ -r "$reg/$sid.wrapper" ]; then
        # THE RETURN CODE IS NOT THE TEST, THE VALUE IS — `_acct_read_credential`'s
        # own rule at a second address, and this task's plan snippet had the
        # opposite: `IFS= read -r w < … || w=""`. `_reg_set` (ccd/ccd:1285-1291)
        # writes each field with NO trailing newline, so `read` returns 1 at EOF
        # having ALREADY set the variable — and that `|| w=""` wiped every
        # wrapper name on the way past, which would have answered `live: []` for
        # every box on earth while claiming a measurement.
        w=""
        IFS= read -r w < "$reg/$sid.wrapper"
        [ "$w" = "$id" ] && rows+=("$sid")
      elif [ -e "$reg/$sid.wrapper" ]; then
        # It exists and this process cannot read it: the whole answer is
        # unmeasured, because this row may be the one still holding the old
        # token. `break` rather than `return`, so the shell option below is
        # restored on the way out.
        blind=1
        break
      fi
      # …and an ABSENT `.wrapper` falls through: the registry positively
      # records no lane for that row, so it is not a row on this lane.
    done
    [ "$had_nullglob" -eq 1 ] || shopt -u nullglob
  fi
  if [ "$blind" -eq 1 ]; then
    ACCT_LIVE_MEASURED=false
    ACCT_LIVE_IDS=()
    return 0
  fi
  [ "${#rows[@]}" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  names="$(_plat_timeout 5 tmux list-sessions -F '#{session_name}' 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then ACCT_LIVE_MEASURED=false; return 0; fi
  # The pane's tmux session name is `cc-<id>` — `ccd`'s `_tmux()` (ccd/ccd:1114)
  # is the one place that mapping is spelled, and this is its second reader
  # rather than a looser substring match: `*"$sid"*` would call `cc-orchard`
  # live because `cc-orchard-api` is.
  for sid in "${rows[@]}"; do
    case $'\n'"$names"$'\n' in *$'\n'"cc-$sid"$'\n'*) ACCT_LIVE_IDS+=("$sid") ;; esac
  done
  return 0
}

# ── credential — replace a token lane's secret, and say who has not seen it ─
# Spec §5's `credential` row (spec:419) is a ROTATION, not a first write: the
# roster is not touched, one 0600 file is replaced through the writer `add`
# already uses, and the answer names the panes still holding the old token.
_acct_credential() {
  local id="" cred="" v="" flag=""
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    flag="${1%%=*}"
    case "$1" in
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      --id|--credential)
        [ $# -ge 2 ] \
          || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "the flag $1 takes a value and nothing followed it on the command line. Nothing was written."
        v="$2"; shift ;;
      --id=*|--credential=*) v="${1#*=}" ;;
      *) _acct_refuse 2 unknown-argument "ccrc account credential has no argument \"$1\"" ;;
    esac
    # A FLAG GIVEN AN EMPTY VALUE IS NOT A FLAG NOBODY GAVE (D-2148, D-2154) —
    # the loop `add` and `declare` both carry, at this verb's address and NOT
    # left out because both of this verb's flags happen to have a downstream
    # gate. They do, and both answer the wrong sentence: `--id ''` would reach
    # `_acct_id_or_refuse`'s "an account id is required" and `--credential ''`
    # would reach `credential-not-stdin`, telling an operator who typed an
    # empty value that they typed no value and that they typed a secret on
    # argv. THE GATE IS HERE, ON `$v`, for this pair's reason: both spellings
    # of both flags land in `$v` exactly once.
    [ -n "$v" ] \
      || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "the flag $flag was given an empty value. A flag present with nothing in it is not the same as a flag nobody passed, so it is refused rather than dropped: give it a value, or leave it off. Nothing was written."
    case "$flag" in
      --id)         id="$v" ;;
      --credential) cred="$v" ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  _acct_id_or_refuse "$id"
  _acct_lane "$id"

  # THE LANE KIND IS THE FIRST QUESTION, because spec:419's sentence asks it
  # first: "Refuse unless X is generated or upstream-with-declared-secretsFile".
  # Everything below this line is about a lane ccrc may write; this line is
  # about whether it may write the lane at all.
  #
  # `external` IS THE ONE KIND THAT CAN NEVER BE ROTATED, and the warrant is not
  # only the spec. Decision 22(c)'s whole argument for `declare` is that ccrc
  # RECORDS an external launcher and NEVER TOUCHES IT, so putting a 0600 file on
  # disk for one contradicts the sentence that makes `declare` safe. It is also
  # this verb's own stated safety property — "a lane ccrc cannot prove it owns
  # is told so" — and an external lane is by definition such a lane: its
  # launcher is somebody else's program and reads whatever credential that
  # program was written to read.
  #
  # IT IS A CODE OF ITS OWN, NOT A THIRD `not-managed` (review round 1). That
  # code already carries two conditions three lines down, and a third meaning on
  # it would be the overloaded seam this cluster keeps closing. It is also why
  # this gate is FIRST rather than tucked in beside them: an external lane is an
  # external lane whether or not it happens to name a `secretsFile`, and routing
  # one fact to two codes on a field that has nothing to do with it is the same
  # defect wearing the other hat. `rosterFromJson` admits `secretsFile` on all
  # three exec kinds (shared/roster-json.mjs:231-250), so before this gate a
  # HAND-WRITTEN `external` entry naming provider `openrouter` and a matching
  # secretsFile was ACCEPTED AND ROTATED — measured, exit 0, `{"ok":true,…}`,
  # a live token in a 0600 file ccrc had promised never to write.
  #
  # THIS IS `ACCT_KIND`'S FIRST AND ONLY READER. `_acct_lane` sets seven
  # globals and three of them — `ACCT_KIND`, `ACCT_BASEURL`, `ACCT_HOMEABLE` —
  # had no reader in bash at all; Task 32 is written against all seven. Two
  # still have none, which is a fact about those two and not a licence.
  [ "$ACCT_KIND" != external ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 external-lane "account $id is an external lane: its launcher $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id is somebody else's program, which ccrc records and never writes. Whatever credential that launcher reads is the launcher's to rotate, so this verb will not put a file on disk for it - even when exec.secretsFile names one. Re-auth it the way its own launcher documents. Nothing was written."

  # TOKEN LANES ONLY, and `not-managed` names the difference rather than hiding
  # it. A login lane's credential is Claude Code's own `.credentials.json`
  # inside the config dir. That is not a file this verb may replace, and it
  # re-auths some other way.
  #
  # THE REMEDY NAMES A SUBCOMMAND WAVE 1 DOES NOT SHIP, deliberately, and
  # `_acct_add`'s `credential-not-managed` sentence says the same thing at its
  # own address: `auth-start` is §5's own name for the sign-in door and lands
  # with wave 2's routes. On a wave-1 box the same act is `ccd-account-auth <id>
  # login` (Tasks 51-53) or `ccd account-pane --id <id> --method setup-token`
  # (Task 50). The sentence is written for the finished system because a remedy
  # the operator reads a month from now should name the verb they have; naming
  # `ccd-account-auth` in a message that outlives wave 2 by years would be a
  # remedy that ages into a lie.
  #
  # TWO GATES, TWO SENTENCES, BECAUSE THEY ARE TWO CONDITIONS. The first is a
  # lane with no secrets file at all; the second is a lane that names one whose
  # PROVIDER keeps no env var — `rosterFromJson` puts no kind predicate on
  # `exec.provider` either, so a hand-written `generated` or `upstream` entry
  # naming `openai` reaches the second while the first passes (measured: the
  # `lane` op answers `generated / openai / .cc-secrets/… / <empty>`). One code,
  # because the operator's next move is the same (re-auth elsewhere); two
  # details, because what they are looking at is not.
  #
  # BOTH ARE NOW `generated`-OR-`upstream` ONLY, since the kind gate above runs
  # first — so this sentence no longer hedges across an external lane it can
  # never see. A refusal that names a branch it cannot reach is the comment
  # shaped like a mechanism this wave has twelve examples of.
  [ -n "$ACCT_SECRETS" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 not-managed "account $id declares no secretsFile, so there is no credential file for ccrc to replace - this is a login lane, whose credential is the .credentials.json Claude Code writes inside its own config directory, and it re-auths with: ccrc account auth-start --id $id --method login. Nothing was written."
  [ -n "$ACCT_SECRET_ENV" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 not-managed "account $id's provider keeps its credential outside ccrc, so there is no env file for this verb to write, whatever exec.secretsFile says. Nothing was written."

  # THE PATH IS RE-MEASURED, NOT TRUSTED. `_acct_write_secret` DERIVES its
  # destination from <id> and the tag `_acct_secret_tag` decides, and `add`
  # records exactly that path, so on a lane ccrc created these agree by
  # construction. On a hand-written roster entry they may not — and writing the
  # new token into a file the wrapper does not source, under a success message,
  # is worse than refusing. One writer, one umask discipline; a lane ccrc cannot
  # prove it owns is told so.
  #
  # `${ACCT_SECRETS_DIR#"$HOME/"}` RATHER THAN A LITERAL `.cc-secrets`: the
  # roster stores the path relative to $HOME and the writer's own directory
  # constant is the only spelling of it in this file (:943).
  _acct_secret_tag "$ACCT_PROVIDER" "$ACCT_SECRET_ENV"
  local want="${ACCT_SECRETS_DIR#"$HOME/"}/$id-$ACCT_SECRET_TAG.env"
  [ "$ACCT_SECRETS" = "$want" ] \
    || _acct_refuse 1 secrets-path-unmanaged "account $id's roster entry names \$HOME/$ACCT_SECRETS, and this verb writes \$HOME/$want - rotating would fill a file $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id does not source. Either edit exec.secretsFile to $want, or replace \$HOME/$ACCT_SECRETS by hand. Nothing was written."

  # THE CREDENTIAL IS READ LAST, `_acct_add`'s order and its reason: it is the
  # one value in this run that must never be read for a request the box was
  # going to refuse anyway.
  _acct_read_credential "$cred"                # -> ACCT_CREDENTIAL, or refuses
  _acct_write_secret "$id" "$ACCT_PROVIDER" "$ACCT_SECRET_ENV"
  _acct_live "$id"

  # `${arr[@]+"${arr[@]}"}` — this tree's empty-array idiom under `set -u`.
  local -a args=(--id "$id" --measured "$ACCT_LIVE_MEASURED")
  local sid=""
  for sid in ${ACCT_LIVE_IDS[@]+"${ACCT_LIVE_IDS[@]}"}; do args+=(--live "$sid"); done
  # CAPTURED WITH THIS SITE'S OWN CLAUSE (D-2130, D-2135), not passed straight
  # through: by this line the 0600 file has been replaced and cannot be rolled
  # back, so a shared "Nothing was written." would state the opposite of what
  # happened — and what this run can no longer tell the operator is precisely
  # the list it was about to print.
  _acct_read_op "The 0600 credential file $ACCT_SECRET_WRITTEN WAS replaced and nothing rolls it back; the roster was not touched and no session was restarted. Only this report failed, so this run cannot name the panes still holding the OLD token: a wrapper sources its secrets file at exec time, so every session already running on \"$id\" keeps the old value until it is recreated." \
    rotated "${args[@]}"
  printf '%s\n' "$ACCT_OUT"
}

# ── check, half one: the question that costs nothing ──────────────────────
# THE EXIT CODE IS NOT THE ANSWER. Measured 2026-09-07 on a scratch config dir:
# `auth status --json`, `auth status` and `auth status --text` ALL exit 1 when
# `loggedIn` is false — the command SUCCEEDED and reported a fact. A reader that
# took rc as the verdict would classify every signed-out lane as `unknown`,
# which is the one verdict that means "nobody could tell". So: parse stdout, and
# treat a non-zero exit carrying parseable JSON as an ANSWER.
#
# `--json` IS PASSED FOR INTENT, NOT FOR EFFECT. With stdout not a tty the flag
# and the bare form are byte-identical (same measurement), so nothing here may
# assume the flag is what makes the output JSON.
#
# NO PARSEABLE ANSWER IS NOT A VERDICT, and the classifier says so with EXIT 3
# and an empty stdout rather than with a verdict word. An `unknown` row would BE
# a verdict, and the `[ -n "$ACCT_HEALTH" ]` test below would short-circuit on
# it — which is exactly how a first draft made its own fall-through unreachable.
#
# NEITHER IS `loggedIn: true`, AND THAT IS EXIT 4. The short-circuit is
# ONE-SIDED: only `loggedIn: false` is a verdict. A present credential says
# nothing about whether the lane's base URL answers, whether its model map names
# a model the endpoint serves, or whether the subscription has quota, and §8's
# `ok` is defined as exit 0 from a real `-p`, which this has not run. So the
# signed-in case is a FACT and not a verdict: exit 4, with the one sentence to
# carry on stdout, appended to ACCT_NOTES here. Three and four are two
# conditions the caller phrases differently — "the cheap question gave no
# parseable answer" versus "the lane says it is signed in" — so they must not
# share an exit code any more than they may share an empty string (CLAUDE.md,
# "No overloaded null at a seam").
#
# Extra codes on a node HELPER are this file's neighbour's idiom: `_check_wrappers`'
# roster reader answers 3, 4 and 6 for three unreadable-roster conditions and
# `ccrc-doctor-checks:2300-2316` branches on each. Nothing propagates 3 or 4 to the
# operator, so they are invisible to this file's own 0/1/2 table (:24-33).
#
# `_acct_node` DIRECTLY, NOT `_acct_read_op`, and the reason is measured rather
# than asserted: that wrapper is `_acct_run_op`, whose body is
# `[ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && return 0` / `[ -n "$ACCT_OUT" ] || _acct_no_answer …` /
# `exit "$rc"`. On exit 3 (empty stdout by contract) it would call
# `_acct_no_answer` and REFUSE the fall-through this verb exists to have; on
# exit 4 it would print the plain-text note as though it were the verb's answer
# and `exit 4`. Both are the "every non-zero is a refusal to re-emit" reading,
# and this reader has two non-zero codes that are neither.
#
# BOTH OUT-PARAMETERS ARE DECLARED HERE, above their first reader. `set -u`
# makes an unset array fatal on read, and `_acct_check` resets both before every
# call — the reason `BOX_*`'s two readers declare theirs at file scope too
# (:1082-1084).
ACCT_HEALTH=""
ACCT_NOTES=()

# ── the knob is validated where it is used, and `0` is REFUSED (D-2221) ────
# `CCRC_ACCOUNT_AUTH_TIMEOUT` is passed straight to `_plat_timeout`, and the two
# implementations behind that shim read a bad value in OPPOSITE directions —
# measured on this box, GNU coreutils 9.4:
#
#   value    GNU `timeout`                         the bash fallback
#   0        NO TIMEOUT — `timeout 0 sleep 2` ran   `sleep 0` returns at once, the
#            the full 2s and exited 0               watcher stamps and kills: 124
#                                                   IMMEDIATELY
#   -1/abc/  exit 125 WITHOUT EXEC'ING the          `sleep abc` fails, `|| exit 0`
#   5x/''    launcher; `invalid time interval`      fires, and the call gets NO
#            swallowed by the caller's 2>/dev/null  DEADLINE AT ALL
#   1e20     ACCEPTED and clamped — `timeout        `sleep 1e20` is accepted too:
#            99999999999999999999 sleep 1` took     no deadline in practice
#            1005ms and exited 0
#
# So the ONE value an operator types to mean "do not wait" means "wait forever"
# on the box that has coreutils and "give up instantly" on the box that does
# not, and an enormous value is a second spelling of unbounded. None of that is
# a fact about the launcher, but every one of them USED to be reported as one:
# 125 reaches `_acct_auth_status`'s exit-3 arm and gets D-2220's sentence about
# a launcher that "gave no parseable answer", about a launcher that was never
# run. Refusing the value is the only answer that names what actually happened.
#
# `0` IS REFUSED RATHER THAN GIVEN A MEANING. The two candidate meanings are the
# two the platforms already disagree about, so ANY ruling here makes one box's
# behaviour the standard and the other box's a bug to emulate in shell. And
# neither meaning is wanted: "unbounded" defeats the knob's entire purpose (its
# own header calls it "a deadline against a hung binary"), while "skip the cheap
# question" is a different request that would deserve a different spelling than
# a duration of zero. An operator who wants no auth-status call has one: a lane
# whose provider is not `anthropic` is never asked at all.
#
# THE CAP IS 3600 AND IT IS NOT DECORATION. Above it the knob stops bounding
# anything a human waits for, and the values that GNU accepts and clamps are
# `0`'s defect wearing more digits. The length gate comes FIRST because it has
# to: `[ 99999999999999999999 -gt 3600 ]` is not false, it is
# `integer expression expected` on stderr and a non-zero `[` — a guard that
# fails OPEN on precisely the input it exists to catch.
#
# AN OUT-PARAMETER, NOT A `$( )`, and the reason is a trap rather than a
# preference: `_acct_refuse` prints its envelope on STDOUT, so a validator
# called as `d="$(_acct_auth_deadline)"` would capture its own refusal into `d`
# and `exit 2` would leave only the SUBSHELL. The operator would see nothing.
# This file's `ACCT_*`/`BOX_*` out-parameter idiom is what a refusing helper has
# to use, and it is declared at file scope for `set -u` like its siblings.
ACCT_AUTH_DEADLINE=""
_acct_auth_deadline() {   # -> ACCT_AUTH_DEADLINE (whole seconds), or refuses at 2
  local v="$CCRC_ACCOUNT_AUTH_TIMEOUT" n=""
  # EXIT 2, the USAGE class (:29-33). An env knob is part of how the verb was
  # invoked and the mistake is the caller's, decidable before anything runs —
  # the same class `--id ''` is refused in, one function down.
  case "$v" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*)
      _acct_refuse 2 bad-timeout "CCRC_ACCOUNT_AUTH_TIMEOUT is \"$v\", which is not a whole number of seconds. This verb passes it to timeout(1), which refuses a value like this by exiting 125 without ever running the launcher - and a box with no coreutils would instead run the launcher with no deadline at all. Give it a positive whole number of seconds, or leave it unset for the default of 15. Nothing was written." ;;
  esac
  # Leading zeros stripped by pattern rather than by `$(( 10#$v ))`, which
  # OVERFLOWS SILENTLY on a 20-digit value and would hand the comparisons below
  # a wrapped negative number.
  n="${v#"${v%%[!0]*}"}"; [ -n "$n" ] || n=0
  [ "$n" = 0 ] \
    && _acct_refuse 2 bad-timeout "CCRC_ACCOUNT_AUTH_TIMEOUT is \"$v\", and zero is refused rather than given a meaning: GNU timeout(1) reads a duration of zero as NO deadline at all - measured on this fleet, a zero-second call sat through a two-second sleep in full - while a box with no coreutils falls back to a shell sleep that returns at once and reports the deadline as already expired. One value cannot mean both. If you want to bound this call, give it a positive whole number of seconds; if you want it not to run at all, that is a property of the lane's provider, not a duration. Nothing was written."
  { [ "${#n}" -gt 4 ] || [ "$n" -gt 3600 ]; } \
    && _acct_refuse 2 bad-timeout "CCRC_ACCOUNT_AUTH_TIMEOUT is \"$v\", which is above the 3600-second ceiling this verb will honour. 'claude auth status' is a local credential read, so this is a deadline against a hung binary rather than a budget, and a value this large does not bound one: timeout(1) accepts it and clamps it, which is the same unbounded call zero asks for. Give it a positive whole number of seconds up to 3600. Nothing was written."
  ACCT_AUTH_DEADLINE="$n"
}

# ── one sentence per reason the cheap question produced no verdict (D-2220) ──
# `--exit "$rc"` was passed to the classifier and read by nothing, so five
# conditions arrived at the operator as one sentence — "auth status gave no
# parseable answer on this box" — and four of them were FALSE. The worst is the
# one an operator acts on: a lane that was never asked reported as a lane that
# answered badly, which sends them to look at a launcher instead of at the fact
# that there is no launcher.
#
# THE TRIAGE IS `_acct_no_answer`'S, NOT A SECOND ONE. That function is this
# file's convention for exactly this shape (:3916-3927) — an if/elif chain on
# `$rc`, one `cause` sentence per branch, a residual `else` that says plainly
# that the tree cannot name a cause — and the codes it already triages are the
# codes `timeout(1)` and bash hand back here. 124 is NOT in this table: it never
# reaches this function, because `_acct_auth_status` branches on it one caller
# up and gives it a `timeout` VERDICT rather than a note.
#
# EVERY BRANCH STILL ENDS IN THE PROBE. None of these is evidence about the
# credential, so none of them is a verdict; what changes is the sentence, not
# the routing, and each says so in its own words so the note is never mistaken
# for the answer.
_acct_auth_why() {   # <rc> <id> -> the one note explaining why there is no verdict
  local rc="$1" id="$2"
  if [ "$rc" -eq 127 ]; then
    printf '%s' "auth status was never asked: there is no launcher at $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id to run, so nothing about account $id's credential was measured and the verdict below is the probe's. Run 'ccrc wrappers' to write the missing launcher."
  elif [ "$rc" -eq 126 ]; then
    printf '%s' "auth status was never asked: $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id exists but could not be executed, so nothing about account $id's credential was measured and the verdict below is the probe's. Check its mode bits and that its interpreter line names a shell this box has."
  elif [ "$rc" -eq 125 ]; then
    printf '%s' "auth status was never asked: timeout(1) refused to run $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id and exited 125, which it does when its own arguments are wrong rather than when the launcher fails. The verdict below is the probe's."
  elif [ "$rc" -gt 128 ]; then
    printf '%s' "auth status did not answer because it was KILLED rather than because it replied badly: exit $rc is signal $((rc - 128)). Look for an OOM kill on this box, not for a broken launcher. The verdict below is the probe's."
  else
    printf '%s' "auth status gave no parseable answer on this box, so the verdict below is the probe's"
  fi
}

_acct_auth_status() {   # <id> -> ACCT_HEALTH (a HealthRow) or ""; may append to ACCT_NOTES
  local id="$1" out="" rc=0 row="" crc=0
  ACCT_HEALTH=""
  _acct_auth_deadline
  out="$(_plat_timeout "$ACCT_AUTH_DEADLINE" "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id" auth status --json 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -eq 124 ]; then
    # rc 124 is `_plat_timeout`'s own report of expiry (:406-411), and it is a
    # THIRD thing again: neither an answer nor a parse failure. The body is
    # whatever the killed launcher had flushed, so it is not offered at all.
    row="$(printf '' | _acct_node classify --source auth-status --exit 124 \
      --timed-out true --deadline "$ACCT_AUTH_DEADLINE")" || crc=$?
  else
    row="$(printf '%s' "$out" | _acct_node classify --source auth-status --exit "$rc")" || crc=$?
  fi
  if [ "$crc" -eq 3 ]; then
    # THE LAUNCHER'S OWN EXIT CODE, READ AT LAST (D-2220). `$rc` was already
    # being handed to the classifier as `--exit` and discarded there; the
    # sentence belongs on this side because `_acct_no_answer`'s triage — the
    # convention this follows — is this file's, and because 127 and 126 are
    # facts about a PATH lookup that node never saw.
    ACCT_NOTES+=("$(_acct_auth_why "$rc" "$id")")
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "$crc" -eq 4 ]; then
    # The classifier's one line of plain text, carried verbatim: it names the
    # authMethod the lane reported, and the sentence is the classifier's because
    # that is the half that read the field.
    ACCT_NOTES+=("$row")
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "$crc" -ne 0 ]; then
    [ -n "$row" ] \
      || _acct_refuse 1 classify-failed "the health classifier exited $crc without printing anything, so this box measured nothing about account $id - read the account-op: line above. Nothing was written."
    printf '%s\n' "$row"
    exit "$crc"
  fi
  ACCT_HEALTH="$row"
}

# ── check, half two: the question that costs a request ────────────────────
# STUB. Task 30 replaces this body whole and adds the deadline, the scratch cwd
# and the `~/.cc-limits` measurement. It answers `unknown` rather than `ok`
# because §8's table ends "anything else — never reported as ok", and a build
# that cannot run the probe has measured exactly nothing. The failure arm is not
# decoration: without it a classifier that could not run would leave
# `ACCT_HEALTH` empty and the `health` op would refuse an empty `--row`, which
# is a worse sentence than this one.
ACCT_LIMITS_TOUCHED=false
_acct_probe() {   # <id> -> sets ACCT_HEALTH and ACCT_LIMITS_TOUCHED
  ACCT_LIMITS_TOUCHED=false
  ACCT_HEALTH="$(printf '%s' '{}' | _acct_node classify --source probe --exit 0)" \
    || _acct_refuse 1 classify-failed "the health classifier could not run, so this box measured nothing about account $1 - read the account-op: line above. Nothing was written."
}

# ── a note is bounded before it reaches argv (D-2222) ─────────────────────
# `classify` reads the auth-status BODY from stdin on purpose — the body is a
# launcher's output and a launcher's output has no size this tree controls. The
# NOTE derived from that body then went out on argv as `--note "$n"`, where the
# only bound was the kernel's. Measured: an `authMethod` of 100 000 bytes rides
# through and produces a 100 KB note; 200 000 bytes hits `Argument list too
# long` (MAX_ARG_STRLEN, 131 072 bytes for one argument) and node exits 126, at
# which point the verb answers `no-answer` with "Nothing in this tree is known
# to exit 126 with an empty body" — a shrug about a lane it had measured
# perfectly. The reasoning that put the body on stdin applies unchanged here.
#
# THE CAP IS ON THE ARGV BOUNDARY, NOT ON THE FIELD. `classify`'s own
# `shapeOf` already keeps launcher text out of the sentence it writes (D-2223's
# fix), so today nothing unbounded is left — but this is the choke point EVERY
# note passes through, including the ones Task 30 adds, and a cap that has to be
# re-applied per note-writer is a cap that will be missed by one of them.
#
# 1024 CHARACTERS, AND THE NUMBER IS STATED IN THE TRUNCATED NOTE ITSELF so an
# operator reading a clipped sentence knows it was clipped by ccrc and by how
# much. `${#n}` counts characters rather than bytes, so the byte bound is at
# worst 4 KB in UTF-8 — two orders of magnitude under MAX_ARG_STRLEN, which is
# the margin that makes this a cap rather than a second guess at the limit. The
# longest sentence this file writes is under 400 characters, so no note ccrc
# composes is truncated: what the cap bounds is what a LAUNCHER put there.
ACCT_NOTE_MAX=1024
_acct_note_cap() {   # <note> -> the note, or its first $ACCT_NOTE_MAX characters plus a marker
  local n="$1"
  if [ "${#n}" -le "$ACCT_NOTE_MAX" ]; then printf '%s' "$n"; return 0; fi
  printf '%s… (this note was truncated by ccrc at %s characters)' \
    "${n:0:$ACCT_NOTE_MAX}" "$ACCT_NOTE_MAX"
}

_acct_check() {
  local id="" v="" flag=""
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    flag="${1%%=*}"
    case "$1" in
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      --id)
        [ $# -ge 2 ] \
          || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "the flag $1 takes a value and nothing followed it on the command line. Nothing was written."
        v="$2"; shift ;;
      --id=*) v="${1#*=}" ;;
      *) _acct_refuse 2 unknown-argument "ccrc account check has no argument \"$1\"" ;;
    esac
    # A FLAG GIVEN AN EMPTY VALUE IS NOT A FLAG NOBODY GAVE (D-2144, D-2148) —
    # the loop `add`, `declare` and `credential` all carry, at this verb's
    # address, and a DEVIATION from this task's plan snippet, which had none.
    # This verb's one flag does have a downstream gate, and that gate answers
    # the wrong sentence: `--id ''` reaches `_acct_id_or_refuse`'s "an account
    # id is required: pass --id", which tells an operator who typed an empty
    # value that they typed no flag at all.
    [ -n "$v" ] \
      || _acct_refuse 2 missing-value "the flag $flag was given an empty value. A flag present with nothing in it is not the same as a flag nobody passed, so it is refused rather than dropped: give it a value, or leave it off. Nothing was written."
    case "$flag" in --id) id="$v" ;; esac
    shift
  done
  _acct_id_or_refuse "$id"
  _acct_lane "$id"
  ACCT_HEALTH=""
  ACCT_NOTES=()
  # THE THIRD OUT-PARAMETER, RESET WHERE ITS TWO SIBLINGS ARE (D-2223 F8). It
  # used to be reset only inside `_acct_probe`, so on the SHORT-CIRCUIT path —
  # a verdict from `auth status`, no probe — the value shipped on the wire came
  # from file scope rather than from this run. Harmless in a process that runs
  # `check` once and exits, wrong the moment anything loops it over a roster,
  # and `limitsTouched` becomes API in Task 30. D-2127's unreset array, two
  # tasks later, at the address that carries the same three-value contract.
  ACCT_LIMITS_TOUCHED=false

  # ANTHROPIC ONLY, AND GATED ON THE PROVIDER, NOT THE KIND. An OpenRouter or
  # `compatible` lane authenticates through the settings.json env block and the
  # `openai` lane's credential is its launcher's, so neither has anything for
  # Claude Code's own credential store to report. The provider comes from
  # `_acct_lane`, where an `upstream` account resolves to `anthropic` by
  # construction and a `generated` account with no `provider` resolves to
  # `anthropic` under §4.1's absence-permitting rule.
  #
  # THE NOTE IS THE READER'S, NOT THIS FUNCTION'S. An earlier draft phrased it
  # here, from `[ -n "$ACCT_HEALTH" ]` — which cannot tell "nothing answered"
  # from "the lane says it is signed in", the two conditions that both leave
  # ACCT_HEALTH empty. `_acct_auth_status` branches on the classifier's exit
  # code and appends the right sentence itself; all this needs to know is
  # whether a verdict came back.
  if [ "$ACCT_PROVIDER" = anthropic ]; then
    _acct_auth_status "$id"
  fi
  [ -n "$ACCT_HEALTH" ] || _acct_probe "$id"

  local -a args=(--id "$id" --row "$ACCT_HEALTH" --limits-touched "$ACCT_LIMITS_TOUCHED")
  local n=""
  for n in ${ACCT_NOTES[@]+"${ACCT_NOTES[@]}"}; do args+=(--note "$(_acct_note_cap "$n")"); done
  # `_acct_answer`, NOT BARE `_acct_node` — a DEVIATION from this task's plan
  # snippet, and D-2051's defect at a new address. `_acct_add`'s own tail states
  # the general case (:4917-4919): a half-updated box, one whose
  # `account-op.mjs` has no `health` op, answers exit 2 with an EMPTY body, and
  # a verb whose whole contract is one JSON object would hand its caller an exit
  # code and nothing to parse. `_acct_answer` is right HERE and was wrong at
  # `added`'s address for one reason: it hard-codes "Nothing was written." as
  # its no-answer clause, and that clause is TRUE of `check`, which reads a
  # credential store and a roster and writes nothing at all.
  _acct_answer health "${args[@]}" || exit $?
}

cmd_account() {
  local sub="${1:-}"
  [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift
  # BEFORE the dependency probes, so `-h` answers on a box that has neither.
  case "$sub" in
    -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
  esac

  # BY NAME, ONCE, AND BEFORE THE FIRST REFUSAL IS PRINTED — `cmd_install`'s
  # guard (stated :5323-5335, probed :5337) in its own shape. Without it a box
  # with no node cannot
  # even hear "that id is taken": the refusal would arrive as `_acct_node`
  # failing, i.e. as a missing dependency wearing a bad request's clothes. THIS
  # refusal is the ONLY path with no JSON on stdout, and it says so — node
  # itself is what would have to run the JSON emitter, so its own absence is
  # the one condition this verb cannot phrase as JSON. jq is checked SECOND,
  # with node already proven present two lines up, so a missing jq is an
  # ordinary box-said-no and goes through `_acct_refuse` like any other.
  command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _ccrc_die "node is required by 'ccrc account' — it runs ${CCRC_HERE%/*}/deploy/account-op.mjs, the only thing on this box that writes this verb's JSON — but is not on PATH. Install Node (this repo's floor is in server/package.json's \"engines\") or put it on PATH, then re-run. Nothing on this box was written or changed, and this is the one refusal this verb cannot phrase as JSON — there is no node left to run the file that would write it."
  command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _acct_refuse 1 jq-missing "jq is required by 'ccrc account' — it merges each account's settings.json the way $HOME/.cc-sessions/install-session-hooks.sh merges hooks, validating before and after every swap — but is not on PATH. Install jq, then re-run. Nothing on this box was written or changed."

  if [ -z "$sub" ]; then
    _acct_refuse 2 missing-subcommand "ccrc account needs a subcommand. This build implements: ${ACCT_SUBS:-nothing yet — this commit is the dispatch skeleton}"
  fi
  local known=0 s
  for s in $ACCT_SUBS; do [ "$s" = "$sub" ] && known=1; done
  [ "$known" -eq 1 ] \
    || _acct_refuse 2 unknown-subcommand "ccrc account has no subcommand \"$sub\". This build implements: ${ACCT_SUBS:-nothing yet — this commit is the dispatch skeleton}"

  case "$sub" in
    add)
      # NO `[ $# -eq 0 ]` GATE, unlike the two arms below: this is the one
      # subcommand that TAKES arguments, and `_acct_add_parse` owns every
      # refusal about them (`unknown-argument` for a flag it does not have,
      # `missing-value` for one with nothing after it). `exit` rather than
      # `return` is what every failure path inside `_acct_add` uses, so the
      # `return 0` below is reached only when the whole ordered write landed.
      _acct_add "$@"
      return 0
      ;;
    roster)
      [ $# -eq 0 ] || _acct_refuse 2 unknown-argument "ccrc account roster takes no arguments, and got \"$1\""
      _acct_answer roster --file "$(_acct_roster_path)" || exit $?
      return 0
      ;;
    candidates)
      [ $# -eq 0 ] || _acct_refuse 2 unknown-argument "ccrc account candidates takes no arguments, and got \"$1\""
      # `|| exit $?`, THE SAME AS `roster` ABOVE (review round 1). It
      # propagates nothing today — every failure path inside `_acct_candidates`
      # `exit`s — and that is exactly the point: two arms of one dispatcher must
      # not differ in a way that is only correct by accident. The first future
      # path that RETURNS non-zero instead of exiting would otherwise be
      # swallowed here and answered as exit 0 over whatever stdout carried.
      _acct_candidates || exit $?
      return 0
      ;;
    credential)
      # NO `[ $# -eq 0 ]` GATE, `add`'s reason at a third address: this
      # subcommand TAKES arguments and `_acct_credential` owns every refusal
      # about them. Every failure path inside it `exit`s, so the `return 0`
      # below is reached only when the file was replaced and the report printed.
      _acct_credential "$@"
      return 0
      ;;
    check)
      # NO `[ $# -eq 0 ]` GATE, `add`'s reason at a fourth address: this
      # subcommand TAKES arguments and `_acct_check` owns every refusal about
      # them. Every failure path inside it `exit`s, so the `return 0` below is
      # reached only when the answer was printed.
      _acct_check "$@"
      return 0
      ;;
    declare)
      # NO `[ $# -eq 0 ]` GATE, `add`'s reason at this second address: this
      # subcommand TAKES arguments and `_acct_declare` owns every refusal about
      # them. `exit` rather than `return` is what every failure path inside it
      # uses, so the `return 0` below is reached only when the whole ordered
      # write landed.
      _acct_declare "$@"
      return 0
      ;;
    *) _acct_refuse 1 internal-no-arm "ccrc account lists \"$sub\" as implemented and has no arm for it — this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box, and nothing was written" ;;
  esac
}

# ── cmd_install — the verb this CLI is named for ──────────────────────────
# `ccrc install` converges THIS box from the tree `ccrc` itself was run out of:
# it seeds the config a box cannot boot without, installs the executables,
# units and hooks, generates the account wrappers and finishes by running
# doctor. It is the local, single-box translation of `deploy/deploy.sh` — same
# doctrine, no ssh — and stage 2d builds it one step at a time (Task 6: the
# spine and the three SEEDING steps; Task 7: the tree, executables and stamp;
# Task 8: units, hooks, wrappers and the doctor tail).
#
# ── THE SHAPE: A SPINE OF `_inst_*` STEPS ────────────────────────────────
# `cmd_install` is a fixed SEQUENCE and nothing else; each step is one small
# function that does one thing and prints one line. Three properties this
# arrangement is chosen for, all of which a single long function loses:
#   - an operator reads the sequence itself, in `cmd_install`, without reading
#     the work;
#   - each step's output is one `install: <step>: <result>` line on STDOUT (the
#     RESULT register — an install's transcript is what the operator asked
#     for), so a re-run is a diff of two transcripts;
#   - ORDER is stated in one place. It is load-bearing here in a way it is not
#     in doctor: `accounts.sh` cannot be generated before the roster is placed,
#     and `ccd` refuses to run at all without `accounts.sh` (deploy.sh:274's
#     "roster before ccd" rule, which this file inherits).
#
# ── THE STEP CONTRACT: RETURN 0, OR DIE ──────────────────────────────────
# Every `_inst_*` function either returns 0 or calls `_ccrc_die`, which exits
# the whole run at 1 with the reason on stderr. That is why the sequence below
# checks no return codes and why this file's lack of `set -e` (see the header)
# costs nothing here: a step that FAILED has already stopped the process, and a
# step that is merely a no-op on this box (nothing to do, already converged)
# says so in its line and returns 0. A future step that wants to be survivable
# — Task 8's linger, which needs sudo an operator may not have — PRINTS what it
# could not do and returns 0 deliberately, rather than returning non-zero and
# hoping a caller notices.
#
# ── SEED-ONCE vs REGENERATE, THE ONE DISTINCTION EVERY STEP TURNS ON ─────
# `deploy/deploy.sh:196-206` states it for the remote lane and this verb obeys
# it locally: `~/.ccrc/accounts.json` and `~/.ccrc/ccrc.env` are USER-OWNED —
# created once, NEVER overwritten, because they hold the operator's own edits
# (and, in ccrc.env's case, their tokens). `~/.ccrc/accounts.sh` is ccrc-OWNED
# — a projection of the roster, rewritten from it on every run. Getting this
# backwards in either direction is the worst thing this verb could do: one way
# it destroys an operator's config, the other it leaves a stale bash roster
# describing accounts the box no longer has.
cmd_install() {
  # `cmd_wrappers`' loop (:1040-1048), minus the three flags it has and this
  # verb does not have yet. `-h`/`--help` prints usage at exit 0 rather than
  # being the usage error it is for `cmd_version`: this is a verb that CHANGES
  # THE BOX, so "what will this do to my box" must be answerable without
  # running it. Anything else is a usage error at exit 2, refused BEFORE the
  # first step — an install that half-ran because argument 2 was a typo is
  # worse than one that did not start.
  # ── `--role server|fleet|both` (Stage 4, Task 5 — D-73 closes) ─────────
  # The role decides WHICH long-running unit this box gets and whether the
  # agent's env file is seeded. `both` is the default and is today's exact
  # single-box behaviour; `fleet` makes this box a fleet host (agent.env +
  # `ccrc-agent.service`, no server); `server` is today's spine minus nothing
  # — the difference from `both` is reserved for later stages. A role that is
  # none of the three is a USAGE error, refused before the first step, with
  # its own sentence rather than `_ccrc_usage_die`'s "unknown argument" — the
  # operator typed the right flag and the wrong value, which are two different
  # mistakes to fix.
  local role=both
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      --role)
        [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "$PROG: --role needs a value: server, fleet or both" >&2; usage >&2; exit 2; }
        role="$2"; shift ;;
      --role=*) role="${1#--role=}" ;;
      *) _ccrc_usage_die "$1" ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  case "$role" in
    server|fleet|both) ;;
    *) echo "$PROG: unknown --role: $role (expected server, fleet or both)" >&2; usage >&2; exit 2 ;;
  esac
  # A GLOBAL, like `INST_DEGRADED` below: the `_inst_*` steps take no
  # arguments by contract (the spine reads as a sequence), so the one input
  # that varies a step's behaviour is stated once, here, before any step runs.
  INST_ROLE="$role"

  # BY NAME, ONCE, AND BEFORE THE FIRST STEP — `cmd_wrappers:1069-1076`'s guard,
  # in the same shape and for a sharper version of the same reason. Every step
  # that touches the roster runs `deploy/gen-accounts.mjs`, which IS node, and
  # without this probe an absent interpreter arrives as that generator
  # "failing": `_inst_roster` reports "the shipped roster seed does not
  # validate" and `_inst_accounts_sh` reports "$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json does
  # not validate — fix it (or move it aside to reseed)". Both are false, and the
  # second one instructs an operator to disturb the USER-OWNED file this verb
  # exists to protect, for a cause that has nothing to do with it. That is the
  # overloaded-seam mistake this file's header bans — "a missing dependency" and
  # "a corrupt config file" are two conditions an operator acts on completely
  # differently — and `install` is the ONE verb here that runs on a box which
  # may legitimately not have node yet. (Round-1 review, Important 1; measured
  # on a PATH holding every coreutil and no node.)
  command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _ccrc_die "node is required by 'ccrc install' — it runs ${CCRC_HERE%/*}/deploy/gen-accounts.mjs to write \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh — but is not on PATH. Install Node (this repo's floor is in server/package.json's \"engines\") or put it on PATH, then re-run. Nothing on this box was written or changed."

  # THE SECOND BY-NAME PROBE, for the same reason and at the same point (fix
  # round 1, Minor 2). `_inst_enable` is step 10 of 14, and on a box with no
  # systemd — macOS, WSL1, a bare container — it used to arrive as "systemctl
  # --user daemon-reload failed … read what it says: systemctl --user status
  # ccrc.service", a remedy naming the command the box does not have. Two
  # conditions an operator acts on completely differently ("this box has no
  # systemd" and "systemd refused these units") collapsed into one sentence,
  # and by then eight steps had already written `~/.ccrc`, `~/.local/bin`,
  # `~/ccrc` and the operator's `~/.tmux.conf`. `ccrc-doctor-checks` already
  # keeps the same distinction as its own verdict ("systemctl is not on PATH,
  # but this box has unit files"), so refusing here is the install-time half of
  # a line doctor already draws.
  #
  # `loginctl` is deliberately NOT probed beside it: `_inst_linger` is the one
  # step built to survive its own failure, so an absent loginctl degrades to
  # "install: linger: could not enable" and doctor's own `linger` check reports
  # "loginctl is not on PATH" with its own remedy. A box with no systemd cannot
  # run a fleet at all; a box that cannot enable linger today still installs.
  # THE SERVICE-MANAGER PROBE, one per platform, refusing for the same reason
  # on both: `_inst_enable` is step 10 of 14, and a box that cannot supervise
  # anything must be told so BEFORE nine steps have written ~/.ccrc,
  # ~/.local/bin, ~/ccrc and the operator's ~/.tmux.conf. What changed when
  # macOS arrived is only WHICH binary is missing — the refusal, its timing
  # and its "nothing was written" promise are unchanged.
  #
  # `launchctl` is in the macOS base system and cannot realistically be
  # absent, so the Darwin arm reads as paranoia. It is kept deliberately
  # symmetrical anyway: this line is where a reader learns what this verb
  # needs, and an arm that silently assumes its dependency is the one a later
  # port copies.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    command -v launchctl >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || _ccrc_die "launchctl is required by 'ccrc install' — every ccrc service and every ccd session is a launchd user agent, and this box has no launchctl on PATH. Nothing on this box was written or changed."
    # THE ONE macOS DEPENDENCY THAT REALLY IS MISSING ON A FRESH BOX, and the
    # reason it is checked HERE rather than left to fail somewhere in ccd:
    # macOS ships bash 3.2.57 (2007, GPLv2), and `ccd` needs 4.4+ — it uses
    # `local -A`, `[[ -v arr[k] ]]`, `mapfile` and `BASHPID`. A box whose
    # `bash` is 3.2 does not fail cleanly: `ccd` dies on a SYNTAX ERROR at a
    # line number, mid-session, having already started a pane. Refusing at
    # install time, by version, with the remedy, is the whole difference.
    local _bv; _bv="${BASH_VERSINFO[0]:-0}.${BASH_VERSINFO[1]:-0}"
    if [ "${BASH_VERSINFO[0]:-0}" -lt 5 ] \
       && { [ "${BASH_VERSINFO[0]:-0}" -lt 4 ] || [ "${BASH_VERSINFO[1]:-0}" -lt 4 ]; }; then
      _ccrc_die "bash $_bv is too old for ccrc — ccd needs 4.4 or newer (associative arrays, \`[[ -v \]]\`, BASHPID — and empty-array expansions under set -u, fatal before 4.4) and macOS ships 3.2.57 as /bin/bash for licensing reasons. Install a current bash and put it ahead of /bin on PATH: brew install bash. Nothing on this box was written or changed."
    fi
    # tmux IS THE SUBSTRATE, not an optional nicety: every ccd session is a
    # tmux session, and a box without it installs cleanly and then cannot
    # start a single pane. macOS does not ship it.
    command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || _ccrc_die "tmux is required by ccrc — every ccd session is a tmux session — and macOS does not ship it. Install it: brew install tmux. Nothing on this box was written or changed."
    # flock: ccd already refuses BY NAME at three workspace sites ("flock
    # (util-linux) is unavailable — refusing to create a workspace
    # unserialised"), so without it this box installs and then refuses every
    # workspace it is asked to make. macOS has no flock; Homebrew's `flock`
    # formula is the portable one and takes the same flags.
    command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || _ccrc_die "flock is required by ccrc — ccd serialises every workspace creation with it and refuses rather than racing — and macOS does not ship it. Install it: brew install flock. Nothing on this box was written or changed."
  else
    command -v systemctl >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _ccrc_die "systemctl is required by 'ccrc install' — every ccrc service and every ccd session is a systemd --user unit, and this box has no systemctl on PATH. Install/enable systemd, or do not run a fleet on this box. Nothing on this box was written or changed."
  fi

  INST_DEGRADED=()
  _inst_banner
  _inst_roster
  _inst_accounts_sh
  _inst_env
  _inst_agent_env
  _inst_rc
  _inst_tree
  _inst_bins
  _inst_files
  _inst_stamp
  _inst_units
  _inst_enable
  _inst_linger
  _inst_dirs
  _inst_graphify_engine
  _inst_hooks
  _inst_skills
  _inst_graphify_skill
  _inst_graph_always_on_off
  _inst_graph_noise
  _inst_graph_excludes
  _inst_graph_hooks_off
  _inst_wrappers

  # ── THE LANDING BLOCK, AND WHY DOCTOR IS THE LAST WORD ─────────────────
  # Every step above either converged or died, so "done" is a statement about
  # the steps and nothing else — which is exactly why it is not the last line.
  # The spec's requirement is that `install` ENDS WITH `ccrc doctor`, and
  # doctor's exit code becomes this verb's: a box whose doctor fails is not a
  # finished install, and reporting exit 0 beside `FAIL linger` would be this
  # file's own "asserting a state that was never measured" defect with the
  # measurement printed directly above it. The house table needs no translation
  # — doctor's 1 already means "the tool ran and the answer was bad" — and the
  # install steps' own failures exited 1 through `_ccrc_die` long before here.
  #
  # THE ADDRESS IS READ BACK OUT OF THE FILE THIS RUN WROTE, through
  # `_box_env_value`/`BOX_ENV_FILE` — the reader and the path this file already
  # holds exactly one of (D-85, D-88). Hardcoding `127.0.0.1:7788` would be
  # right on a fresh box and a lie on every re-run over an operator's edited
  # `ccrc.env`, which is the one case where a person actually needs this line.
  # The literals below are reached only when the file names neither key: a
  # hand-edited env file that dropped them still boots, because
  # `server/src/config.ts` carries the same two defaults.
  # THE CLOSING LINE IS A MEASUREMENT TOO (fix round 1, Minor 1). "every step
  # above converged" is false in exactly the case this verb added an exception
  # for — a linger nobody could enable — and the transcript is the one thing an
  # operator scrolls back to when the exit code is 1. So the clean run keeps the
  # sentence it earned and the degraded one names what it is missing, in the
  # same words the step above used.
  if [ "${#INST_DEGRADED[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "install: done — every step above converged"
  else
    local word="steps" names
    [ "${#INST_DEGRADED[@]}" -eq 1 ] && word="step"
    names="$(IFS=', '; printf '%s' "${INST_DEGRADED[*]}")"
    echo "install: done — converged with ${#INST_DEGRADED[@]} degraded $word ($names)"
  fi
  # ── THE ROLE GATE ON THE SERVER-ONLY LINES (Stage 4, Task 5) ───────────
  # The PWA address and the passphrase gate are statements about a SERVER, and
  # a fleet box runs none: printing either there would send its operator to a
  # console that does not exist and a passphrase file nothing reads (doctor's
  # `auth` check SKIPs on that box for exactly this reason). What a fleet box
  # lands with instead is the one fact its operator needs next: the agent is
  # up, and the SERVER box's ccrc.env is where the pairing happens.
  if [ "$INST_ROLE" = fleet ]; then
    echo "install: agent: fleet role — ccrc-agent.service serves this box; set CCRC_AGENT_URL and CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN in the SERVER box's ccrc.env (CCRC_FLEET=remote) to reach it"
    echo "install: next: add your first session with: ccd menu   (and read $BOX_ENV_FILE)"
    cmd_doctor
    return
  fi
  local host="" port=""
  if [ -f "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    host="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_HOST)"
    port="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_PORT)"
  fi
  [ -n "$host" ] || host=127.0.0.1
  [ -n "$port" ] || port=7788
  echo "install: PWA: http://$host:$port/ (CCRC_HOST/CCRC_PORT in $BOX_ENV_FILE change this)"
  echo "install: next: add your first session with: ccd menu   (and read $BOX_ENV_FILE)"
  # ── THE ONE LINE ABOUT THE GATE, AND WHY THIS RUN DID NOT SET IT ───────
  # `install` writes NO passphrase, and that is the seed-once doctrine applied
  # to a credential rather than an omission: a passphrase this run invented
  # would be a password nobody chose sitting on disk, and a passphrase this run
  # PROMPTED for cannot be read at all — `install.sh` is documented as
  # `curl … | bash`, where stdin is the script itself, so a `read` here would
  # consume the installer's own text (`cmd_passwd`'s tty refusal is the same
  # hazard from the other side). So the box lands with the gate DARK, which is
  # the shipped default (`CCRC_AUTH` off), and doctor's `auth` check WARNs
  # about exactly that four lines below this one.
  #
  # THE THREE VARIABLES ARE NAMED TOGETHER because setting `CCRC_AUTH` alone
  # produces a console that can read and cannot act: `CCRC_ORIGIN` also gates
  # every /ws/* upgrade and every non-exempt write, and the server cannot warn
  # about a wrong one at boot (behind `tailscale serve` it never learns the
  # hostname it is reached under). An operator who arms the flag from this line
  # alone would find that out one tap at a time.
  echo "install: gate: this box has NO PWA passphrase — install never writes one. To arm the gate: ccrc passwd, then set CCRC_AUTH=on together with CCRC_RP_ID and CCRC_ORIGIN in $BOX_ENV_FILE, then: $(_svc_restart_hint ccrc.service)"
  cmd_doctor
}

# The two facts every later step is computed FROM, printed before any of them
# runs: which box this is (`$HOME` — not "this machine", because a run under
# `sudo` or as another user converges a different box entirely) and which tree
# it is converging from (`$CCRC_HERE`'s parent — the checkout, or `~/ccrc` on a
# box a deploy has already touched). Both are silent inputs otherwise, and a
# wrong one produces a run that SUCCEEDS at the wrong thing; naming them in
# line 1 turns that from a deduction into a reading.
#
# TWO MEASUREMENTS AND NOTHING ELSE. This line used to end "— single-box, local
# fleet mode on localhost", which is a CLAIM ABOUT THE BOX made before anything
# has been read, and it is false on the one box most likely to be running this:
# an install onto a box whose `ccrc.env` says `CCRC_FLEET=remote` keeps that
# file (it is user-owned) and then prints a banner asserting a mode the box does
# not have and this run did not establish. `$HOME` and the tree are things this
# process KNOWS; the mode belongs to `_inst_env`, which says it only in the case
# where it wrote it, and the address belongs to Task 8's landing block, which
# reads it back from the file. (Round-1 review, Minor 2.)
_inst_banner() {
  printf 'install: box: %s\n' "$HOME"
  printf 'install: tree: %s\n' "${CCRC_HERE%/*}"
}

_inst_roster() {   # seed-once: validate the SEED before placing it (deploy F1);
  # an existing roster is USER-OWNED and is never overwritten.
  #
  # The validation is `deploy.sh:229-246`'s `check_local_roster`, translated to
  # one box: seeding an unusable roster onto a box that had none poisons that
  # box PERMANENTLY, because the very rule that makes the file safe to own —
  # never overwritten — is also what stops the next run from fixing it. A
  # human would have to delete it by hand. So the seed is proven usable while
  # the only cost of being wrong is an exit code.
  local seed="$CCRC_HERE/../deploy/accounts.default.json" dest="$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json"
  mkdir -p "$HOME/.ccrc" || _ccrc_die "cannot create \$HOME/.ccrc"
  if [ -e "$dest" ]; then
    echo "install: roster: kept (user-owned, never overwritten)"
    return 0
  fi
  [ -f "$seed" ] || _ccrc_die "no roster seed at $seed — run install from inside the shipped tree"
  node "$CCRC_HERE/../deploy/gen-accounts.mjs" "$seed" >/dev/null \
    || _ccrc_die "the shipped roster seed does not validate — refusing to seed a box with it"
  local tmp="$dest.tmp.$$"
  cp "$seed" "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$dest" || { rm -f "$tmp"; _ccrc_die "seeding $dest failed"; }
  echo "install: roster: seeded single-account default"
}

_inst_accounts_sh() {   # ccrc-OWNED: regenerated every run FROM THE INSTALLED roster
  # (the local translation of deploy's read-back-over-ssh rule), atomically,
  # write-skipped when converged so idempotence is measurable on mtime.
  #
  # FROM THE INSTALLED ROSTER, never from the seed: `deploy.sh:290-299` reads
  # the BOX's `accounts.json` back over ssh before generating, precisely so the
  # generated file's own header line ("Generated from ~/.ccrc/accounts.json")
  # is true on a box whose operator edited that file. Here the "read back" is a
  # plain read of the file the previous step just guaranteed exists — and the
  # generator's refusal is the whole error path for a roster an operator broke
  # by hand: its `gen-accounts: remedy: …` line reaches stderr verbatim,
  # because stderr is deliberately NOT captured (`cmd_wrappers:2401-2409` makes
  # the same choice for the same reason — re-wording a fix into a shrug helps
  # nobody: :2401-2405 is the reasoning, :2406-2409 the code. This cited
  # :1125-1133 until Task 21's review round 1; that range is inside `usage()`,
  # which starts at :1109, and is the help text for the `wrappers` and `account`
  # verbs — `cmd_wrappers` itself does not begin until :2289).
  local dest="$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh" out tmp
  out="$(node "$CCRC_HERE/../deploy/gen-accounts.mjs" "$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json")" \
    || _ccrc_die "\$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json does not validate — fix it (or move it aside to reseed) and re-run"
  if [ -f "$dest" ] && [ "$out" = "$(cat "$dest")" ]; then
    echo "install: accounts.sh: converged"
    return 0
  fi
  tmp="$dest.tmp.$$"
  printf '%s\n' "$out" > "$tmp" && chmod 644 "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$dest" \
    || { rm -f "$tmp"; _ccrc_die "installing $dest failed"; }
  echo "install: accounts.sh: generated from \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json"
}

_inst_env() {   # seed-once, exactly like the roster: the env file is the box's
  # ONE machine-config file, holds operator secrets once configured, and is
  # never sourced and never overwritten by tooling.
  #
  # THROUGH `$BOX_ENV_FILE`, never the literal path (D-88): this verb is the
  # file's only WRITER and `status`/`doctor` are its readers, which is exactly
  # the arrangement `BOX_ENV_FILE`'s own comment (:91-101) exists to protect —
  # "three copies of a path is how two of them end up reading a file the third
  # does not write". The template below is a LITERAL, not a copy of
  # `deploy/ccrc.env.example`: the example file documents every key including
  # the ones that carry tokens, and a fresh single-box install needs four
  # values and a pointer to that file for the rest.
  local dest="$BOX_ENV_FILE"
  if [ -e "$dest" ]; then
    echo "install: ccrc.env: kept (user-owned, never overwritten)"
    return 0
  fi
  local tmp="$dest.tmp.$$"
  {
    printf '%s\n' "# ccrc box config — written once by ccrc install; edit freely, ccrc never rewrites it."
    printf '%s\n' "# Keys and defaults: deploy/ccrc.env.example in the shipped tree."
    printf '%s\n' "CCRC_HOST=127.0.0.1"
    printf '%s\n' "CCRC_PORT=7788"
    printf '%s\n' "CCRC_FLEET=local"
    printf '%s\n' "CCRC_PROJECTS_ROOT=$HOME/projects"
    # The role is RECORDED here rather than inferred (Stage 4, Task 5): the
    # unit files were the only thing on a box that stated its role at all
    # (D-73, `_box_role`), and a first-write label makes it a fact the box
    # carries. Seed-once applies: an existing box keeps its file and the role
    # stays inferred-with-a-label as today.
    printf '%s\n' "CCRC_ROLE=$INST_ROLE"
  } > "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$dest" || { rm -f "$tmp"; _ccrc_die "writing $dest failed"; }
  echo "install: ccrc.env: written (localhost, local fleet mode)"
}

# ── _inst_agent_env — the fleet role's one credential file (Stage 4, Task 5) ─
# `~/.ccrc/agent.env` is what `ccrc-agent.service`'s REQUIRED
# `EnvironmentFile=` reads, and until this step existed it had no local writer
# at all — which is exactly why `_inst_units` used to refuse the agent's unit
# outright (D-73: a fleet box had no installer path). Seed-once, like every
# user-owned file above it: the box's operator edits it (bind address, port,
# projects root — `deploy/ccrc-agent.env.example` documents the rest) and ccrc
# never rewrites it.
#
# THE PROMPTS ARE TTY-ONLY, `cmd_passwd`'s refusal for `cmd_passwd`'s reason:
# under `curl … | bash` stdin is the INSTALLER SCRIPT, so a read here would
# take a line of shell as this fleet's bearer token. The token itself is read
# with `-s` (never echoed) under the same two traps `cmd_passwd` arms — a
# Ctrl-C mid-read must not leave the terminal with echo off, and must LEAVE.
# The seed-once check runs BEFORE the tty gate, so a converged fleet box
# re-runs (and `ccrc update` re-runs) non-interactively.
#
# 0600 VIA `umask 077` ON THE TEMP *AND* `chmod 600` BEFORE THE RENAME: the
# umask means no instant exists in which the token sits readable at any name,
# and the chmod is the stated mode (a permissive umask must not widen it).
_inst_agent_env() {
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = fleet ] || return 0
  local dest="$HOME/.ccrc/agent.env"
  if [ -e "$dest" ]; then
    echo "install: agent.env: kept (user-owned, never overwritten)"
    return 0
  fi
  [ -t 0 ] \
    || _ccrc_die "stdin is not a terminal, and --role fleet will not read the agent token from a pipe or a file. Under \`curl … | bash\` stdin is the INSTALLER SCRIPT, so a read here would take a line of shell as this fleet's bearer token. Run 'ccrc install --role fleet' from a terminal. Nothing was written to \$HOME/.ccrc/agent.env."
  local url="" token=""
  printf '%s\n' "$PROG: configuring this box as a FLEET host — two values land in \$HOME/.ccrc/agent.env (0600); the token is not echoed; Ctrl-C aborts" >&2
  if ! IFS= read -rp "Server WS URL (where this fleet's ccrc server runs, e.g. ws://203.0.113.7:7788 or https://mybox.example.com): " url; then
    printf '\n' >&2
    _ccrc_die "no server URL was entered — nothing was written"
  fi
  [ -n "$url" ] || _ccrc_die "the server URL is empty — nothing was written"
  # `cmd_passwd`'s two traps, for its stated reason: one restores echo on ANY
  # exit, the other restores it and LEAVES on Ctrl-C — a single handler that
  # only ran `stty echo` would turn Ctrl-C into "echo back on, prompt still
  # running", i.e. the token typed in cleartext.
  trap 'stty echo 2>/dev/null' EXIT
  trap 'stty echo 2>/dev/null; printf "\n" >&2; exit 130' INT TERM
  if ! IFS= read -rsp "Agent token (becomes CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN — the bearer the server must present; generate with: openssl rand -hex 32): " token; then
    printf '\n' >&2
    _ccrc_die "no agent token was entered — nothing was written"
  fi
  printf '\n' >&2
  trap - EXIT INT TERM
  [ -n "$token" ] || _ccrc_die "the agent token is empty — nothing was written"
  local tmp="$dest.tmp.$$"
  if ! (
    umask 077
    {
      printf '%s\n' "# ccrc fleet-box agent config — written once by 'ccrc install --role fleet'; edit freely, ccrc never rewrites it."
      printf '%s\n' "# Keys and defaults: deploy/ccrc-agent.env.example in the shipped tree."
      printf '%s\n' "CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN=$token"
      printf '%s\n' "CCRC_SERVER_URL=$url"
    } > "$tmp"
  ) || ! chmod 600 "$tmp" || ! mv -f "$tmp" "$dest"; then
    rm -f "$tmp"
    _ccrc_die "writing $dest failed"
  fi
  echo "install: agent.env: written (0600 — CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN and CCRC_SERVER_URL set; neither value is ever printed)"
}

_inst_rc() {   # seed-once, the third file on the user-owned side of the rule
  # this verb's header states: `~/.ccrc/remote-control` decides whether `ccd`
  # spawns this box's sessions with `--remote-control` (claude.ai
  # discoverability) or without it. It is OPERATOR-OWNED — created once, never
  # rewritten — and here the usual reason ("their edits, their file") is the
  # weaker half. The strong one: this flag is what every session on the box is
  # SPAWNED AS, so a step that overwrote it would change the shape of every
  # live pane at its next respawn, on a box whose operator asked for an
  # install and not for that. D-99 in the stage-2e plan records the outage
  # shape; `ccd/ccd`'s `_rc_enabled` is the reader and the authority on what
  # the bytes mean.
  #
  # OFF ON A FRESH BOX, and the asymmetry with `deploy.sh`'s fleet lane (which
  # seeds `on`) is deliberate rather than an inconsistency. `--remote-control`
  # PUBLISHES a session to claude.ai; a box nobody asked that of must not
  # start doing it because an installer defaulted it. The fleet lane seeds
  # `on` because that box was ALREADY running RC before the flag existed and
  # off would be a silent change to what it does — the same question, asked
  # about two boxes with two different existing answers.
  #
  # THE TRAILING NEWLINE IS THE CONTRACT, not formatting. `_rc_enabled` reads
  # the file with `IFS= read -r`, and bash's `read` returns NON-ZERO at
  # EOF-before-delimiter: `printf 'off'` with no newline reads as OFF for the
  # wrong reason, and `printf 'on'` with no newline reads as OFF outright.
  # `server/test/ccd-rc-flag.test.ts` pins that measurement and
  # `ccrc-install.test.ts` asserts these bytes WHOLE, newline included.
  #
  # THE PATH IS A LOCAL, not a fourth entry in the top-level block at :91-107.
  # That block exists because `version`, `status` and `doctor` all READ the
  # files in it from this file; this one has exactly one toucher here — the
  # function you are reading — and its readers live in other tools that cannot
  # see a variable of ours anyway (`ccd`'s `CCRC_RC_FILE`, and
  # `ccrc-doctor-checks`' own literal, which is the deliberate second spelling
  # D-92 already records for `CCRC_UNIT_DIR`). `single-definition.test.ts`
  # holds all four spellings equal, which is the mechanism the promise would
  # otherwise be.
  local dest="$HOME/.ccrc/remote-control"
  if [ -e "$dest" ]; then
    echo "install: remote-control: kept (operator-owned)"
    return 0
  fi
  local tmp="$dest.tmp.$$"
  printf '%s\n' "off" > "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$dest" \
    || { rm -f "$tmp"; _ccrc_die "writing $dest failed"; }
  echo "install: remote-control: off (fresh installs default off — edit ~/.ccrc/remote-control to 'on' for claude.ai discoverability)"
}

_inst_tree() {   # place the shipped tree at ~/ccrc — the layout every sibling
  # contract assumes (shim target, _dr_pkg_candidates, BASH_SOURCE siblings; see
  # BOX_TREE_DIR's own header). Runs BEFORE the executables so the launcher's
  # target exists at the moment the launcher lands on PATH.
  #
  # ── THE DIVERGENCE FROM deploy.sh, STATED ONCE ─────────────────────────
  # `deploy.sh` EXCLUDES `dist` from its rsync and runs `npm ci && npm run
  # build` on the box, because it ships SOURCES across boxes and the box is the
  # only place that can build them. `install` IS the box: the checkout it runs
  # from already holds the build (that is what the two preflights below
  # measure), so the tree ships WITH `dist`/`dist-pwa` and the only thing left
  # to fetch is the runtime dependency set, which is not in git at all. Hence
  # `npm ci --omit=dev` and no build step — and hence the preflights, because
  # the artifact deploy would have built on the box is now something this run
  # can only inherit or refuse.
  #
  # TWO PREFLIGHTS AND TWO SENTENCES. A missing server build and a missing PWA
  # bundle are two different builds with two different remedies (`npm run build`
  # in `server/` vs in `pwa/`); collapsing them into one "the tree is not built"
  # is the overloaded seam this file's header bans.
  local src dest="$BOX_TREE_DIR" at
  src="$(cd "$CCRC_HERE/.." && pwd -P)" || _ccrc_die "cannot resolve the running tree"
  [ -f "$src/server/dist/server/src/index.js" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "no server build at $src/server/dist — build first: bash install.sh (or npm run build in server/ and pwa/)"
  [ -f "$src/server/dist-pwa/index.html" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "no PWA bundle at $src/server/dist-pwa — build first: bash install.sh (or npm run build in pwa/)"
  # D-1159 — THE THIRD PREFLIGHT, and it is role-gated because the artifact is.
  # `ccrc-agent.service`'s ExecStart is `$HOME/ccrc/agent/dist/agent/src/index.js`.
  # Without this line a fleet install does not fail HERE — it fails LATER, having
  # already placed the tree and restarted a LIVE fleet's agent onto a directory
  # with no entry point. Measured on the reference fleet: `install.sh` built
  # server and pwa only, so `ccrc install --role fleet` from a source checkout
  # took `ccrc-agent.service` down with MODULE_NOT_FOUND and the server lost its
  # only path to the box until the agent was built by hand. Refusing here is
  # this verb's own stated doctrine — an install that half-ran is worse than one
  # that did not start — and it is the same shape, and the same third sentence,
  # the two preflights above already use.
  #
  # D-1161 — THE GATE IS `fleet`, NOT `!= server`, and the first draft of this
  # line had it wrong in a way its own comment then asserted was right. Only
  # `fleet` installs the unit (`_inst_units`: `role_unit=ccrc-agent.service`
  # under `[ "$INST_ROLE" = fleet ]`) and only `fleet` enables or restarts it
  # (`_inst_enable`: same test). `both` — the DEFAULT role, a single box that
  # serves and runs sessions — gets no agent unit at all, because a single box
  # drives ccd directly in `local` mode and never speaks to an agent. So
  # `!= server` refused the default install over an artifact that role never
  # installs, never enables and never starts: a new failure mode, introduced by
  # the fix for an old one.
  [ "$INST_ROLE" != fleet ] || [ -f "$src/agent/dist/agent/src/index.js" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "no agent build at $src/agent/dist — build first: bash install.sh (or npm run build in agent/)"
  # THE SELF-COPY GUARD, ON PHYSICAL PATHS. On a box a deploy has already
  # touched — and on every re-run of this verb — `ccrc` IS `~/ccrc/ccd/ccrc`,
  # so source and destination are one directory. `rsync -a --delete X X/` is not
  # a no-op there: it copies the tree INTO ITSELF (`~/ccrc/ccrc/…`) and then
  # runs a `--delete` pass across the live tree it is reading from. `pwd -P` on
  # both sides because the two spellings arrive by different routes ($HOME, and
  # `$CCRC_HERE`'s parent from `${BASH_SOURCE[0]}`): a symlinked component in
  # either — /home -> /export/home is the classic — makes a textual comparison
  # answer "different" for one directory, which is the exact case that must
  # not reach rsync.
  at="$dest"
  [ -d "$dest" ] && at="$(cd "$dest" && pwd -P)"
  if [ "$src" = "$at" ]; then
    echo "install: tree: already running from \$HOME/ccrc"
  else
    command -v rsync >/dev/null 2>&1 || _ccrc_die "rsync is required to place the tree — sudo apt install rsync"
    mkdir -p "$dest" || _ccrc_die "cannot create $dest"
    # The excludes are deploy.sh's (`:319-326`) minus `dist`, and they are not
    # tidiness: `*.env` and `ccrc-mail.token` are gitignored files holding LIVE
    # TOKENS, which `-a` would carry into a second, unmanaged copy at whatever
    # mode the checkout has. `node_modules` is excluded in both directions —
    # rsync does not delete what it was told to ignore, so the runtime deps
    # installed below survive every later `--delete` run instead of being
    # destroyed and refetched.
    rsync -a --delete \
      --exclude node_modules --exclude .git --exclude '*.env' --exclude ccrc-mail.token \
      "$src/server" "$src/agent" "$src/shared" "$src/deploy" "$src/ccd" "$dest/" \
      || _ccrc_die "placing the tree at $dest failed"
    echo "install: tree: placed at \$HOME/ccrc"
  fi
  # `>&2` AND NOT `>/dev/null 2>&1` (fix round 1, Important 2). This is the
  # verb's most likely failure — a registry hiccup, no network, a lockfile out
  # of step with `package.json` — and swallowing npm's own error text left the
  # operator with the die below and no way to see which. That contradicts the
  # rule `_inst_accounts_sh` writes down 90 lines up ("stderr is deliberately
  # NOT captured … re-wording a fix into a shrug helps nobody") and diverges
  # from deploy, whose `npm ci` runs over ssh with both streams attached.
  # npm's STDOUT is redirected INTO stderr rather than kept, because an install
  # transcript is one `install: <step>: <result>` line per step on stdout and
  # "added 41 packages in 3s" is not this run's result — the file's two-register
  # split (results on stdout, everything else on stderr), applied to a child.
  ( cd "$dest/server" && npm ci --omit=dev --no-audit --no-fund >&2 ) \
    || _ccrc_die "npm ci in \$HOME/ccrc/server failed — the service cannot start without runtime deps"
  echo "install: tree: server runtime deps in place"
  # D-1161 — THE AGENT NEEDS ITS DEPS TOO, and D-1159 stopped one import short.
  # That fix made the ENTRY POINT exist; it did not make the tree STARTABLE.
  # The rsync above excludes `node_modules` in both directions and this function
  # ran npm in `server/` only, so a fleet install placed `agent/dist` beside no
  # `agent/node_modules` — and `agent/src/server.ts` imports `ws` on line 6
  # (`agent/package.json` declares `ws` and `node-pty` as runtime deps).
  # `_inst_enable` then restarts `ccrc-agent.service` and node dies with the
  # same ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND, one import further in. The reference fleet only
  # escaped it because an earlier `deploy.sh agent` had left a `node_modules`
  # behind — which is why the postmortem saw the missing dist and stopped there.
  # `deploy/build-release.sh` has shipped `agent/package{,-lock}.json` "for
  # `npm ci --omit=dev` on the box" since it was written; this is the call that
  # was always meant to make.
  if [ "$INST_ROLE" = fleet ]; then
    ( cd "$dest/agent" && npm ci --omit=dev --no-audit --no-fund >&2 ) \
      || _ccrc_die "npm ci in \$HOME/ccrc/agent failed — ccrc-agent.service cannot start without runtime deps"
    echo "install: tree: agent runtime deps in place"
  fi
}

# ── _inst_atomic — the local `install_atomic` ─────────────────────────────
# `deploy/deploy.sh:37-58` states the reason and it is not tidiness: bash
# executes a script LAZILY from a saved byte offset, so overwriting the inode of
# a file some process is executing makes that process resume inside the new
# bytes at the old offset. Measured on `ccd`, which is never idle on a fleet
# host — every `claude-session@` supervisor IS a long-running invocation of it.
# `mv -f` replaces the DIRECTORY ENTRY instead (rename(2), atomic): a running
# reader keeps the old inode to EOF and the next invocation gets the new file.
# `chmod` runs on the temp, BEFORE the rename, so the file is never live at its
# final name without its final mode — a supervisor exec'ing it in that window
# would get EACCES, and systemd's Restart=always would turn that into a crash
# loop.
#
# ── THREE THINGS THIS ADDS TO DEPLOY'S VERSION, ALL LOCAL-ONLY ────────────
#   1. IT COMPARES BYTES FIRST. deploy runs from another machine and cannot
#      cheaply read the destination; this runs ON the box. A converger that
#      rewrites what it did not change is one an operator cannot use to see
#      what a run actually did (`_inst_accounts_sh`'s rule, applied to files).
#   2. …but it still `chmod`s the converged file, rather than returning early.
#      Bytes equal and mode wrong is a real state — a `ccd` left at 0600 is a
#      box where every supervisor gets EACCES — and `chmod` moves ctime, never
#      mtime, so repairing costs nothing the idempotence assertions measure.
#   3. THE STRAY SWEEP RUNS AFTER THE RENAME, so the glob can only match
#      leftovers from an ABORTED earlier run: this run's temp has already been
#      renamed away. `cmd_wrappers:1423-1440` records the same finding — a run
#      killed between the copy and the rename leaves a mode-0755 file in a
#      directory that is ON PATH, and nothing else will ever remove it.
#
# `<dest>.tmp.$$` and not `cmd_wrappers`' leading-dot form: the destination
# names here are FIXED (ccd, ccrc, notify.sh …), never roster-derived, so the
# hazard that idiom exists for — a temp file whose name could be read as an
# ACCOUNT id — cannot arise, and `.tmp.` already puts the name outside
# `WRAPPER_ID_RE`. This spelling is the one the three seeding steps above
# already use.
#
# A missing `cmp` makes every destination look different, so the file is
# rewritten with identical bytes: the safe direction, and the reason the
# comparison is not guarded by a `command -v`.
_inst_atomic() {   # <src> <dest> <mode>
  local src="$1" dest="$2" mode="$3" tmp
  [ -f "$src" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "the shipped tree has no $src — is this a complete ccrc tree? (re-run after: bash install.sh)"
  if cmp -s "$src" "$dest" 2>/dev/null; then
    chmod "$mode" "$dest" || _ccrc_die "could not set mode $mode on $dest"
    return 0
  fi
  tmp="$dest.tmp.$$"
  if ! { cp -- "$src" "$tmp" && chmod "$mode" "$tmp" && mv -f -- "$tmp" "$dest"; }; then
    rm -f -- "$tmp"
    _ccrc_die "could not install $dest — nothing at that path was replaced"
  fi
  rm -f -- "$dest.tmp."*
}

# ── _inst_tree_copy — `_inst_atomic` for a DIRECTORY ─────────────────────
# `_inst_atomic` is single-file by construction (one `cmp`, one `cp`, one
# `chmod`, one `mv`), and a skill is a DIRECTORY: a `SKILL.md` beside a
# `references/` its own text sends a model to read by name. This is the same
# contract for that shape, and it keeps the three properties the file-level
# helper's callers already depend on:
#
#   1. IT COMPARES FIRST. `diff -r -q` is the identical check both skill
#      installers make before they touch a converged home, so "nothing to do"
#      costs one walk and rewrites nothing — the measurement an operator uses to
#      see what a run actually did (`_inst_accounts_sh`'s rule, applied to a
#      tree).
#   2. THE SWAP IS A RENAME, and the tree it replaces is moved ASIDE rather than
#      deleted, so a failed `mv` restores it. Deleting the destination first
#      would open a window in which a session resolving its skills finds neither
#      the old tree nor the new one — the half-installed state the installers'
#      own headers call worse than none.
#   3. IT DIES rather than degrades, like every step that calls it.
#
# A missing `diff` makes every destination look different, so the tree is
# re-copied with identical bytes: the safe direction, and the reason the
# comparison is not guarded by a `command -v` — exactly as `_inst_atomic`
# leaves its `cmp` unguarded. The installers this step runs afterwards refuse BY
# NAME without diff, so a box that has none is told so rather than left
# guessing.
_inst_tree_copy() {   # <src-dir> <dest-dir>
  local src="$1" dest="$2" tmp old
  [ -d "$src" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "the shipped tree has no $src — is this a complete ccrc tree? (re-run after: bash install.sh)"
  if [ -d "$dest" ] && diff -r -q "$src" "$dest" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    return 0
  fi
  # `.tmp.$$` and `.old.$$` are the two skill installers' own staging names, and
  # `.tmp.` is the spelling `_inst_atomic`'s stray sweep and the test suite's
  # both already look for.
  tmp="$dest.tmp.$$"
  old="$dest.old.$$"
  rm -rf -- "$tmp" "$old"
  if ! { mkdir -p -- "$tmp" && cp -a "$src/." "$tmp/"; }; then
    rm -rf -- "$tmp"
    _ccrc_die "could not stage $dest — nothing at that path was replaced"
  fi
  if [ -e "$dest" ] && ! mv -- "$dest" "$old"; then
    rm -rf -- "$tmp"
    _ccrc_die "could not move the existing $dest aside — nothing at that path was replaced"
  fi
  if ! mv -- "$tmp" "$dest"; then
    rm -rf -- "$tmp"
    [ -e "$old" ] && mv -- "$old" "$dest"
    _ccrc_die "could not install $dest"
  fi
  rm -rf -- "$old"
}

_inst_keep_aside() {   # <src> <dest> — copy a PERSONAL file aside before replacing it
  # Two of the files this verb installs land in the OPERATOR's namespace rather
  # than in ccrc's own: `~/.tmux.conf` and `~/.claude/statusline-command.sh`.
  # `deploy.sh` overwrites both without asking, and is right to — it runs
  # against a box that is by definition a fleet host. `ccrc install` runs
  # against whatever machine somebody typed it on, where a `~/.tmux.conf`
  # predating ccrc by years is an ordinary thing to find. Eating it is the
  # damage the verb's own contract ("re-running converges; it never damages an
  # existing install") promises not to do.
  #
  # ONLY WHEN THE BYTES DIFFER, which is what makes this run exactly once: after
  # the first install the file at that path IS the shipped one, so the second
  # run has nothing to save. `cmd_wrappers`' backup naming (`.pre-ccrc-<UTC>`)
  # and `cp -p`, so the copy keeps the mode and timestamps of what it replaced.
  local src="$1" dest="$2" stamp saved
  [ -e "$dest" ] || return 0
  cmp -s "$src" "$dest" 2>/dev/null && return 0
  stamp="$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)"
  saved="$dest.pre-ccrc-$stamp"
  cp -p -- "$dest" "$saved" \
    || _ccrc_die "could not copy $dest aside to $saved, so nothing at that path was replaced"
  echo "install: files: kept $saved (the file that was there)"
}

# ── _inst_shim — the launcher `ccrc` on PATH ─────────────────────────────
# THE BYTES BELOW ARE `deploy/deploy.sh`'s `install_ccrc_shim` HEREDOC, EXACTLY,
# and `server/test/ccrc-install.test.ts` extracts both and compares them. Two
# generators of one artifact is a drift waiting to happen — a box whose
# launcher came from the older of them fails in a way neither generator's own
# tests can see — so the agreement is a pin rather than a convention. It also
# means the BEHAVIOUR tests `agent/test/deploy-verify.test.ts:1478-1511` already
# run against those bytes (argv forwarded, exit code passed through, a by-name
# refusal when `~/ccrc/ccd/ccrc` is gone) hold for this copy without being
# written twice.
#
# Why a launcher at all, rather than `_inst_atomic ccd/ccrc .local/bin/ccrc`:
# `ccrc` is not self-contained (it sources `ccrc-doctor-checks`, which sources
# `ccrc-wrapper-shape`, and `cmd_adopt` execs `ccrc-adopt`, all resolved through
# `${BASH_SOURCE[0]}`, which bash does not resolve through a symlink), and
# installing all four files into `~/.local/bin` instead breaks `_check_node`,
# whose candidates would become `~/.local/{server,agent}/package.json`. Both
# constraints point at the same place: `ccrc` has to RUN from inside the tree at
# `~/ccrc/ccd/`, which `_inst_tree` has just placed. deploy.sh:95-128 is the
# long form of this paragraph.
#
# A COST THAT USED TO BE STATED HERE: the generated file's provenance line
# used to name only `deploy.sh`, so on a box that installed itself via
# `install.sh` that line was the one inaccurate sentence in the artifact. Task
# 6 (Stage 2e) fixed it in the one commit the pin requires — both copies now
# name both generators, and the refusal itself names both ways a box gets its
# tree ('bash install.sh' on this box, or deploy/deploy.sh against it) rather
# than assuming the deploy lane.
#
# `if`, not `|| { … }`, inside the heredoc: a `}` in column 1 would end THIS
# function as far as every `/name\(\) \{([\s\S]*?)\n\}/` probe in the suites is
# concerned — the idiom this file's own tests read it with. deploy.sh:145-149
# records the same constraint for the same lines.
_inst_shim() {   # the launcher's bytes, to stdout
  cat <<'CCRC_SHIM'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# GENERATED — by deploy/deploy.sh's install_ccrc_shim or ccrc install's
# _inst_shim; the two emit identical bytes and a test pins it. Do not edit on
# the box. Edit ccd/ccrc or deploy/deploy.sh in the repo and re-run whichever
# generator made this file; this file is only a launcher.
#
# `ccrc` finds its check table, its wrapper-shape library, its adopt script and
# the shipped package.json RELATIVE TO ITSELF, so it has to be run from inside
# the tree the deploy rsyncs. That is why this is an exec into ~/ccrc/ccd and
# not a copy of the script: bash does not resolve ${BASH_SOURCE[0]} through a
# symlink, and a copy on PATH would resolve every one of those to ~/.local.
#
# `if`, not `|| { … }`: a `}` in column 1 would end the enclosing shell
# function as far as every `/name\(\) \{([\s\S]*?)\n\}/` probe in
# agent/test/deploy-verify.test.ts is concerned — that is the idiom this file's
# helpers are all read with, and a heredoc that quietly truncates the body
# those tests see is worse than a slightly longer refusal.
CCRC_SHIPPED="$HOME/ccrc/ccd/ccrc"
if [ ! -x "$CCRC_SHIPPED" ]; then
  echo "ccrc: $CCRC_SHIPPED is missing or not executable — this box's shipped ccrc tree is gone, so there is nothing for this launcher to run. Re-install: run 'bash install.sh' from a ccrc checkout on this box, or (on a fleet-deployed box) re-run deploy/deploy.sh against it." >&2
  exit 1
fi
exec "$CCRC_SHIPPED" "$@"
CCRC_SHIM
}

_inst_bins() {   # what has to be ON PATH: the two executables, and the launcher
  # AFTER `_inst_tree`, always. The launcher execs `~/ccrc/ccd/ccrc` and refuses
  # by name when that file is absent, so installing it first would put a
  # `ccrc` on PATH that can only refuse — on the box of an operator who is at
  # that moment watching an install run.
  #
  # OUT OF THE PLACED TREE (`$BOX_TREE_DIR`), never out of `$CCRC_HERE/..`.
  # After `_inst_tree` the two are identical, so no assertion can tell them
  # apart — but the invariant can: the `ccd` on PATH and the `ccd` inside the
  # tree the launcher execs are then one version BY CONSTRUCTION, rather than
  # by both happening to come from the same run. It is the local form of the
  # "four files arrive in ONE rsync of ONE tree" property deploy.sh:114-118
  # gives as the reason the launcher exists.
  local bin="$HOME/.local/bin" tree="$BOX_TREE_DIR" gen
  mkdir -p "$bin" || _ccrc_die "cannot create $bin"
  _inst_atomic "$tree/ccd/ccd" "$bin/ccd" 755
  # `ccd-cap-scopes` FINDS UNCAPPED tmux pane CGROUP SCOPES and caps them.
  # macOS has neither cgroups nor scopes, and `_inst_units`' Darwin arm
  # installs no timer to run it — so placing the binary there would put a file
  # on PATH that can only ever be a no-op, and name it in the line below as
  # though this box had gained something. Skipped, and said out loud.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" != darwin ]; then
    _inst_atomic "$tree/ccd/ccd-cap-scopes" "$bin/ccd-cap-scopes" 755
    # graphify Task 10 (O3/O6b): the sweep executable, every ROLE but — like
    # `ccd-cap-scopes` above — not Darwin: its timer only installs on the
    # systemd arm, and the script leans on GNU stat/date and flock(1), which
    # macOS does not ship. A binary whose runner never fires there would be
    # the same no-op cap-scopes' own comment warns about. (Measured: the macos
    # CI leg ran the sweep suite against BSD userland — every pass aborted.)
    # `deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs`'s `TOOLCHAIN_EXECUTABLES` knows the name, or
    # the orphan scan in `_inst_wrappers` (every role) would report it.
    _inst_atomic "$tree/ccd/ccd-graph-sweep" "$bin/ccd-graph-sweep" 755
  fi
  # The generated launcher is staged under a name `_inst_atomic`'s own sweep
  # matches (`<dest>.tmp.*`), so a run killed between these two lines leaves
  # nothing the next run does not remove.
  gen="$bin/ccrc.tmp.$$.new"
  _inst_shim > "$gen" || { rm -f -- "$gen"; _ccrc_die "could not generate the ccrc launcher at $gen"; }
  _inst_atomic "$gen" "$bin/ccrc" 755
  rm -f -- "$gen"
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    echo "install: bins: ccd and the ccrc launcher in \$HOME/.local/bin (no ccd-cap-scopes — it caps cgroup scopes, and macOS has none; no ccd-graph-sweep — its timer is systemd-only)"
  else
    echo "install: bins: ccd, ccd-cap-scopes, ccd-graph-sweep and the ccrc launcher in \$HOME/.local/bin"
  fi
}

_inst_files() {   # the artifacts the box RUNS that are not on PATH
  # Stage 1's list (`deploy.sh:374-387`): the session hook and its installer,
  # the swap-notify hook, the tmux config that carries truecolor to an attaching
  # client, and the statusline that writes `~/.cc-limits` telemetry. Every one
  # of them was running on the live fleet host with nothing installing it.
  local tree="$BOX_TREE_DIR"
  mkdir -p "$HOME/.cc-sessions" || _ccrc_die "cannot create \$HOME/.cc-sessions"
  _inst_atomic "$tree/ccd/session-hook.sh" "$HOME/.cc-sessions/session-hook.sh" 755
  _inst_atomic "$tree/ccd/install-session-hooks.sh" "$HOME/.cc-sessions/install-session-hooks.sh" 755
  _inst_atomic "$tree/deploy/notify.sh" "$HOME/.cc-sessions/notify.sh" 755
  mkdir -p "$HOME/.claude" || _ccrc_die "cannot create \$HOME/.claude"
  # The two files in the operator's own namespace — see `_inst_keep_aside`.
  # `statusline-command.sh` is 0644 in the repository and 0755 on the box: a
  # copy that preserved the source mode leaves a statusline that never runs,
  # and a box that silently writes no rate-limit telemetry at all.
  _inst_keep_aside "$tree/ccd/tmux.conf" "$HOME/.tmux.conf"
  _inst_atomic "$tree/ccd/tmux.conf" "$HOME/.tmux.conf" 644
  _inst_keep_aside "$tree/ccd/statusline-command.sh" "$HOME/.claude/statusline-command.sh"
  _inst_atomic "$tree/ccd/statusline-command.sh" "$HOME/.claude/statusline-command.sh" 755
  echo "install: files: session hooks, notify.sh, tmux.conf and the statusline in place"
}

# ── _inst_stamp_shipped — the release-artifact arm (stage 4, Task 6) ──────
# A box installing from an extracted release tarball has no repository to
# measure — `git rev-parse` fails there BY DESIGN — but the artifact carries
# its identity: `deploy/build-release.sh` writes `build.json` (sha, ref,
# builtAt, dirty:false, version) into the tree it tars, and the per-file
# MANIFEST covers it. When the git measurement is impossible and the tree
# ships that stamp, installing it IS a measurement — of the artifact, made on
# the release machine, verified file-by-file on this box before this step
# ran. It is validated through `_box_build_fields` (this file's ONE stamp
# parser — a second jq filter here is exactly what single-definition.test.ts
# forbids) and a stamp that does not parse is refused, falling through to the
# caller's ordinary skip line. The GIT measurement still wins whenever it is
# possible: a checkout's own state is the truth about a checkout, and a
# stray build.json beside a real repository must never outvote it.
_inst_stamp_shipped() {
  local shipped="$CCRC_HERE/../build.json"
  [ -f "$shipped" ] || return 1
  _box_build_fields "$shipped" || return 1
  mkdir -p "${BOX_STAMP_FILE%/*}" || _ccrc_die "cannot create ${BOX_STAMP_FILE%/*}"
  _inst_atomic "$shipped" "$BOX_STAMP_FILE" 644
  local said=""
  [ "${BOX_BUILD[3]}" = true ] && said=", dirty"
  [ -n "${BOX_BUILD[4]}" ] && said="$said, ${BOX_BUILD[4]}"
  echo "install: stamp: ${BOX_BUILD[0]} (${BOX_BUILD[1]}$said, shipped in the release artifact)"
  return 0
}

_inst_stamp() {   # what this box is RUNNING, stated by the box itself
  # `deploy.sh:75-93`'s `stamp_build`, translated to one machine. Until this
  # step existed the ONLY writer of `~/.ccrc/build.json` was a deploy, so a box
  # that installed itself reported "unstamped" for ever — honest, and useless:
  # it is the answer `ccrc version` and `ccrc status` exist to give, and the
  # first question any incident asks.
  #
  # MEASURED, NEVER ASSUMED. A dirty tree may install (stage 1 does not forbid
  # it) but the stamp SAYS dirty — "a clean sha nobody measured is the forgery
  # class this repo bans by name", in deploy's own words. Same reason the
  # skipped case below writes NOTHING rather than carrying a previous stamp
  # forward: a stamp that outlives the tree it described is a lie with a
  # timestamp on it.
  #
  # THROUGH `"$BOX_STAMP_FILE"`, never the literal path — `ccrc:91-101`'s rule,
  # which D-88 already applied to `ccrc.env` when `install` became that file's
  # writer. This file holds the stamp's only reader (`_box_build_fields`) and,
  # from here, its second writer; two spellings is how a reader and a writer
  # come to disagree about which file they mean.
  # ── THE SKIP NAMES ITS OWN CAUSE (fix round 1, Important 1) ─────────────
  # Not stamping is a legitimate outcome — `~/ccrc` on a box a deploy touched is
  # an rsync of a tree and not a repository (deploy.sh says so where it explains
  # why `stamp_build` runs on the deploying machine), and an install is not a
  # failure for having been run from a directory rather than a clone. So this
  # stays a SKIP in every arm below, at exit 0.
  #
  # What it may not do is claim a cause it did not measure. The one sentence
  # this step shipped with — "not a git checkout" — covered THREE conditions,
  # and was false in two of them: git absent from PATH (the fix is `apt install
  # git`, not looking for a repository), and git PRESENT AND REFUSING a
  # repository that really is one — `fatal: detected dubious ownership`, exit
  # 128, which is what a clone owned by another user, or the same clone reached
  # under sudo, produces. Both measured. The cost of guessing wrong is not
  # cosmetic: the box goes on reporting `unstamped` for ever, which is the exact
  # condition this step exists to end, while the transcript sends the operator
  # to inspect a directory instead of at git.
  #
  # So: probe the dependency BY NAME first (`cmd_install`'s own node rule, one
  # register down), then let GIT say why — its stderr, first line only,
  # control bytes stripped, truncated. Never raw foreign bytes on a terminal:
  # `_box_build_fields:264-267` rejects a stamp field carrying a control byte
  # for the same reason ("a backspace would let the printed sha lie on a
  # terminal"), and a git that writes a megabyte of stderr must not become the
  # install transcript.
  local src sha ref dirty version vfield tmp why rc=0 dest="$BOX_STAMP_FILE"
  src="$CCRC_HERE/.."
  if ! command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _inst_stamp_shipped && return 0
    echo "install: stamp: skipped (no git on PATH) — ccrc version will say unstamped"
    return 0
  fi
  sha="$(git -C "$src" rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null)" || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ -z "$sha" ]; then
    _inst_stamp_shipped && return 0
    echo "install: stamp: skipped (git rev-parse HEAD exited 0 but printed no sha) — ccrc version will say unstamped"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    # The one ORDINARY landing of the shipped-stamp arm: an extracted release
    # tarball is exactly "a tree git refuses to call a repository".
    _inst_stamp_shipped && return 0
    # A SECOND invocation, reached only because the first one failed, and only
    # to read the diagnosis the first one discarded. Capturing both streams from
    # one call needs a temp file or fd juggling, for a saving of one process on
    # a path that is already the failure path. If this call disagrees with the
    # first — a repository that appeared in between — `why` is empty and the
    # line says so rather than inventing a reason.
    why="$(git -C "$src" rev-parse HEAD 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
    why="${why%%$'\n'*}"
    why="${why//[[:cntrl:]]/}"
    why="${why:0:200}"
    [ -n "$why" ] || why="it printed nothing on stderr"
    echo "install: stamp: skipped (git rev-parse HEAD exited $rc: $why) — ccrc version will say unstamped"
    return 0
  fi
  ref="$(git -C "$src" rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null)" || ref=""
  [ -n "$ref" ] || ref="HEAD"
  if git -C "$src" diff --quiet 2>/dev/null && git -C "$src" diff --cached --quiet 2>/dev/null; then
    dirty=false
  else
    dirty=true
  fi
  # Stage 4, Task 1: the release tag rides as an ADDITIVE fifth field, present
  # iff a vX.Y.Z tag points at the installed commit — `deploy.sh`'s own
  # derivation, translated to this machine. Only the release shape qualifies
  # (which also keeps refname characters a JSON string can't carry, like `"`,
  # out of the stamp); no match is the ordinary case, not a failure.
  version="$(git -C "$src" tag --points-at HEAD 2>/dev/null \
    | grep -E '^v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$' | head -n1)" || version=""
  vfield=""
  [ -n "$version" ] && vfield=",\"version\":\"$version\""
  mkdir -p "${dest%/*}" || _ccrc_die "cannot create ${dest%/*}"
  tmp="$dest.tmp.$$"
  printf '{"sha":"%s","ref":"%s","builtAt":"%s","dirty":%s%s}\n' \
    "$sha" "$ref" "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" "$dirty" "$vfield" > "$tmp" \
    && chmod 644 "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$dest" \
    || { rm -f "$tmp"; _ccrc_die "writing $dest failed"; }
  local said=""
  [ "$dirty" = true ] && said=", dirty"
  [ -n "$version" ] && said="$said, $version"
  echo "install: stamp: $sha ($ref$said)"
}

# ── _inst_units — the unit files, and the one this box's ROLE decides ─────
# `deploy.sh:402-417`'s copy set, role-gated, plus the escape that set records.
#
# ── THE ROLE GATE THAT REPLACED THE BLANKET `ccrc-agent.service` REFUSAL ──
# This verb used to refuse the agent's unit OUTRIGHT, and the reason was one
# character: `deploy/ccrc-agent.service:6` is `EnvironmentFile=%h/.ccrc/agent.env`
# with NO leading `-`, i.e. REQUIRED — and no step here wrote `agent.env`, so
# installing the unit manufactured one systemd refuses to start, a red line in
# every `systemctl --user` listing describing a thing the operator never asked
# for. Stage 4's Task 5 keeps that reasoning and moves the gate onto the ROLE:
# `--role fleet` now WRITES `agent.env` (`_inst_agent_env`, seed-once, before
# this step runs), so on a fleet box the required file exists by the time the
# unit lands — and it is `ccrc.service` that must not be given to THAT box,
# because a fleet host runs no server. Every other role keeps today's exact
# set: `ccrc.service` in, agent unit out. (Compare `deploy/ccrc.service:12`,
# whose `EnvironmentFile=-%h/.ccrc/ccrc.env` carries the dash and therefore
# boots on a box with no env file at all.)
#
# ── THE `\x2d` IN THE DESTINATION IS LOAD-BEARING ────────────────────────
# `deploy.sh:404-407` states it and this is the same fact from the other side:
# the repository directory is plainly named `app-claude-session.slice.d`, and
# the DESTINATION must be `app-claude\x2dsession.slice.d` — systemd's own
# escaping of `-` in the unit name `app-claude\x2dsession.slice` — or systemd
# never reads the drop-in and every pane runs without its memory cap, silently.
# Inside double quotes bash keeps the backslash (`\x` is not one of the four
# sequences it consumes there), so the literal below IS the directory name.
# ── THE DARWIN ARMS OF THE THREE UNIT STEPS ──────────────────────────────
# `_inst_units`, `_inst_enable` and `_inst_linger` are the only three of this
# verb's seventeen steps that talk to the service manager; the other fourteen
# write files and are already platform-neutral. Each keeps its Linux body
# byte-for-byte and gains a branch to one of the functions below.
#
# WHAT macOS DOES NOT GET, stated here once rather than discovered later:
#
#   • ccd-cap-scopes.{service,timer} — its whole job is to find tmux pane
#     cgroup scopes that escaped the memory ceiling and cap them. macOS has
#     no cgroups and no scopes, so there is nothing for it to cap and no
#     ceiling for it to defend. Not installing it is the correct behaviour,
#     not a gap: installing a timer that can only ever be a no-op would put a
#     job in the operator's `launchctl list` that lies about what it does.
#
#   • the two limits.conf drop-ins (MemoryHigh/MemoryMax on the session unit
#     and on app-claude\x2dsession.slice) — launchd has no memory ceiling of
#     any kind. `ProcessType=Background` in the plists below is the nearest
#     thing macOS offers and it governs CPU/IO scheduling, not memory. A box
#     running many sessions has no aggregate cap on this platform, and
#     `ccrc doctor` says so rather than reporting a limit nobody enforces.
#
#   • claude-session@.service — launchd has NO TEMPLATE UNITS. There is
#     nothing to install once for all sessions; `ccd`'s `_svc_write_session_plist`
#     writes one plist per session at the moment it is enabled, which is the
#     same file the template would have produced, minted per id.

# `EnvironmentFile=` HAS NO LAUNCHD COUNTERPART, and that is the one thing in
# this translation with a behavioural difference worth reading twice. systemd
# reads `~/.ccrc/ccrc.env` and then `~/.ccrc/exposure.env`, both optional,
# later-wins. launchd's `EnvironmentVariables` is a fixed dictionary baked into
# the plist at write time — it cannot read a file at all, and a box whose
# operator edits ccrc.env would keep running the values from install day.
#
# So the job is a SHELL that sources the two files and execs node. Same two
# paths, same order, same `-`-optionality, and — unlike a baked dictionary —
# the files are re-read on every restart, which is what `systemctl restart
# ccrc.service` means to an operator who just edited one.
#
# `set -a` around the sourcing is what makes the assignments EXPORTED, which
# is what an EnvironmentFile does. The difference that remains: systemd parses
# KEY=VALUE with its own quoting rules and this is bash's `.`, so a value
# containing an unquoted space or a `$` behaves differently. Every key these
# files carry today is a scalar (host, port, token, origin) — and the shapes
# that would diverge are also the shapes systemd itself documents as needing
# quotes.
_inst_plist_server() {   # <label> <log> -> the plist bytes, on stdout
  local label="$1" log="$2" env1="$BOX_ENV_FILE" env2="$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE"
  local entry="$HOME/ccrc/server/dist/server/src/index.js"
  # `&amp;&amp;` — this string lands inside XML, where a bare `&` is a parse
  # error and `plutil` refuses the whole file.
  local cmd="set -a; [ -f '$env1' ] &amp;&amp; . '$env1'; [ -f '$env2' ] &amp;&amp; . '$env2'; set +a; exec /usr/bin/env node '$entry'"
  cat <<PL
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>$label</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>$cmd</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key><true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key><integer>3</integer>
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key><string>$(_svc_job_path)</string>
    <key>HOME</key><string>$HOME</string>
  </dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>$log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>$log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
PL
}

_inst_units_darwin() {
  local role_unit=ccrc.service
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = fleet ] && role_unit=ccrc-agent.service
  local dir="$BOX_UNIT_DIR" label log gen
  label="$(_svc_label "$role_unit")"
  log="$HOME/.ccrc/logs/${label}.log"
  mkdir -p "$dir" "$HOME/.ccrc/logs" || _ccrc_die "cannot create $dir and \$HOME/.ccrc/logs"
  gen="$dir/$label.plist.tmp.$$.new"
  _inst_plist_server "$label" "$log" > "$gen" \
    || { rm -f -- "$gen"; _ccrc_die "could not generate the $label job at $gen"; }
  # VALIDATED BEFORE IT LANDS. `launchctl bootstrap` on a malformed plist
  # fails with "Could not find specified service", a message about the wrong
  # thing entirely — the same overloaded-seam mistake `_inst_units`' Linux arm
  # avoids by landing whole files by rename. `plutil -lint` is in the base
  # system and reads the file this run just wrote.
  if command -v plutil >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    plutil -lint "$gen" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || { rm -f -- "$gen"; _ccrc_die "the generated $label job is not a valid plist — refusing to install it. This is a bug in ccrc, not in this box; nothing was placed."; }
  fi
  _inst_atomic "$gen" "$dir/$label.plist" 644
  rm -f -- "$gen"
  echo "install: units: $label in \$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents (launchd)"
  echo "install: units: no ccd-cap-scopes and no memory drop-ins on macOS — launchd has no cgroups, so there is no per-session or fleet-wide memory ceiling on this box"
}

_inst_enable_darwin() {
  local main=ccrc.service
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = fleet ] && main=ccrc-agent.service
  local label; label="$(_svc_label "$main")"
  # NO daemon-reload EQUIVALENT AND NONE NEEDED — launchd reads the plist at
  # bootstrap, so the read systemd needs forcing has already happened by the
  # time the next line returns.
  #
  # BOOTOUT FIRST, and this is not belt-and-braces: `bootstrap` on a label
  # launchd already holds fails, and a re-run of `ccrc install` over a running
  # box is the ordinary case this verb is built for ("re-running is safe: the
  # verb converges"). Removing then re-adding is how launchd spells "load the
  # file I just rewrote" — and it is also what makes this step pick up an
  # edited plist, which `systemctl restart` does on the other platform.
  _svc_launchctl bootout "$(_svc_domain)/$label" 2>/dev/null
  _svc_launchctl enable "$(_svc_domain)/$label" 2>/dev/null
  _svc_launchctl bootstrap "$(_svc_domain)" "$BOX_UNIT_DIR/$label.plist" \
    || _ccrc_die "launchctl bootstrap failed for $label, so this box has the job file and not the service — read what it says: launchctl print $(_svc_domain)/$label"
  # ── AND THEN THE STAY-UP GATE, WHOSE FAILURE IS FATAL ────────────────────
  # `bootstrap` returns as soon as launchd accepts the job — the same fork-
  # time success `systemctl enable --now` reports, and the same gap the Linux
  # arm closes with deploy/verify-service.sh below its restart. The doctrine
  # comment above `_inst_enable` binds this arm too: "A box whose service
  # will not stay up is a FAILED install, not a warning." Two pid samples
  # across a window longer than the plist's ThrottleInterval are that check
  # in launchd's vocabulary (`_ccrc_job_stayed_up`).
  _ccrc_job_stayed_up "$main" \
    || _ccrc_die "$label was bootstrapped and did not stay up ($CCRC_STAYED_DETAIL), so this box has the job file and not the service — read what it says: launchctl print $(_svc_domain)/$label"
  # `install: services:`, NOT `install: enable:` — the STEP is the same one
  # the Linux arm reports under that name, and the transcript is read by step
  # (a test walks the whole list; an operator greps for the step that did not
  # print). A second vocabulary for one step would make both readers wrong.
  echo "install: services: $label bootstrapped into $(_svc_domain) and re-measured still up"
}

# LINGER HAS NO macOS EQUIVALENT, and the honest thing is to say which
# guarantee is missing rather than to print a success line for a step that did
# not happen.
#
# `loginctl enable-linger` makes a user's systemd units run with nobody logged
# in — that is what lets the fleet box reboot unattended and come back with
# every session up. A macOS LaunchAgent runs in the user's GUI login session:
# it starts at login and stops at logout. Nothing in `launchctl` changes that;
# the only thing on this platform that runs with nobody logged in is a
# LaunchDAEMON, which is root-owned, lives in /Library/LaunchDaemons, and would
# run this box's whole fleet as a different user with a different keychain —
# a security posture nobody asked for and this verb must not choose silently.
#
# So this step DEGRADES, using the mechanism `_inst_linger` already has for
# exactly this case, and names what the operator loses.
_inst_linger_darwin() {
  # NOT `INST_DEGRADED`, and the distinction is the whole point of that array.
  # A degraded step is one that WAS SUPPOSED TO HAPPEN and did not — the
  # closing line counts it so an operator knows this box is missing something
  # a re-run might fix. Linger on macOS is not that: there is no such thing to
  # enable, no command that would enable it, and no re-run that would change
  # the answer. Counting it would make EVERY macOS install report DEGRADED
  # forever, which trains an operator to ignore the word on the one day it
  # means something.
  #
  # The fact still has to reach them, so it is said here and carried by
  # `ccrc doctor`'s own `linger` check as a standing WARN — the right home for
  # a permanent property of the platform, as opposed to an event in this run.
  echo "install: linger: not a macOS concept — a LaunchAgent runs in your login session, so this box's server and its sessions stop at logout and start again at login. For an always-on box keep it logged in, and turn off sleep (System Settings > Lock Screen). 'ccrc doctor' reports this too."
  return 0
}

_inst_units() {
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then _inst_units_darwin; return $?; fi
  local tree="$BOX_TREE_DIR" dir="$BOX_UNIT_DIR" role_unit=ccrc.service
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = fleet ] && role_unit=ccrc-agent.service
  local slice="$dir/app-claude\x2dsession.slice.d"
  mkdir -p "$dir" "$dir/claude-session@.service.d" "$slice" \
    || _ccrc_die "cannot create $dir and its drop-in directories"
  # 644 and never 755: these are read by systemd, not executed. And they land by
  # rename for a sharper version of `_inst_atomic`'s own reason — `daemon-reload`
  # below reads whatever is at these paths the instant it runs, and half a unit
  # file parses as a broken unit rather than as no unit at all.
  _inst_atomic "$tree/deploy/$role_unit" "$dir/$role_unit" 644
  _inst_atomic "$tree/ccd/claude-session@.service" "$dir/claude-session@.service" 644
  _inst_atomic "$tree/deploy/systemd/ccd-cap-scopes.service" "$dir/ccd-cap-scopes.service" 644
  _inst_atomic "$tree/deploy/systemd/ccd-cap-scopes.timer" "$dir/ccd-cap-scopes.timer" 644
  # graphify Task 10 (O3/O6b): ROLE-GATED, unlike every unit above — a server
  # box runs no per-tree AST sweep at all, so its unit files stay off it
  # entirely (`_inst_enable`'s own gate on the ENABLE side matches this one).
  if [ "$INST_ROLE" != server ]; then
    _inst_atomic "$tree/deploy/systemd/ccd-graph-sweep.service" "$dir/ccd-graph-sweep.service" 644
    _inst_atomic "$tree/deploy/systemd/ccd-graph-sweep.timer" "$dir/ccd-graph-sweep.timer" 644
  fi
  _inst_atomic "$tree/deploy/systemd/claude-session@.service.d/limits.conf" \
    "$dir/claude-session@.service.d/limits.conf" 644
  _inst_atomic "$tree/deploy/systemd/app-claude-session.slice.d/limits.conf" \
    "$slice/limits.conf" 644
  echo "install: units: $role_unit, claude-session@.service, ccd-cap-scopes.{service,timer} and both drop-ins in \$HOME/.config/systemd/user"
}

# ── _inst_enable — ask systemd to read them, then prove one stayed up ─────
# `deploy.sh:461-466`'s chain, in the same order and for the same reasons, with
# the agent's unit replaced by this box's own.
#
# EVERY ENABLE RUNS AFTER EVERY UNIT FILE HAS LANDED, which is why this is a
# separate step from `_inst_units` rather than an enable beside each copy:
# `daemon-reload` is systemd's one read of the directory, and a unit enabled
# before its drop-in exists runs WITHOUT that drop-in until something reloads
# again — for the slice cap, a box whose panes are uncapped while its transcript
# says they are not. The test reads the recorded argv AND what was on disk when
# each call arrived.
#
# XDG_RUNTIME_DIR AND DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS defaulted exactly as `_box_units`
# defaults them (see its own header) and `ccd:93-95` before it: without them
# `systemctl --user` cannot find the bus from a non-interactive SSH context —
# which is precisely how an install is most often run — and every call here
# fails for a reason that has nothing to do with this box.
#
# ── AND THEN verify-service.sh, WHOSE FAILURE IS FATAL ───────────────────
# `systemctl enable --now` returns success the moment systemd FORKS. A server
# that throws during ESM evaluation crash-loops every `RestartSec=3` behind an
# install that exited 0 — the gap `deploy/verify-service.sh` exists to close
# (its own header, and deploy.sh:448-465). A box whose service will not stay up
# is a FAILED install, not a warning: the verb's whole promise is a box that
# runs, and doctor's `services` check at the end would otherwise report the same
# thing only after every remaining step had run against it.
#
# GUARDED BY `[ -f ]` because the tree is the input this verb trusts least: a
# partial tree that passed `_inst_tree`'s two preflights without carrying this
# script must not turn into "verification failed", which is a claim about the
# SERVICE. The `CCRC_VERIFY_*` knobs are the script's own (settle, window, log
# lines) and are honoured here by not being touched.
_inst_enable() {
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then _inst_enable_darwin; return $?; fi
  # THE ROLE'S UNIT, `_inst_units`' own gate applied to the asking side: on a
  # fleet box the long-running unit this step must enable, restart and verify
  # is the agent's, and `ccrc.service` — which that box was never given — must
  # never appear in a systemctl argv here. Every message below is templated on
  # the same variable so the remedy always names the unit this box actually has.
  local main=ccrc.service
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = fleet ] && main=ccrc-agent.service
  : "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:=/run/user/$UID}"; export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
  : "${DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS:=unix:path=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}/bus}"; export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
  systemctl --user daemon-reload \
    || _ccrc_die "systemctl --user daemon-reload failed, so systemd never read the units this run installed — read what it says: systemctl --user status $main"
  local u
  for u in "$main" ccd-cap-scopes.timer; do
    systemctl --user enable --now "$u" \
      || _ccrc_die "systemctl --user enable --now $u failed — read what it says: systemctl --user status $u"
  done
  # graphify Task 10 (O3/O6b): the sweep timer, gated exactly as
  # `_inst_units` gates its unit files — SKIPPED on a server box, which runs
  # no per-tree AST sweep at all. UNLIKE the loop above, a refusal here
  # DEGRADES rather than dies (`_inst_linger`'s own idiom): a systemd that
  # will not take this one timer must not turn an otherwise-converged install
  # into a failed one the way `ccrc.service`'s own enable is entitled to — the
  # sweep is convenience, not the guardrail `ccd-cap-scopes.timer` is.
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = server ] || systemctl --user enable --now ccd-graph-sweep.timer \
    || echo "install: graphify: could not enable ccd-graph-sweep.timer — run: systemctl --user enable --now ccd-graph-sweep.timer" >&2
  # ── THE RESTART, AND WHY `enable --now` IS NOT ENOUGH ─────────────────
  # `deploy.sh:719-721` has this line between the enable and the verify, and
  # Task 8 shipped without it. Measured consequence on the SECOND run — the
  # path this verb's own promise ("re-running converges") points every operator
  # at: `enable --now` on an already-active unit is a no-op, and
  # `ccrc.service` is `ExecStart=… node %h/ccrc/server/dist/server/src/index.js`,
  # so the running process is pinned to the `dist` it started with. `_inst_tree`
  # rsyncs a new one, `_inst_stamp` writes the new sha, the transcript says
  # every step converged and `ccrc version` reports the new build — while the
  # box goes on serving the old code. Nothing catches it on a single box:
  # `_check_fleet` SKIPs in local mode, and no check compares the running
  # server's build to the stamp. That is this file's own named defect (a state
  # asserted and never measured) with the measurement printed above it.
  #
  # It also restores `verify-service.sh`'s documented contract: its header
  # calls it "Post-restart verification", and with nothing restarted MainPID is
  # trivially stable and the script certifies the process the PREVIOUS install
  # started. On a fresh box the restart is harmless — the unit has just been
  # started by `enable --now`, and restarting it costs one process cycle before
  # anything is serving.
  #
  # ONLY `ccrc.service`. The timer is `enable --now`-idempotent by construction
  # (it fires `ccd-cap-scopes` on a schedule and holds no code in memory), and
  # every `claude-session@` unit beside it is a long-lived `ccd supervise` this
  # verb must never touch (CLAUDE.md). Deploy's supervisor sweep is a fleet-host
  # operation with its own KillMode preflight; a single-box installer does not
  # inherit it.
  systemctl --user restart "$main" \
    || _ccrc_die "systemctl --user restart $main failed, so this box is still running the server the previous install started — read what it says: systemctl --user status $main"
  local verify="$CCRC_HERE/../deploy/verify-service.sh"
  if [ -f "$verify" ]; then
    bash "$verify" "$main" \
      || _ccrc_die "$main was restarted and did not stay up, so this box has the unit and not the service — read what it says: systemctl --user status $main"
  fi
  echo "install: services: $main and ccd-cap-scopes.timer enabled, and $main restarted onto the tree this run placed"
}

# ── _inst_linger — the one step that reports a failure and CONTINUES ──────
# Without linger, `/run/user/$UID` is torn down when the last login session
# ends, taking every `systemd --user` unit with it — the whole fleet, plus the
# service the step above just started (`_check_linger`'s header says the same
# thing from the diagnosis side).
#
# ENABLING IT NEEDS PRIVILEGE THIS PROCESS MAY NOT HAVE, and that is the entire
# reason this step is written differently from every other one. `_ccrc_die`
# here would abort an install at its tenth step, on a box where everything
# before it converged, over a thing one `sudo` command fixes and which nothing
# else in this run depends on. So it prints the exact command and returns 0 —
# the shape `cmd_install`'s own header reserved for it before it existed — and
# doctor, four steps later, reports `FAIL linger` with the same remedy and
# carries it into the exit code. The operator is told twice and stopped never.
#
# loginctl's own stderr is NOT redirected: "Interactive authentication
# required" and "Failed to enable linger: Access denied" send an operator to two
# different places, and this file's rule (`_inst_accounts_sh`, `_inst_tree`) is
# that a tool's own diagnosis reaches them verbatim rather than re-worded into a
# shrug.
_inst_linger() {
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then _inst_linger_darwin; return $?; fi
  if loginctl enable-linger "$UID"; then
    echo "install: linger: enabled for uid $UID — this box's units survive logout"
    return 0
  fi
  echo "install: linger: could not enable — run: sudo loginctl enable-linger $UID"
  # NAMED, so the closing line can count it rather than claim it did not happen.
  # It was the ONLY writer of `INST_DEGRADED` when this comment was first
  # written, and that sentence stopped being true the day the graphify steps
  # landed: `graphify-read-rule` (`_inst_graph_always_on_off`, D-1244) and
  # `graphify-path` (`_inst_graphify_engine`, D-1348/D-1349) write it too, for
  # the same reason and under the same rule — a step that neither converged nor
  # is entitled to take the box down says so and is counted. Kept as a census
  # rather than a claim, because a hand-kept "only" is exactly the sentence a
  # later step falsifies without noticing (D-1347 is the same defect one
  # function over).
  INST_DEGRADED+=(linger)
  return 0
}

# ── _inst_dirs — the directories the hooks installer will not create ──────
# `install-session-hooks.sh` iterates the roster's config dirs and `continue`s
# past any that is not there ("a box that does not have some of the roster's
# wrappers is ordinary", its own comment). That is right for a deploy onto a
# fleet host whose accounts have been running for months, and exactly wrong on a
# FRESH box, where none of them exists yet: the installer would walk the whole
# roster, skip every entry, exit 0, and leave a box whose sessions start with no
# hooks and no statusline — a silence with a successful exit code in front of
# it. This step creates what the next one expects to find.
#
# SOURCED IN A SUBSHELL, and that is not caution about the file's contents. The
# generated projection declares `_ccrc_cfg_dir`, `_ccrc_label`, `_ccrc_hue` and
# friends — the `_ccrc_*` namespace THIS FILE's own refusal helpers live in
# (`_ccrc_die`, `_ccrc_usage_die`; see the note about the three namespaces in
# play near the top). Sourcing it into this shell would let a future emitter
# change redefine one of them out from under the installer mid-run. A subshell
# means the roster's code cannot outlive the step, and the only thing that
# crosses back is a count.
_inst_dirs() {
  local sh="$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh" n
  n="$(
    # shellcheck source=/dev/null
    . "$sh" || exit 1
    made=0
    for a in ${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[@]+"${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[@]}"}; do
      d="$(_ccrc_cfg_dir "$a")"
      [ -n "$d" ] || continue
      mkdir -p "$d" || exit 1
      made=$((made + 1))
    done
    printf '%s' "$made"
  )" || _ccrc_die "could not create the account config directories named by $sh — the session hooks would have nowhere to be installed"
  echo "install: dirs: config directory in place for $n account(s) named by \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh"
}

# Engine into a ccrc-owned venv (spec §A): PEP-668-immune, one rm -rf to remove,
# resolved by ABSOLUTE PATH everywhere — /usr/local/bin/graphify on the reference
# fleet is a root-owned symlink an unprivileged install can neither update nor
# remove, so `command -v` is actively wrong here.
_inst_graphify_engine() {
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = server ] && return 0
  local venv="$HOME/.ccrc/graphify-venv"
  if [ ! -x "$venv/bin/python" ]; then
    python3 -m venv "$venv" \
      || _ccrc_die "python3 -m venv failed for $venv — install python3-venv (apt: python3.12-venv) and re-run; nothing else was changed"
  fi
  "$venv/bin/python" -m pip install --quiet "graphifyy==$GRAPHIFY_PIN" \
    || _ccrc_die "pip install graphifyy==$GRAPHIFY_PIN failed in $venv — network/index unreachable; nothing else was changed"
  local got
  got="$("$venv/bin/graphify" --version 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $2}')"
  [ "$got" = "$GRAPHIFY_PIN" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "graphify --version reports '${got:-nothing}' but the pin is $GRAPHIFY_PIN — the venv at $venv did not converge"
  printf '%s\n' "$GRAPHIFY_PIN" > "$HOME/.ccrc/graphify.pin"
  # ── R3: the engine SESSIONS run is the engine ccrc pins ────────────────
  # The write side resolves this engine by ABSOLUTE PATH everywhere. The read
  # side was never given a path at all: a session types `graphify`, PATH answers
  # `~/.local/bin/graphify`, and on this fleet that was a pip console-script
  # shim importing a July copy of the package with no dist-info and no
  # `__version__`, while every graph on the box was built by the 0.9.9 venv.
  # The drift is invisible until a query fails strangely.
  #
  # `cmd_wrappers`' DISCIPLINE, and no flags: write when absent; do NOTHING
  # when the link already resolves into the venv; back up and then repoint a
  # pip/pipx console-script shim (matched BY CONTENT — a `#!` first line and a
  # `from graphify.__main__ import main` line, the shape measured on this
  # fleet, never assumed); REFUSE anything else — a hand-written launcher is
  # the operator's, a link this box cannot resolve is unmeasured, and this step
  # has no `--force` for anyone to type.
  #
  # CONTENT DECIDES, AND IT DECIDES THROUGH A LINK TOO (D-1351). "Symlink" is
  # not a verdict here — only three link SHAPES get answered as links: one that
  # resolves into the venv (converged), one whose target cannot be reached
  # (`-e` false: left in place), and one neither resolution could be measured
  # for (left in place). Everything else is judged by what is at the end of the
  # link, because `[ -f ]` and the two content probes follow it: so
  # `~/.local/bin/graphify -> ~/.local/pipx/venvs/graphify/bin/graphify`, the
  # single commonest state on a box that ever ran `pipx install graphify`, is a
  # SHIM by content and is replaced like any other shim. Two consequences worth
  # saying out loud, both pinned by the suite: the keep-aside `cp -p` cuts is a
  # DEREFERENCED regular-file copy of the shim, not a copy of the link, and the
  # pipx-owned file the link pointed at is left exactly where it was — ccrc
  # unlinks its own `$HOME/.local/bin` entry and touches nothing it did not
  # find there.
  #
  # ONE PATH ONLY: `$HOME/.local/bin`. The root-owned copy a shared box may
  # carry higher up the system tree is an unprivileged install's business
  # nowhere — the header above this function already says why — and it is not
  # spelled out anywhere inside this body, because the guard in
  # `server/test/ccrc-install-graphify.test.ts` whose name begins `never
  # touches ` slices this function from `_inst_graphify_engine() {` to its
  # closing brace and refuses that literal. The slice starts AT the signature,
  # so the header comment is outside it; a sentence like this one is inside.
  # CITING THE GUARD BY ITS FULL NAME IS ITSELF A VIOLATION — the name ends in
  # the path — which is how the wording originally drafted for this paragraph
  # went red against the guard it was describing (D-1344). Name the path in the
  # header, never here, not even in quotation.
  local gpath="$HOME/.local/bin/graphify" gvenv="$venv/bin/graphify"
  local gnow="" gwant="" gbak="" gtmp=""
  # BOTH SIDES RESOLVED ONCE, AND "COULD NOT RESOLVE" IS ITS OWN ANSWER
  # (D-1348). The first cut of this step compared the two `readlink -f`
  # substitutions inline, and a box whose readlink has no `-f` (macOS below
  # 12.3 — a floor this repo accepts rather than enforces,
  # `macos-platform.test.ts`) answers EMPTY on both sides: empty = empty is
  # TRUE, so ANY symlink, including one pointing at a completely different
  # engine, was declared converged and left in place. That is the overloaded
  # null this codebase bans at a seam, in the shape D-1244 already named one
  # step below — and `_inst_graph_always_on_off` takes the path this one now
  # takes: an unresolvable link is LEFT IN PLACE, said out loud, and counted
  # as a degraded step rather than acted on unmeasured.
  #
  # TWO TOOLS, ONE FAILURE SEMANTICS. This step resolves with `readlink -f` and
  # `_check_graphify-path` (`ccd/ccrc-doctor-checks`) resolves with `realpath`;
  # `macos-platform.test.ts` allows both, and neither is preferred here — the
  # install already reaches for `readlink -f` one step below (D-1244) and the
  # doctor's own file already reaches for `realpath`. What is NOT optional is
  # that they agree about EMPTY: on both sides an unresolvable pair is
  # "unmeasured", never "unequal". This step degrades and leaves the link; the
  # doctor SKIPs and names the missing tool (D-1350). If either ever falls back
  # to the raw path instead, the two halves start disagreeing about a box
  # neither of them measured.
  #
  # AND EMPTY IS ITSELF OVERLOADED (D-1352). `readlink -f` answers empty for a
  # userland that has no `-f` AND for a chain it cannot walk — measured with
  # GNU coreutils 9.4: a link at `/gone/deeper/bin/graphify` exits 1 with no
  # output, because `-f` requires every component but the last to exist. The
  # first cut of this arm printed "(its readlink has no -f)" as the cause,
  # which is one of the two, picked and stated as fact — the same overloaded
  # null one paragraph up, moved into the sentence describing it. So the
  # distinguishable half is MEASURED FIRST, the way `_check_graphify-path`
  # measures a missing engine with `[ ! -e ]` before it compares anything: a
  # link whose target cannot be reached gets its own arm and its own sentence,
  # naming the target it read with a plain one-hop `readlink`. What is left
  # after that arm — no `-f`, or something rarer — is named for what was
  # measured and nothing else, exactly as `_inst_graph_always_on_off` says it
  # ("is a symlink this box cannot resolve", no cause claimed).
  if [ -L "$gpath" ]; then
    gnow="$(readlink -f "$gpath" 2>/dev/null)" || gnow=""
    gwant="$(readlink -f "$gvenv" 2>/dev/null)" || gwant=""
  fi
  if ! mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/bin" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "install: graphify: cannot create \$HOME/.local/bin — bare 'graphify' still resolves to whatever is on PATH" >&2
    INST_DEGRADED+=(graphify-path)
  elif [ -L "$gpath" ] && [ ! -e "$gpath" ]; then
    echo "install: graphify: $gpath is a symlink whose target this box cannot reach — it points at $(readlink "$gpath" 2>/dev/null), and nothing there answers. Left in place. Remedy: inspect it (ls -l \"$gpath\"), and if you want ccrc's pinned engine on PATH, move it aside and re-run" >&2
    INST_DEGRADED+=(graphify-path)
  elif [ -L "$gpath" ] && { [ -z "$gnow" ] || [ -z "$gwant" ]; }; then
    echo "install: graphify: $gpath is a symlink this box cannot resolve — left in place rather than judged unmeasured. Remedy: inspect it (ls -l \"$gpath\"), and if it is not the pinned engine, move it aside and re-run" >&2
    INST_DEGRADED+=(graphify-path)
  elif [ -L "$gpath" ] && [ "$gnow" = "$gwant" ]; then
    echo "install: graphify: graphify on \$PATH already points at the pinned venv"
  elif [ ! -e "$gpath" ] && [ ! -L "$gpath" ]; then
    ln -sfn "$gvenv" "$gpath" \
      && echo "install: graphify: \$HOME/.local/bin/graphify -> the pinned venv engine (written)" \
      || { echo "install: graphify: cannot write $gpath" >&2; INST_DEGRADED+=(graphify-path); }
  else
    # SNIFFED THROUGH A PIPE, NEVER THROUGH `$(…)`. The first cut read the file
    # into a variable with `gcur="$(head -c 4096 …)"` and matched against that,
    # and the candidate this arm exists to judge may be a COMPILED launcher: a
    # command substitution over binary makes bash print `warning: command
    # substitution: ignored null byte in input` on the install's own stderr,
    # noise from a step that has not decided anything yet. Two piped reads cost
    # one extra `head` on a file that is at most 4 KB and say the same thing
    # with nothing to warn about; `grep -q` stays silent on binary input where
    # a bare `grep` would announce `Binary file … matches`.
    if [ -f "$gpath" ] && [ -r "$gpath" ] \
       && head -c 4096 "$gpath" 2>/dev/null | head -n1 | grep -q '^#!' \
       && head -c 4096 "$gpath" 2>/dev/null | grep -qxF 'from graphify.__main__ import main'; then
      # BACKED UP FIRST, THEN SWAPPED ATOMICALLY (D-1349) — `cmd_wrappers`'
      # discipline in FULL, which the comment above claimed while this arm did
      # neither. A pip console-script shim is a file ccrc did not write: it is
      # replaceable because its content proves what it is, not because it is
      # ccrc's, so it gets `cmd_wrappers`' own `<name>.pre-ccrc-<UTC>` copy
      # (`cp -p`, mode and timestamps preserved) BEFORE anything is unlinked,
      # and a failed backup degrades instead of replacing unbacked — the rule
      # `_inst_graph_always_on_off` states as "left in place rather than
      # rewritten unbacked". The "." in the backup name is load-bearing for the
      # reason `cmd_wrappers` gives: no legal wrapper id can carry one, so the
      # copy is invisible to `ccrc adopt`'s scan and to `_check_wrappers`, and
      # the leading "." on the temp name keeps a leftover out of the `*` glob
      # `_uninst_wrappers` walks. The swap itself is `ln` to that temp name
      # then `mv -f`, one rename, because `ln -sfn` over a live path is
      # unlink+symlink: a session that types `graphify` in that window gets
      # ENOENT.
      gbak="$gpath.pre-ccrc-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)"
      gtmp="$HOME/.local/bin/.graphify.tmp.$$"
      if ! cp -p -- "$gpath" "$gbak" 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "install: graphify: cannot copy $gpath aside to $gbak — left in place rather than replaced unbacked. Remedy: make \$HOME/.local/bin writable and re-run" >&2
        INST_DEGRADED+=(graphify-path)
      elif ln -sfn "$gvenv" "$gtmp" 2>/dev/null && mv -f -- "$gtmp" "$gpath"; then
        echo "install: graphify: \$HOME/.local/bin/graphify was a pip console-script shim -> repointed at the pinned venv (the shim it replaced is at $gbak)"
      else
        rm -f -- "$gtmp"
        echo "install: graphify: cannot replace $gpath — the file that was there is untouched, and the copy at $gbak is the same file" >&2
        INST_DEGRADED+=(graphify-path)
      fi
    else
      echo "install: graphify: $gpath is not a pip shim and ccrc did not write it — left in place. Remedy: inspect it (ls -l \"$gpath\"), and if you want ccrc's pinned engine on PATH, move it aside and re-run; nothing here overrides that." >&2
      INST_DEGRADED+=(graphify-path)
    fi
  fi
  echo "install: graphify: engine graphifyy==$GRAPHIFY_PIN in \$HOME/.ccrc/graphify-venv (pin stamped)"
}

# ── _inst_hooks — run the installer, from where the BOX keeps it ──────────
# `deploy.sh` runs `bash ~/.cc-sessions/install-session-hooks.sh` — the
# INSTALLED copy, never the one in the tree — and this runs the same path for
# the same reason: `_inst_files` has just placed it there, and running the
# tree's copy instead would leave the box's own copy untested by the very run
# that installed it. It is also the copy every future run, and every operator,
# will reach.
#
# ITS FAILURE IS FATAL. `settings.json` is what registers `session-hook.sh` for
# every event; a box without it runs sessions that report nothing to the server,
# which is indistinguishable from a fleet that is idle. The script is
# deliberately paranoid about that file (jq-validated, backed up, converged
# byte-level) and returns non-zero having touched nothing when it refuses, so
# there is nothing to unwind here.
_inst_hooks() {
  bash "$HOME/.cc-sessions/install-session-hooks.sh" \
    || _ccrc_die "install-session-hooks.sh refused — read its lines above; it never rewrites a settings.json it could not validate, so nothing was damaged"
  echo "install: hooks: session-hook.sh and the statusline registered in each account's settings.json"
}

# ── _inst_skills — the two skills, placed and then INSTALLED ──────────────
# THE ASYMMETRY THIS STEP CLOSES. `deploy/deploy.sh agent <host>` has shipped
# the coordinator skill to the fleet host since Build 7, and now ships the
# worker skill beside it — while a box that installed ITSELF got NEITHER,
# because no step of this verb had ever heard of a skill. That gap is not
# cosmetic. Both installers exist because skills resolve per
# `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`, and a session's ACCOUNT drifts on swap while its id does
# not: a coordinator (or a worker) is placed with no pinned account, so it has
# to find its skill in EVERY rostered home. On a self-installed box it found one
# in none of them, and the failure is silent — the model does not announce that
# it has no protocol, it improvises one.
#
# TWO HALVES PER SKILL, IN DEPLOY'S OWN ORDER. The TREE is placed under
# `~/.cc-sessions/<name>` — the same path `deploy.sh` rsyncs it to, which is
# what makes each installer's own `CCRC_SKILL_SRC` default correct on a box
# converged by either lane — and then the INSTALLER is run from the copy this
# step just placed beside it. That is `_inst_hooks`' doctrine and its reason
# holds unchanged here: the box's own copy is the one every future run and every
# operator reaches, and running the tree's copy would leave the installed one
# untested by the very run that installed it. `CCRC_SKILL_SRC` is set
# EXPLICITLY rather than left to that default, because a step that silently
# depended on a default's current value would convey none of this.
#
# THE COORDINATOR GOES FIRST, for deploy.sh's reason: the worker skill carries
# no `references/` of its own and points a live worker at the coordinator's
# installed tree by relative path (`../ccrc-coordinator/references/…`, both
# skills side by side under one config dir).
#
# ITS FAILURE IS FATAL, exactly as `_inst_hooks`' is. Each installer converges
# per home, backs up what it is about to replace and rolls back a swap it could
# not finish, so a refusal has damaged nothing and there is nothing to unwind —
# and a box whose sessions cannot find their protocol is not a finished install.
_inst_skills() {
  local tree="$BOX_TREE_DIR" name
  mkdir -p "$HOME/.cc-sessions" || _ccrc_die "cannot create \$HOME/.cc-sessions"
  for name in coordinator-skill worker-skill; do
    _inst_tree_copy "$tree/ccd/$name" "$HOME/.cc-sessions/$name"
    _inst_atomic "$tree/ccd/install-$name.sh" "$HOME/.cc-sessions/install-$name.sh" 755
    CCRC_SKILL_SRC="$HOME/.cc-sessions/$name" bash "$HOME/.cc-sessions/install-$name.sh" \
      || _ccrc_die "install-$name.sh refused — read its lines above; it converges one account's skill directory at a time and rolls back a swap it could not finish, so no home was left without the skill it already had"
  done
  echo "install: skills: ccrc-coordinator and ccrc-worker in each account's skills directory"
}

# The graphify skill is NOT one of _inst_skills' names on purpose (spec §B):
# that loop pins CCRC_SKILL_SRC to a vendored ~/.cc-sessions tree, and this
# skill's source of truth is the installed package.
_inst_graphify_skill() {
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = server ] && return 0
  _inst_atomic "$BOX_TREE_DIR/ccd/install-graphify-skill.sh" "$HOME/.cc-sessions/install-graphify-skill.sh" 755
  bash "$HOME/.cc-sessions/install-graphify-skill.sh" \
    || _ccrc_die "install-graphify-skill.sh refused — read its lines above"
  echo "install: graphify: skill assembled from the venv package into each account's skills directory"
}

# D' (spec §4): the exclude precondition needs a WRITER, or the sweep refuses
# every un-excluded repo forever (ccrc-pwa fails the gate today — D-996).
# ── _inst_graph_noise — ccrc's own footprint, out of every corpus ─────────
# D-1160. `ccd-graph-sweep`'s corpus guard refuses a build when any path
# `detect()` picks up is not git-tracked. That guard is right and stays. What
# was wrong is that ccrc's OWN tooling writes untracked files into every repo a
# session touches — `.remember/` transcripts, `.superpowers/` specs, `.claude/`
# settings, a `CLAUDE.local.md` — so ccrc poisoned the corpus and then refused
# the build over its own mess. Measured on the reference fleet: 14 of 57 trees
# refused, 186 of 304 breach paths being those four names, five repos blocked
# by nothing else at all.
#
# CONVERGED, NOT KEPT, and the asymmetry is the point: this file is ccrc's
# (regenerated every install, so a name added to the shipped list reaches every
# box) while `<repo>.list` beside it is the operator's and is never written
# here. The sweep unions the two.
#
# D-1161: `_inst_atomic`, not `cp`. The first draft used a bare unchecked `cp`
# after an unchecked `mkdir -p` and then printed "converged" whatever happened —
# the one install-time converger in this file that bypassed `_inst_atomic`,
# while its own sibling on the deploy lane used `install_atomic`. It matters
# here more than most: the sweep timer reads this file every 15 minutes, so a
# non-atomic write has a live reader, and a plain `cp` follows a symlink at the
# destination instead of replacing it.
# ── _inst_graph_always_on_off — take back the account-wide block (D-1245) ─
# D-1243 installed graphify's `always_on/claude-md.md` into every rostered
# home's config-dir `CLAUDE.md`. Two things were wrong with that, and neither is
# a wording problem:
#
#   1. The block is written for a PROJECT file. Its first sentence is "This
#      project has a knowledge graph at graphify-out/". Planted account-wide it
#      asserts that of every project the account ever opens, including the trees
#      the sweep refuses.
#   2. That file is the OPERATOR's, not ccrc's. Every one of D-1244's six
#      data-loss classes — substring markers, a lost end marker, two blocks, the
#      line-1 splice, the unchecked `&&` chain, the symlink fallback — existed
#      ONLY because ccrc was rewriting a file it does not own.
#
# MEASURED EFFECT, which is what settled it: graphify's own query log over the
# one day since it was deployed (2026-09-01, measured 2026-09-02) showed 109
# query/path/explain calls across 4 corpora, 103 of them in the one repository
# whose PROJECT CLAUDE.md had carried graphify's block, committed, since July.
# Zero in ccrc-pwa, the busiest project on the fleet, with five fresh graphs.
# The design's other row is the WEEK one — 265 calls across 11 corpora over the
# last 7 days, ccrc-pwa zero in both — and the two are named apart here because
# this comment once paired that row's window word with this row's figures
# (D-1357/D-1362). "5/5 homes converged" was shape; that is effect.
# The read side now lives in `ccd/session-hook.sh`'s SessionStart card (R1),
# worker clause 12 (R2), `_inst_graphify_engine`'s PATH converge (R3), and the
# `graphQueries` counter in the hookstate the hook already writes (R4) —
# artifacts ccrc installs and owns outright.
#
# THIS IS D-1244'S CENSUS DOING ITS LAST JOB, not a second implementation of it:
# the same whole-line marker count, the same exactly-one-well-ordered-pair rule,
# the same symlink resolution that SKIPS rather than falling back to the link,
# the same `_plat_mode` preservation, the same backup-before-write. It deletes
# lines ls..le (plus the one separating blank line the append path wrote) where
# the converge spliced a block in.
#
# `_inst_graph_hooks_off` IS THE IDIOM, not a wart: ccrc already has a step
# whose whole job is removing what an earlier layer planted, and it states the
# rule this one follows — anything not provably WHOLLY ccrc's own is "left in
# place; remove by hand", counted, and reported. That is the TREE's spelling of
# the phrase — `_inst_graph_hooks_off`'s chained-content refusal, the one other
# place the tree says it — not the spec's em-dashed one; D-1247 records the
# divergence, and the half-block test asserts the semicolon. Cited BY NAME, not
# by line (D-1343): the plan prescribed `:5411 and :5249` verbatim, but :5249
# was the converge's unmarked-`## graphify` refusal, which THIS step's commit
# deletes, and every number in this file moves the moment a line is added above
# it. This step stays in the tree afterwards exactly as that one does.
_inst_graph_always_on_off() {
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = server ] && return 0
  local n=0 kept=0 ts backups
  if ! source "$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh" 2>/dev/null || [ "${#CCRC_ACCOUNTS[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
    # DEGRADES, NEVER DIES — `_inst_enable`'s rule for the sweep timer, and it
    # binds harder here: a block ccrc failed to REMOVE is a stale instruction,
    # not a broken box, and taking the whole install down over it would be a
    # fix worse than the thing being fixed.
    echo "install: graphify: no usable roster at \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh — always-on read rule not cleared"
    INST_DEGRADED+=(graphify-read-rule)
    return 0
  fi
  ts=$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S); backups="$HOME/ccrc-backups/$ts"
  local a dir f start end phys ns ne ls le lb mode tmp
  start='<!-- ccrc:graphify-always-on:start -->'
  end='<!-- ccrc:graphify-always-on:end -->'
  for a in "${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[@]}"; do
    dir="$(_ccrc_cfg_dir "$a" 2>/dev/null)" || continue
    [ -n "$dir" ] || continue
    f="$dir/CLAUDE.md"
    [ -e "$f" ] || [ -L "$f" ] || continue
    # A SYMLINK IS RESOLVED, OR THE HOME IS SKIPPED (D-1244). `readlink -f`
    # answers empty for a link whose target's parent does not exist yet, and
    # falling back to the LINK path collapses "resolved" and "could not
    # resolve" into one value — the overloaded null this codebase forbids at a
    # seam, and here it would delete lines out of a file nobody identified.
    # Two homes on this fleet ARE symlinks to a third's file, so this is the
    # ordinary case, not the exotic one.
    if [ -L "$f" ]; then
      phys="$(readlink -f "$f" 2>/dev/null)" || phys=""
      if [ -z "$phys" ]; then
        echo "install: graphify: $f is a symlink this box cannot resolve — left in place; remove the block by hand" >&2
        kept=$((kept+1)); continue
      fi
      f="$phys"
    fi
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    # THE MARKER CENSUS, ON WHOLE LINES, EXACTLY ONE ORDERED PAIR OR NOTHING.
    # A substring match would cut a file at the operator's own MENTION of the
    # markers; a missing end marker would delete everything after the start.
    ns="$(grep -cxF "$start" "$f" 2>/dev/null || true)"; ns="${ns:-0}"
    ne="$(grep -cxF "$end" "$f" 2>/dev/null || true)"; ne="${ne:-0}"
    if [ "$ns" -eq 0 ] && [ "$ne" -eq 0 ]; then
      continue   # nothing of ours here; idempotence lives on this line
    fi
    if [ "$ns" -ne 1 ] || [ "$ne" -ne 1 ]; then
      echo "install: graphify: $f carries $ns start and $ne end markers, and exactly one pair is required — left in place; remove by hand" >&2
      kept=$((kept+1)); continue
    fi
    ls="$(grep -nxF "$start" "$f" | head -n1 | cut -d: -f1)"
    le="$(grep -nxF "$end" "$f" | head -n1 | cut -d: -f1)"
    if [ "$ls" -ge "$le" ]; then
      echo "install: graphify: $f carries ccrc's end marker before its start marker — malformed, left in place; remove by hand" >&2
      kept=$((kept+1)); continue
    fi
    # THE ONE SEPARATING BLANK LINE the append path wrote before the block
    # (`printf '%s\n\n' "$cur"`), and only when it really is blank and really
    # is ours to take: without this, removal leaves a growing run of blank
    # lines behind on a home the converge had appended to.
    lb="$ls"
    if [ "$ls" -gt 1 ] && [ -z "$(sed -n "$((ls-1))p" "$f")" ]; then lb=$((ls-1)); fi
    # PRESERVE THE FILE'S OWN MODE (D-1244): a symlink was resolved first, so
    # the file being rewritten may not even sit inside a ccrc home, and forcing
    # 644 would widen a CLAUDE.md an operator had restricted. `_plat_mode`, not
    # a bare `stat -c %a` — this file ships to macOS and `macos-platform.test.ts`
    # refuses a GNU-only call outside the platform block.
    mode="$(_plat_mode "$f" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    [ -n "$mode" ] || mode=644
    if ! { mkdir -p "$backups" && cp -a "$f" "$backups/$(echo "$f" | tr / _)"; }; then
      echo "install: graphify: cannot back up $f — left in place rather than rewritten unbacked" >&2
      kept=$((kept+1)); continue
    fi
    tmp="$f.tmp.$$"
    # EVERY PIECE'S STATUS, NOT JUST THE LAST (D-1244): a `{ a; b; } > tmp`
    # group carries only b's status, and b writes NOTHING when the block ends
    # the file — the layout the append path itself creates. The `&&` chain makes
    # the group's status the FIRST failure.
    #
    # `[ "$lb" -le 1 ] ||` GUARDS AN EMPTY PREFIX and is not padding: `sed -n
    # "1,0p"` does not print nothing — an addr2 at or below addr1 makes sed
    # match the ONE line at addr1, so a block at line 1 would have its own
    # start marker re-emitted. That layout is what the append path produced for
    # a home that had no CLAUDE.md at all.
    if ! { { [ "$lb" -le 1 ] || sed -n "1,$((lb-1))p" "$f"; } &&
           sed -n "$((le+1)),\$p" "$f"; } > "$tmp" 2>/dev/null; then
      rm -f "$tmp"
      echo "install: graphify: could not rewrite $f — left in place" >&2
      kept=$((kept+1)); continue
    fi
    if ! { chmod "$mode" "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$f"; }; then
      rm -f "$tmp"
      echo "install: graphify: could not replace $f — left in place" >&2
      kept=$((kept+1)); continue
    fi
    n=$((n+1))
  done
  [ "$kept" -eq 0 ] || INST_DEGRADED+=(graphify-read-rule)
  echo "install: graphify: always-on read rule — $n home(s) cleared, $kept left in place"
}

_inst_graph_noise() {
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = server ] && return 0
  local src="$BOX_TREE_DIR/ccd/graph-noise.default.list" dest="$HOME/.ccrc/graph-noise"
  [ -f "$src" ] || _ccrc_die "no default noise list at $src — the placed tree is incomplete"
  mkdir -p "$dest" || _ccrc_die "cannot create $dest"
  _inst_atomic "$src" "$dest/_default.list" 644
  echo "install: graphify: default noise list converged ($dest/_default.list)"
}

_inst_graph_excludes() {
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = server ] && return 0
  local roots=("$HOME/projects" "$HOME/worktrees") root d common n=0
  for root in "${roots[@]}"; do
    [ -d "$root" ] || continue
    for d in "$root"/*/ "$root"/*/*/; do
      [ -d "$d" ] || continue
      git -C "$d" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1 || continue
      common=$(git -C "$d" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir) || continue
      mkdir -p "$common/info"
      grep -qxF 'graphify-out/' "$common/info/exclude" 2>/dev/null \
        || { echo 'graphify-out/' >> "$common/info/exclude"; n=$((n+1)); }
      grep -qxF '.graphifyignore' "$common/info/exclude" 2>/dev/null \
        || echo '.graphifyignore' >> "$common/info/exclude"
    done
  done
  echo "install: graphify: exclude lines converged (graphify-out/, .graphifyignore; $n new)"
}

# ── _inst_graph_hooks_off — remove the legacy graphify git hooks (O6b) ────
# The legacy graphify git hooks are UNCONTAINED (detached Popen, no
# nice/MemoryMax/slice, a 16-way fan-out mid-wave) and their interpreter is
# pinned at install time (`_PINNED=`) to a path the venv this file's own
# `_inst_graphify_engine` provisions replaces. Remove hooks that are WHOLLY
# graphify-generated; refuse (and report) any that chain other content —
# never eat an operator's own hook partially.
#
# DETECTION (Task 0 step 4, measured on all 9 hooked repos in the fleet):
# every hooked post-commit and post-checkout is byte-identical and WHOLLY
# graphify's own output — line 1 the shebang, line 2 the opening marker
# (`# graphify-hook-start` for post-commit, `# graphify-checkout-hook-start`
# for post-checkout), a body, and the matching closing marker
# (`# graphify-hook-end` / `# graphify-checkout-hook-end`) as the file's LAST
# NON-EMPTY line, with nothing after it. That shape — the opening marker on
# line 2 AND the closing marker as the last non-empty line — is the
# WHOLLY-graphify test below. It is stronger and more faithful to what
# graphify actually writes than grepping every non-shebang, non-blank line
# for the word "graphify" would be (a plan-stage sketch this function does
# NOT use), and it is what lets a hook that CHAINS other content — an
# operator's own lines before or after graphify's block — be told apart and
# refused rather than partially eaten.
_inst_graph_hooks_off() {
  [ "$INST_ROLE" = server ] && return 0
  local roots=("$HOME/projects" "$HOME/worktrees") root d hooks h n=0 kept=0
  local ts backups start end line2 last
  ts=$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S); backups="$HOME/ccrc-backups/$ts"
  for root in "${roots[@]}"; do
    [ -d "$root" ] || continue
    for d in "$root"/*/ "$root"/*/*/; do
      [ -d "$d" ] || continue
      git -C "$d" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1 || continue
      hooks="$(git -C "$d" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)/hooks"
      for h in "$hooks"/post-commit "$hooks"/post-checkout; do
        [ -f "$h" ] || continue
        grep -qi graphify "$h" || continue
        case "${h##*/}" in
          post-commit) start='# graphify-hook-start'; end='# graphify-hook-end' ;;
          post-checkout) start='# graphify-checkout-hook-start'; end='# graphify-checkout-hook-end' ;;
          *) continue ;;
        esac
        line2="$(sed -n '2p' "$h")"
        last="$(grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' "$h" | tail -n1)"
        if [ "$line2" != "$start" ] || [ "$last" != "$end" ]; then
          echo "install: graphify: $h chains non-graphify content — left in place; remove by hand" >&2
          kept=$((kept+1)); continue
        fi
        mkdir -p "$backups"
        cp -a "$h" "$backups/$(echo "$h" | tr / _)" && rm -f "$h" && n=$((n+1))
      done
    done
  done
  echo "install: graphify: legacy git hooks — $n removed (backed up), $kept chained ones reported"
}

# ── _inst_wrappers — the converger, called as a function ──────────────────
# NOT `ccrc wrappers` as a subprocess, and not a copy of its logic: it is in
# this file, so it is one function call, and its output (per-account lines,
# refusals with remedies, the `summary:` line) reaches the operator's terminal
# exactly as it would from the standalone verb. NO FLAGS, deliberately —
# `--force` and `--adopt` are the two that decide what may be overwritten, and
# an install that passed either would be an install that can destroy a
# hand-written launcher without anybody typing the flag that authorises it.
#
# A REFUSAL IS A FAILED INSTALL. `cmd_wrappers` returns 1 when it refused
# something, having already printed the file, the reason and the remedy for each
# one; this adds the one sentence that turns "some files were refused" into
# "this install did not finish", and deliberately does not re-word the refusals
# themselves (the rule `_inst_accounts_sh` states, again).
#
# On the default single-account roster this writes NOTHING: the one account is
# `upstream`, which this verb never writes under any flag, so the summary reads
# `1 account(s) … 0 written`. That is the converged answer, not a no-op to be
# optimised away — the run still proves the roster and `$HOME/.local/bin` agree,
# which is the state doctor's `wrappers` check judges two steps later.
_inst_wrappers() {
  cmd_wrappers || _ccrc_die "wrapper convergence refused — read the lines above"
  echo "install: wrappers: converged (the summary line above is ccrc wrappers' own)"
}

# ── cmd_update — the box moves to a published release (stage 4, Task 6) ───
# Per box, explicit, never automatic (spec §4). The sequence, each step
# refusing loudly rather than degrading:
#   1. FETCH + VERIFY (`_upd_fetch`): SHA256SUMS first (under `latest/` the
#      tag is unknown until the sums file names the tarball — install.sh's
#      own order), then the tarball; `sha256sum -c` BEFORE extraction (the
#      outer checksum guards transport), then the per-file MANIFEST after it
#      (the manifest guards the SET — a file corrupted after the MANIFEST was
#      generated ships in a tarball whose SHA256SUMS is perfectly honest).
#      A box with no network answers with curl's failure and changes nothing.
#   2. BACK UP (`_upd_backup`): coord.db by VACUUM INTO (never cp — the WAL
#      has been measured holding 10x the main file; backup-coord.mjs's
#      header), plus dists, ccd and the units, all under ONE
#      `~/ccrc-backups/<ts>/`. BEFORE the install touches anything, so a
#      fault at any later step leaves the backup complete — the ordering is
#      pinned by a test that injects exactly that fault.
#   3. INSTALL: the `_inst_*` spine, re-run FROM THE STAGED TREE by invoking
#      the staged `ccrc install` (role-aware, per the CCRC_ROLE this box
#      recorded in ccrc.env). Not a re-implementation and not a sourced
#      function list: the sequence is spelled ONCE, in `cmd_install`, and a
#      step added there must run on update without anyone remembering to add
#      it here too. The staged verb ends with `cmd_doctor` from the NEW tree,
#      which is this verb's doctor too — running a second doctor from the
#      tree this run just replaced would measure the box with the old check
#      table and print every verdict twice.
#   4. SWEEP (`_upd_sweep`): every live `claude-session@*` supervisor is
#      try-restarted onto the ccd this run just installed — behind the
#      MANDATORY KillMode=process preflight (R1, granted 2026-08-21; the
#      one scoped exception to CLAUDE.md's never-touch rule). A failed
#      preflight refuses the SWEEP, never the UPDATE: the run continues to
#      its report, degraded and saying so.
#   5. REPORT (`_upd_report`): from → to off the build stamps — the old one
#      read before anything changed, the new one re-measured off disk, never
#      inferred from what step 3 was expected to do. A `--to` DOWNGRADE also
#      prints the coord.db restore commands from this run's backup and NEVER
#      performs them: migrations are forward-only, and an older server READS
#      a newer coord.db (shipped behavior) — auto-restoring would destroy
#      rows to fix a compatibility problem the system does not have.
#
# THE FLEET-FIRST WARNING (`_upd_fleet_warn`, D-150 aware): on a box that
# knows its server's address, `/api/fleet/health` saying `skewed` earns a
# WARN naming the order (fleet box first) — a warning, never a refusal,
# because the operator mid-rollout is exactly who must be able to proceed.
# A 401 is the session gate answering correctly from outside (D-150), and an
# unreachable server is a box that cannot compare; both skip the comparison
# SILENTLY — a diagnostic that is not available is not a finding.
cmd_update() {
  local to=""
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      --to)
        [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "$PROG: --to needs a value: a release tag shaped vX.Y.Z" >&2; usage >&2; exit 2; }
        to="$2"; shift ;;
      --to=*) to="${1#--to=}" ;;
      *) _ccrc_usage_die "$1" ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  if [ -n "$to" ] && [[ ! "$to" =~ ^v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$ ]]; then
    echo "$PROG: --to expects a release tag shaped vX.Y.Z (got: $to)" >&2
    usage >&2
    exit 2
  fi

  # BY NAME, BEFORE ANYTHING RUNS — `cmd_install`'s own probe discipline: a
  # missing tool three steps in would arrive as that step "failing", and two
  # conditions an operator acts on differently (install the tool / debug the
  # release) must not share a signal. `node` is probed here even though the
  # staged install probes it again, because `_upd_backup` runs it FIRST
  # (backup-coord.mjs) and a backup that failed for a missing interpreter
  # must not read as a broken coord.db.
  local t
  # awk: _upd_sweep's unit enumeration rides it — absent, the per-unit
  # preflight would silently narrow to the template probe (branch review).
  for t in curl tar gzip node awk; do
    command -v "$t" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || _ccrc_die "$t is required by 'ccrc update' but is not on PATH — nothing on this box was changed"
  done

  # The OLD identity, read before anything changes — the report's "from"
  # half. `rc` per condition, same split `cmd_status` renders: absent is the
  # honest "unstamped", anything else unreadable — never collapsed into a
  # version nobody measured.
  local old_desc="unstamped" old_version="" rc=0
  _box_build_fields || rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) old_version="${BOX_BUILD[4]}"
       if [ -n "$old_version" ]; then old_desc="$old_version (${BOX_BUILD[0]})"
       else old_desc="${BOX_BUILD[0]}"; fi ;;
    3) old_desc="unstamped" ;;
    *) old_desc="unreadable" ;;
  esac

  _upd_fleet_warn
  _upd_fetch "$to"
  _upd_backup

  # THE RECORDED ROLE (spec §5): `ccrc.env`'s CCRC_ROLE, written by
  # `_inst_env` at first install. Absent — an older box whose env file
  # predates the key — means the staged install runs BARE and its own
  # default (both, today's single-box shape) decides, exactly as a fresh
  # `ccrc install` would. An unrecognisable value is treated as absent for
  # the same reason: passing it through would make the staged install refuse
  # a run this box's operator never mistyped.
  local role=""
  if [ -f "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ] && [ -r "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    role="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_ROLE)"
  fi
  case "$role" in server|fleet|both) ;; *) role="" ;; esac

  echo "update: installing $UPD_VERSION from the verified staged tree${role:+ (recorded role: $role)}"
  local inst_rc=0
  if [ -n "$role" ]; then
    bash "$UPD_TREE/ccd/ccrc" install --role "$role" || inst_rc=$?
  else
    bash "$UPD_TREE/ccd/ccrc" install || inst_rc=$?
  fi
  [ "$inst_rc" -eq 0 ] \
    || _ccrc_die "the staged install (which ends with doctor) exited $inst_rc — read its lines above. The backup taken BEFORE it ran is complete at $UPD_BACKUP_DIR"

  _upd_sweep
  _upd_report "$old_desc" "$old_version"
}

# One warning, or silence — never a refusal and never a guess. Address
# resolution and the health call are the two readers this file already has
# (`_box_server_addr`, `_box_health`); every non-zero answer from either is a
# comparison that is NOT AVAILABLE, which is not a finding: no address (a
# fleet box, ordinarily), a gate that answered 401 (D-150 — correct behaviour
# by both parties), a server that is down. Only a server that ANSWERED and
# said `skewed` produces a line.
_upd_fleet_warn() {
  local addr_out addr
  addr_out="$(_box_server_addr)" || return 0
  addr="${addr_out%%$'\n'*}"
  _box_health "$addr" || return 0
  if [ "${BOX_HEALTH[2]}" = skewed ]; then
    echo "update: WARN: /api/fleet/health at $addr says the two boxes' builds are SKEWED — the update order is fleet box first, then this server box. This run proceeds; update the fleet box too, then re-check with: ccrc status"
  fi
  return 0
}

# Fetch + verify, into a throwaway staging dir this process removes on ANY
# exit. The URL space is GitHub's release layout (`latest/download/<asset>`,
# `download/<tag>/<asset>`) under the one owner/repo pair at the top;
# `install.sh --release` walks the same space with the same order — SUMS
# first, because under `latest/` only the sums file knows the tarball's name.
_upd_fetch() {   # $1 = pinned tag, or "" for the newest release
  local to="$1" base url_dir _sum tarname
  base="${CCRC_RELEASE_BASE_URL:-https://github.com/$CCRC_RELEASE_OWNER/$CCRC_RELEASE_REPO/releases}"
  if [ -n "$to" ]; then url_dir="$base/download/$to"; else url_dir="$base/latest/download"; fi
  UPD_STAGE="$(_plat_mktemp_d)" || _ccrc_die "mktemp -d failed — no staging dir could be made, nothing was fetched"
  # The staging tree holds nothing this run does not either install or
  # refuse, so it is removed on every exit — success, die, or Ctrl-C.
  trap '[ -z "$UPD_STAGE" ] || rm -rf "$UPD_STAGE"' EXIT
  echo "update: fetching $url_dir/SHA256SUMS …"
  curl -fsSL -o "$UPD_STAGE/SHA256SUMS" "$url_dir/SHA256SUMS" \
    || _ccrc_die "download failed: $url_dir/SHA256SUMS (is there a release?) — nothing on this box was changed"
  read -r _sum tarname < "$UPD_STAGE/SHA256SUMS" \
    || _ccrc_die "SHA256SUMS is empty — refusing; nothing on this box was changed"
  tarname="${tarname#\*}"
  case "$tarname" in
    ccrc-*.tar.gz) ;;
    *) _ccrc_die "SHA256SUMS names no ccrc tarball (got: $tarname) — refusing; nothing on this box was changed" ;;
  esac
  UPD_VERSION="${tarname#ccrc-}"
  UPD_VERSION="${UPD_VERSION%.tar.gz}"
  echo "update: fetching $url_dir/$tarname …"
  curl -fsSL -o "$UPD_STAGE/$tarname" "$url_dir/$tarname" \
    || _ccrc_die "download failed: $url_dir/$tarname — nothing on this box was changed"
  # Verify BEFORE extracting: a tarball that fails its checksum never puts a
  # file on disk, let alone runs one (install.sh's rule, same words).
  ( cd "$UPD_STAGE" && _plat_sha256_check SHA256SUMS >/dev/null 2>&1 ) \
    || _ccrc_die "checksum verification FAILED for $tarname — refusing to extract or install; nothing on this box was changed"
  UPD_TREE="$UPD_STAGE/tree"
  mkdir "$UPD_TREE" || _ccrc_die "cannot create $UPD_TREE"
  tar -xzf "$UPD_STAGE/$tarname" -C "$UPD_TREE" \
    || _ccrc_die "extracting $tarname failed — nothing outside the staging dir was touched"
  [ -f "$UPD_TREE/MANIFEST" ] \
    || _ccrc_die "the release ships no MANIFEST — refusing to install a set nothing can verify"
  ( cd "$UPD_TREE" && _plat_sha256_check MANIFEST >/dev/null 2>&1 ) \
    || _ccrc_die "MANIFEST verification FAILED after extraction — a file in the release set does not match its recorded digest; refusing to install"
  echo "update: verified $tarname (transport checksum, then the per-file MANIFEST)"
}

# Deploy's backup set (deploy.sh:363-369 agent lane, :762-766 server lane),
# both lanes' union because a single box may be both, each entry guarded —
# absence is the ordinary state of the role a box is not. coord.db goes
# through the shipped backup-coord.mjs (VACUUM INTO — a cp of a WAL-mode db
# is a plausible-looking backup missing everything recent, worse than none),
# resolved out of THIS tree (`$CCRC_HERE/..`): the tool that snapshots the
# old world ships beside the ccrc doing the snapshotting, never fetched.
_upd_backup() {
  # The optional argument (Task 8) is the LINE PREFIX for the one
  # announcement below — `cmd_backup` runs this very function standalone and
  # a transcript line that said "update:" from a verb nobody ran would be a
  # claim about a run that never happened. The SET and the directory shape
  # are the same by construction: one function, two callers.
  local say="${1:-update: backup}"
  local ts
  ts="$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)"
  UPD_BACKUP_DIR="$BOX_BACKUP_ROOT/$ts"
  mkdir -p "$UPD_BACKUP_DIR" || _ccrc_die "cannot create $UPD_BACKUP_DIR — refusing to install with no backup taken"
  if [ -f "$HOME/.ccrc/coord.db" ]; then
    node --no-warnings "$CCRC_HERE/../deploy/backup-coord.mjs" \
      "$HOME/.ccrc/coord.db" "$UPD_BACKUP_DIR/coord.db" \
      || _ccrc_die "the coord.db snapshot failed — refusing to install with an incomplete backup (read backup-coord.mjs's lines above)"
  fi
  _upd_backup_copy "$BOX_TREE_DIR/server/dist"     server-dist
  _upd_backup_copy "$BOX_TREE_DIR/server/dist-pwa" dist-pwa
  _upd_backup_copy "$BOX_TREE_DIR/agent/dist"      agent-dist
  _upd_backup_copy "$HOME/.local/bin/ccd"          ccd
  _upd_backup_copy "$HOME/.cc-sessions/session-hook.sh" session-hook.sh
  _upd_backup_copy "$HOME/.cc-sessions/notify.sh"  notify.sh
  # THE JOB FILES, UNDER THE NAMES THIS BOX ACTUALLY HAS THEM. On Linux that
  # is the unit list plus the session TEMPLATE; on macOS there is no template
  # (launchd has none — `ccd` mints a plist per session) and the names are
  # labels, so the loop below would have copied nothing and the backup would
  # have been silently short of the very files a rollback needs. Measured by
  # `ccrc-update.test.ts`'s "the backup is COMPLETE before the install step
  # runs".
  #
  # The per-SESSION plists are deliberately not swept: an update does not
  # touch them (they carry no version), and copying every live session's job
  # into every backup would grow with the fleet for no rollback value.
  local u
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    for u in "${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[@]}"; do
      _upd_backup_copy "$BOX_UNIT_DIR/$(_svc_label "$u").plist" "$(_svc_label "$u").plist"
    done
  else
    for u in "${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[@]}" claude-session@.service; do
      _upd_backup_copy "$BOX_UNIT_DIR/$u" "$u"
    done
  fi
  echo "$say: $UPD_BACKUP_DIR (coord.db snapshot, dists, ccd, units — taken before anything was installed)"
}

_upd_backup_copy() {   # <src> <name under $UPD_BACKUP_DIR> — absent src is ordinary
  [ -e "$1" ] || return 0
  cp -a "$1" "$UPD_BACKUP_DIR/$2" \
    || _ccrc_die "backing up $1 failed — refusing to install with an incomplete backup"
}

# ── _upd_sweep — step 4: the supervisor sweep (stage 4, Task 7; R1) ───────
# Every `claude-session@*` unit is a LONG-LIVED `ccd supervise`: even after
# the atomic install above, each live supervisor keeps executing the
# PRE-update ccd (the old inode) until restarted, so auto-swap, auto-compact
# and uuid-sync run yesterday's logic for days. deploy.sh's sweep
# (deploy.sh:662-673) exists for exactly this, and this function is its
# in-process twin — the operator ruling of 2026-08-21 (R1) grants BOTH the
# one scoped exception to CLAUDE.md's never-touch rule, and a source pin in
# `server/test/single-definition.test.ts` holds the two implementations to
# the same preflight so neither can drop it silently.
#
# THE PREFLIGHT IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE BLOCK, and it is MANDATORY:
# systemd's default KillMode is control-group, and whenever the tmux server
# placement falls back to a bare create (no systemd-run, no D-Bus — see
# deploy.sh's sweep header, "THE KillMode GUARD SURVIVES THE PLACEMENT FIX")
# every session on this box is a child of ONE tmux server sitting in
# whichever claude-session@ cgroup created it: without KillMode=process the
# try-restart below is a fleet kill, not a restart. So the guard ASKS SYSTEMD
# (`systemctl show` resolves the whole override chain — base, drop-ins,
# lexical order, last wins), per unit the sweep would touch PLUS an
# uninstantiated template probe (a fresh box with zero sessions would
# otherwise check nothing at all), and the enumeration is deliberately
# UNFILTERED — the opposite of the verify loop's `--state=active`, for
# deploy.sh's stated reason: this is a CONFIG gate, and over-listing is the
# cheap direction.
#
# A FAILED PREFLIGHT REFUSES THE SWEEP, NEVER THE UPDATE (spec §6): the
# install above already converged, and unwinding a good update over a
# refused sweep would leave the box worse than degraded. The refusal is loud
# (stderr names the resolved value and the drop-in that fixes it) and the
# degraded line on stdout says exactly what did not happen.
#
# PANES ARE NEVER TOUCHED; TMUX IS NEVER TOUCHED. KillMode=process keeps the
# tmux substrate — the sessions themselves — alive across the restart, and
# `cmd_supervise` re-enters via `cmd_ensure`, which ATTACHES to a live
# session rather than spawning a second one. A test pins that no tmux argv
# appears anywhere in a sweep.

# ── the launchd stay-up gate — verify-service.sh's technique, in-process ──
# `launchctl kickstart -k` and `launchctl bootstrap` return as soon as launchd
# ACCEPTS the job, exactly as `systemctl restart` returns at the fork — they
# say nothing about whether the process stayed up. verify-service.sh closes
# that gap on Linux with two MainPID samples either side of a window LONGER
# than the unit's RestartSec; these two helpers are the same promise against
# launchd, whose restart delay is the plist's ThrottleInterval (3, written by
# `_svc_write_session_plist` and `_inst_plist_server`). A crash-looping job
# changes its pid inside a window longer than that; a pid that did not change
# is a pid that did not change. The knobs are verify-service.sh's own
# (`CCRC_VERIFY_SETTLE`/`CCRC_VERIFY_WINDOW`), honoured for the same reason:
# a test must not wait 8 real seconds per case.
#
# Darwin-only by CALL SITE, not by branch: both callers (`_upd_sweep`'s and
# `_inst_enable`'s Darwin arms) already sit inside a `$CCD_OS = darwin` arm,
# so these carry no platform switch of their own — the Linux twin is
# deploy/verify-service.sh, which reads systemd's vocabulary and stays the
# one implementation there (single-definition's sweep pin holds the Linux
# halves together; the Darwin halves are pinned by the same file's Darwin-arm
# test).
_ccrc_job_pid() {   # <unit> -> the job's pid on stdout, or nothing when it has none
  local out
  out="$(_svc_launchctl print "$(_svc_domain)/$(_svc_label "$1")" 2>/dev/null)" || return 0
  case "$out" in
    *"pid = "*) out="${out#*pid = }"; printf '%s' "${out%%[!0-9]*}" ;;
  esac
  return 0
}
_ccrc_job_stayed_up() {   # <unit> -> 0 iff ONE pid held across the window; detail in CCRC_STAYED_DETAIL
  local p1 p2
  sleep "${CCRC_VERIFY_SETTLE:-3}"
  p1="$(_ccrc_job_pid "$1")"
  sleep "${CCRC_VERIFY_WINDOW:-5}"
  p2="$(_ccrc_job_pid "$1")"
  CCRC_STAYED_DETAIL="pid ${p1:-none} -> ${p2:-none} across ${CCRC_VERIFY_SETTLE:-3}s+${CCRC_VERIFY_WINDOW:-5}s"
  [ -n "$p1" ] && [ "$p1" = "$p2" ]
}
_upd_sweep() {
  # ── THE DARWIN ARM ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Same job, same guard, different observable. The sweep exists because a
  # live supervisor keeps executing the OLD ccd until it is restarted, and its
  # refusal exists because a restart that kills the whole process group would
  # take every pane with it. On Linux the property is `KillMode=process`, read
  # back from systemd. On macOS it is `AbandonProcessGroup` — and it is read
  # back from the PLIST ON DISK rather than from launchd, because that file is
  # what launchd will act on at the next kickstart and `launchctl print` does
  # not report the key at all.
  #
  # A plist missing the key is a session enabled by an OLDER ccd, before the
  # key was written. Refusing is right for exactly the Linux reason: the sweep
  # would kill panes. The remedy is per-session and safe — `ccd start <id>`
  # rewrites the plist through `_svc_write_session_plist`.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    local u sid plist missing=0
    for u in $(_svc_list_sessions); do
      plist="$(_svc_plist "$u")"
      [ -f "$plist" ] || continue
      if ! grep -q 'AbandonProcessGroup' "$plist"; then
        # `ccd start` takes the BARE session id — both decorations stripped,
        # or the remedy hands the operator an id ccd does not recognise.
        sid="${u#claude-session@}"; sid="${sid%.service}"
        echo "update: sweep REFUSED — $(_svc_plist "$u") carries no AbandonProcessGroup, and the sweep needs it: without that key launchd kills the job's whole process group on restart, and whenever the tmux server placement falls back to a bare create every session on this box is a child of ONE tmux server — so a restart would take the lot. Re-enable that session to rewrite its job file: ccd start $sid" >&2
        missing=1
      fi
    done
    if [ "$missing" = 1 ]; then
      # STDOUT, exactly as the Linux arm's DEGRADED line is: the two-stream
      # split is the function's stated contract (the header above — stderr is
      # the diagnosis, stdout is the transcript of what the update did and did
      # not do), and a stdout-only capture of a macOS run must not read as a
      # clean update. The remedy tail names the per-session fix the same way
      # the Linux line names its try-restart.
      echo "update: DEGRADED: the supervisor sweep did not run — every live claude-session@ supervisor keeps executing the PREVIOUS ccd until restarted. Fix the job files above (ccd start <id> rewrites each), then restart per session: launchctl kickstart -k $(_svc_domain)/<label>"
      return 0
    fi
    # `_svc_try_restart`'s `_svc_is_loaded || return 0` skips a job launchd no
    # longer holds — which is exactly where a `.svcfailed` session sits: the
    # start-limit emulation booted it out when it gave up (`cmd_supervise`).
    # The Linux arm warns about `--state=failed` units try-restart skipped;
    # silence here would be the same session, skipped with no line at all.
    # A plist that is merely NOT LOADED (next login will bootstrap it) is not
    # this update's business and is skipped without comment, exactly as a
    # stopped unit is on Linux.
    local -a kicked=()
    for u in $(_svc_list_sessions); do
      if [ "$(_svc_is_active "$u")" = failed ]; then
        sid="${u#claude-session@}"; sid="${sid%.service}"
        echo "update: warning: $u is FAILED (start-limit stamp) — the sweep skipped it and did not verify it. On the box: ccd start $sid (it clears the stamp and re-bootstraps the job)" >&2
        continue
      fi
      _svc_is_loaded "$u" || continue
      _svc_try_restart "$u" \
        || _ccrc_die "restarting $u failed — the supervisors' state is now mixed; read: $(_svc_status_hint "$u"). The pre-update backup is complete at $UPD_BACKUP_DIR"
      kicked+=("$u")
    done
    # THE STAY-UP GATE, and it is the half the first cut of this arm dropped:
    # kickstart's exit is the exit of the KICK, not of the process surviving
    # it — the exact gap deploy/verify-service.sh's header describes for
    # systemd, and the reason the Linux arm below runs that script per active
    # unit. Each restarted supervisor is held to the same standard here, and
    # the failure names the rollback the way the Linux `_ccrc_die` does —
    # this is the one line in the whole update path that points an operator
    # at the backup, and on macOS it must be able to fire.
    for u in ${kicked[@]+"${kicked[@]}"}; do
      _ccrc_job_stayed_up "$u" \
        || _ccrc_die "$u was restarted and did not stay up ($CCRC_STAYED_DETAIL) — read: $(_svc_status_hint "$u"). The pre-update backup is complete at $UPD_BACKUP_DIR"
    done
    echo "update: sweep: every live claude-session@ supervisor now runs the ccd this update installed (AbandonProcessGroup verified in every job file; ${#kicked[@]} restarted and re-measured still up)"
    return 0
  fi
  # The bus contingency `_inst_enable` carries, for the same reason: without
  # XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, `systemctl --user` from a non-interactive context fails
  # for a reason that has nothing to do with this box.
  : "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:=/run/user/$UID}"; export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
  : "${DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS:=unix:path=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}/bus}"; export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
  local u km
  for u in $(systemctl --user list-units "claude-session@*" --plain --no-legend | awk '{print $1}') \
           claude-session@ccrc-update-preflight.service; do
    km="$(systemctl --user show -p KillMode "$u" 2>/dev/null)" || km=""
    if [ "$km" != "KillMode=process" ]; then
      echo "update: sweep REFUSED — $u resolves to ${km:-no answer from systemd}, and the sweep needs KillMode=process: systemds default is control-group, and whenever the tmux server placement falls back to a bare create every session on this box is a child of ONE tmux server sitting in a claude-session@ cgroup, so try-restart would kill the lot. A drop-in under $BOX_UNIT_DIR/claude-session@.service.d/ can set this without the base unit changing a byte." >&2
      echo "update: DEGRADED: the supervisor sweep did not run — every live claude-session@ supervisor keeps executing the PREVIOUS ccd until restarted. Fix the KillMode above, then: systemctl --user try-restart 'claude-session@*'"
      return 0
    fi
  done
  systemctl --user try-restart "claude-session@*" \
    || _ccrc_die "systemctl --user try-restart claude-session@* failed — the supervisors' state is now mixed; read: systemctl --user list-units 'claude-session@*'"
  # try-restart is a no-op on a failed unit (its own man page), so a
  # pre-existing failed session was neither restarted nor verified — not this
  # update's doing, must not fail it, must not be silent either.
  for u in $(systemctl --user list-units "claude-session@*" --state=failed --plain --no-legend | awk '{print $1}'); do
    echo "update: warning: $u is FAILED — try-restart skipped it and this sweep did not verify it. On the box: systemctl --user reset-failed $u, then ccd start the session" >&2
  done
  # Each restarted supervisor is held to the same standard as the services the
  # staged install just verified — verify-service.sh out of the NEW tree,
  # guarded by [ -f ] exactly as `_inst_enable` guards it. `--state=active`
  # here and NOT above: handing verify-service.sh a unit try-restart never
  # touched is deploy.sh's final-review finding 6.
  local verify="$BOX_TREE_DIR/deploy/verify-service.sh"
  for u in $(systemctl --user list-units "claude-session@*" --state=active --plain --no-legend | awk '{print $1}'); do
    if [ -f "$verify" ]; then
      bash "$verify" "$u" \
        || _ccrc_die "$u was restarted and did not stay up — read: systemctl --user status $u. The pre-update backup is complete at $UPD_BACKUP_DIR"
    fi
  done
  echo "update: sweep: every live claude-session@ supervisor now runs the ccd this update installed (KillMode=process verified per unit before any restart; panes untouched)"
}

# The from → to report, off MEASUREMENTS: the "from" was read before anything
# changed and arrives as arguments; the "to" is re-read off disk here — never
# inferred from what the install was expected to write, which is exactly how
# a stub of a step would go unnoticed. The downgrade lines fire on a version
# comparison (`sort -V`) between the OLD stamp and the ARTIFACT's version —
# both vX.Y.Z by construction — and print the restore; nothing here ever
# writes coord.db.
_upd_report() {   # $1 = old description, $2 = old version ("" when unknown)
  local old_desc="$1" old_version="$2" new_desc="unstamped" rc=0
  _box_build_fields || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
    if [ -n "${BOX_BUILD[4]}" ]; then new_desc="${BOX_BUILD[4]} (${BOX_BUILD[0]})"
    else new_desc="${BOX_BUILD[0]}"; fi
  elif [ "$rc" -ne 3 ]; then
    new_desc="unreadable"
  fi
  echo "update: build: $old_desc -> $new_desc"
  if [[ "$old_version" =~ ^v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$ ]] \
     && [[ "$UPD_VERSION" =~ ^v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$ ]] \
     && [ "$UPD_VERSION" != "$old_version" ] \
     && [ "$(printf '%s\n%s\n' "$old_version" "$UPD_VERSION" | sort -V | head -n1)" = "$UPD_VERSION" ]; then
    echo "update: rollback: $old_version -> $UPD_VERSION is a DOWNGRADE. coord.db was NOT restored — migrations are forward-only, and an older server reads a newer coord.db (shipped behavior). To restore the pre-update snapshot by hand:"
    if [ -f "$UPD_BACKUP_DIR/coord.db" ]; then
      echo "update: rollback:   $([ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ] && printf 'launchctl bootout %s/%s' "$(_svc_domain)" "$(_svc_label ccrc.service)" || printf 'systemctl --user stop ccrc.service')"
      echo "update: rollback:   cp $UPD_BACKUP_DIR/coord.db \$HOME/.ccrc/coord.db"
      echo "update: rollback:   $(_svc_restart_hint ccrc.service)"
    else
      echo "update: rollback:   (no coord.db existed before this run — there is nothing to restore)"
    fi
  fi
}

# ── cmd_backup — update's step 2 standalone (stage 4, Task 8; spec §7) ────
# The SAME set to the SAME directory shape, because it is the same function:
# `_upd_backup`, called with this verb's own line prefix. What this verb adds
# is the pruning `cmd_update` deliberately does not do: an operator running
# `ccrc backup` on a schedule is the caller deploy.sh's `prune_backups`
# exists for, and a backup directory that only ever grows is that function's
# own measured failure case (18MB/13 dirs before it existed).
cmd_backup() {
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      *) _ccrc_usage_die "$1" ;;
    esac
  done
  # BY NAME, before anything runs — `cmd_update`'s own probe discipline, for
  # the same first-step reason: backup-coord.mjs is a node program, and a
  # backup that failed for a missing interpreter must not read as a broken
  # coord.db.
  command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _ccrc_die "node is required by 'ccrc backup' (it runs deploy/backup-coord.mjs for the coord.db snapshot) but is not on PATH — nothing was backed up"
  _upd_backup backup
  _bak_prune
}

# Keep the newest ${CCRC_BACKUP_KEEP:-10} TIMESTAMPED backups —
# `deploy.sh`'s `prune_backups` glob exactly (`[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{6}`, the shape
# `_upd_backup`'s `date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S` writes), because the directory also
# holds hand-made siblings (a real `pre-flip-agent-dist` exists on the fleet
# host today) that a bare newest-N sweep would silently destroy. The glob
# expansion is already lexically sorted, which for this shape IS
# chronological order.
_bak_prune() {
  local keep="${CCRC_BACKUP_KEEP:-10}"
  [[ "$keep" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] \
    || _ccrc_die "CCRC_BACKUP_KEEP must be a number (got a non-numeric value) — nothing was pruned"
  local -a dirs=()
  local had_nullglob=0 d
  shopt -q nullglob && had_nullglob=1
  shopt -s nullglob
  for d in "$BOX_BACKUP_ROOT"/[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]; do
    [ -d "$d" ] && dirs+=("$d")
  done
  [ "$had_nullglob" -eq 1 ] || shopt -u nullglob
  local n=${#dirs[@]} i
  for ((i = 0; i < n - keep; i++)); do
    rm -rf -- "${dirs[$i]}" \
      || _ccrc_die "could not prune ${dirs[$i]} — the newer backups above it are all intact"
    echo "backup: pruned ${dirs[$i]} (keeping the newest $keep timestamped backups; hand-made siblings are never touched)"
  done
}

# ── cmd_logs — the journal, thin (stage 4, Task 8; spec §7) ───────────────
# `journalctl --user -u <this box's unit>` with `-f`/`-n` passed straight
# through — no parsing, no filtering: journalctl's own interface is the
# feature, and this verb only answers the question an operator would
# otherwise have to know the topology for: WHICH unit is this box's.
# The RECORDED role decides (`ccrc.env`'s CCRC_ROLE, `cmd_update`'s own
# reader), never a probe of what happens to be running: a fleet box whose
# agent is down is exactly the box whose logs are being asked for.
cmd_logs() {
  local -a pass=()
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      -f) pass+=(-f) ;;
      -n)
        [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "$PROG: -n needs a value: how many journal lines" >&2; usage >&2; exit 2; }
        pass+=(-n "$2"); shift ;;
      *) _ccrc_usage_die "$1" ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  local role="" unit=ccrc.service
  if [ -f "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ] && [ -r "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    role="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_ROLE)"
  fi
  [ "$role" = fleet ] && unit=ccrc-agent.service
  # ── macOS HAS NO JOURNAL, so the job writes a FILE and this verb reads it ──
  # systemd captures a unit's stdout/stderr into the journal and `journalctl
  # --user -u` is how an operator gets it back. launchd has no such store: the
  # only way a LaunchAgent's output is kept at all is `StandardOutPath` /
  # `StandardErrorPath` in its own plist, which `_inst_plist_server` sets to
  # $HOME/.ccrc/logs/<label>.log. So the file this reads is the file the
  # installer told launchd to write — one decision, spelled in the plist, read
  # back here.
  #
  # `tail` TAKES THE SAME TWO FLAGS this verb already accepts (-f, -n N), so
  # the arguments an operator types do not change across platforms; that is
  # why they are collected into `pass` above rather than translated here.
  #
  # THE MISSING FILE IS ITS OWN REFUSAL, and it is not the same sentence as a
  # missing journal: a box whose server has never started has no log yet, and
  # telling an operator to go and read a file that is not there is the dead
  # end this arm exists to avoid.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    local log="$HOME/.ccrc/logs/$(_svc_label "$unit").log"
    [ -f "$log" ] \
      || _ccrc_die "no log at $log — launchd writes one only once $(_svc_label "$unit") has run at least once. Check the job first: $(_svc_status_hint "$unit")"
    exec tail ${pass[@]+"${pass[@]}"} "$log"
  fi
  command -v journalctl >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _ccrc_die "journalctl is not on PATH — this box's service journal cannot be read from here"
  exec journalctl --user -u "$unit" ${pass[@]+"${pass[@]}"}
}

# ── cmd_uninstall — off the box, reinstall left safe (stage 4, Task 8) ────
# Spec §7's three sets, exactly:
#   REMOVES  the units (stop, disable, delete — both drop-ins and the slice
#            escape included), ccrc's managed settings.json hook entries
#            (through the installer's OWN predicate, `--remove` mode, with
#            its per-file backup), marker-verified wrappers ONLY
#            (`shared/mark.mjs` — the marker is the proof ccrc wrote the
#            file; nothing else is ccrc's to delete), ccrc's own artifacts
#            inside ~/.cc-sessions file-by-file, `~/ccrc`, and the
#            executables `_inst_bins` placed.
#   PRESERVES `~/.ccrc` whole (the box's config, roster and identity — what
#            makes reinstall converge instead of starting over), the
#            registry rows and operator switches in ~/.cc-sessions,
#            worktrees (sessions' WORK — never ccrc's), ~/ccrc-backups, and
#            the two operator-namespace files (`~/.tmux.conf`,
#            `~/.claude/statusline-command.sh`) whose `.pre-ccrc-<UTC>`
#            keep-asides get their restore commands PRINTED, never run.
#   REFUSES  while live sessions exist (counted the way ccd counts them,
#            `_box_sessions`) unless --force: uninstalling under a live
#            fleet orphans every supervisor mid-carry.
# `--purge` additionally removes `~/.ccrc` and ~/ccrc-backups — NEVER
# worktrees, never tmux state (no tmux argv exists anywhere in this verb).
cmd_uninstall() {
  local force=0 purge=0
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      --force) force=1 ;;
      --purge) purge=1 ;;
      -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
      *) _ccrc_usage_die "$1" ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  # BY NAME, before anything is removed — the file's own probe discipline.
  # node is the marker verifier (shared/mark.mjs) and jq is the settings.json
  # surgeon (install-session-hooks.sh); a removal pass that discovered either
  # missing halfway through would leave a box neither installed nor not.
  local t
  for t in node jq; do
    command -v "$t" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
      || _ccrc_die "$t is required by 'ccrc uninstall' but is not on PATH — nothing on this box was removed"
  done

  # ── THE LIVE-SESSION GATE, FIRST ────────────────────────────────────────
  # `_box_sessions` answers "N in <reg> (…)" or "0 (…)"; the count is its
  # first word. The refusal is the DEFAULT: an operator who really means it
  # says so with the flag, and the message tells them both halves.
  local sessions count
  sessions="$(_box_sessions)"
  count="${sessions%% *}"
  if [ "$count" != 0 ] && [ "$force" -eq 0 ]; then
    _ccrc_die "this box holds $count live ccd session(s) ($sessions) — uninstalling under them orphans every supervisor. Stop them first (ccd list, then ccd stop each), or run: ccrc uninstall --force"
  fi

  _uninst_units
  _uninst_hooks
  _uninst_wrappers
  _uninst_cc_sessions
  _uninst_graphify_skills
  _uninst_tree_bins
  _uninst_keep_asides
  [ "$purge" -eq 1 ] && _uninst_purge
  if [ "$purge" -eq 1 ]; then
    echo "uninstall: done — this box is off ccrc. Worktrees and the session registry rows were preserved; ~/.ccrc and ~/ccrc-backups are gone (--purge)"
  else
    echo "uninstall: done — this box is off ccrc, and reinstall is safe: ~/.ccrc (config, roster, identity), the session registry rows, worktrees and ~/ccrc-backups were all preserved"
  fi
}

# Stop, disable, delete — in that order, because a unit file removed under a
# still-enabled unit leaves systemd holding a dangling enablement symlink.
# `disable --now` per unit that is actually installed here; a failure is
# REPORTED and the removal continues (the unit FILE going away is this
# step's contract — a wedged systemd must not leave the box half-uninstalled
# with the transcript claiming otherwise). `claude-session@*` INSTANCES are
# never a systemctl target: the never-touch rule holds even on the way out —
# deleting the template file does not touch the live instances, and their
# operator stops them through ccd, not through an uninstall's side effect.
_uninst_units() {
  local dir="$BOX_UNIT_DIR"
  # ── THE DARWIN ARM: different files, same three promises ────────────────
  # Stop it, forget it, remove it. There is no template unit and no drop-in
  # directory to sweep here (launchd has neither), and the per-session plists
  # are deliberately NOT touched for the same reason the Linux arm leaves
  # `claude-session@<id>` instances alone: an operator stops sessions through
  # ccd, not through an uninstall's side effect.
  #
  # `bootout` BEFORE the file is removed, and `disable` after it: a label that
  # is booted out but still enabled comes back at the next login, and a plist
  # deleted while launchd still holds the job leaves a job nothing on disk
  # explains — the state this box was in after this port's first test run.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    local u lbl
    if _have_systemctl; then
      for u in "${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[@]}"; do
        lbl="$(_svc_label "$u")"
        [ -f "$dir/$lbl.plist" ] || continue
        _svc_launchctl bootout "$(_svc_domain)/$lbl" 2>/dev/null \
          || echo "uninstall: units: bootout $lbl failed (continuing — the job file is removed below; read: $(_svc_status_hint "$u"))"
        _svc_launchctl disable "$(_svc_domain)/$lbl" 2>/dev/null || :
      done
    else
      echo "uninstall: units: launchctl is not on PATH — the job files are removed but nothing was stopped or disabled"
    fi
    for u in "${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[@]}"; do
      rm -f -- "$dir/$(_svc_label "$u").plist" \
        || _ccrc_die "removing the job files under $dir failed"
    done
    echo "uninstall: units: stopped, disabled and removed (no drop-ins or slice on macOS — launchd has neither)"
    return 0
  fi
  # The `\x2d` literal is `_inst_units`' own (see its header: systemd's
  # escaping of the `-` in the slice name, kept verbatim by double quotes).
  local slice="$dir/app-claude\x2dsession.slice.d"
  local u
  if command -v systemctl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    : "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:=/run/user/$UID}"; export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
    : "${DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS:=unix:path=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}/bus}"; export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
    for u in "${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[@]}" ccd-cap-scopes.timer ccd-cap-scopes.service \
      ccd-graph-sweep.timer ccd-graph-sweep.service; do
      [ -f "$dir/$u" ] || continue
      systemctl --user disable --now "$u" \
        || echo "uninstall: units: disable --now $u failed (continuing — the unit file is removed below; read: systemctl --user status $u)" >&2
    done
  else
    echo "uninstall: units: systemctl is not on PATH — the unit files are removed but nothing was stopped or disabled"
  fi
  rm -f -- "$dir/${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[0]}" "$dir/${BOX_UNIT_NAMES[1]}" \
    "$dir/claude-session@.service" "$dir/ccd-cap-scopes.service" "$dir/ccd-cap-scopes.timer" \
    "$dir/ccd-graph-sweep.service" "$dir/ccd-graph-sweep.timer" \
    || _ccrc_die "removing the unit files under $dir failed"
  rm -rf -- "$dir/claude-session@.service.d" "$slice" \
    || _ccrc_die "removing the drop-in directories under $dir failed"
  if command -v systemctl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    systemctl --user daemon-reload \
      || echo "uninstall: units: daemon-reload failed — systemd may still list units whose files are gone until the next reload" >&2
  fi
  echo "uninstall: units: stopped, disabled and removed (incl. both drop-ins and the slice escape)"
}

# THE TREE'S OWN installer, in `--remove` mode — never the INSTALLED copy at
# ~/.cc-sessions (the opposite of `_inst_hooks`' choice, for a reason that
# only exists on this path): an installed copy that predates the flag does
# not KNOW `--remove`, and its argument handling would read the call as a
# default INSTALL run — an uninstall that re-registered every hook it was
# asked to remove. The sibling at $CCRC_HERE is version-locked to this very
# function by shipping in the same tree.
_uninst_hooks() {
  local inst="$CCRC_HERE/install-session-hooks.sh"
  if [ ! -f "$inst" ]; then
    echo "uninstall: hooks: $inst is missing (a partial tree) — no settings.json was touched; remove ccrc's entries by hand if any remain"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ ! -f "$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh" ]; then
    echo "uninstall: hooks: no roster projection at \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh — no account homes to sweep, no settings.json was touched"
    return 0
  fi
  bash "$inst" --remove \
    || _ccrc_die "install-session-hooks.sh --remove refused — read its lines above; it never rewrites a settings.json it could not validate, so nothing was damaged"
  echo "uninstall: hooks: ccrc's managed entries removed from each account's settings.json (unmanaged entries survive byte-identically; every rewritten file was backed up first)"
}

# MARKER-VERIFIED ONLY. The candidates are every legal-id-named file in the
# wrapper directory, and the verdict on each is `shared/mark.mjs`'s
# `verifyMarker` — the same module `gen-wrappers.mjs` classifies with, read
# through node because bash re-implementing the digest would be the second
# definition the marker exists to prevent. The verdicts and what they earn:
#   ccrc-unmodified -> REMOVED: exactly what ccrc last wrote, nothing lost.
#   ccrc-edited     -> KEPT, and said: the marker proves ccrc wrote it, the
#                      hash proves someone worked on it since — that work is
#                      the operator's, exactly as `cmd_wrappers` refuses to
#                      overwrite it without --force.
#   foreign / oversize / unverifiable -> KEPT SILENTLY: a marker-less file
#                      with a wrapper's name is somebody's hand-written
#                      launcher (or the upstream binary itself), and "I
#                      could not verify it" is never license to delete it.
# The >1MiB stat guard is `gen-wrappers.mjs`'s own OVERSIZE gate from the
# other language (D-92's cross-language class): the 324MB upstream binary is
# never read, let alone judged.
_uninst_wrappers() {
  local shape="$CCRC_HERE/ccrc-wrapper-shape"
  if [ ! -f "$shape" ]; then
    echo "uninstall: wrappers: the shape contract is missing ($shape) — no wrapper was judged and none was removed"
    return 0
  fi
  # shellcheck source=ccrc-wrapper-shape
  . "$shape" \
    || _ccrc_die "the wrapper shape contract failed to load: $shape"
  local mark="$CCRC_HERE/../shared/mark.mjs"
  if [ ! -f "$mark" ]; then
    echo "uninstall: wrappers: the marker module is missing ($mark) — nothing can be verified, so nothing was removed"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ ! -d "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR" ]; then
    echo "uninstall: wrappers: no $WRAPPER_BIN_DIR on this box — nothing to remove"
    return 0
  fi
  local f name verdict removed=0
  for f in "$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR"/*; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    name="${f##*/}"
    # The four executables are `_uninst_tree_bins`' subject, not wrappers
    # (graphify Task 10/fix-round F2 adds `ccd-graph-sweep` to this case,
    # or the wrapper arm would misclassify it instead of the bin arm below
    # ever getting a say).
    case "$name" in ccd|ccrc|ccd-cap-scopes|ccd-graph-sweep) continue ;; esac
    [[ "$name" =~ $WRAPPER_ID_RE ]] || continue
    verdict="$(node --no-warnings --input-type=module -e '
import { readFileSync, statSync } from "node:fs";
import { pathToFileURL } from "node:url";
const [markPath, filePath] = process.argv.slice(1);
if (statSync(filePath).size > 1024 * 1024) { console.log("oversize"); process.exit(0); }
const { verifyMarker } = await import(pathToFileURL(markPath).href);
console.log(verifyMarker(readFileSync(filePath, "utf8")));
' "$mark" "$f" 2>/dev/null)" || verdict=""
    case "$verdict" in
      ccrc-unmodified)
        rm -f -- "$f" || _ccrc_die "could not remove $f"
        removed=$((removed + 1))
        echo "uninstall: wrappers: removed $f (marker-verified: exactly what ccrc last wrote)" ;;
      ccrc-edited)
        echo "uninstall: wrappers: kept $f — ccrc wrote it but it has been edited since, and that edit is yours; remove it by hand if it is worthless" ;;
      *) : ;;   # foreign, oversize, or unverifiable — not ccrc's to remove.
    esac
  done
  echo "uninstall: wrappers: $removed marker-verified wrapper(s) removed; everything ccrc could not prove it wrote was left in place"
}

# File-by-file, NEVER a directory sweep of ~/.cc-sessions: the registry rows
# (`<id>.uuid` and friends) and the operator switches (`coordinator-paused`,
# `mail-disabled` — removable only by a human at a terminal, CLAUDE.md) live
# in the same directory, and a glob one character too wide here is a fleet
# whose sessions all lost their identity. The list below is `_inst_files` +
# `_inst_skills`' install set, exactly.
_uninst_cc_sessions() {
  local reg="$HOME/.cc-sessions"
  if [ ! -d "$reg" ]; then
    echo "uninstall: ~/.cc-sessions: not present — nothing of ccrc's to remove there"
    return 0
  fi
  rm -f -- "$reg/session-hook.sh" "$reg/install-session-hooks.sh" "$reg/notify.sh" \
    "$reg/install-coordinator-skill.sh" "$reg/install-worker-skill.sh" \
    "$reg/install-graphify-skill.sh" \
    || _ccrc_die "removing ccrc's files under $reg failed"
  rm -rf -- "$reg/coordinator-skill" "$reg/worker-skill" \
    || _ccrc_die "removing the skill trees under $reg failed"
  echo "uninstall: ~/.cc-sessions: ccrc's own artifacts removed (hooks, notify, both skill trees); the registry rows and operator switches stay"
}

# ── _uninst_graphify_skills — the per-home graphify skill directories ─────
# `_uninst_cc_sessions` removes ccrc's OWN artifacts under `$HOME/.cc-sessions`
# — including the deployed `install-graphify-skill.sh` itself — but the skill
# it assembles lives one level down, in EACH ROSTERED HOME's own
# `skills/graphify`: the identical asymmetry `_uninst_wrappers` and
# `_uninst_hooks` already sweep by roster rather than by a single directory,
# because skills resolve per `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` and there is no one place
# under `~/.cc-sessions` that holds every home's copy.
#
# SOURCED IN A SUBSHELL, `_inst_dirs`' reason: the roster projection declares
# `_ccrc_cfg_dir` in THIS FILE's own `_ccrc_*` namespace, and a subshell means
# its code cannot outlive this one step.
#
# realpath-de-duplicated for the reason `install-graphify-skill.sh` de-dupes on
# the way IN: two rostered homes can symlink `skills/` into the same real
# directory (`.claude-gpt` -> `.claude/skills` on the reference fleet), and a
# second `rm -rf` through that symlink would be a no-op anyway — de-duping
# first keeps the reported count honest about how many directories this run
# actually touched.
_uninst_graphify_skills() {
  local sh="$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh"
  if [ ! -f "$sh" ]; then
    echo "uninstall: graphify skills: no roster projection at $sh — no account homes to sweep"
    return 0
  fi
  local n
  n="$(
    # shellcheck source=/dev/null
    . "$sh" || exit 1
    removed=0
    declare -A seen=()
    for a in ${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[@]+"${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[@]}"}; do
      d="$(_ccrc_cfg_dir "$a")"
      [ -n "$d" ] && [ -d "$d/skills" ] || continue
      real="$(realpath "$d/skills" 2>/dev/null)" || continue
      [ -n "${seen[$real]:-}" ] && continue
      seen[$real]=1
      [ -d "$real/graphify" ] || continue
      rm -rf -- "$real/graphify" || exit 1
      removed=$((removed + 1))
    done
    printf '%s' "$removed"
  )" || _ccrc_die "could not remove the graphify skill directories named by $sh"
  echo "uninstall: graphify skills: removed from $n account home(s); other skills in the same directories were left alone"
}

# The tree, then the executables `_inst_bins` placed OUT of it. Removing the
# file bash is currently executing is safe — the inode outlives the
# directory entry until this process exits — which is why this step needs no
# staging dance on the way down.
_uninst_tree_bins() {
  rm -rf -- "$BOX_TREE_DIR" \
    || _ccrc_die "removing $BOX_TREE_DIR failed"
  # `ccd-cap-scopes` is `_inst_bins`' third executable: an uninstall that
  # removed its units (above) and left the binary would strand an orphan on
  # PATH for ever (spec §7 predates the third bin; the ledger records this).
  # `ccd-graph-sweep` is the fourth (graphify Task 10/fix-round F2, same
  # reasoning exactly — its own units were already removed by `_uninst_units`
  # above, and leaving the binary behind orphans it the same way).
  rm -f -- "$HOME/.local/bin/ccd" "$HOME/.local/bin/ccrc" "$HOME/.local/bin/ccd-cap-scopes" \
    "$HOME/.local/bin/ccd-graph-sweep" \
    || _ccrc_die "removing the executables from \$HOME/.local/bin failed"
  echo "uninstall: tree: ~/ccrc removed; ccd, ccd-cap-scopes, ccd-graph-sweep and the ccrc launcher removed from \$HOME/.local/bin"
  # ── the FIFTH entry, and the only one that is not ccrc's own binary ──────
  # `_inst_graphify_engine` converges `$HOME/.local/bin/graphify` onto the
  # pinned venv, and the rule the four names above follow binds here too: an
  # uninstall that removed the engine's home (`--purge` takes `~/.ccrc` whole,
  # a few lines below) and left the link would leave a DANGLING `graphify`
  # first on every session's PATH — strictly worse than the box was before
  # ccrc, because the pip shim that used to answer there was copied aside by
  # the install and never put back (`_uninst_keep_asides` prints that `mv`).
  # D-1347: the install grew a fifth name and this census did not, because it
  # is hand-kept.
  #
  # ONLY IF IT IS OURS, which is `_uninst_wrappers`' rule one function up:
  # "everything ccrc could not prove it wrote was left in place". Proof here is
  # the link's own target, read with plain `readlink` — ONE HOP, no `-f`, so no
  # box is asked for a tool it may not have (D-1348's lesson on the install
  # side) — against the exact literal `_inst_graphify_engine` writes. A regular
  # file (the hand-written launcher that step REFUSES to touch) and a symlink
  # pointing anywhere else are both reported and kept.
  local glink="$HOME/.local/bin/graphify" gtgt=""
  if [ -L "$glink" ]; then
    gtgt="$(readlink "$glink" 2>/dev/null)" || gtgt=""
    if [ "$gtgt" = "$HOME/.ccrc/graphify-venv/bin/graphify" ]; then
      rm -f -- "$glink" || _ccrc_die "removing $glink failed"
      echo "uninstall: tree: graphify removed from \$HOME/.local/bin (it was ccrc's link into the pinned venv)"
    else
      echo "uninstall: tree: kept $glink — it is a symlink to ${gtgt:-something this box cannot read}, not the link ccrc wrote; remove it by hand if it is worthless"
    fi
  elif [ -e "$glink" ]; then
    echo "uninstall: tree: kept $glink — ccrc never wrote it (the install refuses to touch a launcher it did not write); remove it by hand if it is worthless"
  fi
}

# The keep-asides `_inst_keep_aside` wrote for the two operator-namespace
# files. PRINTED, never performed: the file at the live path may carry the
# operator's own edits made since the install, and a restore that ate them
# would be the very damage the keep-aside existed to prevent. Restoring is
# one visible `mv`, and it is theirs to run.
#
# THE THIRD GLOB is the graphify shim `_inst_graphify_engine` copies aside
# before repointing `$HOME/.local/bin/graphify` (D-1349), and it belongs here
# for exactly the reason the other two do: `_uninst_tree_bins` has just removed
# ccrc's link from that path, so the `mv` this loop prints puts the operator's
# own pip shim back where it was — the difference between "off ccrc" and "off
# ccrc with a hole where your graphify used to be". It runs AFTER that step
# (see `cmd_uninstall`'s sequence), so the target path is already free.
_uninst_keep_asides() {
  local f said=0
  local had_nullglob=0
  shopt -q nullglob && had_nullglob=1
  shopt -s nullglob
  for f in "$HOME/.tmux.conf.pre-ccrc-"* "$HOME/.claude/statusline-command.sh.pre-ccrc-"* \
    "$HOME/.local/bin/graphify.pre-ccrc-"*; do
    if [ "$said" -eq 0 ]; then
      echo "uninstall: keep-asides: the files ccrc copied aside before replacing them are still there — restore any of them with:"
      said=1
    fi
    echo "uninstall: keep-asides:   mv $f ${f%.pre-ccrc-*}"
  done
  [ "$had_nullglob" -eq 1 ] || shopt -u nullglob
  return 0
}

# `--purge` only. NEVER worktrees (~/worktrees is sessions' WORK — ccd's
# WORKTREES_ROOT, human-gated even inside ccd itself) and never tmux state:
# no tmux argv exists anywhere in this verb, purge included.
_uninst_purge() {
  rm -rf -- "$HOME/.ccrc" "$BOX_BACKUP_ROOT" \
    || _ccrc_die "purging ~/.ccrc and $BOX_BACKUP_ROOT failed"
  echo "uninstall: purge: ~/.ccrc and ~/ccrc-backups removed (worktrees and tmux state are never touched)"
}

# Guard so the script can be `source`d (the way server/test/ccd-clip.test.ts:32
# and server/test/ccd-workspaces.test.ts:487 source ccd itself, to call one
# function directly without running the whole CLI) without running a command
# — same guard ccd carries at its own dispatch (ccd/ccd:13487). (Round-1
# review, Minor 4: this comment used to cite server/test/roster-generate.test.ts,
# which sources the GENERATED ~/.ccrc/accounts.sh — a different file, whose
# `_ccrc_*` helpers come from shared/generate.mjs, not from ccd. Fixed to name
# the tests that actually source ccd.)
#
# TASK 22: THAT ROUND-1 FIX NAMED THE RIGHT FILES AND TWO WRONG LINES, and it
# stood that way for a whole build — which is why this note exists rather than a
# silent re-edit. Measured at Task 22: `ccd-workspaces.test.ts:394` is a
# `.toBe(false)` inside a `_reg_purge` assertion (its `source "${CCD}"` is at
# :487), and `ccd/ccd:8578` is a comment inside an attic helper (that guard is
# at :13487). Both corrected by READING THE TARGET LINE — the step the round-1
# fix skipped, and the only one that distinguishes a correction from a
# reroll. `ccd-clip.test.ts:32` was re-read and HOLDS. The same wrong `:394`
# survives in server/test/ccrc-cli.test.ts:590, deliberately out of scope here.
if [[ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" == "${0}" ]]; then

# ── top-level argument parsing ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Only -h/--help is recognised ahead of the verb; anything else in that
# position IS the verb (data, not a flag), so there is no --flag/--flag=value
# loop to run at this level yet. That idiom (ccrc-adopt:97-105: --out PATH /
# --out=PATH, --force, -h|--help, unknown -> usage+exit 2) is what each
# cmd_<verb> follows once a verb grows flags of its own.
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
    -*) _ccrc_usage_die "$1" ;;
    *) break ;;
  esac
done

VERB="${1:-}"
[ $# -eq 0 ] || shift

if [ -z "$VERB" ]; then
  echo "$PROG: missing verb" >&2
  usage >&2
  exit 2
fi

case "$VERB" in
  version) cmd_version "$@" ;;
  doctor)  cmd_doctor "$@" ;;
  status)  cmd_status "$@" ;;
  adopt)   cmd_adopt "$@" ;;
  wrappers) cmd_wrappers "$@" ;;
  account) cmd_account "$@" ;;
  install) cmd_install "$@" ;;
  update)  cmd_update "$@" ;;
  uninstall) cmd_uninstall "$@" ;;
  backup)  cmd_backup "$@" ;;
  logs)    cmd_logs "$@" ;;
  passwd)  cmd_passwd "$@" ;;
  expose)  cmd_expose "$@" ;;
  *) _ccrc_usage_die "$VERB" ;;
esac

fi
